const e="65 chapters",t=[{id:"chapter-12",number:12,title:"-는 것 같다 · -잖아(요) vs -거든(요)",goal:"Use -는 것 같다 to express soft opinions and guesses without sounding arrogant",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 생각~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"생각",romanization:"saenggak",en:"thought, opinion, idea",pos:"noun",nuance:"생각 is the native Korean catch-all noun for a 'thought', 'idea', or 'opinion' — anything that happens in the mind: memories, intentions, plans, beliefs, judgments. It is far broader and softer than Sino-Korean 의견 (a defined stance for discussion): 좋은 생각이에요 praises an idea, whereas 의견 frames it as a formal position. It also overlaps with 마음 ('heart/mind' as feeling/will), but 생각 is the cognitive 'thinking' side while 마음 is the emotional/volitional side — 생각이 바뀌다 = I changed my mind (reasoning), 마음이 바뀌다 = my heart changed (feelings). Register-neutral and one of the highest-frequency nouns in the language. Key collocations: 생각이 나다 (come to mind/remember), 생각이 들다 (a thought arises), 생각해 보다 (think it over), ~라고 생각하다 (to think that ~). The big English-speaker pitfall is the verb pattern: 'I think that it's true' is 사실이라고 생각해요 with the quotative ~라고, not 사실 생각해요; and 'I remember' is most naturally 생각나요, not a literal translation. Don't confuse 생각나다 (it comes to mind, intransitive) with 생각하다 (to actively think, transitive)."},sentence:{ko:"제 생각에는 이 방법이 더 좋을 것 같아요.",en:"In my opinion, I think this method is better."},target:"생각",options:["thought, opinion, idea","reason","development, pacing (of a story)"],note:"제 생각에는 = 'in my view' — a standard phrase for introducing soft opinions in conversation"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"의견",romanization:"uigyeon",en:"opinion, view",pos:"noun",nuance:"의견 is Sino-Korean (意見, 意 'mind/intention' + 見 'view/seeing'), literally 'how your mind sees something' — a considered position or judgment you can defend, usually about a topic, plan, or issue. The crucial contrast is with 생각: 생각 is the broad word for any 'thought' that crosses your mind, while 의견 is specifically a stance offered for discussion or evaluation, so a meeting asks for 의견 (positions), not just 생각 (musings). It is register-neutral and very common in school and workplace settings; the polite request 의견을 여쭤봐도 될까요? raises it a notch. Natural collocations: 의견을 말하다/내다/제시하다 (state/put forward), 의견이 다르다/같다 (disagree/agree), 의견을 모으다 (gather opinions), 반대 의견 (an opposing view). The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 의견 every time you'd say 'I think' — 'I think it's good' is 좋은 것 같아요 or 좋다고 생각해요, NOT 좋은 의견이에요; 의견 is the opinion itself as a thing, not the act of thinking. Also don't say 의견하다 — there is no such verb; you 의견을 말하다."},sentence:{ko:"당신의 의견을 말해 주세요.",en:"Please share your opinion."},target:"의견",options:["opinion, view","reason","development, pacing (of a story)"],note:"More formal than 생각 — used in meetings, discussions, and written Korean"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이유",romanization:"iyu",en:"reason",pos:"noun",nuance:"이유 is Sino-Korean (理由, 理 'principle/logic' + 由 'cause/origin'), the 'reason' or grounds behind something — the answer to 'why?'. Its sharpest contrast is with 원인 (原因, 'cause'): 이유 is the justification or motive a person gives ('the reason I'm late'), often subjective and explainable, while 원인 is the objective, often impersonal cause of a result ('the cause of the fire', 화재의 원인). So you ask a friend 무슨 이유로 늦었어? but an investigator looks for the 원인 of an accident. Also distinguish 까닭, a more native, slightly literary synonym of 이유. Register-neutral and extremely common. Core collocations: 이유가 있다/없다, 이유를 묻다/대다/설명하다, 이유 없이 (for no reason), 그런 이유로 (for that reason), ~는 이유는 (the reason that ~ is). The English-speaker pitfall: don't bolt 이유 onto a 'because' clause redundantly — Korean already has 왜냐하면 ... 때문이에요 / ~기 때문에 to say 'because', so '왜냐하면 그 이유는...' is clunky; pick one. Also, 'the reason is because...' should be ~는 이유는 ... 때문이에요, where the sentence ends in 때문이다, not a second 이유."},sentence:{ko:"이유가 있거든요. 설명할게요.",en:"There's a reason, you see. Let me explain."},target:"이유",options:["thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view","reason"],note:"-거든요 introduces the fact of having a reason as new information, then promises the full explanation"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지루하다",romanization:"jirukhada",en:"to be boring, tedious",pos:"adjective",nuance:"지루하다 is 'boring/tedious' for something that DRAGS — a long meeting, a dull movie, a slow lecture — the quality of an experience that wears your patience thin. This is the mirror image of 심심하다, and keeping them straight is one of the most useful distinctions in this set: 지루하다 describes the boring THING/experience (영화가 지루해 = 'the movie is boring'), while 심심하다 describes a bored PERSON with nothing to do (나는 심심해 = 'I'm bored'). So 'I'm bored' (idle) ≠ 'this is boring' (tedious) — the first is 심심해요, the second 지루해요. As a regular 하다-adjective it is 지루해요 (not 지루하요), modifier 지루한. A near-synonym is 따분하다, which is similar 'dull/tedious' with a slightly more listless, fed-up flavor; 지루하다 is the neutral default. English-speaker pitfall: because English uses one word 'boring/bored' for both the cause and the feeler, learners say 나는 지루해요 meaning 'I'm bored' — but that literally implies 'I am a boring thing.' For your own idle boredom say 심심해요; reserve 지루하다 for the tedious thing itself."},sentence:{ko:"그 강의 진짜 지루한 것 같아요.",en:"That lecture seems really boring."},target:"지루한",options:["to be interesting, fun","to be boring, tedious","thought, opinion, idea"],note:"Adjective → -(으)ㄴ 것 같다: 지루한 것 같다. Bare 지루해요 would sound like a harsh verdict"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"재미있다",romanization:"jaemiitda",en:"to be interesting, fun",pos:"adjective",nuance:"재미있다 is literally 재미 (fun, enjoyment) + 있다 (to exist) — 'fun exists [in it]' — which is exactly why it conjugates like 있다 and gives 재미있어요, and why the noun-modifying form is the 있다-style 재미있는 N (재미있는 영화), never 재미있은. It spans both English 'fun' and 'interesting': a party, a game, or a class can all be 재미있어요, so let context choose the English. The near-synonym to separate is 재미있다 vs 흥미롭다: 재미있다 is the warm, everyday 'enjoyable/entertaining' you say about a movie or a friend, while 흥미롭다 is a cooler, more bookish 'intriguing/of interest' you might use for a research finding or a phenomenon — calling a thrilling roller coaster 흥미로워요 sounds oddly academic. Its direct opposite is the single word 재미없다 (재미 + 없다, 'fun does not exist') = boring; do not say 안 재미있다 as your default negative — 재미없어요 is the natural 'it's boring', and 안 재미있어요 reads more like 'it's not [particularly] fun'. The big English-speaker pitfall is the conjugation: because the headword ends in 있다, learners over-apply -하다 or vowel rules and produce 재미있다요 or 재미있아요; the only correct polite form is 재미있어요, with past 재미있었어요."},sentence:{ko:"저는 재미있었거든요. 오히려 좋았어요.",en:"I found it fun, actually. I rather liked it."},target:"재미있었",options:["to be boring, tedious","to feel a slight lack, to be a (gentle) disappointment","to be interesting, fun"],note:"-거든요: introducing a contrasting opinion the other person didn't know you held — new information to explain your reaction"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전개",romanization:"jeongae",en:"development, pacing (of a story)",pos:"noun",nuance:"Use 전개 for the flow of a story or argument, not for a person's speed."},sentence:{ko:"이 소설은 전개가 느린 것 같아요.",en:"I think this novel's pacing is slow."},target:"전개",options:["thought, opinion, idea","development, pacing (of a story)","opinion, view"],note:"느리다 is an adjective → 느린 것 같다. 전개 is commonly used in film and book reviews"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"유진",ko:"그래요? 저는 꽤 재미있었는데요. 전개가 좀 느린 것 같긴 했지만.",en:"Really? I found it quite fun. Though I did think the pacing was a bit slow."},hl:"재미있"}],canDo:"Soften an opinion so it sounds less blunt (-는 것 같다)",id:"chapter-12-b1",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -는 것 같다",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-는 것 같다",sub:"soft opinions and impressions",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밖에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같",post:"아요.",hit:!0}],ko:"지금 밖에 비가 오는 것 같아요.",en:"It seems to be raining outside right now.",romanization:"jigeum bakke biga oneun geot gatayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"책은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"어려운",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같",post:"아요.",hit:!0}],ko:"이 책은 좀 어려운 것 같아요.",en:"This book seems a bit hard.",romanization:"i chaegeun jom eoryeoun geot gatayo."}],rule:{name:"-는 것 같다",rows:[{when:"Action verb, present (happening now)",add:"verb stem + 는 것 같다",ex:"오다 → 오는 것 같다 (seems to be coming); 먹다 → 먹는 것 같다"},{when:"Adjective, present (vowel-ending stem)",add:"stem + ㄴ 것 같다",ex:"비싸다 → 비싼 것 같다 (seems expensive); 크다 → 큰 것 같다"},{when:"Adjective, present (받침-ending stem)",add:"stem + 은 것 같다",ex:"작다 → 작은 것 같다 (seems small); 좋다 → 좋은 것 같다"},{when:"Verb, past",add:"stem + (으)ㄴ 것 같다",ex:"가다 → 간 것 같다 (seems to have gone); 먹다 → 먹은 것 같다"},{when:"Future / guess",add:"stem + (으)ㄹ 것 같다",ex:"오다 → 올 것 같다 (will probably come); 없다 → 없을 것 같다"},{when:"Noun",add:"noun + 인 것 같다",ex:"학생 → 학생인 것 같다 (seems to be a student)"}]},more:{func:"Use -는 것 같다 to say 'it seems / I think / probably' instead of stating something as a flat fact. It softens your opinion so you sound less blunt and more polite, even when you're fairly sure.",keyPoint:{label:"present 는 것 같다 vs future 을 것 같다",body:"Use -는 것 같다 for something you can sense right now (비가 오는 것 같아요 — I can hear/see it). Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같다 for a guess about the future or when you have no direct evidence yet (비가 올 것 같아요 — the sky looks like it). For the past, use -(으)ㄴ 것 같다 (간 것 같아요). Don't worry about every irregular form yet — just get the present/past/future split right."},pronunciation:"것 같다 sounds like [걷 깓따]: 것 ends in a [t], which tenses the next ㄱ so 같 comes out as [깓]. In casual speech it often blurs to [거 같아요]."}}],canDo:"Soften an opinion so it sounds less blunt (-는 것 같다)",id:"chapter-12-b2",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 결말~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"결말",romanization:"gyeolmal",en:"ending, conclusion (of a story)",pos:"noun",nuance:"결말 is about narrative ending; 끝 is more general and less review-like."},sentence:{ko:"결말이 좀 아쉬운 것 같아요.",en:"I think the ending was a bit disappointing."},target:"결말",options:["ending, conclusion (of a story)","thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view"],note:"아쉽다 (to feel a lack, to be slightly disappointing) → 아쉬운 것 같다. Softening a criticism with -것 같다 is natural here"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"배우",romanization:"baeu",en:"actor/actress",pos:"noun",nuance:"배우 is 俳優 (both 俳 and 優 historically meant 'performer/entertainer'), an old Sino-Korean compound that already covers actors of any gender in film, TV drama, and theater — so there is no need to mark gender, and the English loan 액터 is essentially never used in normal speech. The thing an actor does is 연기 'acting' (연기를 하다 / 연기를 잘하다), not '배우를 하다'; that 연기 vs 배우 split (craft vs person) mirrors 요리 vs 요리사 and trips learners up. Distinguish 배우 from 가수 'singer', 개그맨/코미디언 'comedian', and the broader 연예인 'celebrity/entertainer', which includes all of them. The verb 나오다 ('come out') is the idiomatic way to say an actor 'appears in' a film or show. After the vowel, the copula is 배우예요, and one 배우가 된다."},sentence:{ko:"그 배우 연기 정말 잘하거든요. 꼭 봐 보세요.",en:"That actor is genuinely great at acting, you see. Definitely watch it."},target:"배우",options:["actor/actress","thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view"],note:"-거든요 here shares a personal assessment as new information, then follows up with a recommendation"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"연기",romanization:"yeongi",en:"acting, performance",pos:"noun",nuance:"연기 is the noun; 연기하다 is the verb 'to act/perform.'"},sentence:{ko:"연기가 자연스러운 것 같아요.",en:"The acting seems natural."},target:"연기",options:["acting, performance","thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view"],note:"자연스럽다 (to be natural) → irregular: 자연스러운 것 같다. The ㅂ irregular applies here"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아쉽다",romanization:"aswipda",en:"to feel a slight lack, to be a (gentle) disappointment",pos:"adjective",nuance:"아쉽다 is mild, wistful regret — 'that's a shame / I wish it were otherwise' — the feeling when something nice didn't happen, ended too soon, or fell just short. It's soft; it's NOT deep sadness. The variant ladder: 아쉽다/아쉬워 (casual) → 아쉬워요 (polite) → 아쉽네(요) (in-the-moment reaction to news) → 좀 아쉽다 (softened, noting a small lack). Note the conjugation: 아쉽다 is a -ㅂ irregular adjective, so the polite stem is 아쉬워요 (아쉽+어요 → 아쉬워요), never 아쉽어요. The dividing line against 슬프다 and 속상하다: 아쉽다 is a gentle 'aw, too bad' about a missed chance or something pleasant ending; 슬프다 is real sadness (loss, grief); 속상하다 is being genuinely hurt or upset inside (frustration, heartache). Sold-out tickets → 아쉽다; a pet dying → 슬프다; your work criticized unfairly → 속상하다. 아쉽다 also covers the sense of 'feeling a lack / something's missing' (뭔가 아쉽다 'something feels lacking'), so context decides. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 슬프다 for everyday letdowns — to a Korean ear that's melodramatic."},sentence:{ko:"끝이 좀 아쉬운 것 같아요.",en:"I think the end was a bit unsatisfying."},target:null,options:["to feel a slight lack, to be a (gentle) disappointment","to be interesting, fun","thought, opinion, idea"],note:"ㅂ irregular adjective: 아쉽다 → 아쉬운 것 같다. This exact phrase is very common in Korean reviews and casual talk"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"주인공",romanization:"juingong",en:"main character, protagonist",pos:"noun",nuance:"주인공 can refer to a fictional main character or the lead person in a real event."},sentence:{ko:"주인공이 너무 완벽한 것 같아요.",en:"The protagonist seems a bit too perfect."},target:"주인공",options:["main character, protagonist","thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view"],note:"완벽하다 (to be perfect) → 완벽한 것 같다. Common critique in drama reviews"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공감하다",romanization:"gonggamhada",en:"to empathize, to relate to",pos:"verb",nuance:"공감하다 means more than understanding the facts: it means emotionally relating to someone’s feeling, experience, or point of view. The usual target takes 에 — 그 말에 공감해요, 주인공의 마음에 공감해요 — whereas 동의하다 focuses on agreeing that an opinion or proposal is correct. You can understand someone without agreeing with them, so 이해하다, 공감하다, and 동의하다 are not interchangeable. In conversation, 공감돼요 is extremely common and presents the reaction as something that naturally ‘lands’ with you: 저도 그 부분이 공감돼요. 공감하다 is useful for films, relationships, workplace stories, and any moment when you want to say ‘I can relate.’"},sentence:{ko:"그 인물한테 공감하기 어려운 것 같아요.",en:"I think it's hard to relate to that character."},target:"공감하",options:["to empathize, to relate to","to recommend","thought, opinion, idea"],note:"공감하기 어렵다 = 'it's hard to empathize' — the -기 turns 공감하다 into a noun, then 어렵다 (hard) applies → 어려운 것 같다"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"유진",ko:"음, 그래도 배우들 연기가 굉장히 좋은 것 같았어요.",en:"Hmm, but I thought the acting was really good though."},hl:"배우"}],canDo:"Give a reason as new information the listener didn't know (-거든요)",id:"chapter-12-b3",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -잖아(요) vs -거든(요)",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-잖아(요) vs -거든(요)",sub:"shared info vs new info",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"빨리",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"우리",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없",mid:"잖아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"빨리 가요. 우리 시간 없잖아요.",en:"Let's hurry. We don't have time, you know.",romanization:"ppalli gayo. uri sigan eopjanayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"A:",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"왜",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"안",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"와요?",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"B:",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"일이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많",mid:"거든요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"A: 왜 안 와요? B: 지금 일이 많거든요.",en:"A: Why aren't you coming? B: The thing is, I've got a lot of work right now.",romanization:"A: wae an wayo? B: jigeum iri mankeodeunyo."}],rule:{name:"-잖아(요) vs -거든(요)",rows:[{when:"-잖아요 after a verb/adjective stem (present)",add:"stem + 잖아요",ex:"있다 → 있잖아요; 바쁘다 → 바쁘잖아요 (you know I'm busy)"},{when:"-잖아요 after past",add:"stem + 았/었/했 + 잖아요",ex:"가다 → 갔잖아요 (we did go, remember); 먹다 → 먹었잖아요"},{when:"-잖아요 after a noun",add:"noun + (이)잖아요",ex:"학생 → 학생이잖아요; 친구 → 친구잖아요"},{when:"-거든요 after a verb/adjective stem (present)",add:"stem + 거든요",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘거든요 (the thing is, I'm busy); 좋다 → 좋거든요"},{when:"-거든요 after past",add:"stem + 았/었/했 + 거든요",ex:"가다 → 갔거든요 (the thing is, I went); 먹다 → 먹었거든요"},{when:"-거든요 after a noun",add:"noun + (이)거든요",ex:"학생 → 학생이거든요; 친구 → 친구거든요"}]},more:{func:"Both end a sentence to give a reason or background, but they point in opposite directions. -잖아(요) reminds the listener of something you BOTH already know ('you know...', 'remember?'). -거든(요) gives information that's NEW to the listener, often the reason behind what you just said.",keyPoint:{label:"-잖아요 (you know) vs -거든요 (the thing is)",body:"Ask yourself: does the listener already know this? If YES, use -잖아요 ('you know...', 'remember?'). If NO and you're telling them for the first time, use -거든요 ('the thing is...', 'because...'). Using -잖아요 for brand-new info can sound like you're scolding them for not already knowing."},pronunciation:"-잖아요 is pronounced [자나요]: 잖 has a silent ㅎ in its 받침, so it just links smoothly into 아요. -거든요 is simply [거든뇨], with the final 요 tucked on — no special change."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"We watched that movie together yesterday, remember? It was really fun.",sentence:"어제 그 영화 같이 봤___. 진짜 재미있었어요.",options:[{text:"잖아요",ok:!0},{text:"거든요",ok:!1}],explanation:"-잖아요 over a shared memory ('remember?')."}],canDo:"Give a reason as new information the listener didn't know (-거든요)",id:"chapter-12-b4",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 개인적으로~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"개인적으로",romanization:"gaeinjeoguro",en:"personally, in my personal opinion",pos:"adverb",nuance:"개인적으로 does not mean privately here; it marks a personal opinion."},sentence:{ko:"개인적으로는 이 영화가 좀 과대평가된 것 같아요.",en:"Personally, I think this movie is a bit overrated."},target:"개인적으로",options:["personally, in my personal opinion","reason","to be boring, tedious"],note:"개인적으로 + -것 같다 is a double-hedging strategy very common in Korean — softens with both 'personally' and 'seems'"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"솔직히",romanization:"soljiki",en:"honestly, frankly",pos:"adverb",nuance:"솔직히 is more direct than 개인적으로, so pair it with softer grammar when criticizing."},sentence:{ko:"솔직히 말하면 좀 실망했거든요.",en:"To be honest, I was a bit disappointed, you see."},target:"솔직히",options:["thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view","honestly, frankly"],note:"-거든요 after an honest admission: the speaker is sharing the real reason/feeling the listener may not have guessed"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"평가하다",romanization:"pyeonggahada",en:"to evaluate, to review",pos:"verb",nuance:"평가하다 means to evaluate, assess, or rate the value of something — work, performance, art, or even a person (評 'judge/criticize' + 價 'price/value', literally 'to price-judge'). That 價 'value' core is why it feels measured and official, the word on report cards, performance reviews, and product reviews. As a 하다 verb it contracts to 평가해요. With 높게/좋게 평가하다 it means 'to think highly of', and 낮게 평가하다 means 'to underrate' — note these always need the ADVERB (높게), a spot English speakers trip on by reaching for 높은. The synonym line to hold is 평가하다 vs 판단하다: 평가하다 weighs worth on a scale (high/low, good/bad), while 판단하다 reaches a yes/no decision about a situation. Because 평가하다 can sound like you are sitting in judgment, Koreans soften it when appraising people directly, or switch to 판단하다."},sentence:{ko:"사람마다 다르게 평가하는 것 같아요.",en:"It seems everyone evaluates it differently."},target:"평가하",options:["thought, opinion, idea","opinion, view","to evaluate, to review"],note:"Verb → -는 것 같다: 평가하는 것 같다. Using this form for a general observation about people in general is very natural"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기대하다",romanization:"gidaehada",en:"to expect, to look forward to",pos:"verb",nuance:"기대하다 is 期待(기대, 'period-await', i.e. 'await a hoped-for time') + 하다, and unlike English 'expect' it leans positive: it means to anticipate something good and look forward to it. The thing awaited takes 을/를 (여행을 기대해요). Its hugely common intransitive twin 기대되다/기대가 되다 ('to be exciting, to be looked forward to') describes the feeling welling up in you — 내일이 기대돼요 — and beginners must keep the two apart: you 기대하다 a thing, but a thing/situation 기대된다 to you. Contrast with 기다리다 ('wait'), which is just neutral temporal waiting with no hope ('버스를 기다려요'); and with 바라다 ('wish/hope') which is a desire without the sense of confident anticipation. As a 하다 verb: 기대해요 / 기대했어요. The pitfall: English speakers map 'expect' onto neutral or negative situations ('I expect rain', 'I expect you to be late') — Korean 기대하다 cannot carry that. For neutral prediction use 예상하다 (豫想); 기대하다 is only for things you hope will be good."},sentence:{ko:"기대했는데 좀 아쉬웠거든요.",en:"I had high expectations, so I was a bit let down, you see."},target:"기대했",options:["to expect, to look forward to","to recommend","thought, opinion, idea"],note:"-거든요 again here: explaining personal background (had expectations) that the listener didn't know — this is why the disappointment makes sense"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"추천하다",romanization:"chucheonhada",en:"to recommend",pos:"verb",nuance:"추천하다 is neutral and practical. In speech, 추천해 주세요 is much more useful than the dictionary form."},sentence:{ko:"이 드라마 추천할 만한 것 같아요.",en:"I think this drama is worth recommending."},target:"추천할",options:["to empathize, to relate to","to recommend","to expect, to look forward to"],note:"추천할 만하다 = 'worth recommending' → 추천할 만한 것 같다. The -(으)ㄹ 만한 것 같다 construction softens a recommendation"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민준",ko:"그래서 결말이 좀 더 강했으면 좋았을 것 같아요. 솔직히 좀 아쉬웠거든요.",en:"That's why I think it would've been better if the ending were stronger. Honestly, I was a bit disappointed, you see."},hl:"솔직히"}],canDo:"Remind the listener of something you both already know (-잖아요)",id:"chapter-12-b5",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Minjun and Yujin are having coffee after watching a film at the cinema. They're comparing their reactions.",lines:[{speaker:"민준",ko:"유진 씨, 영화 어땠어요? 저는 좀 아쉬웠어요. 기대가 컸거든요.",en:"Yujin, how was the movie? I was a bit disappointed. My expectations were high, you see.",romanization:"Yujin ssi, yeonghwa eottaesseoyo? Jeoneun jom aswiwosseoyo. Gidaega keotgeodeunyo."},{speaker:"유진",ko:"그래요? 저는 꽤 재미있었는데요. 전개가 좀 느린 것 같긴 했지만.",en:"Really? I found it quite fun. Though I did think the pacing was a bit slow.",romanization:"Geuraeyo? Jeoneun kkwae jaemiisseonneundeyo. Jeongaega jom neurin geot gatgin haetjiman."},{speaker:"민준",ko:"맞아요! 전개가 너무 느리잖아요. 2시간 동안 뭔가 일어나는 게 별로 없었어요.",en:"Right! The pacing really is too slow, you know. Not much happens in those two hours.",romanization:"Majayo! Jeongaega neomu neurijanayo. Du-sigan dongan mwonga ireonaneun ge byeollo eopseosseoyo."},{speaker:"유진",ko:"음, 그래도 배우들 연기가 굉장히 좋은 것 같았어요.",en:"Hmm, but I thought the acting was really good though.",romanization:"Eum, geuraedo baeudeul yeongiga goengjanghi joeun geot gatassoyo."},{speaker:"민준",ko:"그건 저도 동의해요. 특히 주인공 배우가 정말 잘하거든요.",en:"I agree on that. Especially the lead actor — they're genuinely great, you see.",romanization:"Geugeon jeodo donguihaeyo. Teuki juingong baeuga jeongmal jalhageodeunyo."},{speaker:"유진",ko:"맞아요, 연기가 자연스러운 것 같아요. 억지스럽지 않잖아요.",en:"Exactly, the acting seems natural. It's not forced, you know.",romanization:"Majayo, yeongiga jayeonseureoun geot gatayo. Eokjiseureopji anjanayo."},{speaker:"민준",ko:"그래서 결말이 좀 더 강했으면 좋았을 것 같아요. 솔직히 좀 아쉬웠거든요.",en:"That's why I think it would've been better if the ending were stronger. Honestly, I was a bit disappointed, you see.",romanization:"Geuraeseo gyeolmari jom deo ganghaesseumyeon joasseul geot gatayo. Soljiki jom aswieotgeodeunyo."},{speaker:"유진",ko:"개인적으로는 이런 열린 결말을 좋아하거든요. 생각할 거리가 많잖아요.",en:"Personally, I like open endings like this, you see. There's a lot to think about, you know.",romanization:"Gaeinjeogeuro-neun ireon yeollin gyeolmareul joahageodeunyo. Saenggakhal georiga manchanayo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I think this movie is a little boring.'",tokens:["이 영화는","좀","지루한 것 같아요"],correct:"이 영화는 좀 지루한 것 같아요",explanation:"좀 softens the criticism, and -한 것 같아요 makes it an impression rather than a blunt fact. This is much safer than saying 이 영화는 지루해요 directly."}],canDo:"Soften an opinion so it sounds less blunt (-는 것 같다)",id:"chapter-12-b6",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 영화 보고 나서 (After the Movie)",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"영화 보고 나서 (After the Movie)",chunks:["유진: 민준 씨, 아까 그 영화 어땠어요? 저는 꽤 좋았거든요. 민준: 저는 좀 아쉬웠어요. 기대가 너무 컸던 것 같아요. 유진: 아, 그래요? 전 결말이 인상적인 것 같았는데. 민준: 저는 결말이 좀 급한 것 같았어요. 그리고 스토리가 단순한 것 같기도 하고요.","유진: 음... 그래도 배우들 연기만큼은 정말 좋았잖아요. 민준: 그건 맞아요. 특히 주인공 배우가 굉장한 것 같아요. 이제 팬이 됐거든요! 유진: 저도요! 다음에 그 배우 나오는 드라마 같이 봐요."],translation:`Yujin: Minjun, how was that movie earlier? I quite liked it, you see.
Minjun: I was a bit disappointed. I think my expectations were too high.
Yujin: Oh, really? I thought the ending was impressive.
Minjun: I thought the ending felt a bit rushed. And I thought the story was kind of simple too.
Yujin: Hmm... But the acting was really good, you know.
Minjun: That's true. Especially the lead actor seems amazing. I've become a fan, you see!
Yujin: Me too! Next time let's watch the drama that actor's in together.`,qas:[{q:"How does Minjun feel about the movie overall? What specific issues does he mention?",a:"Minjun was disappointed (아쉬웠어요). He thought his expectations were too high (기대가 너무 컸던 것 같아요), the ending felt rushed (결말이 좀 급한 것 같았어요), and the story was a bit simple (스토리가 단순한 것 같기도 하고요)."},{q:"What do both Minjun and Yujin agree on?",a:"They both agree the acting was great, especially the lead actor (배우들 연기만큼은 정말 좋았잖아요 / 주인공 배우가 굉장한 것 같아요). They both became fans of the actor (저도요!)."},{q:"Find every -것 같다, -잖아요, and -거든요 in the text. For each one, explain why that ending was chosen and not one of the other two.",a:"1) 기대가 너무 컸던 것 같아요 — Minjun uses -것 같다 (past: -던 것 같다) to frame his high expectations as a personal impression/self-reflection, softening self-criticism. 2) 결말이 인상적인 것 같았는데 — Yujin hedges her positive reaction as a personal impression (past -것 같다), not a fact. 3) 결말이 좀 급한 것 같았어요 and 스토리가 단순한 것 같기도 하고요 — Minjun consistently uses -것 같다 to soften criticisms into impressions. 4) 배우들 연기만큼은 정말 좋았잖아요 — -잖아요 fits here because both speakers JUST WATCHED the film together — the good acting is shared, observable knowledge Yujin is reminding Minjun of. 5) 좋았거든요 (Yujin's opening) — -거든요 because Yujin is volunteering her positive reaction as NEW personal information Minjun didn't know yet. 6) 이제 팬이 됐거든요 — -거든요 because Minjun is sharing a new personal fact (becoming a fan) that Yujin didn't know."}]}],canDo:"Give a reason as new information the listener didn't know (-거든요)",id:"chapter-12-b7",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Your friend says '나 왜 화났는지 알아?' You reply: '어제 네가 약속을 안 지켰___ .' Which ending fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"잖아",ok:!0},{text:"거든",ok:!1},{text:"는 것 같아",ok:!1}],explanation:"-잖아 is correct here. The person you're talking TO is the one who broke the promise — they KNOW they did it. You're reminding them of their own action as the reason for your anger. -거든 would mean 'just so you know you broke the promise' — but they clearly already know. -는 것 같아 would express uncertainty about whether they broke it, which is too weak for this situation."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"At work, a colleague offers to switch to English because you look unsure. You want to say you can keep using Korean because you're actually quite good at it. Which is correct?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"사실 저 한국어 꽤 잘하잖아요.",ok:!1},{text:"사실 저 한국어 꽤 잘하거든요.",ok:!0},{text:"사실 저 한국어 꽤 잘하는 것 같아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-거든요 is correct because you're giving NEW background information as a reason to continue in Korean. -잖아요 would imply they already knew you were good at Korean. -는 것 같아요 would sound strangely uncertain — 'I seem to be good at Korean?' That doesn't convey confidence; it sounds like you're guessing about your own skill."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"약속___ 있거든요. (I have plans, you see.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이",ok:!0},{text:"을",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"있거든요 still uses 이/가 for the thing that exists or is possessed. 을 would wrongly treat 약속 as an object of an action."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I think this movie is a little boring.'",tokens:["이 영화는","좀","지루한 것 같아요"],correct:"이 영화는 좀 지루한 것 같아요",explanation:"좀 softens the criticism, and -한 것 같아요 makes it an impression rather than a blunt fact. This is much safer than saying 이 영화는 지루해요 directly."}],canDo:"Remind the listener of something you both already know (-잖아요)",id:"chapter-12-b8",chapterId:"chapter-12",index:7}]},{id:"chapter-13",number:13,title:"-(ㄴ/는)다고 하다 · -냐고 하다",goal:"Report what someone said using -(ㄴ/는)다고 하다 (said that)",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 사회~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사회",romanization:"sahoe",en:"society",pos:"noun",nuance:"사회 (社會, 社 'community/shrine-group' + 會 'gathering') means 'society' as the whole body of people living together, and as a noun-modifier it stacks directly: 현대 사회, 사회 문제, 사회 복지. The trap for English speakers is 사회생활: it does NOT mean 'social life / hanging out', but working life and managing adult/workplace relationships, so 사회생활을 잘하다 praises someone for handling colleagues and obligations well. Contrast 사회 with 모임 — a 모임 is one concrete small gathering (study group, dinner club), while 사회 is the abstract collective; never use 사회 for 'a get-together'. The particle to learn is 에 for location-of-existence (사회에 문제가 있다), not 에서, when you simply say something exists in society."},sentence:{ko:"우리 사회가 빠르게 변한다고 생각해요.",en:"I think our society is changing rapidly."},target:"사회",options:["society","history","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)"],note:"다고 생각해요 = 'I think that...' — reported-thought structure using the same 다고 pattern"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"문화",romanization:"munhwa",en:"culture",pos:"noun",nuance:"문화 means 'culture' in the broad sense — the customs, arts, food, and shared ways of life of a group. It is Sino-Korean, 文化 ('writing/civilization' 文 + 'become/transform' 化), literally 'becoming civilized', so it leans toward the refined, collective sense of culture rather than one person's habits. The nearest trap-word is 문학 文學 (literature): the two look and sound almost identical, but 학 'study' vs 화 'transform' completely changes the meaning, so an English speaker who half-hears the word can easily swap them. 문화 also is NOT used for bacterial 'culture' (that is 배양) or for being a 'cultured/refined person' (that is 교양 있다). It loves to form compounds — 대중문화 (pop culture), 문화재 (cultural heritage), 문화생활 (cultural life/leisure) — and the habitual verb is 경험하다 (to experience), as in 다른 문화를 경험하다. The everyday phrase 문화 차이 (cultural difference) is how Koreans tactfully explain awkward moments. Register is neutral, fine in both casual chat and formal writing."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 한국 문화가 재미있다고 했어요.",en:"My friend said Korean culture is interesting."},target:"문화",options:["culture","economy","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)"],note:"재미있다 is 있다-type → add -다고 directly (adjective rule), not -(느)ㄴ다고"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전통",romanization:"jeontong",en:"tradition",pos:"noun",nuance:"전통 (傳統, 傳 'hand down/pass on' + 統 'lineage/unbroken line') is a custom, value, or practice passed down across generations, and the hanja literally pictures an unbroken line being handed on — which is why it carries a respectful, valued tone. Contrast it sharply with 습관 (習慣, 'habit'): 전통 is shared and inherited by a group, while 습관 is one individual's repeated personal routine (제 습관, never 제 전통). For the descriptive 'it is traditional', you must switch to the adjective 전통적이다 → 전통적인 + noun; 전통 alone is the abstract noun. The classic 직역 trap is time-length: English 'a long tradition' tempts 긴 전통, but Korean fixes this as 오랜 전통, because 오랜 expresses depth in time while 긴 means physically long."},sentence:{ko:"이 마을은 오래된 전통을 그대로 이어 가고 있어요.",en:"This village is carrying on its old traditions just as they are."},target:"전통",options:["history","economy","tradition"],note:"이어 가다 = keep carrying on; present progressive 가고 있어요 emphasizes the ongoing nature"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"역사",romanization:"yeoksa",en:"history",pos:"noun",nuance:"역사 means history — both the school subject and the actual record of the past — written 歷史 (歷 'to pass through / experience' + 史 'chronicle, records'), literally 'the record of what has passed'. Distinguish it sharply from 옛날, which is the storytelling adverbial 'long ago / once upon a time': you study 역사 in class, but a folktale starts 옛날 옛적에. It also differs from 역사학, the academic 'study OF history' a scholar specializes in; the everyday school subject is plain 역사. A country's history stacks the country name bare in front — 한국 역사, 세계 역사 — with no 의 needed. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 옛날 or 과거 ('the past') when naming the class; the subject and the discipline are both 역사."},sentence:{ko:"선생님이 한국 역사가 아주 깊다고 하셨어요.",en:"The teacher said Korean history is very deep."},target:"역사",options:["history","society","tradition"],note:"깊다 is an adjective → 깊다고 (plain adjective form + 다고). Teacher gets 하셨어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"경제",romanization:"gyeongje",en:"economy",pos:"noun",nuance:"경제 (經濟, 經 'manage/govern' + 濟 'aid/relieve' — from 經世濟民 'govern the world and relieve the people') means 'the economy' as a system. The biggest learner trap is the adjective 경제적이다, which splits into TWO everyday meanings: (1) 'economical', i.e. money-saving (버스가 더 경제적이에요), and (2) 'economic/financial' (경제적 부담 = financial burden) — context decides which. Equally important is the slump idiom: to say the economy is doing badly, Korean uses 경제가 어렵다 or 경제가 안 좋다, NOT a literal 경제가 나쁘다, because 어렵다 ('hard, strained') is the conventional collocation. Keep 경제 (the system/field) distinct from 경영 (經營, business management of a company); they look similar but 경영 is running a firm."},sentence:{ko:"뉴스에서 경제가 점점 어려워진다고 했어요.",en:"The news said the economy is getting increasingly difficult."},target:"경제",options:["economy","culture","tradition"],note:"어려워지다 has vowel stem 어려워지- → ㄴ다고: 어려워진다고"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"정치",romanization:"jeongchi",en:"politics",pos:"noun",nuance:"정치 (政治, 政 'governing' + 治 'rule/manage' — literally 'governing and ordering [a state]') is the abstract field and activity of politics. Hold three related words apart: 정치 is the field, 정부 (government) is the concrete body, and 정치인 is an individual politician — so 'a politician' is 정치인/정치가, never the literal 정치 사람. The adjective 정치적이다 labels something 'political', usually with an undertone of being calculated, partisan, or socially sensitive. The particle pitfall is interest: 'interested in politics' is 정치에 관심이 있다 (topic marked with 에), not 정치를 관심이 있다. Culturally, 정치 is treated as a touchy subject, so 정치적인 이야기 is something many Koreans deliberately avoid in polite company."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 정치에 관심이 없다고 했어요.",en:"My friend said he has no interest in politics."},target:"정치",options:["tradition","politics","history"],note:"없다 → add -다고 directly (없다고). 없다 is in the same category as 있다 — add -다고, not -는다고."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지연",ko:"민호 씨, 오늘 한국 사회 수업 어땠어요? 저는 못 갔어요.",en:"Minho, how was Korean society class today? I couldn't go."},hl:"사회"}],canDo:"Report what someone said using the plain form plus -(ㄴ/는)다고 하다",id:"chapter-13-b1",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -(ㄴ/는)다고 하다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-(ㄴ/는)다고 하다",sub:"Reporting What Someone Said",rows:[{when:"Action verb, stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄴ다고 하다",ex:"가다 → 간다고 하다 (says he goes)"},{when:"Action verb, stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"-는다고 하다",ex:"먹다 → 먹는다고 하다 (says he eats)"},{when:"Adjective (descriptive verb)",add:"-다고 하다",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘다고 하다 / 좋다 → 좋다고 하다"},{when:"이다 (noun + be)",add:"-(이)라고 하다",ex:"학생이다 → 학생이라고 하다 / 친구다 → 친구라고 하다"},{when:"Past tense (any verb/adj)",add:"-았/었다고 하다",ex:"먹었다 → 먹었다고 하다 / 갔다 → 갔다고 하다"}],examples:[{ko:"친구가 내일 학교에 간다고 했어요.",en:"My friend said she's going to school tomorrow.",romanization:"chin-gu-ga nae-il hak-gyo-e gan-da-go hae-sseo-yo.",note:"가다 is a vowel-stem action verb, so it takes -ㄴ다고."},{ko:"동생이 지금 밥 먹는다고 해요.",en:"My little brother says he's eating now.",romanization:"dong-saeng-i ji-geum bap meong-neun-da-go hae-yo.",note:"먹다 has 받침, so the action-verb form is -는다고. 밥 먹다 ('eat a meal') is the everyday way to say it."}],more:{func:'Use this to report what someone said: "He says/said that …". Put the original verb or adjective into its plain (해라체) form and add -고 하다. It works for both "says" and "said" — 했어요 makes it past.',keyPoint:{label:"Adjectives keep -다고; action verbs split into -ㄴ다고 / -는다고",body:"This is the one spot learners mix up. Action verbs (먹다, 가다) become 먹는다고/간다고, but adjectives (좋다, 바쁘다) and 있다/없다 just take -다고: 좋다고, 바쁘다고, 재미있다고. So 'It's fun' → 재미있다고 했어요, never 재미있는다고. (Why 있다 acts like an adjective here comes later — for now, just memorize 있다고.)"},pronunciation:"In 먹는다고, the ㄱ before ㄴ nasalizes: [멍는다고]. 간다고 is said smoothly with no pause before 고."}}],canDo:"Report what someone said using the plain form plus -(ㄴ/는)다고 하다",id:"chapter-13-b2",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 환경~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"환경",romanization:"hwangyeong",en:"environment",pos:"noun",nuance:"환경 is Sino-Korean 環境 ('ring/encircle' 環 + 'boundary' 境), literally the boundary that rings you — hence it covers BOTH the natural environment (nature, pollution, climate) and the human conditions around a person: 근무 환경 (work environment), 가정 환경 (home background). The near-synonym 주변(周邊) means the physical 'surroundings/vicinity' you can point at, while 환경 is the abstract set of conditions that shape you, so a noisy 주변 creates a bad 환경. Register is neutral and works in essays, news, and speech alike; it collocates with 보호하다 (protect), 오염되다 (be polluted), and 파괴하다 (destroy). The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: say 환경이 좋다 ('the environment is good') or 환경에 좋다 ('good FOR the environment'), never 환경을 좋다 — 좋다 is an adjective and can't take 을/를."},sentence:{ko:"전문가들이 환경이 점점 나빠진다고 경고했어요.",en:"Experts warned that the environment is gradually getting worse."},target:"환경",options:["tradition","environment","history"],note:"나빠지다 (vowel stem 나빠지-) → ㄴ다고: 나빠진다고. 경고했어요 = warned."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"교육",romanization:"gyoyuk",en:"education",pos:"noun",nuance:"교육 is Sino-Korean 敎育 ('teach' 敎 + 'raise/nurture' 育) — literally to teach-and-rear, so it spans schooling, parenting, and on-the-job training, not just classrooms. The crucial direction split is in the verb: the learner says 교육을 받다 ('receive education'), while the provider says 교육하다 or 교육(을) 시키다 ('cause to be educated'). Near-synonym 훈련(訓練) 'training' is narrower and physical/repetitive (군사 훈련 military training, 직업 훈련 vocational training), whereas 교육 includes intellectual and moral formation. In Korea the 공교육(public) vs 사교육(private cram-school) divide is a constant social theme. English-speaker pitfall: don't say 교육을 하다 when you mean 'to study' — that means you're the one teaching; a student always uses 교육을 받다."},sentence:{ko:"부모님이 교육이 가장 중요하다고 항상 말씀하세요.",en:"My parents always say that education is the most important thing."},target:"교육",options:["tradition","history","education"],note:"중요하다 is an adjective → 중요하다고. 말씀하세요 is the honorific form of 말하다 (말씀하시다), used for parents."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계시다",romanization:"gyesida",en:"to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)",pos:"verb",nuance:"계시다 replaces 있다 only for respected people. For things, use 있다: 가방이 있어요, not 가방이 계세요."},sentence:{ko:"사장님이 지금 자리에 안 계세요.",en:"The boss isn't at his desk right now."},target:null,options:["to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to say / to tell (humble — you speaking to someone senior)"],note:"계시다 replaces 있다 when the subject is a person you honor and you are talking about physical presence. 사장님이 있으세요 sounds awkward for the boss's location; 계세요 is the natural respectful form."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"드리다",romanization:"deurida",en:"to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)",pos:"verb",nuance:"드리다 respects the recipient, not the subject. It is different from -(으)시-, which honors the subject."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께 선물을 드렸어요.",en:"I gave my parents a gift."},target:"드렸",options:["to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to eat (very honorific for 먹다/드시다 — for very respected elders)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)"],note:"드리다 is used when YOU give to someone above you. 주다 is the neutral form. 부모님께 uses 께 (honorific 에게)."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"잡수시다",romanization:"japsusida",en:"to eat (very honorific for 먹다/드시다 — for very respected elders)",pos:"verb",nuance:"드시다 is the safer everyday honorific for eating; 잡수시다 sounds very respectful and elder-focused."},sentence:{ko:"할머니, 진지 다 잡수셨어요?",en:"Grandma, have you finished your meal?"},target:"잡수셨",options:["to eat (very honorific for 먹다/드시다 — for very respected elders)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)","to say / to tell (humble — you speaking to someone senior)"],note:"잡수시다 is higher than 드시다 — reserved for grandparents and very senior people. 진지 is the honorific word for 밥 (meal/rice)."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"말씀드리다",romanization:"malsseumdeurida",en:"to say / to tell (humble — you speaking to someone senior)",pos:"verb",nuance:"말씀드리다 points upward to the listener. 말씀하시다 is used when the respected person speaks."},sentence:{ko:"교수님께 제 사정을 말씀드렸어요.",en:"I explained my situation to the professor."},target:"말씀드렸",options:["to say / to tell (humble — you speaking to someone senior)","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to eat (very honorific for 먹다/드시다 — for very respected elders)"],note:"말씀드리다 = humble form of 말하다. 교수님께 uses 께 (honorific 에게). Compare: 친구한테 말했어요 (told a friend, plain)."}],canDo:"Report a question someone asked (-냐고 하다 / 물어보다)",id:"chapter-13-b3",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -냐고 하다",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-냐고 하다",sub:"Reporting a Question",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"내일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"있",mid:"냐고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"물어봤어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"친구가 내일 시간 있냐고 물어봤어요.",en:"My friend asked if I had time tomorrow.",romanization:"chin-gu-ga nae-il si-gan in-nya-go mu-reo-bwa-sseo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"동생이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밥",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹었",mid:"냐고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"동생이 밥 먹었냐고 했어요.",en:"My little brother asked whether I'd eaten.",romanization:"dong-saeng-i bap meo-geon-nya-go hae-sseo-yo."}],rule:{name:"-냐고 하다",rows:[{when:"Action verb",add:"-냐고 하다 / 물어보다",ex:"가다 → 가냐고 / 먹다 → 먹냐고"},{when:"Adjective (descriptive verb)",add:"-냐고 하다 / 물어보다",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘냐고 / 좋다 → 좋냐고"},{when:"이다 (noun + be)",add:"-(이)냐고 하다",ex:"학생이다 → 학생이냐고 / 친구다 → 친구냐고"},{when:"있다/없다",add:"-냐고 하다",ex:"있다 → 있냐고 / 없다 → 없냐고"},{when:"Past tense (any verb/adj)",add:"-았/었냐고 하다",ex:"먹었다 → 먹었냐고 / 갔다 → 갔냐고"}]},more:{func:`Use this to report a question someone asked: "He asked whether/what …". Change the original question's ending to the plain question form -냐 and add -고 하다 — though in real speech people usually say 물어보다 ('ask') instead of 하다.`,keyPoint:{label:"Statement -다고 vs. question -냐고",body:"Both report someone's words, but the ending tells you which: a reported statement ends in -다고 (간다고 했어요 = 'said he's going'), and a reported question ends in -냐고 (가냐고 했어요 = 'asked if he's going'). Yes/no and wh-questions use the same -냐고; the question word (언제, 누가, 뭐) just stays inside the clause."},pronunciation:"있냐고 and 먹었냐고 nasalize across the boundary: [인냐고], [머건냐고] — the ㅆ/ㄱ before ㄴ becomes [ㄴ]."}}],canDo:"Report a question someone asked (-냐고 하다 / 물어보다)",id:"chapter-13-b4",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 뵙다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"뵙다",romanization:"boepda",en:"to meet / to see (humble — you meeting someone senior)",pos:"verb",nuance:"뵙다 is humble: it lowers your action of meeting so the other person is respected."},sentence:{ko:"내일 교수님을 뵐 수 있을까요?",en:"Would it be possible to see the professor tomorrow?"},target:null,options:["to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to eat (very honorific for 먹다/드시다 — for very respected elders)","to meet / to see (humble — you meeting someone senior)"],note:"뵙다 is the humble form of 만나다/보다 when you are meeting someone you respect. 뵈다 is also used in common conjugations: 뵈어요 → 봬요, 뵈었어요 → 뵀어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"발전하다",romanization:"baljeonhada",en:"to develop / to advance / to progress",pos:"verb",nuance:"발전하다 (發展, 'issue-forth + unfold/spread') means to move to a more advanced, mature, or larger stage — technology, economies, skills, companies, even relationships. It is normally intransitive (실력이 발전하다, 경제가 발전하다), and the 'into' target takes ~(으)로 (연인으로 발전하다 = develop into lovers). Its near-synonym 성장하다 ('grow') overlaps but leans biological/scale growth (아이가 성장하다 = a child grows up), whereas 발전하다 stresses qualitative advancement to a higher level. The number-one trap for learners is the look-alike 발견하다 (to discover) — one syllable apart and totally unrelated, so 신약을 발전하다 wrongly means 'develop a new drug' when you meant 발견하다 (discover). The second trap is using 발전하다 transitively; to actively develop something you need the causative 발전시키다 (기술을 발전시켰어요), never 기술을 발전했어요."},sentence:{ko:"연구자들이 기술이 빠르게 발전한다고 했어요.",en:"The researchers said technology is advancing rapidly."},target:"발전한",options:["to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)","to develop / to advance / to progress"],note:"발전하다 has vowel stem (발전하-) → ㄴ다고: 발전한다고. Classic example of the 다고 pattern with a 하다 verb."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"존재하다",romanization:"jonjaehada",en:"to exist",pos:"verb",nuance:"존재하다 (存在, 'remain + be present') means 'to exist' in an abstract, weighty, or emphatic sense — common in writing, debate, philosophy, and serious topics. Its everyday near-synonym is 있다, and the line is sharp: for 'there is / I have' you must use 있다 (책이 있어요, 질문이 있어요), never 존재하다. Reach for 존재하다 only when you want to spotlight the very fact of something's existence — a method, a being, a problem — or to deny it categorically with 존재하지 않아요. It is intransitive, so the thing that exists takes 이/가 and there is no object. The big English-speaker pitfall is mapping every English 'exist/there is' onto 존재하다: 'there's a café nearby' is 근처에 카페가 있어요, and saying 카페가 존재해요 sounds comically grand. Keep 존재하다 for the abstract and the philosophical."},sentence:{ko:"전통이 아직도 존재한다고 생각해요.",en:"I think traditions still exist."},target:"존재한",options:["to exist","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)"],note:"생각해요 (I think) also uses the 다고 pattern: 존재한다고 생각해요. 생각하다 is another common reporting verb alongside 하다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"참여하다",romanization:"chamyeohada",en:"to participate / to take part in",pos:"verb",nuance:"참여하다 (參與, '參 take part + 與 join in') means to actively take part in and contribute to an activity, event, or process, so its core is involvement, not physical presence. The single most useful contrast for a learner: 참석하다 (出席) is simply attending — being bodily present at a meeting, wedding, or ceremony — whereas 참여하다 means you actually engage and add something. You 참석 a friend's wedding (you show up and sit there), but you 참여 a project or campaign (you roll up your sleeves); a related word 가담하다 is 'join in' with a negative slant (joining a crime or a fight). What you join is marked with 에 (행사에 참여하다), and it pairs habitually with 적극적으로 and the bare noun in 참여율 (participation rate), 시민 참여 (citizen participation), 참여형 (participatory). As a regular 하다-verb it is 참여해요 / 참여했어요. The English-speaker pitfall is using one word for both 'attend' and 'participate' the way English does — if you only mean you were physically there, say 참석했어요; save 참여했어요 for when you genuinely took part."},sentence:{ko:"선생님이 행사에 꼭 참여하라고 하셨어요.",en:"The teacher said to be sure to participate in the event."},target:"참여하",options:["to participate / to take part in","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)"],note:"This is a reported command (not statement) → -(으)라고: 참여하라고 하셨어요. Compare with 다고 (statement) and 냐고 (question)."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"변하다",romanization:"byeonhada",en:"to change (intransitive)",pos:"verb",nuance:"변하다 is Sino-Korean: 變 (byeon, 'change, transform') + 하다, so it carries the feeling of something turning into a different state on its own, by nature or over time. The sharp contrast English speakers must learn is 변하다 vs 바꾸다: 변하다 is intransitive — the thing changes by itself (날씨가 변해요 = the weather changes), while 바꾸다 is transitive — somebody deliberately swaps or replaces a thing (계획을 바꿔요 = I change the plan). A related pair is 변하다 vs 바뀌다 (the passive of 바꾸다): 바뀌다 implies an external switch (신호등이 바뀌었어요 = the traffic light switched), whereas 변하다 implies inner transformation (성격이 변했어요 = his personality changed). Register is neutral and everyday. The big pitfall is using 변하다 with a direct object — you cannot say *계획을 변해요 to mean 'I change the plan'; if there is an actor doing the changing, you need 바꾸다. 변하다 only ever describes the subject itself becoming different."},sentence:{ko:"사회가 빠르게 변해도 전통은 남아요.",en:"Even as society changes rapidly, traditions remain."},target:"변해",options:["to change (intransitive)","to be (honorific for 있다 — a person's presence)","to give (humble — you giving to someone senior)"],note:"변해도 = even though (it) changes (-(아/어)도 concessive). 남다 (to remain) contrasts with 변하다 (to change) in this classic Chapter 36 sentence."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지연",ko:"그럼 교수님께 제가 참여하고 싶다고 말씀드릴게요.",en:"Then I'll tell the professor that I want to participate."},hl:"참여하"}],canDo:"Use humble verbs when I speak to someone senior (말씀드리다 · 드리다)",id:"chapter-13-b5",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Jiyeon missed a Korean society and culture class, so Minho relays what the professor said about the group presentation",lines:[{speaker:"지연",ko:"민호 씨, 오늘 한국 사회 수업 어땠어요? 저는 못 갔어요.",en:"Minho, how was Korean society class today? I couldn't go.",romanization:"Minho ssi, oneul hanguk sahoe sueop eottaesseoyo? Jeoneun mot gasseoyo."},{speaker:"민호",ko:"교수님이 다음 주에 한국 전통 발표를 한다고 하셨어요.",en:"The professor said we're doing a presentation on Korean traditions next week.",romanization:"Gyosunimi daeum jue hanguk jeontong balpyoreul handago hasyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"정말요? 주제가 뭐냐고 물어봤어요?",en:"Really? Did anyone ask what the topic is?",romanization:"Jeongmallyo? Jujega mwonyago mureobwasseoyo?"},{speaker:"민호",ko:"네, 소연 씨가 물어봤어요. 교수님이 현대 사회에 남아 있는 전통이라고 하셨어요.",en:"Yes, Soyeon asked. The professor said it's traditions that remain in modern society.",romanization:"Ne, Soyeon ssiga mureobwasseoyo. Gyosunimi hyeondae sahoe-e nama inneun jeontongirago hasyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"팀은 몇 명이라고 하셨어요?",en:"How many people did the professor say are in each team?",romanization:"Timeun myeot myeongirago hasyeosseoyo?"},{speaker:"민호",ko:"네 명이라고 하셨어요. 그리고 조교님이 자료는 역사와 문화 쪽으로 찾으라고 하셨어요.",en:"The professor said four people. And the teaching assistant said to look for materials on history and culture.",romanization:"Ne myeongirago hasyeosseoyo. Geurigo jogyonimi jaryoneun yeoksawa munhwa jjogeuro chajeurago hasyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"그럼 교수님께 제가 참여하고 싶다고 말씀드릴게요.",en:"Then I'll tell the professor that I want to participate.",romanization:"Geureom gyosunimkke jega chamyeohago sipdago malsseumdeurilgeyo."},{speaker:"민호",ko:"좋아요! 교수님이 지금 연구실에 계세요. 바로 말씀드려요.",en:"Great! The professor is in the office right now. Tell them right away.",romanization:"Joayo! Gyosunimi jigeum yeongusire gyeseyo. Baro malsseumdeuryeoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'My friend said she is going to school tomorrow.'",tokens:["친구가","내일 학교에 간다고","했어요"],correct:"친구가 내일 학교에 간다고 했어요",explanation:"The reported content ends in 간다고 before 했어요. 내일 학교에 가요 becomes 내일 학교에 간다고 했어요 when reported."}],canDo:"Report what someone said using the plain form plus -(ㄴ/는)다고 하다",id:"chapter-13-b6",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 단체 카톡 (Group KakaoTalk Chat)",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"단체 카톡 (Group KakaoTalk Chat)",chunks:["지훈: 방금 교수님이 내일 발표가 없어진다고 하셨어. 다들 들었어? 수현: 진짜? 나는 못 들었는데. 왜 없어지냐고 물어봤어? 지훈: 응, 소연이가 물어봤는데 교수님이 다음 주로 미룬다고 하셨어. 수현: 다행이다!",`그럼 우리 오늘 발표 준비 안 해도 되겠다. 소연: 아, 근데 교수님이 대신 에세이 제출하라고 하셨어. 다음 주 월요일까지. 수현: 어머, 그건 물어봤을 때 왜 말 안 해줬어? ㅋㅋ

지훈: 죄송... 교수님이 한국 사회와 문화를 이해하려면 역사도 봐야 한다고 하셨어. 소연: 맞아, 교수님께서 한국 전통이 현대 사회에서 어떻게 발전하고 존재하는지 생각해 보라고도 하셨어.`],translation:`Jihoon: The professor just said tomorrow's presentation is cancelled. Did everyone hear?

Suhyeon: Really? I didn't hear. Did anyone ask why it's cancelled?

Jihoon: Yeah, Soyeon asked and the professor said it's being pushed to next week.

Suhyeon: What a relief! So we don't have to prepare the presentation today.

Soyeon: Oh, but the professor said to submit an essay instead. By next Monday.

Suhyeon: Oh no, why didn't you say that when I asked? lol

Jihoon: Sorry... The professor said that to understand Korean society and culture, we need to look at history too.

Soyeon: Right, the professor also said to think about how Korean traditions develop and exist in modern society.`,qas:[{q:"What did the professor say about tomorrow's presentation?",a:"교수님이 내일 발표가 없어진다고 하셨어 — The professor said tomorrow's presentation is cancelled (pushed to next week)."},{q:"What did Soyeon report about the essay?",a:"교수님이 대신 에세이 제출하라고 하셨어 — The professor said to submit an essay instead by next Monday. Note this uses -(으)라고 (reported command), not -다고 (statement)."},{q:"Find all instances of -다고 하다, -냐고 물어보다, and -(으)라고 하다 in the text. Why does each one use the form it uses?",a:"다고 하셨어 (×3): 없어진다고 (발표가 없어지다 = action verb, vowel stem → ㄴ다고), 미룬다고 (미루다 = action verb, vowel stem → ㄴ다고), 봐야 한다고 (보다 + -아/어야 하다 → 봐야 하다, then + 다고). | 냐고 물어봤어: 없어지냐고 — relaying a question (original had a question mark) → -냐고. | 라고 하셨어 (×2): 제출하라고, 생각해 보라고 — both relay commands (imperatives) → -(으)라고. The ending matches the type of sentence being relayed: period → 다고, question mark → 냐고, command → (으)라고."}]}],canDo:"Report a question someone asked (-냐고 하다 / 물어보다)",id:"chapter-13-b7",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Your colleague relays this: '팀장님이 오늘 회의가 취소됐___ 하셨어요.' What ending goes in the blank?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"냐고",ok:!1},{text:"다고",ok:!0},{text:"(으)라고",ok:!1}],explanation:"취소됐다 is a verb in past tense — it's a statement of fact (the meeting is cancelled), not a question or a command. Statements → -다고. Past tense: 취소됐다 → 취소됐다고. '냐고' would mean the team lead asked if it was cancelled — different meaning. '(으)라고' would mean the team lead commanded someone to cancel it — again, different."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence is correct Korean?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"할아버지가 지금 집에 있으세요.",ok:!1},{text:"할아버지께서 지금 집에 계세요.",ok:!0},{text:"할아버지께서 지금 집에 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"'할아버지가 있으세요' combines the wrong subject marker (가 instead of 께서) with the wrong presence verb (있으세요 instead of 계세요). '할아버지께서 있어요' uses 께서 correctly but forgets to honor grandpa's presence with 계시다. The fully correct form uses 께서 (honorific subject marker) and 계세요 (honorific form of 있다 for a person's presence) together."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"할머니___ 선물을 드렸어요. (I gave a present to my grandmother.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"께",ok:!0},{text:"에서",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:'께 is the respectful "to" particle for an elder, matching the humble verb 드렸어요. 에서 marks a place, and 를 would make 할머니 the object instead of the recipient.'},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'My friend said she is going to school tomorrow.'",tokens:["친구가","내일 학교에 간다고","했어요"],correct:"친구가 내일 학교에 간다고 했어요",explanation:"The reported content ends in 간다고 before 했어요. 내일 학교에 가요 becomes 내일 학교에 간다고 했어요 when reported."}],canDo:"Use humble verbs when I speak to someone senior (말씀드리다 · 드리다)",id:"chapter-13-b8",chapterId:"chapter-13",index:7}]},{id:"chapter-14",number:14,title:"-기로 하다 · -(으)ㄹ까 봐",goal:"Use -기로 하다 to announce a firm decision or agreed-upon plan",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 사업~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사업",romanization:"saeop",en:"business / enterprise",pos:"noun",nuance:"사업 (事業, literally 'affair-work') is business as an active undertaking — running a company, launching a commercial project, or being self-employed. The key boundary is against 산업 (産業, industry as a whole sector): 사업 is what one person or firm concretely does, while 산업 is the field that many businesses belong to (자동차 산업 = the auto industry, but 자동차 사업 = a particular auto-related business venture). It is also broader than the English word — a government or non-profit project is a 사업 too (복지 사업 = welfare project), so don't restrict it to 'for-profit'. Register is neutral; everyone from a small shop owner to a CEO uses it. The classic English-speaker pitfall is treating 사업하다 as a fixed block: the moment an object marker appears you must break it apart (사업을 하다, 사업을 시작하다), never 사업하다를. Also resist translating 'do business with someone' word-for-word — Koreans say 거래하다 (to trade/deal) for that, not 사업을 하다."},sentence:{ko:"작은 사업을 시작하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to start a small business."},target:"사업",options:["worry / concern","responsibility","business / enterprise"],note:"기로 했어요 — a settled decision to begin the business"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시작하다",romanization:"sijakhada",en:"to start / begin",pos:"verb",nuance:"시작하다 means 'to start / begin' and is a Sino-Korean 하다 verb — 시작 (始作, literally 始 'begin' + 作 'make/do', i.e. 'set in motion') plus 하다. As a 하다 verb it conjugates regularly with 하다 → 해요: 시작해요 / 시작합니다 / 시작했어요. A point that trips learners is that 시작하다 works both transitively and intransitively: with a doer it takes an object (저는 일을 시작해요 'I start work', 을/를), and with no doer the thing simply begins and takes the subject marker (수업이 시작해요 'class starts', 이/가). Contrast it with the pure intransitive 시작되다 (始作되다, 'to be started/begin'), which is more formal and never takes an object — 회의가 시작됐어요 'the meeting began'. Its natural opposite is 끝나다 ('to end'). The English-speaker pitfalls are (1) conjugating the 하다 wrongly as 시작하요 instead of 시작해요, and (2) forcing an object onto the no-doer case (수업을 시작해요 when you simply mean class begins) — when nothing actively starts it, use 수업이 시작해요 or the passive 시작돼요."},sentence:{ko:"새 프로젝트를 시작하게 됐어요.",en:"I ended up starting a new project."},target:"시작하",options:["to fail","to invest","to start / begin"],note:"게 됐어요 — the project arose through circumstances, not purely personal choice"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"실패하다",romanization:"silpaehada",en:"to fail",pos:"verb",nuance:"실패하다 means that an attempt, plan, project, or goal did not achieve its intended result. The target commonly takes 에: 사업에 실패하다, 시도에 실패하다. For exams and auditions, Korean often chooses the more specific 떨어지다 or 불합격하다 instead of the heavier 시험에 실패하다. Keep 실패하다 separate from 실수하다: 실패 is an unsuccessful overall result, while 실수 is a mistake made during the process. A failed attempt can be retried; a mistake can happen even inside a successful project."},sentence:{ko:"실패할까 봐 잠도 못 자요.",en:"I can't even sleep because I'm afraid of failing."},target:"실패할",options:["to start / begin","to decide","to fail"],note:"실패하다 → 실패할까 봐: feared outcome expressed with -(으)ㄹ까 봐"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"성공하다",romanization:"seonggonghada",en:"to succeed",pos:"verb",nuance:"성공하다 means reaching a desired result in a project, career, business, experiment, or attempt. The field or task commonly takes 에: 사업에 성공하다, 다이어트에 성공하다, 예약에 성공하다. Korean often uses more specific verbs where English broadly says ‘succeed’: pass an exam is 시험에 합격하다, win a competition is 대회에서 우승하다, and a plan ‘goes well’ is 일이 잘되다. 성공하다 can sound goal-focused and substantial, while 잘되다 is the more conversational choice for something simply turning out well."},sentence:{ko:"열심히 하다 보니까 성공하게 됐어요.",en:"As I kept working hard, I came to succeed."},target:"성공하",options:["to decide","to challenge oneself / attempt","to succeed"],note:"하다 보니까 + 게 됐어요: a natural combination — persistent effort led to the circumstantial outcome"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"걱정",romanization:"geokjeong",en:"worry / concern",pos:"noun",nuance:"걱정 is care or anxiety about something uncertain — the knot in your stomach over a result, a person's health, or money. It splits across two verbs: active 걱정하다 ('to worry about X,' X = object → 건강을 걱정해요) and spontaneous 걱정이 되다 ('X worries me / I become worried,' the worry arises → subject 이). Contrast 걱정 with 고민 (a problem you are mulling over, more like deliberation/a dilemma you turn in your mind) and with 불안 不安 (free-floating anxiety/unease without a clear object): you 걱정 about a specific thing, you 고민 over a decision, you feel 불안 as a mood. The most useful chunk is 걱정하지 마세요/걱정 마 — the go-to way to comfort someone. The English-speaker pitfall is the 하다 particle error 걱정이 해요 (use 걱정을 해요), and saying 걱정에 대해 생각해요 when you simply mean 걱정해요."},sentence:{ko:"걱정이 많아서 잠을 잘 못 자요.",en:"I have so many worries that I can't sleep well."},target:"걱정",options:["business / enterprise","income / earnings","worry / concern"],note:"걱정이 많다 — literally 'worries are many'; Korean treats worry as a countable thing"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"결정하다",romanization:"gyeoljeonghada",en:"to decide",pos:"verb",nuance:"결정하다 is built on the 한자 決定 (決 'resolve, cut through' + 定 'fix, settle'), so it literally means 'cut through and settle' — reaching a firm choice after deliberation. Three near-synonyms divide the space: 정하다 is the everyday, lighter 'set/decide' (그냥 정해요 = let's just settle it); 선택하다 (選擇) is 'select' from given options; 고르다 is the casual 'pick' (고르다 a fruit, a color). Use 결정하다 for consequential, considered decisions (a job, a move, a date) — for which thing to pick off a shelf, 고르다 or 선택하다 fits better. To decide to DO an action, the partner pattern is -기로 결정하다 (가기로 결정했어요); English speakers often forget the -기로 and say 결정했어요 가다. As a 하다 verb the negative splits: 결정 안 해요, 결정 못 해요. The heavier idiom 결정을 내리다 frames it as 'handing down' a decision."},sentence:{ko:"이미 결정했어요. 기로 했어요.",en:"I've already decided. I've made up my mind."},target:"결정했",options:["to start / begin","to decide","to fail"],note:"결정했어요 and 기로 했어요 both express decisions, but 기로 했어요 attaches to the specific action decided upon"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지은",ko:"사실 오래전부터 사업을 해 보고 싶었는데, 이번에 좋은 기회를 잡게 됐어요.",en:"Actually I've wanted to try running a business for a long time, and this time I ended up getting a good opportunity."},hl:"사업"}],canDo:"Say I've decided or agreed to do something (-기로 하다)",id:"chapter-14-b1",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -기로 하다",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-기로 하다",sub:"deciding / agreeing to do something",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"내일부터",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"매일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"운동하",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"내일부터 매일 운동하기로 했어요.",en:"I've decided to exercise every day from tomorrow.",romanization:"naeil-buteo maeil undonghagiro haesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구하고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주말에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"친구하고 주말에 영화 보기로 했어요.",en:"My friend and I decided to watch a movie this weekend.",romanization:"chinguhago jumare yeonghwa bogiro haesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"-기로 하다",rows:[{when:"Any verb (받침 or no 받침)",add:"verb stem + 기로 하다 (no harmony, no stem change)",ex:"가다 → 가기로 하다, 먹다 → 먹기로 하다"},{when:"Reporting a decision you've made (most common)",add:"verb stem + 기로 했어요",ex:"운동하다 → 운동하기로 했어요, 읽다 → 읽기로 했어요"},{when:"Agreeing with someone",add:"…하고 + verb stem + 기로 했어요",ex:"친구하고 만나기로 했어요"},{when:"Deciding NOT to do something",add:"안 + verb + 기로 하다 / verb + 지 않기로 하다",ex:"안 먹기로 했어요, 먹지 않기로 했어요"}]},more:{func:"Use -기로 하다 to say you've decided or agreed to do something. The verb stem + 기로 names the action and 하다 marks the act of deciding. It's usually in the past (-기로 했어요) because the decision is already made.",keyPoint:{label:"-기로 했어요 vs -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:"Both point to the future, but -기로 했어요 reports a decision that's now fixed ('I've decided to…'), while -(으)ㄹ 거예요 just states a plan or guess ('I'm going to…'). Use -기로 하다 when the point is that a choice was made — a promise to yourself or a deal with someone. (How the 했어요 past ending works is covered separately.)"},pronunciation:""}}],canDo:"Say I've decided or agreed to do something (-기로 하다)",id:"chapter-14-b2",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 도전하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도전하다",romanization:"dojeonhada",en:"to challenge oneself / attempt",pos:"verb",nuance:"도전하다 = to take something on, rise to a challenge, brave a new or daunting endeavor — overwhelmingly positive and aspirational (a job change, a new hobby, scary spicy food, a hard exam). The hanja 挑戰 literally means 挑 'provoke, stir up' + 戰 'battle', i.e. 'to pick a fight', and that combative root explains both senses: figuratively you 'do battle' with a goal. Particle pitfall for English speakers: it takes 에, not 을/를 — 마라톤에 도전하다 ('take on the marathon'), 시험에 도전하다. Contrast it with 노력하다 (sustained, grinding effort over time) and 시도하다 (a neutral, often one-off 'attempt'): 도전하다 specifically frames the target as bold or intimidating, with a brave-the-unknown flavor. Note: it does NOT mean confronting a person in an argument — 'to challenge someone' in that sense is 이의를 제기하다 or 도전장을 내밀다, not plain 도전하다. The everyday encouragement is 도전해 봐."},sentence:{ko:"한번 도전해 보기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to give it a try."},target:"도전해",options:["to fail","to challenge oneself / attempt","to succeed"],note:"도전해 보기로 했어요 — 해 보다 (try doing) + 기로 했어요: decided to at least attempt"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"포기하다",romanization:"pogihada",en:"to give up",pos:"verb",nuance:"포기하다 = to give up, abandon, or relinquish a goal, plan, claim, or even a person. The hanja 抛棄 is vivid: 抛 'to throw/hurl away' + 棄 'to discard, abandon', so the image is hurling something aside and walking off — a decisive letting-go, not a gentle fade. Contrast 포기하다 (stop pursuing a goal entirely) with 그만두다 (stop/quit an ongoing activity — a job, a habit) and with 단념하다 (a more bookish 'resign oneself to abandoning hope'); in speech 포기하다 is the everyday choice. Grammar: it pairs with 을/를 (목표를 포기하다) or with 는 거 / 는 걸 (사는 걸 포기하다 = 'give up on buying'). Culturally the word is loaded: N포세대, the 'give-up generation', names young Koreans said to have surrendered dating, marriage, and kids to economic pressure. English-speaker pitfall: the negative is 포기 안 해 / 포기 못 해 (noun + 안/못 + 해), NOT 안 포기해."},sentence:{ko:"포기하지 않기로 했어요.",en:"I decided not to give up."},target:"포기하",options:["to start / begin","to fail","to give up"],note:"-지 않기로 하다 = decided NOT to: the negative form of 기로 하다"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"수입",romanization:"suip",en:"income / earnings",pos:"noun",nuance:"수입 for personal money means income or revenue — the money coming in — and lives in opposition to 지출 (spending going out). Crucially it is a homograph hiding two different hanja: 收入 ('gather + enter') is income, while 輸入 ('transport + enter') is imports, the opposite of 수출 (輸出, exports). Spoken aloud they are identical, so only context tells you whether 수입 means a paycheck or a shipment of foreign goods; in an economics lecture both senses can appear in one paragraph. The near-synonym for the income sense is 소득 (所得, already in vocab): 소득 is the more formal/statistical word (소득세 = income tax, 소득 수준 = income level), whereas 수입 feels closer to everyday earnings, especially 한 달 수입 (monthly take). The English-speaker pitfall is assuming 'income' maps only to one fixed word and then panicking when 수입 shows up meaning 'imports' — train yourself to read the surrounding sentence. Also note 수입 is a noun describing a state (수입이 많다), so you don't conjugate it like a verb."},sentence:{ko:"수입이 줄어들까 봐 걱정이에요.",en:"I'm worried that my income might decrease."},target:"수입",options:["worry / concern","income / earnings","responsibility"],note:"줄어들다 → 줄어들까 봐: the feared outcome is shrinking income"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"투자하다",romanization:"tujahada",en:"to invest",pos:"verb",nuance:"투자하다 is not limited to stocks or real estate. You can invest money, time, energy, or attention in a company, skill, relationship, or future goal. The resource takes 을/를 and the destination takes 에: 교육에 시간을 투자하다, 회사에 돈을 투자하다. Keep it separate from 저축하다, which means saving money for later, and 소비하다, which means spending or consuming. 투자하다 implies giving resources now because you expect future value, but the word itself does not guarantee profit or success."},sentence:{ko:"그 사업에 투자하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to invest in that business."},target:"투자하",options:["to start / begin","to invest","to fail"],note:"기로 했어요 — a definitive financial commitment, not an on-the-spot promise"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"퇴직하다",romanization:"toejikhada",en:"to retire / resign",pos:"verb",nuance:"퇴직하다 is a formal, broad verb for leaving one’s position or employment, especially in documents, company notices, benefits, and retirement contexts. 퇴사하다 specifically means leaving a company, while 은퇴하다 means retiring from an entire career or public field, and 그만두다 is the everyday ‘quit/stop.’ A person can 퇴사하다 from one company without 은퇴하다 from working life. The workplace commonly takes 에서: 회사에서 퇴직하다. 정년퇴직하다 means retiring at the official retirement age; 퇴직 후 means after leaving employment."},sentence:{ko:"내년에 퇴직하게 될 것 같아요.",en:"It looks like I'll end up retiring next year."},target:"퇴직하",options:["to succeed","to retire / resign","to decide"],note:"게 될 것 같아요 — future form of 게 되다: circumstances will likely lead to retirement"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"책임",romanization:"chaegim",en:"responsibility",pos:"noun",nuance:"책임 (責任, 責 'demand/blame' + 任 'duty entrusted') is the abstract weight of being answerable for an outcome, and it lives almost entirely through verbs: 책임을 지다 (shoulder it) and 책임이 있다 (it falls on you). The dividing line you must learn is 책임 vs 의무: 책임 is accountability AFTER the fact (who answers if it goes wrong), while 의무 is a duty you are required to perform regardless of result. Also distinguish the bare noun 책임 from 책임감, which is the inner sense of duty a person feels, so describe an obligation with 책임 but a person's character with 책임감. The classic English-speaker trap is treating 책임 like the verb 'to be responsible' and saying 책임하다; Korean has no such verb, so you must reach for 책임을 지다 or 책임이 있다."},sentence:{ko:"책임을 질까 봐 두려워요.",en:"I'm afraid of having to take responsibility."},target:"책임",options:["business / enterprise","responsibility","worry / concern"],note:"지다 → 질까 봐: 지다 is a regular vowel-final stem, so it takes ㄹ까 봐"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"현우",ko:"대단하네요. 수입이 줄어들까 봐 걱정되진 않아요?",en:"That's impressive. Aren't you worried your income might decrease?"},hl:"수입"}],canDo:"Explain that I act because I'm afraid something might happen (-(으)ㄹ까 봐)",id:"chapter-14-b3",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -(으)ㄹ까 봐",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-(으)ㄹ까 봐",sub:`doing something because you're worried ("in case / afraid that")`,rows:[{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"stem + ㄹ까 봐",ex:"자다 → 잘까 봐, 오다 → 올까 봐"},{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in 받침",add:"stem + 을까 봐",ex:"늦다 → 늦을까 봐, 먹다 → 먹을까 봐"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop ㄹ first)",add:"drop ㄹ, then + ㄹ까 봐",ex:"멀다 → 멀까 봐, 힘들다 → 힘들까 봐"},{when:"Worry about something that already happened",add:"past stem 았/었 + 을까 봐",ex:"갔다 → 갔을까 봐, 잊었다 → 잊었을까 봐"}],examples:[{ko:"비가 올까 봐 우산을 가져왔어요.",en:"I brought an umbrella in case it rains.",romanization:"biga olkka bwa usaneul gajyeowasseoyo.",note:"올까 봐 is the feared event; the main clause is the precaution."},{ko:"학교에 늦을까 봐 일찍 나왔어요.",en:"I left early because I was afraid I'd be late for school.",romanization:"hakgyoe neujeulkka bwa iljjik nawasseoyo.",note:"받침 stem 늦- takes 을까 봐."}],more:{func:"Use -(으)ㄹ까 봐 to give the worried 'what if' that's behind an action. It means 'because I'm afraid that…' or 'in case…', and the main clause says what you do to head that off.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ까 봐 vs -(으)니까",body:"Both give reasons, but -(으)니까 states a plain fact ('because it's raining'), while -(으)ㄹ까 봐 gives a worried 'what if' that hasn't happened ('in case it rains'). Use -(으)ㄹ까 봐 only when the reason is something you're afraid of or want to prevent, and don't put a command or suggestion in the main clause."},pronunciation:"-(으)ㄹ까 tenses the 까: 올까 sounds like [올까], 늦을까 like [느즐까] — the 까 is always a hard, tense sound."}}],canDo:"Explain that I act because I'm afraid something might happen (-(으)ㄹ까 봐)",id:"chapter-14-b4",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 알게 되다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"알게 되다",romanization:"alge doeda",en:"to come to know / find out",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"알게 되다 emphasizes the process of finding out. 알다 simply states that you know."},sentence:{ko:"나중에 그 사실을 알게 됐어요.",en:"I found out that fact later (it came to me)."},target:"알게 됐",options:["business / enterprise","to come to know / find out","to start / begin"],note:"알다 + 게 됐어요: knowing came about through circumstances — you didn't actively seek the info, it found you"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계획",romanization:"gyehoek",en:"plan",pos:"noun",nuance:"계획(計劃, 'count/reckon + plan') is a worked-out plan or scheme for the future. Its single most important quirk is the verb: Koreans BUILD a plan with 세우다 (literally 'to erect/stand up') — 계획을 세우다 — not 만들다 ('make') as the English calque suggests. Other partners: 계획이 있다 (have a plan) and the smooth adverb 계획대로 ('as planned'). Distinguish 계획 from 예정 豫定, which is a SCHEDULED arrangement that is already set ('내일 출발할 예정이에요' = I'm scheduled/due to leave tomorrow), and from 약속, a personal commitment/appointment with someone — 계획 is your own roadmap, 예정 is a fixed scheduling, 약속 involves another party. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly 계획을 만들다 (use 세우다), and translating 'plan to do' with 계획 as a verb — Korean uses -(으)려고 하다 or -(으)ㄹ 계획이다: 여행할 계획이에요 ('I plan to travel'), not 여행을 계획해요 in casual speech."},sentence:{ko:"5년 계획을 세우기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to make a 5-year plan."},target:"계획",options:["plan","business / enterprise","worry / concern"],note:"세우다 → 세우기로 했어요: the decision is to create the plan itself"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"두려워하다",romanization:"duryeowohada",en:"to fear / be afraid of",pos:"verb",nuance:"두려워하다 means to fear a person, thing, change, or possible result. The feared target takes 을/를: 실패를 두려워하다, 변화를 두려워하다. Its adjective counterpart 두렵다 describes the state directly — 실패가 두려워요 — while 두려워하다 presents the person as showing or carrying that fear. 무섭다 is more common for something immediately scary, such as a horror movie, a threatening person, or a loud sound; 두렵다/두려워하다 often fits deeper or more abstract fears such as failure, change, and uncertainty."},sentence:{ko:"실패를 너무 두려워하지 마세요.",en:"Don't be too afraid of failure."},target:"두려워하",options:["to fear / be afraid of","to start / begin","to fail"],note:"두려워하다 expresses fear about a noun; -(으)ㄹ까 봐 is used when the feared thing is an action/event"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"용기",romanization:"yongi",en:"courage",pos:"noun",nuance:"용기 is the Sino-Korean noun 'courage/bravery', 용기 勇氣 — 勇 'brave' + 氣 'spirit/energy' — literally 'brave spirit'. It is the positive inner strength to face fear or do something hard, a trait Koreans openly praise. The most important thing to learn is its fixed collocation: courage is something you 내다 'put out / produce', so 'work up the courage' is 용기를 내다, and the everyday imperative 용기를 내세요 means 'be brave / take heart' — using 가지다 'have' or 만들다 'make' here sounds wrong. Contrast 용기 with 자신감 (自信感) 'confidence/self-assurance': 자신감 is belief in your own ability ('confidence in your English'), built from skill, while 용기 is the nerve to act despite fear, regardless of skill. You also 얻다 'gain' or 잃다 'lose' 용기. The English-speaker pitfall is the verb choice — saying 용기를 가지다 instead of the idiomatic 용기를 내다 — and conflating 'courage' with 'confidence' (자신감), which are distinct in Korean."},sentence:{ko:"용기를 내기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to summon my courage."},target:"용기",options:["courage","business / enterprise","worry / concern"],note:"용기를 내다 (to gather courage) + 기로 했어요: even emotional decisions use 기로 하다"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"인맥",romanization:"inmek",en:"network / personal connections",pos:"noun",nuance:"인맥 means a network of personal connections — people you know through school, work, hobbies, or introductions who may exchange information or opportunities. It is not an internet network; that is 네트워크 or 통신망. 인맥 can be neutral or positive in 인맥이 넓다 and 인맥을 쌓다, but 인맥을 이용하다 may sound calculating if the relationship is treated only as a tool. 인연 is different: it emphasizes the meaningful connection or fate that brought people together, not the practical network itself."},sentence:{ko:"일을 하다 보니까 인맥이 생기게 됐어요.",en:"As I worked, I ended up building a network of connections."},target:"인맥",options:["income / earnings","responsibility","network / personal connections"],note:"하다 보니까 + 게 됐어요: a natural growth over time through circumstances, not planned directly"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"현우",ko:"용기 있네요! 저도 이 회사에 처음 오게 됐을 때 많이 떨렸어요.",en:"That takes courage! When I first ended up coming to this company, I was really nervous too."},hl:"용기"}],canDo:"Show a situation came about by circumstance, not my own choice (-게 되다)",id:"chapter-14-b5",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -게 되다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-게 되다",sub:"how something ended up happening (not by your own choice)",rows:[{when:"Any verb (받침 or no 받침)",add:"verb stem + 게 되다 (no stem change)",ex:"가다 → 가게 되다, 먹다 → 먹게 되다"},{when:"Saying how it turned out (most common)",add:"verb stem + 게 됐어요",ex:"살다 → 살게 됐어요, 알다 → 알게 됐어요"},{when:"A change that's now true",add:"verb stem + 게 됐어요 / 되었어요",ex:"잘하다 → 잘하게 됐어요"},{when:"Future: it'll end up that way",add:"verb stem + 게 될 거예요",ex:"만나다 → 만나게 될 거예요"}],examples:[{ko:"일 때문에 부산에서 살게 됐어요.",en:"I ended up living in Busan because of work.",romanization:"il ttaemune busaneseo salge dwaesseoyo.",note:"되다 shows the move came from circumstances, not free choice."},{ko:"한국 친구를 만나서 한국어를 잘하게 됐어요.",en:"After I made a Korean friend, I got good at Korean.",romanization:"hanguk chingureul mannaseo hangugeoreul jalhage dwaesseoyo.",note:"A gradual change in ability — a natural result, not a decision."}],more:{func:"Use -게 되다 to show a situation came about through circumstances rather than your own decision. It's the difference between 'I do X' and 'it ended up that I do X', usually because of outside events or other people.",keyPoint:{label:"-게 되다 vs plain past",body:"Plain past (살았어요 'I lived') just states the fact. -게 됐어요 ('I ended up living') adds that it happened through circumstances or other people, not purely by your own plan. Reach for -게 되다 when you want to sound modest about a result or play down your own role in it."},pronunciation:""}}],canDo:"Show a situation came about by circumstance, not my own choice (-게 되다)",id:"chapter-14-b6",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Jieun and her colleague Hyunwoo talk over lunch about her decision to leave the company and start a business",lines:[{speaker:"지은",ko:"저 이번에 회사를 그만두기로 했어요.",en:"I've decided to quit the company this time.",romanization:"jeo ibeone hoesareul geumandugiro haesseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"정말요? 왜요? 갑자기라서 좀 놀랐어요.",en:"Really? Why? It's sudden, so I'm a bit surprised.",romanization:"jeongmallyo? waeyo? gapjagiraseo jom nollaasseoyo."},{speaker:"지은",ko:"사실 오래전부터 사업을 해 보고 싶었는데, 이번에 좋은 기회를 잡게 됐어요.",en:"Actually I've wanted to try running a business for a long time, and this time I ended up getting a good opportunity.",romanization:"sasil oraejeonbuteo saeobeul hae bogo sipeonneunde, ibeone joheun gihoereul japge dwaesseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"대단하네요. 수입이 줄어들까 봐 걱정되진 않아요?",en:"That's impressive. Aren't you worried your income might decrease?",romanization:"daedanhaneyo. suibi jureodleulkka bwa geokjeongdoejin anayo?"},{speaker:"지은",ko:"솔직히 실패할까 봐 두려운 건 사실이에요. 그래도 해 보기로 했어요.",en:"Honestly, I'm afraid it might fail — that's true. But I decided to try anyway.",romanization:"soljiki silpaehalkka bwa duryeoun geon sasilieyo. geuraedo hae bogiro haesseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"용기 있네요! 저도 이 회사에 처음 오게 됐을 때 많이 떨렸어요.",en:"That takes courage! When I first ended up coming to this company, I was really nervous too.",romanization:"yongi inneyo! jeodo i hoesae cheoeum oge dwaesseul ttae mani tteollyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"지은",ko:"그 말 들으니까 위로가 되네요. 잘될지는 모르지만 일단 시작하기로 마음먹었어요.",en:"Hearing that is reassuring. I don't know if it'll go well, but I've made up my mind to start at least.",romanization:"geu mal deureunikka wiroga doeneyo. jaldoeljineun moreujiman ildan sijakhagiro maeumeogeosseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"응원할게요! 궁금한 것 있으면 언제든지 물어보세요.",en:"I'll cheer you on! If you have any questions, feel free to ask anytime.",romanization:"eungwonhalkkeyo! gunggeumhan geot isseumyeon eonjedeunji mureoboseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I brought an umbrella because I was worried it might rain.'",tokens:["비가 올까 봐","우산을 가져왔어요"],correct:"비가 올까 봐 우산을 가져왔어요",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ까 봐 gives the worry that motivated the action. The umbrella action follows because rain was the feared outcome."}],canDo:"Say I've decided or agreed to do something (-기로 하다)",id:"chapter-14-b7",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 새로운 시작 (A New Beginning)",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"새로운 시작 (A New Beginning)",chunks:["오늘 드디어 회사에 사직서를 냈어요. 몇 달 전부터 생각해 오던 일이지만 막상 하고 나니 실감이 안 나요. 솔직히 수입이 없어질까 봐 몇 번이나 포기할 뻔했어요. 하지만 오래전부터 내 사업을 하기로 마음먹었던 게 맞는 것 같아서, 결국 사직서를 내기로 했어요.","친구 지수는 '잘 안될까 봐 걱정되지 않냐'고 물었는데, 솔직히 걱정은 돼요. 그래도 아무것도 안 하다 보면 평생 후회할 것 같아서요. 어쩌다 보니 이렇게 큰 결정을 하게 됐는데, 지금은 오히려 홀가분한 느낌이에요."],translation:"Today I finally handed in my resignation at the company. It's something I'd been thinking about for months, but now that I've actually done it, it doesn't feel real. Honestly, I nearly gave up several times because I was afraid I'd lose my income. But since I'd long ago made up my mind to run my own business, I ultimately decided to hand in the resignation. My friend Jisu asked if I wasn't worried it might not go well — and honestly, I am worried. Still, I feel like if I just do nothing, I'll regret it for the rest of my life. Somehow I ended up making this big decision, and right now I actually feel strangely light.",qas:[{q:"What two things was the writer afraid might happen? (Find the -(으)ㄹ까 봐 phrases.)",a:"수입이 없어질까 봐 (afraid of losing income) and 잘 안될까 봐 (afraid it won't go well)."},{q:"How does the writer describe making this big decision — did they plan it carefully or did it happen circumstantially? Which phrase tells you this?",a:"어쩌다 보니 이렇게 큰 결정을 하게 됐는데 — 'Somehow I ended up making this big decision.' 하게 됐는데 (게 되다 form) frames the decision as something that came about through circumstances, even though the writer had been thinking about it for a long time."},{q:"Find all the -기로 하다 / 기로 마음먹다 forms in the text. What decision does each one refer to, and why is 기로 하다 (rather than -(으)ㄹ게요) used in each case?",a:"내 사업을 하기로 마음먹었던 게: long-ago personal resolution to run a business — settled contract, not a live promise. 사직서를 내기로 했어요: the ultimate decision to actually resign — also already made before this diary entry. Neither is an on-the-spot promise to a listener, so -(으)ㄹ게요 would be wrong in both cases."}]}],canDo:"Explain that I act because I'm afraid something might happen (-(으)ㄹ까 봐)",id:"chapter-14-b8",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly expresses 'I'm afraid I might be late for the meeting'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"회의에 늦을까요? 걱정이에요.",ok:!1},{text:"회의에 늦을까 봐 걱정이에요.",ok:!0},{text:"회의에 늦어서 걱정이에요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Option 1 (늦을까요?) asks the listener 'Do you think I'll be late?' — a question, not a worry. Option 3 (늦어서) uses a causal connector for something that already happened — but the meeting hasn't started yet. Only option 2 correctly uses -(으)ㄹ까 봐 to express a feared future outcome as the reason for worry."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Your Korean friend asks why you moved to Seoul. Which answer gives a natural reason and uses -게 됐어요 well?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"제가 결정해서 서울로 이사했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"일 때문에 서울로 이사하게 됐어요.",ok:!0},{text:"서울로 이사하기로 했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"The friend asked why, so the best answer includes a reason: 일 때문에. -게 됐어요 then frames the move as a life circumstance that came about naturally. Option 1 over-emphasizes personal choice, and option 3 only says you decided to move, without answering the why."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"사업___ 시작하기로 했어요. (I decided to start a business.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"시작하다 is an action verb, and 사업 is what you start, so it takes 을. 이 would make 사업 the subject and break the intended meaning."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I brought an umbrella because I was worried it might rain.'",tokens:["비가 올까 봐","우산을 가져왔어요"],correct:"비가 올까 봐 우산을 가져왔어요",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ까 봐 gives the worry that motivated the action. The umbrella action follows because rain was the feared outcome."}],canDo:"Show a situation came about by circumstance, not my own choice (-게 되다)",id:"chapter-14-b9",chapterId:"chapter-14",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-15",number:15,title:"-는 척하다 · -(으)ㄹ 뻔하다",goal:"Use -는 척하다 to say someone is pretending or faking a state or action",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 감동~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"감동",romanization:"gamdong",en:"deep emotion; being moved or touched",pos:"noun",nuance:"감동 is the warm, welling-up feeling of being moved or touched — the lump-in-the-throat reaction to a kind gesture, a film, or a reunion. It is Sino-Korean, 感動 ('feel' 感 + 'move/stir' 動), literally 'the heart being moved', which is exactly the English metaphor 'to be moved', so the image transfers nicely. The key learner difficulty is the verb frames: Korean splits 'being moved' (감동을 받다, 'receive emotion') from 'moving others' (감동을 주다, 'give emotion'), and the standalone verb 감동하다/감동받다 means YOU felt it — never 'to move a physical object' (that is 옮기다 or 움직이다). Compare the near-synonym 인상 印象 (impression): 인상 깊다 ('it left a deep impression') is more neutral and cognitive, while 감동 is purely emotional and positive. Compare also 감격 (overwhelming emotion, often tears) which is stronger and rarer. Natural collocations: 감동을 받다/주다, 큰 감동 (not 많은 감동), 감동적인 N. Tone is sincere and a touch earnest; the slang 감동 먹다 is friendly but casual only."},sentence:{ko:"그 영화 너무 감동적이어서 울 뻔했어요.",en:"That movie was so moving I almost cried."},target:"감동",options:["fear; dread","anger; rage","deep emotion; being moved or touched"],note:"감동적이다 = to be moving/touching; 울 뻔했어요 shows the emotional near-miss naturally"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"두려움",romanization:"duryeoum",en:"fear; dread",pos:"noun",nuance:"두려움 is the noun 'fear/dread', from the adjective 두렵다 (a ㅂ-irregular: 두렵 + 음 → 두려움). It names a deep, often anticipatory inner fear — of the future, failure, the unknown — more reflective and lasting than a sudden jolt. Separate two relatives: 무서움/무섭다 is concrete, gut-level scared ('a scary movie', 'the dog is scary'), the word you actually say in conversation, whereas 두려움 is heavier, more abstract and literary, and is the one paired with big life anxieties. A third, 공포 (恐怖), is sheer terror/horror, the genre word for horror films and panic. So a spider triggers 무서움, an uncertain future triggers 두려움, a horror movie deals in 공포. The English-speaker pitfall: 'fear' maps to all of these, so beginners overuse 두려움 in casual speech where 무서워요 is natural, and forget the ㅂ-irregular spelling (두렵다 → 두려워요, 두려움)."},sentence:{ko:"그 사람 표정을 보니까 많이 두려워하나 봐요.",en:"From that person's expression, I guess they're really afraid."},target:null,options:["deep emotion; being moved or touched","anger; rage","fear; dread"],note:"두려워하다 is an action verb, so it takes -나 봐요. The visible expression is the evidence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"분노",romanization:"bunno",en:"anger; rage",pos:"noun",nuance:"분노 is the Sino-Korean noun for 'anger/wrath', 분노 憤怒 — 憤 'indignation' + 怒 'rage' — so by its very characters it is righteous, intense fury, not petty annoyance. The crucial contrast for learners is the everyday word 화 (火, literally 'fire'): in conversation Koreans say 화가 나다 'anger arises' or 화를 내다 'show anger' for ordinary irritation, while 분노 is reserved for grave, often moral or collective rage — social injustice, betrayal, public outrage — and shows up in news, speeches, and writing far more than in casual chat. Saying 분노했어요 over a late bus sounds comically overblown; 짜증 났어요 'I got annoyed' or 화가 났어요 fits. The pitfall: English 'angry' is one word covering all intensities, so learners overuse 분노. Match the register — keep 분노 for the heavy stuff and use 화/짜증 for daily friction."},sentence:{ko:"팀장님 말투가 그러는 걸 보니까 화나신 건가 봐요.",en:"The way the team leader is talking, I'm guessing they're angry."},target:null,options:["deep emotion; being moved or touched","anger; rage","fear; dread"],note:"-(으)ㄴ가 봐요 = inferring the emotion from observable evidence (their tone)"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"만족",romanization:"manjok",en:"satisfaction; contentment",pos:"noun",nuance:"만족 (滿足, 滿 'full' + 足 'enough/sufficient') is literally being 'filled to sufficiency' — the settled feeling that something has met or exceeded your expectations. The synonym line to hold: 만족 is being content with what you have, whereas 행복 is broad life-happiness and 만끽(滿喫) is actively savoring something to the full — you 만족하다 with a meal's quality but 만끽하다 the flavor moment by moment. Register is neutral and everyday; 만족하다 names the person's state, 만족스럽다 calls the thing satisfying. English-speaker pitfall: 'satisfied WITH' makes English speakers write N을 만족하다, but the particle is 에 — 결과에 만족하다, never 결과를 만족하다. Also resist 만족하다 for an object's quality ('the result is satisfying' = 결과가 만족스럽다, not 결과가 만족하다)."},sentence:{ko:"합격했다는 소식을 듣고 만족스러워서 소리를 지를 뻔했어요.",en:"When I heard I passed, I was so satisfied I almost shouted."},target:"만족",options:["satisfaction; contentment","fear; dread","anger; rage"],note:"만족스럽다 = to feel satisfied; 소리를 지르다 = to shout; 지를 뻔했어요 = almost-shouted close call"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"신뢰",romanization:"silloe",en:"trust; confidence (in someone)",pos:"noun",nuance:"신뢰 (信賴, 信 'believe' + 賴 'rely on') is trust as a reasoned, earned noun — more formal and weighty than the everyday verb 믿다 (to believe/trust someone in the moment). The key contrast for learners is 신뢰 vs 믿음: 믿음 leans emotional or even religious (faith), while 신뢰 is confidence built on a track record, which is why it fits people, workplaces, and institutions alike. You say 신뢰가 가다 ('trust flows') when someone earns your confidence and 신뢰가 무너지다 when it shatters. Note that unlike 우정, 신뢰 does take 하다: 신뢰하다 means 'to trust (someone),' taking the person as a 을/를 object. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 믿다 in formal writing where Korean prefers the noun 신뢰 (e.g. 신뢰를 쌓다, not 믿음을 만들다)."},sentence:{ko:"그분이 모르는 척한 것 때문에 신뢰를 잃었어요.",en:"Because he pretended not to know, I lost trust in him."},target:"신뢰",options:["deep emotion; being moved or touched","fear; dread","trust; confidence (in someone)"],note:"모르는 척하다 (pretending not to know) combined with 신뢰를 잃다 (lose trust) — common real-life pairing"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"존경",romanization:"jongyeong",en:"respect; admiration",pos:"noun",nuance:"존경 (尊敬, 尊 'revere/honor' + 敬 'respect') stacks two reverence characters into deep admiration for a person's character, effort, or achievement — stronger than mere 인정(認定) 'acknowledgment.' The synonym line every learner needs: 존경 is looking UP to a person, while 존중(尊重) is respecting someone's rights, opinions or choices on equal terms — you 존경하다 a mentor you admire, but 존중하다 a colleague's differing view. Register: formal and warm; you'll hear 존경하는 before a title in speeches and letters, and 존경받는 사람 is high praise. English-speaker pitfall: English 'respect' covers both senses, so learners say 존경 when they mean 존중 (e.g. 'I respect your decision' must be 존중해요, not 존경해요). Also keep 존경 for people — to 'respect/value' an abstract thing like time or rules, use 중요하게 여기다 or 존중하다, not 존경하다."},sentence:{ko:"그분 이야기가 나오면 다들 조용해지는 걸 보니까 많이 존경하나 봐요.",en:"Seeing everyone quiet down when that person comes up, I guess they respect them a lot."},target:"존경",options:["respect; admiration","deep emotion; being moved or touched","anger; rage"],note:"존경하다 is an action verb, so it takes -나 봐요. The group reaction gives the clue."}],canDo:"Say someone is faking or putting on an act (-는 척하다)",id:"chapter-15-b1",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -는 척하다",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-는 척하다",sub:"pretend / act like",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"왔는데",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"척",post:"했어요.",hit:!0}],ko:"친구가 왔는데 자는 척했어요.",en:"My friend came over, but I pretended to be asleep.",romanization:"chinguga wanneunde janeun cheokaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"모르면서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"아",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"척",post:"하지",hit:!0},{pre:"마.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"잘 모르면서 아는 척하지 마.",en:"Don't act like you know when you don't.",romanization:"jal moreumyeonseo aneun cheokaji ma."}],rule:{name:"-는 척하다",rows:[{when:"Action verb, present (pretend to be doing now)",add:"-는 척하다",ex:"자다 → 자는 척하다 (pretend to be sleeping)"},{when:"Action verb, past (pretend to have done)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 척하다",ex:"먹다 → 먹은 척하다 / 보다 → 본 척하다"},{when:"Adjective (descriptive verb)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 척하다",ex:"괜찮다 → 괜찮은 척하다 / 아프다 → 아픈 척하다"},{when:"이다 (noun + be)",add:"-인 척하다",ex:"학생이다 → 학생인 척하다 (pretend to be a student)"}]},more:{func:"Use -는 척하다 to say someone pretends to do or be something — putting on an act that doesn't match reality. It marks behavior that's deliberately faked.",keyPoint:{label:"-는 척하다 vs -(으)ㄴ 척하다",body:"The ending works just like a modifier (관형사형): an action happening now takes -는 (자는 척), a finished action takes -(으)ㄴ (먹은 척), and adjectives also take -(으)ㄴ (아픈 척). You can swap 척 for 체 with no change in meaning (자는 체하다), though 척 is more common in everyday speech."},pronunciation:"척했어요 sounds like [처캐써요] — ㄱ + ㅎ merge into the aspirated [ㅋ]."}}],canDo:"Say someone is faking or putting on an act (-는 척하다)",id:"chapter-15-b2",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 성실하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"성실하다",romanization:"seongsilhada",en:"to be diligent; conscientious; sincere",pos:"adjective",nuance:"성실하다 (誠實하다, from 誠 'sincerity' + 實 'substance/truth') is one of the most valued character words in Korean work and school culture: it means earnest, diligent, and reliable in carrying out duties — the steady person who shows up and follows through. Because it praises consistent effort rather than results, it is the single safest strength to claim in an interview ('저는 성실합니다'). The key near-synonym to separate is 정직하다 (honest): 성실 is about diligence and faithfulness to your work, while 정직 is about not lying — a lazy person can still be 정직, and a liar can technically be 성실. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: do not double the 하 (the 요-form is 성실해요, never 성실하해요), and do not reach for 성실하다 when you mean 'sincere' in the sense of heartfelt feelings — that emotional sincerity is 진실하다 or 진심이다; 성실 is sincerity expressed as hard work."},sentence:{ko:"그분 정말 성실한 것 같아요.",en:"That person seems really diligent."},target:"성실한",options:["to be diligent; conscientious; sincere","tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy","deep emotion; being moved or touched"],note:"성실하다 is an adjective → 성실한 것 같다 (soft opinion); contrast with -나 보다 which implies outside evidence"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"정직하다",romanization:"jeongjikada",en:"to be honest; truthful; upright",pos:"adjective",nuance:"정직하다 (正直하다, 正 'correct/upright' + 直 'straight') means honest and truthful in the moral sense: not lying, not cheating, dealing straight with people. The near-synonym you must distinguish is 솔직하다 (率直, frank/candid): 솔직하다 is about openly saying what you really think or feel ('솔직히 말하면…' = to be frank…), whereas 정직하다 is about not deceiving anyone — a moral judgment, not a communication style. You praise a child, a clerk, or a business as 정직하다; you call someone 솔직하다 when they bluntly share an opinion. Register: 정직하다 is a weighty ethical compliment, while 솔직하다 is everyday and can even be a mild warning ('너무 솔직해' = a bit too blunt). The English-speaker pitfall is mapping both English 'honest' senses onto one word — in 'Honestly, I think…' English 'honestly' means 솔직히, not 정직히. Also, 정직해 보이다 means 'seems honest/trustworthy,' not 'looks smart.'"},sentence:{ko:"그 사람이 사실대로 말하는 걸 보니까 정직한 사람인가 봐요.",en:"Seeing that person tell the truth, I guess they're an honest person."},target:"정직한",options:["tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy","deep emotion; being moved or touched","to be honest; truthful; upright"],note:"정직한 사람인가 봐요 uses noun phrase 사람인가 봐요 after the adjective modifier 정직한."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"신중하다",romanization:"sinjunghada",en:"to be careful; prudent; cautious in decisions",pos:"adjective",nuance:"신중하다 (愼重하다, 愼 'careful/prudent' + 重 'weighty/grave') means prudent and careful specifically about decisions and words — weighing things seriously before you act or speak, rather than rushing. It is the polished, positive way to describe caution, and a great interview strength (the mature flip side of being slow). Crucially, separate it from two look-alikes: 조심하다 is physical 'be careful / watch out' (조심하세요 = mind the step, take care on the road), so you never use 신중하다 for bodily caution; and 소심하다 is fearful timidity. 신중하다 is thoughtful deliberation, neither physical wariness nor anxiety. Its negative twin is 우유부단하다 (indecisive): 신중하다 weighs wisely and then chooses, while 우유부단 just can't choose. Register: fully positive and usable formally (신중을 기하다 in notices). The English-speaker pitfall is using 신중하다 like English 'careful' for everything — 'be careful with the knife' is 칼 조심하세요, not 신중. Stem note: 신중해요, not 신중하해요."},sentence:{ko:"그 사람 말을 잘 안 하는 걸 보니까 신중한가 봐요.",en:"They don't say much — I'm guessing they're the careful type."},target:"신중한",options:["to be coolheaded; level-headed; cold; detached","to be careful; prudent; cautious in decisions","tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy"],note:"신중하다 → -(으)ㄴ가 봐요; inferring personality from observable behavior"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"냉정하다",romanization:"naengjeonghada",en:"to be coolheaded; level-headed; cold; detached",pos:"adjective",nuance:"냉정하다 (冷靜하다, 冷 'cold' + 靜 'quiet/calm') is two-faced and context decides everything. Admiringly it means 'coolheaded / objective' — judging without being swayed by emotion, a real strength under pressure (냉정한 판단, 냉정하게 분석하다). Critically it means 'cold / detached' — treating a person with chilly distance (냉정하게 대하다, 냉정한 말투), which hurts. The same sentence frame can flip: 냉정한 판단 praises, 냉정한 사람 usually faults. Two synonyms sharpen the picture: 침착하다 is purely positive composure (no cold undertone at all), so for a job interview you say 침착하다, not 냉정하다, about yourself; and 차갑다 is the native word that leans almost entirely on the cold-hearted reading and never carries the 'objective judgment' praise. The English-speaker pitfall is assuming 냉정하다 is always negative like 'cold-blooded' — calling your own decision-making 냉정하다 is actually a compliment, while calling a friend 냉정하다 is a complaint. Stem note: 냉정해요, not 냉정하해요."},sentence:{ko:"냉정한 척했지만 사실 힘들었어요.",en:"I pretended to be coolheaded, but it was actually hard."},target:"냉정한",options:["to be careful; prudent; cautious in decisions","tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy","to be coolheaded; level-headed; cold; detached"],note:"냉정한 척하다 uses the target adjective directly: acting detached even when you are shaken inside."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"까다롭다",romanization:"kkadaropda",en:"tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy",pos:"adjective",nuance:"까다롭다 describes something *tricky and hard to satisfy* — fiddly exam questions, fussy requirements, or a finicky, hard-to-please person. It is a ㅂ-irregular adjective: the final ㅂ turns to 우 before a vowel, giving 까다로워요 (present), 까다로웠어요 (past), and 까다로운 (before a noun) — memorize these because regularizing to 까다롭아요 is the single most common learner error. The crucial meaning contrast is 까다롭다 vs 어렵다: 어렵다 is plainly 'difficult/hard' in a neutral way, but 까다롭다 adds a layer of *finickiness* — picky, particular, full of fiddly conditions. So a math problem can be 어렵다 (just hard) or 까다롭다 (sneaky, full of traps); a person who is 까다롭다 is hard to please, which 어렵다 can't convey. Applied to people it leans negative (demanding, fussy), so use care describing someone's character. Habitual partners: 문제가 까다롭다, 조건이 까다롭다, 입맛이 까다롭다, 까다로운 교수님."},sentence:{ko:"심사 기준이 까다로운가 봐요. 다들 탈락했네요.",en:"I guess the judging criteria are strict — everyone got eliminated."},target:null,options:["tricky; demanding; picky; hard to satisfy","to be diligent; conscientious; sincere","to be honest; truthful; upright"],note:"까다롭다 → -(으)ㄴ가 봐요 (evidence-based inference from the result); ㅂ-irregular: 까다롭 + 은가 → 까다로운가"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하마터면",romanization:"hamateomyeon",en:"'I almost...'; by a narrow margin (near-miss adverb)",pos:"adverb",nuance:"하마터면 is dramatic but natural when the speaker feels relieved that the event did not happen."},sentence:{ko:"하마터면 지갑을 잃어버릴 뻔했어요.",en:"I almost lost my wallet!"},target:"하마터면",options:["deep emotion; being moved or touched","fear; dread","'I almost...'; by a narrow margin (near-miss adverb)"],note:"하마터면 always pairs with -(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요 to emphasize the narrowness of the near-miss"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지연",ko:"하마터면 제가 먼저 물어볼 뻔했는데 참았어요.",en:"I almost asked first, but I held back."},hl:"하마터면"}],canDo:"Describe a close call that almost happened (-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다)",id:"chapter-15-b3",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -(으)ㄹ 뻔하다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔하다",sub:"almost happened (a close call)",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ 뻔하다",ex:"넘어지다 → 넘어질 뻔하다 (almost fell)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"-을 뻔하다",ex:"죽다 → 죽을 뻔하다 / 늦다 → 늦을 뻔하다"},{when:"ㄹ-stem verb (ㄹ stays)",add:"-ㄹ 뻔하다",ex:"울다 → 울 뻔하다 (almost cried)"},{when:"Everyday use: always past tense",add:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다",ex:"잊어버리다 → 잊어버릴 뻔했어요 (I almost forgot)"}],examples:[{ko:"길에서 넘어질 뻔했어요.",en:"I almost fell on the street.",romanization:"gireseo neomeojil ppeonhaesseoyo.",note:"넘어지다 ends in a vowel → -ㄹ 뻔하다; past 뻔했어요."},{ko:"늦잠을 자서 학교에 늦을 뻔했어요.",en:"I overslept and almost got to school late.",romanization:"neutjameul jaseo hakgyoe neujeul ppeonhaesseoyo.",note:"늦다 ends in 받침 ㅈ → -을 뻔하다."}],more:{func:"Use -(으)ㄹ 뻔하다 to say something nearly happened but didn't — a near miss. It's almost always used in the past tense (-(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요) because the close call already happened.",keyPoint:{label:"Always past tense, always 'didn't happen'",body:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 means the event did NOT happen — it only came close. So 넘어질 뻔했어요 = 'I almost fell (but didn't).' Don't put the main verb in the past too: say 넘어질 뻔했어요, never 넘어졌을 뻔했어요."},pronunciation:"뻔했어요 sounds like [뻔해써요]; the opening ㅃ is a tense (fortis) sound — keep it hard, not a soft [ㅂ]."}}],canDo:"Describe a close call that almost happened (-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다)",id:"chapter-15-b4",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 눈치채다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"눈치채다",romanization:"nunchichaeda",en:"to pick up on; to sense; to figure out (from cues)",pos:"verb",nuance:"눈치채다 means noticing or figuring something out from indirect signs: someone’s expression, unusual behavior, a changed tone, or a small clue. It describes the moment you catch on. 눈치를 보다 is different — it is the ongoing act of watching the mood and adjusting your behavior — and 눈치가 빠르다 describes a person who catches such signals quickly. 알아차리다 is a more neutral ‘notice/realize’; 눈치채다 specifically suggests social or contextual clues. The word is commonly written as one verb, 눈치채다, although the noun phrase 눈치를 채다 is also used."},sentence:{ko:"걔가 눈치챈 것 같아서 아무것도 모르는 척했어요.",en:"I felt like they were picking up on it, so I pretended not to know anything."},target:"눈치챈",options:["to pick up on; to sense; to figure out (from cues)","to be obvious; to show; to give away (that you're faking)","to tremble; to be shaky (with nerves or emotion)"],note:"눈치채다 → trigger for using 척하다: when someone might catch on, you put on the ignorance mask"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"감추다",romanization:"gamchuda",en:"to hide; to conceal (feelings or information)",pos:"verb",nuance:"감추다 is a transitive verb meaning to conceal something from sight or knowledge: 감정을 감추다, 사실을 감추다, 물건을 감추다. 숨기다 is the more frequent everyday verb and works broadly for objects, information, and feelings; 감추다 often sounds slightly more deliberate, literary, or focused on keeping something from showing. The hidden thing takes 을/를. The result can be expressed with 감춰지다 or 감추어지다, and ‘cannot hide it’ is 감출 수 없다 or 감춰지지 않다 depending on whether the focus is ability or visible result."},sentence:{ko:"감정을 감추려고 괜찮은 척했어요.",en:"I pretended to be fine in an attempt to hide my feelings."},target:"감추",options:["to hide; to conceal (feelings or information)","to be obvious; to show; to give away (that you're faking)","to tremble; to be shaky (with nerves or emotion)"],note:"감추려고 (in order to hide) + 척하다 — a very natural Korean expression of masking emotions"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"티나다",romanization:"tinada",en:"to be obvious; to show; to give away (that you're faking)",pos:"verb",nuance:"The natural expression is 티가 나다, literally ‘a sign appears’: an emotion, change, lie, effort, or imitation becomes noticeable. In casual speech the particle is often dropped — 티 나요, 너무 티 나 — and online text may join it as 티나다, but standard spacing keeps the noun and verb separate. Compare 티가 나다 with 티를 내다: 티가 나다 means it shows, often unintentionally; 티를 내다 means someone deliberately lets it show or makes a display of it. 티가 안 나다 means the difference is not noticeable and can be positive, as with natural makeup or a well-repaired item."},sentence:{ko:"자는 척했는데 티가 났나 봐요.",en:"I pretended to be asleep but I guess it was obvious."},target:null,options:["to be obvious; to show; to give away (that you're faking)","to pick up on; to sense; to figure out (from cues)","to hide; to conceal (feelings or information)"],note:"티가 났나 봐요 = evidence-based guess (someone's reaction revealed it); 척하다 + 나 봐요 together in one sentence"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"떨리다",romanization:"teollida",en:"to tremble; to be shaky (with nerves or emotion)",pos:"verb",nuance:"떨리다 can be physical shaking or the nervous feeling behind it."},sentence:{ko:"발표하다가 목소리가 떨릴 뻔했어요.",en:"During my presentation, my voice almost started shaking."},target:"떨릴",options:["to tremble; to be shaky (with nerves or emotion)","to pick up on; to sense; to figure out (from cues)","to hide; to conceal (feelings or information)"],note:"떨리다 ends in vowel → -ㄹ 뻔했어요; common near-miss emotion verb in presentations and auditions"}],canDo:"Guess about a situation from what I see or hear (-나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다)",id:"chapter-15-b5",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다",sub:"guessing from evidence",rows:[{when:"Action verb (present)",add:"-나 보다",ex:"오다 → 오나 보다 / 먹다 → 먹나 보다"},{when:"Any verb, past tense",add:"-았/었나 보다",ex:"가다 → 갔나 보다 / 자다 → 잤나 보다"},{when:"Adjective (descriptive verb)",add:"-(으)ㄴ가 보다",ex:"춥다 → 추운가 보다 / 바쁘다 → 바쁜가 보다"},{when:"이다 (noun + be)",add:"-인가 보다",ex:"학생이다 → 학생인가 보다 (guess: must be a student)"}],examples:[{ko:"사람들이 우산을 써요. 비가 오나 봐요.",en:"People are using umbrellas. I guess it's raining.",romanization:"saramdeuri usaneul sseoyo. biga ona bwayo.",note:"오다 is an action verb, present → -나 보다."},{ko:"친구가 전화를 안 받아요. 자나 봐요.",en:"My friend isn't answering the phone. I guess they're sleeping.",romanization:"chinguga jeonhwareul an badayo. jana bwayo.",note:"자다 action verb, present → 자나 봐요."}],more:{func:"Use -나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다 to make a guess based on something you can see or hear, like English 'I guess...' or 'It seems...'. You're inferring about a situation rather than stating it as a fact.",keyPoint:{label:"-나 보다 vs -(으)ㄹ 것 같다",body:"-나 보다 means you're inferring from something you observed ('I see this, so I guess that'). -(으)ㄹ 것 같다 is a softer, more general guess or impression that needs no evidence. Use -나 보다 only when you're reasoning from a clue. (The fuller contrast lives in the conjecture chapter.)"},pronunciation:"봐요 sounds like [봐:요], a contraction of 보아요; keep it one syllable [bwa]."}}],canDo:"Guess about a situation from what I see or hear (-나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다)",id:"chapter-15-b6",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two coworkers, Jiyeon and Minsu, are eating lunch together and talking about a stressful morning",lines:[{speaker:"지연",ko:"오늘 오전에 발표하다가 목소리가 떨릴 뻔했어요.",en:"This morning during my presentation, my voice almost started shaking.",romanization:"oneul ojeone balpyohadaga moksoriga tteollil ppeonhaesseoyo."},{speaker:"민수",ko:"어, 저도 봤는데 엄청 자연스러웠어요. 몰랐어요.",en:"Oh, I was watching and you were super natural. I couldn't tell.",romanization:"eo, jeodo bwanneunde eomcheong jayeonseureowosseoyo. mollasseoyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"고마워요. 사실 많이 떨렸는데 괜찮은 척했어요.",en:"Thank you. I was actually shaking a lot, but I pretended to be fine.",romanization:"gomawoyo. sasil mani tteollyeonneunde gwaenchaneun cheokaesseoyo."},{speaker:"민수",ko:"그래요? 팀장님도 잘 모르시는 것 같던데요.",en:"Really? It seemed like the team leader didn't notice either.",romanization:"geuraeyo? timjangnimdo jal moreusineun geot gatdeondeyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"팀장님은 지금 다른 문제로 머리가 복잡한가 봐요.",en:"I think the team leader's head is full of other problems right now.",romanization:"timjangnimeun jigeum dareun munjero meoriga bokjapanga bwayo."},{speaker:"민수",ko:"맞아요. 아침에 표정이 안 좋으시더라고요. 스트레스 많이 받으시나 봐요.",en:"Right. Their expression this morning didn't look good. I'm guessing they're under a lot of stress.",romanization:"majayo. achime pyojeongi an joeusideoragoyo. seuteureseu mani badeusinabwayo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"하마터면 제가 먼저 물어볼 뻔했는데 참았어요.",en:"I almost asked first, but I held back.",romanization:"hamateomyeon jega meonjeo mureobol ppeonhaenneunde chamasseoyo."},{speaker:"민수",ko:"잘하셨어요. 지금은 모르는 척하는 게 나을 것 같아요.",en:"Good call. Right now, playing dumb is probably better.",romanization:"jalhasyeosseoyo. jigeumeun moreuneun cheokhaneun ge naeul geot gatayo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I pretended to be busy.'",tokens:["바쁜","척했어요"],correct:"바쁜 척했어요",explanation:"Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ before 척하다, so 바쁘다 becomes 바쁜 척했어요. This means you acted as if you were busy."}],canDo:"Say someone is faking or putting on an act (-는 척하다)",id:"chapter-15-b7",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 지연의 하루 일기 (Jiyeon's Day Diary)",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"지연의 하루 일기 (Jiyeon's Day Diary)",chunks:['오늘은 정말 힘든 하루였다. 아침에 중요한 발표가 있었는데, 엄청 긴장해서 울 뻔했다. 발표하는 내내 떨렸지만 괜찮은 척하면서 끝까지 버텼다. 팀장님은 좀 무표정하게 앉아 계셨는데, 뭔가 불만이 있으신가 봐서 마음이 불안했다. 나중에 동료한테 물어봤더니 "요즘 윗사람 때문에 스트레스를 많이 받으시나 봐"라고 했다.',"그 말 듣고 조금 안심이 됐다. 퇴근하다가 지갑을 잃어버릴 뻔했다. 하마터면 큰일 날 뻔했는데, 다행히 가방 안쪽에 있었다. 내일은 오늘보다 나은 하루이길 바란다."],translation:'Today was a really tough day. There was an important presentation in the morning, and I was so nervous I almost cried. I was shaking the whole time but I pretended to be fine and hung in there to the end. The team leader sat there with a blank expression, and I felt uneasy thinking they seemed to have some complaint. I asked a coworker later, and they said, "It looks like the team leader is under a lot of stress because of upper management these days." Hearing that made me feel a bit relieved. On the way out of the office, I almost lost my wallet. That was almost a disaster, but thankfully it was in the inside pocket of my bag. I hope tomorrow is a better day than today.',qas:[{q:"Why did Jiyeon pretend to be fine during the presentation?",a:"She was shaking and almost cried (울 뻔했다), but she put on a mask of composure (괜찮은 척하면서) and pushed through to the end."},{q:"How does Jiyeon learn about the team leader's stress? What Korean pattern does she use for this inference?",a:"She hears it from a coworker. She uses 스트레스를 많이 받으시나 봐 — the -나 봐 pattern (action verb 받다 → 받으시나 봐) because the coworker is making an evidence-based inference, not stating a confirmed fact."},{q:"Find every instance of 척하다, 뻔하다, and -나/-(으)ㄴ가 보다 in the text. For each one, explain WHY that particular pattern is used there.",a:"척하다: 괜찮은 척하면서 — she is deliberately performing composure she does not feel (adjective 괜찮다 → -(으)ㄴ 척). 뻔하다: 울 뻔했다 — she genuinely nearly cried (울다 ㄹ-stem → 울 뻔했다); 잃어버릴 뻔했다 — she genuinely almost lost her wallet (잃어버리다 vowel stem → -ㄹ 뻔했다); 큰일 날 뻔했는데 — almost a disaster (나다 vowel stem → -ㄹ 뻔했는데). -나/-(으)ㄴ가 보다: 불만이 있으신가 봐서 — 불만이 있다 + honorific 있으시다 + -ㄴ가 봐; evidence-based inference from a blank expression. 스트레스를 많이 받으시나 봐 — 받다 is an action verb → -나 봐; the coworker is inferring from workplace clues."}]}],canDo:"Describe a close call that almost happened (-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다)",id:"chapter-15-b8",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"You notice your friend has been very quiet lately and you want to say 'I'm guessing something happened.' Which is correct?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"무슨 일이 있는 척해요.",ok:!1},{text:"무슨 일이 있나 봐요.",ok:!0},{text:"무슨 일이 있을 뻔했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"있다 is a stative verb (to exist/have) → use -나 봐요 for evidence-based inference. Option 1 (척해요) means PRETENDING something happened — totally different, implying your friend is faking. Option 3 (뻔했어요) means something ALMOST happened but didn't — also wrong; you're guessing it DID happen."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly says 'She pretended to be sick'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"아프는 척했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"아픈 척했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"아팠는 척했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"아프다 is an adjective. Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ in modifier position: 아프 + 은 → 아픈. Option 1 (아프는 척) wrongly uses the action-verb pattern -는. Option 3 (아팠는 척) incorrectly attaches -는 to a past tense stem — neither 아팠는 nor 척하다 work that way. The correct and only natural form is 아픈 척했어요."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"친구___ 아픈가 봐요. (I guess my friend is sick.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"가",ok:!0},{text:"를",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"아프다 describes the friend, so 친구 takes the subject marker 가. 를 would make the friend an object, which does not fit an adjective sentence."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I pretended to be busy.'",tokens:["바쁜","척했어요"],correct:"바쁜 척했어요",explanation:"Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ before 척하다, so 바쁘다 becomes 바쁜 척했어요. This means you acted as if you were busy."}],canDo:"Guess about a situation from what I see or hear (-나 보다 / -(으)ㄴ가 보다)",id:"chapter-15-b9",chapterId:"chapter-15",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-16",number:16,title:"-더니 · -길래",goal:"Use -더니 to narrate observed patterns that led to a result",biteCount:11,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 취소되다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취소되다",romanization:"chwisodoeda",en:"to be cancelled (passive)",pos:"verb",nuance:"취소되다 is the passive/intransitive side of 취소하다. The event, appointment, order, or reservation that no longer happens becomes the subject with 이/가: 행사가 취소됐어요, 예약이 취소됐어요. If a person actively cancels something, use 을/를 + 취소하다: 제가 예약을 취소했어요. Do not confuse cancellation with postponement: 연기되다 means it will happen later, while 취소되다 means the plan is no longer going ahead. 변경되다 means details changed but the event may still happen."},sentence:{ko:"비가 오는 바람에 행사가 취소됐어요.",en:"The event got cancelled because of the rain."},target:"취소됐",options:["to practice","to increase / grow / improve","to be cancelled (passive)"],note:"취소되다 is passive — the subject is what was cancelled, not who cancelled it"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바람에",romanization:"barame",en:"because of (unexpected reason)",pos:"connective",nuance:"바람에 usually carries a negative or inconvenient result, unlike neutral -아서/어서."},sentence:{ko:"갑자기 비가 오는 바람에 옷이 다 젖었어요.",en:"My clothes got completely soaked because it suddenly started raining."},target:"바람에",options:["because of (unexpected reason)","to be cancelled (passive)","to practice"],note:"바람에 carries the nuance that the cause was unexpected or unwelcome, stronger than 때문에"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"연습하다",romanization:"yeonseuphada",en:"to practice",pos:"verb",nuance:"연습하다 is Sino-Korean 練習 — 練 'train/temper (as in tempering metal)' + 習 'repeatedly do/learn' — so 'train by repetition', i.e. drill a skill until it improves. The key contrast is with 공부하다 'to study': 공부하다 is taking in knowledge (grammar, facts, theory), whereas 연습하다 is repeated hands-on doing to build a SKILL — you 공부해 grammar but 연습해 speaking and piano. A closely related word is 훈련하다 (訓練, 'train'), which is more systematic/physical (soldiers, athletes), while 연습하다 is everyday practice. The thing practiced is a direct OBJECT with 을/를: 발음을 연습하다. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: don't say 발음에 연습하다 (no 에), and don't reach for 공부하다 when you mean drilling a skill. As a noun+하다 verb it conjugates on 하다 (연습해요, 연습했어요, formal 연습합니다); negative/impossible split the noun (연습 안 해요, 연습 못 해요)."},sentence:{ko:"연습하면 할수록 실수가 줄어요.",en:"The more you practice, the fewer mistakes you make."},target:"연습하",options:["to practice","to be cancelled (passive)","to increase / grow / improve"],note:"Perfect partner for -(으)ㄹ수록: proportional improvement through practice"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"실력",romanization:"sillyeok",en:"skill level / ability",pos:"noun",nuance:"실력 is not confidence or effort; it is the ability itself."},sentence:{ko:"연습할수록 실력이 늘어요.",en:"The more you practice, the more your skills improve."},target:"실력",options:["late-night snack / midnight food","charm / appeal / attractiveness","skill level / ability"],note:"늘다 (to grow/increase) pairs naturally with 실력"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"늘다",romanization:"neulda",en:"to increase / grow / improve",pos:"verb",nuance:"늘다 means an amount, number, or ability grows bigger, and it is the everyday word for skills improving — 실력이 늘다 is THE phrase for 'getting better at something'. It is a ㄹ-irregular verb: the final ㄹ drops before endings beginning with ㄴ, ㅂ, or ㅅ, which is why the formal present is 늡니다 (not *늘습니다) — the ㄹ vanishes before -ㅂ니다. Watch the synonym split: 늘다 is intransitive (the thing increases by itself — 실력이 늘어요), while its causative twin 늘리다 is transitive (you increase a thing on purpose — 시간을 늘려요 = I extend the time). Compare also 증가하다, the stiff Sino-Korean 'increase' used in reports and statistics (인구가 증가했다); 늘다 is the warm spoken equivalent. The English-speaker pitfall is two-fold: first, forgetting the ㄹ-drop and saying *늘습니다; second, saying 'I improved my Korean' as if you act on it — Koreans say 한국어가 늘었어요 (Korean increased) with the skill as the subject, not as an object you operate on."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 쓸수록 실력이 늘어요.",en:"The more you use Korean, the more your skills grow."},target:"늘어",options:["to increase / grow / improve","to be cancelled (passive)","to practice"],note:"늘다 is intransitive — the subject improves on its own; contrast with 늘리다 (to increase something intentionally)"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"야식",romanization:"yasik",en:"late-night snack / midnight food",pos:"noun",nuance:"야식 is food eaten late at night, usually after the normal evening meal. It can be a small snack, but in Korean life it is often a full delivery meal such as chicken, tteokbokki, ramen, or jokbal, so ‘midnight snack’ can understate it. 간식 is any snack between meals at any time; 야식 is defined by the late-night timing. Natural verbs are 야식을 먹다 and 야식을 시키다, and people commonly ask 오늘 야식 뭐 먹지? when choosing delivery food at night."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 매일 야식을 먹더니 살이 찐 것 같아요.",en:"My friend kept eating late-night snacks, and it seems they gained weight."},target:"야식",options:["skill level / ability","late-night snack / midnight food","charm / appeal / attractiveness"],note:"Classic -더니 scenario: you observed the habit over time → you're now noting the result"}],canDo:"Link what I noticed to what followed (-더니)",id:"chapter-16-b1",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -더니",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-더니",sub:'"and then (I noticed)…"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"동생이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"아까",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"울",mid:"더니",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"지금은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"있어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"동생이 아까 울더니 지금은 자고 있어요.",en:"My little brother was crying a moment ago, and now he's sleeping.",romanization:"dongsaeng-i akka uldeoni jigeum-eun jago isseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"열심히",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부하",mid:"더니",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"시험을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"봤어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"친구가 열심히 공부하더니 시험을 잘 봤어요.",en:"My friend studied hard, and (sure enough) did well on the test.",romanization:"chingu-ga yeolsimhi gongbuhadeoni siheom-eul jal bwasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"-더니",rows:[{when:"After a verb or adjective stem",add:"-더니",ex:"먹다 → 먹더니 / 좋다 → 좋더니"},{when:"With a noun + 이다",add:"-(이)더니",ex:"학생이다 → 학생이더니 / 친구다 → 친구더니"},{when:"Your own past action → present result",add:"-았/었더니 (mostly with 나/저)",ex:"밥을 먹다 → 먹었더니 (배가 불러요)"}]},more:{func:"Links two clauses where you personally saw or noticed situation X, and then Y followed — as a result or as a contrast. The first clause is something you observed (usually about someone or something else), not a plan of your own.",keyPoint:{label:"-더니 vs. -았/었더니",body:"Use plain -더니 when you noticed someone else's situation (친구가 공부하더니… = my friend studied, and then…). Use -았/었더니 when YOU did the action and report the result now (제가 먹었더니… = I ate, so now…). Why the past form attaches the way it does is a later topic — for now, just pair plain -더니 with 'someone else' and -았/었더니 with 'I'."},pronunciation:"좋더니 sounds like [조터니] — the ㅎ blends with the following ㄷ into an aspirated [ㅌ]."}}],canDo:"Link what I noticed to what followed (-더니)",id:"chapter-16-b2",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 살이 찌다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"살이 찌다",romanization:"sari jjida",en:"to gain weight",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"찌다 is a single-syllable ㅣ-stem verb, so ㅣ + 어 contracts to ㅕ: the 요-form is 쪄요 and the past is 쪘어요 — a spot where beginners wrongly write 찌어요/찌었어요. It means cooking with steam (hot vapor rising through 찜기), which keeps food moist and soft, and you'll meet it as the 찐- prefix on snacks (찐만두, 찐빵, 찐 고구마) and in the related dish-name suffix 찜 (갈비찜). Distinguish it from its wet-heat neighbours: 삶다 submerges the food in water, 끓이다 boils a liquid, but 찌다 keeps the food ABOVE the water in vapor. The sharpest pitfall, though, is the homonym: this 찌다 is spelled and conjugated identically to the 찌다 in 살이 찌다 ('to gain weight'), so 쪘어요 could mean 'I steamed it' or 'I gained weight' — only context and an object (만두를 쪘어요 vs 살이 쪘어요) tell them apart."},sentence:{ko:"요즘 스트레스를 받더니 살이 쪘어요.",en:"They've been stressed lately, and they've gained weight."},target:"살이 쪘",options:["to be cancelled (passive)","to gain weight","because of (unexpected reason)"],note:"살이 찌다 is an expression — 살 (flesh/fat) + 이 (subject marker) + 찌다 (to fatten/swell)"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"갑자기",romanization:"gapjagi",en:"suddenly / all of a sudden",pos:"adverb",nuance:"갑자기 marks something abrupt, unplanned, and usually unexpected, often coloured with surprise or disruption — it is the direct semantic opposite of 점점. Compare it with 갑작스럽게 and 별안간: 갑작스럽게 is the adverb form of the adjective 갑작스럽다 and sounds a touch more written/formal, while 별안간 (瞥眼間, literally 'in the blink of an eye') is a vivid literary near-synonym you'll meet in stories more than in speech. In conversation 갑자기 왜 이래요/그래요 can sound slightly accusatory or alarmed depending on tone, so mind the situation. A frequent English-speaker pitfall is the spelling: it is 갑자기, never the tempting 갑짜기, even though the second syllable is pronounced tense. Also resist using it for slow change — 'it's gradually getting cold' is 점점 추워져요, not 갑자기 추워져요."},sentence:{ko:"날씨가 맑더니 갑자기 천둥이 쳤어요.",en:"It was clear, and then suddenly there was thunder."},target:"갑자기",options:["to be cancelled (passive)","because of (unexpected reason)","suddenly / all of a sudden"],note:"갑자기 intensifies the contrast in -더니 sentences — the unexpected shift"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"결국",romanization:"gyeolguk",en:"in the end / ultimately / eventually",pos:"adverb",nuance:"결국 means 'in the end / eventually / after all' and sums up the FINAL outcome reached after a process, delay, or struggle. It is Sino-Korean, 결 (結, 'to conclude/tie up') + 국 (局, 'situation/end stage'), literally 'the concluding stage' — so it inherently looks back over a whole drawn-out affair to its settling point. The dividing line from near-synonym 드디어 is important: 드디어 celebrates a happy, long-awaited result with relief and excitement ('finally, at last!'), whereas 결국 is emotionally NEUTRAL and frequently tinged with resignation or a sense that the outcome was inevitable ('it came to this after all'). A second contrast English speakers stumble on: 결국 is NOT the 'finally/lastly' that orders items in a list — that ordinal sense ('firstly... finally...') is 마지막으로. 결국 is about how a story RESOLVED, not where an item sits in a sequence. It commonly follows a lead-in clause with -지만 or -는데 sketching the struggle, and the variant 결국엔 (with topic 은) adds extra weight to the final beat."},sentence:{ko:"열심히 노력하더니 결국 합격했어요.",en:"They kept working hard, and in the end they passed."},target:"결국",options:["to be cancelled (passive)","because of (unexpected reason)","in the end / ultimately / eventually"],note:"결국 often appears in the result clause of -더니 sentences, marking the endpoint of the observed sequence"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쌓이다",romanization:"ssahida",en:"to pile up / accumulate",pos:"verb",nuance:"쌓이다 is the intransitive/passive counterpart of 쌓다. Things pile up or accumulate by themselves: 일이 쌓이다, 스트레스가 쌓이다, 경험이 쌓이다. If a person actively builds or stacks something, use 쌓다 with 을/를: 경험을 쌓다, 상자를 쌓다. The word works for physical piles and abstract accumulation, including dust, messages, work, fatigue, trust, and experience. Because the process is gradual, it pairs naturally with 점점, 계속, and -(으)ㄹ수록."},sentence:{ko:"경험이 쌓일수록 자신감이 생겨요.",en:"The more experience accumulates, the more confidence you gain."},target:"쌓일",options:["to be cancelled (passive)","to practice","to pile up / accumulate"],note:"쌓이다 is the passive of 쌓다 (to stack/pile); -ㄹ수록 pairs perfectly with gradual accumulation"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"매력",romanization:"maengnyeok",en:"charm / appeal / attractiveness",pos:"noun",nuance:"매력 is the quality that draws people in: charm, appeal, or attractiveness. It can describe appearance, personality, a neighborhood, a language, a product, or even a difficult hobby. It is broader than 외모 or 예쁨 because something can be imperfect but still have 매력. 장점 means an objective advantage or strength; 매력 is the subjective appeal that makes you like or want to know something more. Core patterns are 매력이 있다, 매력을 느끼다, and 매력적이다."},sentence:{ko:"한국 음식은 알수록 매력이 있어요.",en:"Korean food has more charm the more you know about it."},target:"매력",options:["charm / appeal / attractiveness","late-night snack / midnight food","self-confidence"],note:"알다 (ㄹ-stem) drops ㄹ → 알수록. 매력이 있다 = to have charm/appeal"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"폐지되다",romanization:"pyejidoeda",en:"to be abolished / cancelled (officially)",pos:"verb",nuance:"폐지되다 means that an official system, rule, service, institution, or regularly produced program is abolished or discontinued. It is stronger and more institutional than 취소되다. A one-time appointment or event is 취소되다; a policy or recurring program that is ended is 폐지되다. 중단되다 means operation stopped, possibly temporarily, and 종영하다 means a broadcast series reached its end. The abolished item takes 이/가, while the authority actively abolishes it with 을/를 + 폐지하다."},sentence:{ko:"시청률이 낮더니 드라마가 폐지됐어요.",en:"The ratings were low, and in the end the drama was cancelled."},target:"폐지됐",options:["to practice","to increase / grow / improve","to be abolished / cancelled (officially)"],note:"폐지되다 is the passive of 폐지하다 (to abolish/cancel); -더니 here: you observed the low ratings → you're reporting the outcome"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지연",ko:"민준 씨가 몇 달 동안 매일 야근하더니 결국 팀장이 됐어요.",en:"Minjun worked late every night for months, and in the end he became team leader."},hl:"결국"}],canDo:"Give the outside trigger behind my own reaction (-길래)",id:"chapter-16-b3",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -길래",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-길래",sub:'"since I saw…, I…"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"가게에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"과일이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"싸",mid:"길래",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"샀어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"가게에서 과일이 싸길래 좀 많이 샀어요.",en:"The fruit was cheap at the store, so I bought quite a lot.",romanization:"gage-eseo gwail-i ssagillae jom mani sasseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"배고파하",mid:"길래",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"사",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"줬어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"친구가 배고파하길래 밥을 사 줬어요.",en:"My friend looked hungry, so I bought them a meal.",romanization:"chingu-ga baegopahagillae bap-eul sa jwosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"-길래",rows:[{when:"After a verb stem",add:"-길래",ex:"팔다 → 팔길래 / 보이다 → 보이길래"},{when:"After an adjective stem",add:"-길래",ex:"싸다 → 싸길래 / 맛있다 → 맛있길래"},{when:"After a noun + 이다",add:"-(이)길래",ex:"학생이다 → 학생이길래 / 친구다 → 친구(이)길래"}]},more:{func:"Gives the reason behind your own reaction or decision: because you noticed situation X, you did Y. The first clause is an outside trigger you observed; the second is your own spontaneous response.",keyPoint:{label:"-길래 vs. -아서/-니까",body:"-아서 and -니까 give a plain reason for almost any clause, including requests and suggestions. -길래 is narrower: the reason is something you yourself noticed, and the result is your own (usually past) action — you can't say 비싸길래 사지 마세요. When telling someone else to do something, use -니까."},pronunciation:"맛있길래 sounds like [마싣낄래] — the ㄱ of 길 tenses to [ㄲ] after the preceding consonant."}}],canDo:"Give the outside trigger behind my own reaction (-길래)",id:"chapter-16-b4",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 자신감~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"자신감",romanization:"jasingam",en:"self-confidence",pos:"noun",nuance:"자신감 is confidence in *what you can do* — a skill, a task, a performance — and it is the everyday word for it. It is Sino-Korean 自信感 (自 'self' + 信 'to trust/believe' + 感 'feeling'), literally 'the feeling of trusting oneself.' Watch the close pair 자신 vs 자신감: 자신 (自信) is used predicatively as 자신 있어요 ('I'm sure I can do it') about a specific challenge, while 자신감 is the noun naming confidence as a *standing quality* (자신감이 있다/없다/생기다). The other contrast is 자신감 vs 자만심(自慢心): 자만심 is *over*confidence/conceit, the bad kind. The English-speaker trap is two-fold: inventing the verb 자신감하다 (impossible — it's a noun, so pair it with 있다/없다/생기다/넘치다), and over-claiming with 자신감 where a modest Korean would soften to 자신감이 좀 부족해요."},sentence:{ko:"성공 경험이 쌓일수록 자신감이 높아져요.",en:"The more success experiences accumulate, the higher your self-confidence rises."},target:"자신감",options:["self-confidence","late-night snack / midnight food","charm / appeal / attractiveness"],note:"자신 (self/oneself) + 감 (feeling/sense) — a great -(으)ㄹ수록 vocabulary word"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"피곤하다",romanization:"pigonhada",en:"to be tired / exhausted",pos:"adjective",nuance:"피곤하다 is physical/general tiredness — worn out, drained, exhausted — typically after work, study, travel, or poor sleep. It is Sino-Korean 疲困 (疲 weary + 困 exhausted), literally 'weary-exhausted', which is why it reads as bodily fatigue rather than sleepiness. Keep it apart from 졸리다 ('sleepy'): you can be 피곤하다 yet wide awake (drained but not nodding off), and 졸리다 is specifically wanting to sleep — don't swap them. It is also milder and more everyday than 지치다 ('to be worn out/burned out'), which implies you've reached your limit. As a regular 하다-adjective it is 피곤해요 (not 피곤하요). One common English-speaker slip: 피곤하다 normally describes a PERSON being tired; to call an activity 'tiring' you also use 피곤하다 (피곤한 일 = 'tiring work'), but to call a person 'tiresome/draining' Koreans say 피곤한 사람 — meaning 'a person who is exhausting to deal with', a nuance worth knowing so you don't misread it as 'a tired person.'"},sentence:{ko:"친구가 요즘 너무 피곤하더니 결국 쓰러졌어요.",en:"My friend seemed so exhausted lately, and they finally collapsed."},target:"피곤하",options:["to be tired / exhausted","to be difficult / hard","to be cancelled (passive)"],note:"-더니 with an adjective: the speaker observed the state of exhaustion over time → reports the result"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보람",romanization:"boram",en:"sense of reward / worth / fulfillment",pos:"noun",nuance:"보람 is the warm payoff feeling when your effort proves *worthwhile* — the quiet reward of seeing hard work bear fruit. It is a *native* Korean word (no hanja), which is part of why it has no clean one-word English equivalent; 'fulfillment / sense of reward / worthwhileness' all circle it. Koreans use it about jobs, study, volunteering, and parenting: 보람을 느끼다 (to feel fulfilled), 보람이 있다 (it was worth it), and the adjective 보람차다 (보람찬 하루 = a fulfilling day). The synonym contrast is 보람 vs 만족(滿足): 만족 is general *satisfaction* with any outcome (a good meal, a fair price), while 보람 specifically requires that your *effort/contribution* made it meaningful — you earned it. The English-speaker trap is reducing it to a material 'reward' (a prize or money is 상/보상, not 보람), and inventing 보람하다 — use 보람을 느끼다, 보람이 있다, or the adjective 보람차다."},sentence:{ko:"노력할수록 더 보람을 느껴요.",en:"The more effort you put in, the greater the sense of fulfillment you feel."},target:"보람",options:["sense of reward / worth / fulfillment","skill level / ability","late-night snack / midnight food"],note:"보람 is a uniquely Korean concept for the rewarding feeling that comes from effort well spent"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어렵다",romanization:"eoryeopda",en:"to be difficult / hard",pos:"adjective",nuance:"어렵다 is ㅂ irregular: 어려워요, 어려운. It is useful in classroom repair conversations because learners often need to say a sentence or lesson is difficult."},sentence:{ko:"한국어는 처음에는 어렵더니 이제는 쉬워요.",en:"Korean seemed hard at first, and now it's gotten easier."},target:"어렵",options:["to be tired / exhausted","to be difficult / hard","to be cancelled (passive)"],note:"-더니 with an adjective: earlier observation (it WAS hard) contrasted with a current changed state (now it's easy)"},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"익숙해지다",romanization:"iksukaejida",en:"to get used to / become accustomed",pos:"verb",nuance:"익숙해지다 describes the change from unfamiliar to familiar: you gradually get used to a place, routine, language, person’s style, or way of doing something. 익숙하다 is the resulting state (‘be familiar/accustomed’), while 익숙해지다 includes the process of becoming that way. The target normally takes 에: 한국 생활에 익숙해지다, 새 시스템에 익숙해지다. For an activity, use V-는 데 익숙해지다: 혼자 운전하는 데 익숙해졌어요. 친숙해지다 is closer to becoming familiar or friendly with something/someone, while 익숙해지다 emphasizes practical adaptation through repetition."},sentence:{ko:"사용할수록 익숙해져요.",en:"The more you use it, the more accustomed you become."},target:"익숙해져",options:["to get used to / become accustomed","to be cancelled (passive)","to practice"],note:"익숙하다 (familiar/accustomed) + -아/어지다 (become) = getting used to. Perfect -(으)ㄹ수록 partner"}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected cause for a bad result (-는 바람에)",id:"chapter-16-b5",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -는 바람에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-는 바람에",sub:'"because of (something unexpected)…"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"늦잠을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"바람에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"늦었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"늦잠을 자는 바람에 학교에 늦었어요.",en:"I overslept, so (unfortunately) I was late to school.",romanization:"neutjam-eul janeun barame hakgyo-e neujeosseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"버스를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"놓치",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"바람에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"약속에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"늦었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"버스를 놓치는 바람에 약속에 늦었어요.",en:"I missed the bus, so I was late to meet up.",romanization:"beoseu-reul nochineun barame yaksok-e neujeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"-는 바람에",rows:[{when:"After a verb stem",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"놓치다 → 놓치는 바람에 / 자다 → 자는 바람에"},{when:"Verb stem ending in ㄹ (ㄹ drops)",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"울다 → 우는 바람에 / 놀다 → 노는 바람에"},{when:"Adjectives — not used",add:"(use a verb instead)",ex:"비싸다 (X) → 값이 오르는 바람에 (O)"}]},more:{func:"Gives a sudden, usually unwanted cause for a bad or unintended result. It signals that X threw things off and Y happened against your plans.",keyPoint:{label:"-는 바람에 vs. -아서/-어서",body:"-아서/-어서 states a plain cause for any kind of result, good or bad. -는 바람에 is saved for an unexpected cause that leads to a bad or unplanned result, and the second clause is normally in the past tense. You wouldn't say 열심히 공부하는 바람에 시험을 잘 봤어요 — for a good result, use -아서/-어서."},pronunciation:"늦는 sounds like [는는] in fast speech — the ㅈ of 늦 becomes [ㄴ] before the ㄴ of 는."}}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected cause for a bad result (-는 바람에)",id:"chapter-16-b6",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -(으)ㄹ수록",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-(으)ㄹ수록",sub:"the more …, the more …",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ수록",ex:"가다 → 갈수록 · 보다 → 볼수록"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"-을수록",ex:"먹다 → 먹을수록 · 읽다 → 읽을수록"},{when:"Often doubled with the same verb + -(으)면",add:"V-(으)면 V-(으)ㄹ수록",ex:"보면 볼수록 · 알면 알수록"}],examples:[{ko:"한국어는 공부할수록 재미있어요.",en:"The more I study Korean, the more fun it is.",romanization:"han-gu-geo-neun gong-bu-hal-su-rok jae-mi-i-sseo-yo.",note:"공부하다 → 공부할수록 (vowel stem → ㄹ수록)."},{ko:"친구는 만날수록 좋아요.",en:"The more I see my friend, the more I like them.",romanization:"chin-gu-neun man-nal-su-rok jo-a-yo.",note:"만나다 → 만날수록."}],more:{func:'-(으)ㄹ수록 attaches to a verb or adjective stem to mean "the more X happens, the more Y." It links a rising cause to a rising result, and is often paired with 점점 ("more and more").',keyPoint:{label:"Pair it with 점점",body:'-(으)ㄹ수록 often comes with 점점 ("gradually, more and more") to stress the rising effect: 시간이 갈수록 점점 추워져요 ("as time goes on, it gets colder and colder"). The doubled V-(으)면 V-(으)ㄹ수록 form (알면 알수록) is a fixed, natural way to say "the more … the more."'},pronunciation:"읽을수록 is pronounced [일글쑤록]: the ㄱ links onto the following vowel and 수 tenses to [쑤]."}}],canDo:"Express 'the more…, the more…' (-(으)ㄹ수록) and state a purpose (-기 위해서)",id:"chapter-16-b7",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -기 위해서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"-기 위해서",sub:"in order to / for the sake of",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"한국어를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"배우",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"한국에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"왔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 한국에 왔어요.",en:"I came to Korea (in order) to learn Korean.",romanization:"han-gu-geo-reul bae-u-gi wi-hae-seo han-gu-ge wa-sseo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"시험을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"열심히",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"시험을 잘 보기 위해서 열심히 공부해요.",en:"I study hard (in order) to do well on the exam.",romanization:"si-heo-meul jal bo-gi wi-hae-seo yeol-sim-hi gong-bu-hae-yo."}],rule:{name:"-기 위해서",rows:[{when:'Verb stem + 기 위해서 ("in order to V")',add:"-기 위해서",ex:"배우다 → 배우기 위해서 · 사다 → 사기 위해서"},{when:'Noun + 을/를 위해서 ("for N")',add:"을/를 위해서",ex:"건강 → 건강을 위해서 · 친구 → 친구를 위해서"},{when:"서 is optional",add:"위해 = 위해서",ex:"배우기 위해 / 배우기 위해서"}]},more:{func:'-기 위해서 marks a purpose — the goal you do something for. Attach -기 위해서 to a verb stem ("in order to V"), or N을/를 위해서 to a noun ("for the sake of N"). The 서 is optional: 위해 = 위해서.',keyPoint:{label:"-기 위해서 vs -(으)러",body:'Both express purpose, but -(으)러 attaches only to a verb and only pairs with going/coming verbs (가다/오다): 밥 먹으러 가요 (I go to eat). -기 위해서 is broader — any verb, any main clause, and it can also take a noun goal (건강을 위해서). Use -(으)러 for "go/come to do," and -기 위해서 for any purpose.'},pronunciation:""}}],canDo:"Link what I noticed to what followed (-더니)",id:"chapter-16-b8",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:7},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Jiyeon and her coworker Hyunwoo chat over coffee after a colleague's promotion",lines:[{speaker:"지연",ko:"민준 씨가 몇 달 동안 매일 야근하더니 결국 팀장이 됐어요.",en:"Minjun worked late every night for months, and in the end he became team leader.",romanization:"Minjun ssiga myeot dal dongan maeil yageunhadeoni gyeolguk timjangi dwaesseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"맞아요. 저도 봤어요. 열심히 하더니 잘 됐네요.",en:"That's right. I saw it too. He worked hard and things worked out well.",romanization:"Majayo. Jeodo bwasseoyo. Yeolsimhi hadeoni jal dwaenneyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"사실 저도 더 노력해야겠다는 생각이 들었어요.",en:"Honestly, it made me think I should try harder too.",romanization:"Sasil jeodo deo noryeokhaeyagettaneun saenggagi deureosseoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"경험이 쌓일수록 기회도 많아지는 것 같아요.",en:"It seems like the more experience piles up, the more opportunities come.",romanization:"Gyeongheomi ssahilsurok gihoedo manajineun geot gatayo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"맞아요. 일할수록 더 잘 알게 되는 것도 있고요.",en:"True. And the more you work, the more you come to understand.",romanization:"Majayo. Ilhalsurok deo jal alge doeneun geotdo itgoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"그런데 민준 씨가 처음에는 많이 힘들어하더니 이제는 여유가 생겼어요.",en:"By the way, Minjun seemed to struggle a lot at first, and now he has breathing room.",romanization:"Geureonde Minjun ssiga cheoeumeneun manhi himdeureohadeoni ijeneun yeoyuga saenggyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"지연",ko:"저도 적응할수록 여유가 생길 거라고 믿어요.",en:"I believe the more I adapt, the more breathing room I'll get too.",romanization:"Jeodo jeogeunghalsurok yeoyuga saengil georago mideoyo."},{speaker:"현우",ko:"물론이죠. 보면 볼수록 지연 씨도 잘하고 있는 게 보여요.",en:"Of course. The more I watch, the more I can see you're doing well too.",romanization:"Mulronijyo. Bomyeon bolsurok Jiyeon ssido jal hago inneun ge boyeoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'The internet suddenly cut out, so I missed class.'",tokens:["갑자기 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에","수업을 못 들었어요"],correct:"갑자기 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에 수업을 못 들었어요",explanation:"-는 바람에 frames the cause as an unexpected disruption. 못 들었어요 gives the bad result that followed."}],canDo:"Link what I noticed to what followed (-더니)",id:"chapter-16-b9",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:8},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 성장의 기록 (A Record of Growth)",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"성장의 기록 (A Record of Growth)",chunks:["처음 한국어를 시작했을 때는 발음이 너무 어렵더니 이제는 많이 익숙해졌어요. 그때는 ㄹ과 ㄴ 소리가 비슷하더니 지금은 차이가 잘 들려요. 발음을 고치기 위해서 매일 제 목소리를 녹음했고, 비가 오길래 집에서 혼자 따라 읽은 날도 많았어요. 한 번은 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에 온라인 수업을 못 들었지만, 그날도 포기하지 않고 복습했어요. 공부할수록 더 많은 것이 보이기 시작했어요.","처음에는 드라마 대사가 너무 빠르더니 요즘은 대충 내용을 이해해요. 어휘가 늘수록 한국 문화도 더 깊이 느껴지는 것 같아요. 가끔 힘들 때도 있지만, 노력할수록 보람이 커지는 게 느껴져요. 한국어는 정말 보면 볼수록 매력 있는 언어예요."],translation:"When I first started Korean, the pronunciation was so hard, but now I've gotten very accustomed to it. Back then the ㄹ and ㄴ sounds seemed similar, but now I can hear the difference clearly. To fix my pronunciation, I recorded my voice every day, and on many rainy days I noticed the rain and practiced reading aloud alone at home. Once, because the internet cut out unexpectedly, I missed an online class, but I still reviewed that day without giving up. The more I studied, the more things started to become visible. At first drama dialogue was too fast, but lately I roughly understand the content. The more my vocabulary grows, the more deeply I seem to feel Korean culture too. Sometimes it's difficult, but the more I put in effort, the more I can feel the sense of fulfillment growing. Korean really is a language that gets more charming the more you look at it.",qas:[{q:"What purpose action did the writer take to improve pronunciation?",a:"발음을 고치기 위해서 매일 제 목소리를 녹음했어요 — they recorded their voice every day in order to fix pronunciation. This uses -기 위해서 for a planned purpose."},{q:"What two cause/reaction connectors appear in the middle of the diary?",a:"비가 오길래 집에서 혼자 따라 읽은 날도 많았어요 uses -길래: the writer noticed rain and reacted by practicing at home. 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에 온라인 수업을 못 들었어요 uses -는 바람에: the internet outage disrupted the online class."},{q:"Find all instances of -더니 and -(으)ㄹ수록 in the text. For each -더니, explain what was observed and what changed. For each -(으)ㄹ수록, identify the two proportional elements.",a:"-더니 instances: (1) 발음이 너무 어렵더니 — observed: pronunciation was difficult; changed: now accustomed. (2) ㄹ과 ㄴ 소리가 비슷하더니 — observed: the sounds seemed similar; changed: now the difference is clear. (3) 드라마 대사가 너무 빠르더니 — observed: drama dialogue was too fast; changed: now the writer roughly understands the content. -(으)ㄹ수록 instances: (1) 공부할수록 더 많은 것이 보이기 시작했어요 — study ↑ → visibility ↑. (2) 어휘가 늘수록 한국 문화도 더 깊이 느껴지는 것 같아요 — vocabulary ↑ → cultural feeling ↑. (3) 노력할수록 보람이 커지는 게 느껴져요 — effort ↑ → fulfillment ↑. (4) 보면 볼수록 매력 있는 언어 — exposure ↑ → charm ↑."}]}],canDo:"Give the outside trigger behind my own reaction (-길래)",id:"chapter-16-b10",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:9},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"You saw your colleague looking exhausted every day for weeks, and then they took sick leave. Which sentence is most natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"동료가 피곤해 보일수록 병가를 냈어요.",ok:!1},{text:"동료가 매일 피곤해 보이더니 결국 병가를 냈어요.",ok:!0},{text:"동료가 피곤해 보이길래 병가를 냈어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Option B (-더니) is correct: you personally observed your colleague looking tired over multiple days (camera rolling), then report the outcome (sick leave). Option A (-ㄹ수록) would mean 'the more tired they looked, the more sick leave they took' — a proportional scale that doesn't match the scenario. Option C (-길래) would mean the tiredness prompted YOU to take an action yourself, not describing their sick leave."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses -(으)ㄹ수록 with the correct attachment?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"읽다 → 읽수록 좋아요.",ok:!1},{text:"읽다 → 읽을수록 좋아요.",ok:!0},{text:"읽다 → 읽어수록 좋아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"읽다 ends in a consonant cluster (ㄱ final). Consonant-ending stems take -을수록: 읽 + 을수록 = 읽을수록. Option A (읽수록) skips the vowel buffer -을 needed after a consonant — this is wrong. Option C (읽어수록) confuses the polite ending 어요 with the -수록 attachment — 수록 never uses the -아/어 vowel; it attaches via -을/ㄹ."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"발표___ 준비하기 위해서 매일 연습해요. (I practice every day to prepare a presentation.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"준비하다 takes the thing being prepared as an object, so 발표를 fits. 가 would make 발표 the subject, not the preparation target."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'The internet suddenly cut out, so I missed class.'",tokens:["갑자기 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에","수업을 못 들었어요"],correct:"갑자기 인터넷이 끊기는 바람에 수업을 못 들었어요",explanation:"-는 바람에 frames the cause as an unexpected disruption. 못 들었어요 gives the bad result that followed."}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected cause for a bad result (-는 바람에)",id:"chapter-16-b11",chapterId:"chapter-16",index:10}]},{id:"chapter-17",number:17,title:"V았/었어요",goal:"Build polite past-tense sentences with -았/었어요",biteCount:7,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 어제~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어제",romanization:"eoje",en:"yesterday",pos:"noun",nuance:"어제 is a native deictic time word meaning 'yesterday', sitting in the set 그저께(엊그제) 'day before yesterday' – 어제 – 오늘 'today' – 내일 'tomorrow' – 모레 'day after tomorrow'. Its defining grammar quirk is that, like 오늘 and 내일, it normally takes NO 에: you say 어제 만났어요, never 어제에. (The particle reappears only when 어제 combines with a sub-period, e.g. 어제 아침에, where 에 attaches to 아침.) 어제 naturally pairs with past tense — 았어요/었어요 — so it's a reliable signal that the verb should be past. Habitual partners: 어제 아침/저녁, 어제부터, 어제 일 'yesterday's matter', 어제 본/한/간 + noun. The big English-speaker pitfall is over-marking with 에 by analogy to 'on' or to other time nouns like 주말에; bare 어제 is correct. A second trap is pairing 어제 with present or future tense; because it's firmly past, 어제 가요 is wrong — it must be 어제 갔어요."},sentence:{ko:"어제 친구를 만났어요.",en:"I met a friend yesterday."},target:"어제",options:["friend","movie","yesterday"],note:"어제 almost always pulls the sentence into past tense."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지난주",romanization:"jinanju",en:"last week",pos:"noun",nuance:"지난주 means the week that just ended, so it lives in the past and pulls past-tense verbs with it (갔어요, 만났어요). 지난 is the adnominal form of 지나다 'to pass,' literally 'the week that has passed,' and 주 is 週 'week.' It is the past member of the set 지난주 / 이번 주 / 다음 주. The most notorious detail is spacing: 지난주 ('last week') is written as ONE word, but 이번 주 and 다음 주 take a space — and confusingly, 지난 주말 ('last weekend') splits back into two words because 주말 is its own noun. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: keep the time particle (지난주에) and don't use present tense — 'I went last week' must be 지난주에 갔어요, not 지난주에 가요."},sentence:{ko:"지난주에 부산에 갔어요.",en:"I went to Busan last week."},target:"지난주",options:["last week","friend","movie"],note:"지난주에 marks when the completed event happened."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"작년",romanization:"jangnyeon",en:"last year",pos:"noun",nuance:"작년 is Sino-Korean 昨年 ('past/previous' 昨, the same 昨 in 작일 'yesterday' + 'year' 年), so it literally means 'the previous year'. Crucially, unlike 올해, it normally DOES take 에 when used as a time point: 작년에 만났어요 (we met last year). It is the everyday word; the synonym 지난해 ('the year that passed') is a touch more formal and common in news writing, while 작년 dominates conversation. It completes the set 작년 – 올해 – 내년. Watch two English-speaker pitfalls. First, mirroring 올해's no-particle habit and dropping 에 — for past-time 작년, 에 is the natural marker. Second, the look-alike 작년/어제: both involve 'past', but 작년 is a whole year back and 어제 is just yesterday, so 'I arrived last year' is 작년에, never 어제."},sentence:{ko:"작년에 한국에 왔어요.",en:"I came to Korea last year."},target:"작년",options:["friend","movie","last year"],note:"오다 becomes 왔어요 in the past."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"만나다",romanization:"mannada",en:"to meet",pos:"verb",nuance:"만나다 takes a person as the object: 친구를 만나요, 선생님을 만나요."},sentence:{ko:"어제 친구를 만났어요.",en:"I met a friend yesterday."},target:"만났",options:["to eat","to meet","to go"],note:"만나다 → 만났어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹다",romanization:"meokda",en:"to eat",pos:"verb",nuance:"먹다 is used for meals and food. For medicine, Korean often uses 약을 먹어요, literally “eat medicine,” which means “take medicine.”"},sentence:{ko:"식당에서 저녁을 먹었어요.",en:"I ate dinner at a restaurant."},target:"먹었",options:["to meet","to see / watch","to eat"],note:"먹다 → 먹었어요."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"어제 뭐 했어요?",en:"What did you do yesterday?"},hl:"어제"}],canDo:"Say a past action with the polite past tense V았/었어요 (먹었어요 · 갔어요)",id:"chapter-17-b1",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V았/었어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V았/었어요",sub:"the polite past tense",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"어제",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"만났",mid:"어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"어제 친구를 만났어요.",en:"I met a friend yesterday.",romanization:"eoje chingu-reul mannasseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"아침에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"었어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"아침에 밥을 먹었어요.",en:"I ate (a meal) in the morning.",romanization:"achim-e bab-eul meogeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V았/었어요",rows:[{when:"Stem's last vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-았어요",ex:"가다 → 갔어요, 보다 → 봤어요, 살다 → 살았어요"},{when:"Any other vowel (ㅓ, ㅜ, ㅣ, ㅡ, ㅔ …)",add:"-었어요",ex:"먹다 → 먹었어요, 마시다 → 마셨어요, 읽다 → 읽었어요"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하다 → 했어요",ex:"하다 → 했어요, 공부하다 → 공부했어요, 일하다 → 일했어요"}]},more:{func:'Use V았/었어요 to say an action or state happened in the past. It is the polite, everyday way to answer questions like "What did you do yesterday?"',keyPoint:{label:"이다 → 였어요 / 이었어요",body:'The copula 이다 ("to be") has its own past form. After a vowel use 였어요 (친구이다 → 친구였어요), and after a final consonant use 이었어요 (학생이다 → 학생이었어요; 책이다 → 책이었어요). For now just memorize these two endings — the full ins and outs of 이다 come in its home chapter.'},pronunciation:"먹었어요 links across the boundary and sounds like [머거써요]; 했어요 sounds like [해써요] — the 어 is swallowed and the ㅆ is tense."}}],canDo:"Say a past action with the polite past tense V았/었어요 (먹었어요 · 갔어요)",id:"chapter-17-b2",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 보다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보다",romanization:"boda",en:"to see / watch",pos:"verb",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"친구랑 영화를 봤어요.",en:"I watched a movie with a friend."},target:"봤",options:["to eat","to see / watch","to go"],note:"보다 → 봤어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가다",romanization:"gada",en:"to go",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 에 for destination: 학교에 가요, 집에 가요. Do not use 에서 for destination."},sentence:{ko:"지난주에 부산에 갔어요.",en:"I went to Busan last week."},target:"갔",options:["to meet","to go","to eat"],note:"가다 → 갔어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"오다",romanization:"oda",en:"to come",pos:"verb",nuance:"오다 is from the speaker’s point of view. If someone moves toward you or your place, 오다 is usually right."},sentence:{ko:"집에 늦게 왔어요.",en:"I came home late."},target:"왔",options:["to come","to meet","to eat"],note:"오다 → 왔어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하다",romanization:"hada",en:"to do",pos:"verb",nuance:"하다 combines with many nouns: 공부하다, 일하다, 운동하다. It is one of the most important Korean verbs."},sentence:{ko:"어제 숙제를 했어요.",en:"I did homework yesterday."},target:"했",options:["to meet","to do","to eat"],note:"하다 → 했어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"친구",romanization:"chingu",en:"friend",pos:"noun",nuance:"친구 can mean friend or same-age peer depending on context."},sentence:{ko:"친구랑 카페에 갔어요.",en:"I went to a cafe with a friend."},target:"친구",options:["yesterday","last week","friend"],note:"친구랑 is natural in casual polite conversation."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"친구랑 영화를 봤어요. 그다음에 식당에서 밥을 먹었어요.",en:"I watched a movie with a friend. After that, we ate at a restaurant."},hl:"친구"}],canDo:'Answer "what did you do yesterday?" in one clean sentence (어제 …었어요)',id:"chapter-17-b3",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:2},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 영화~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"영화",romanization:"yeonghwa",en:"movie",pos:"noun",nuance:"영화 is Sino-Korean, 映畵 ('project-picture'), literally a projected image, and it is the standard word for a movie/film. It pairs with 보다 ('see/watch'), never 듣다 ('listen'), because film is something you view. A single film is counted with the bound noun 편 (영화 두 편 = two movies), not 개. Don't confuse 영화 with 드라마: 드라마 is a serialized TV series, while 영화 is a self-contained cinema film, so a Korean TV soap is a 드라마, not a 영화. The cinema building is 영화관 (or the loanword 극장). The classic English-speaker pitfall is letting 'a movie' steer you to the generic counter 개 (영화 한 개 sounds wrong) — use 편; and don't calque 'go to a movie' as 영화에 가다, since you say 영화(를) 보러 가다, 'go in order to watch a movie'."},sentence:{ko:"영화가 조금 길었어요.",en:"The movie was a little long."},target:"영화",options:["yesterday","movie","last week"],note:"Adjectives also take past tense: 길다 → 길었어요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"식당",romanization:"sikdang",en:"restaurant / eatery",pos:"noun",nuance:"식당 is 食堂 — 食 'eat' + 堂 'hall' — an 'eating hall', i.e. an ordinary, everyday place to have a meal: a neighborhood eatery, a noodle shop, or a 구내식당(cafeteria). The register contrast is 레스토랑, the loanword reserved for fancier, often Western, sit-down dining; you'd call a humble 백반집 a 식당, never a 레스토랑, and a candlelit Italian place a 레스토랑, not a plain 식당. The crucial grammar point is particle choice: use 에서 for an action done there (식당에서 먹어요 'eat AT the restaurant') but 에 for it as a destination (식당에 가요 'go TO the restaurant'). The English-speaker pitfall is exactly this 에/에서 split, since English 'at/to' don't map one-to-one; another is reaching for 레스토랑 as the default word for any restaurant when 식당 is the everyday term."},sentence:{ko:"식당에서 밥을 먹었어요.",en:"I ate at a restaurant."},target:"식당",options:["restaurant / eatery","yesterday","last week"],note:"Use 에서 for where the action happened."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"집",romanization:"jip",en:"home / house",pos:"noun",nuance:"집 can mean home or house. 집에 often means “home” in movement/location sentences."},sentence:{ko:"일찍 집에 왔어요.",en:"I came home early."},target:"집",options:["yesterday","home / house","last week"],note:"Destination takes 에."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"날씨",romanization:"nalssi",en:"weather",pos:"noun",nuance:"날씨 is the pure-Korean word for the overall feel of the weather right now and is the single most common small-talk opener in Korea. Distinguish it from the Sino-Korean 기온(氣溫, 'air-temperature'), which is the measured number, and from 일기(日氣) / 기상(氣象), which are bookish words used in 일기예보 ('weather forecast'). You almost always pair 날씨 with the descriptive adjectives 좋다, 나쁘다, 춥다, 덥다, 맑다, 흐리다. The English-speaker pitfall is double: first, English says 'the weather IS cold' so learners reach for 이다, but Korean needs the adjective verb directly — 날씨가 추워요, never 날씨가 춥다이에요. Second, when stating a plain fact about the weather, default to the subject particle 가 (날씨가 좋아요), not the topic 는; 날씨는 좋아요 sounds like you are contrasting the weather against something else that is bad."},sentence:{ko:"날씨가 추웠어요.",en:"The weather was cold."},target:"날씨",options:["weather","yesterday","last week"],note:"A common past-tense adjective sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"춥다",romanization:"chupda",en:"to be cold",pos:"adjective",nuance:"춥다 is the ㅂ-irregular adjective for ambient air being unpleasantly cold — winter, a chilly room, a draft. The crucial contrast for English speakers is 춥다 vs 차갑다: 춥다 is how the WEATHER/AIR feels to your whole body, while 차갑다 is how a cold OBJECT feels to your hand (차가운 물 'cold water', 차가운 손 'cold hands'). Never say 추운 물 — water is 차갑다. As a ㅂ-irregular it conjugates 춥다 → 추워요 → 추운 → 추우니까 → 추워서, so writing 춥어요 or 춥은 (the regular pattern) is the classic beginner slip. It is register-neutral and universal. The matching noun is 추위, and 추위를 타다 means 'to be cold-sensitive,' the exact mirror of 더위를 타다."},sentence:{ko:"어제 정말 추웠어요.",en:"It was really cold yesterday."},target:"추웠",options:["to be cold","yesterday","last week"],note:"ㅂ irregular: 춥다 → 추웠어요."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"친구랑 영화를 봤어요. 그다음에 식당에서 밥을 먹었어요.",en:"I watched a movie with a friend. After that, we ate at a restaurant."},hl:"영화"}],canDo:"Conjugate the past tense correctly by vowel harmony (만났어요 · 봤어요 · 했어요)",id:"chapter-17-b4",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:3},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends talk about yesterday after class.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"어제 뭐 했어요?",en:"What did you do yesterday?",romanization:"eoje mwo haesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"친구랑 영화를 봤어요. 그다음에 식당에서 밥을 먹었어요.",en:"I watched a movie with a friend. After that, we ate at a restaurant.",romanization:"chingurang yeonghwareul bwasseoyo. geudaeume sikdangeseo babeul meogeosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"영화는 어땠어요?",en:"How was the movie?",romanization:"yeonghwaneun eottaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"조금 길었지만 재미있었어요.",en:"It was a little long, but it was fun.",romanization:"jogeum gireotjiman jaemiisseosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"집에는 몇 시쯤 왔어요?",en:"About what time did you come home?",romanization:"jibeneun myeot sijjeum wasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"열한 시쯤 왔어요. 날씨가 추워서 바로 잤어요.",en:"I came home around eleven. It was cold, so I went straight to sleep.",romanization:"yeolhan sijjeum wasseoyo. nalssiga chuwoseo baro jasseoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I met a friend yesterday.'",tokens:["어제","친구를 만났어요"],correct:"어제 친구를 만났어요",explanation:"어제 sets the past time, and 만났어요 marks the completed meeting. 친구 is the person met, so 친구를 comes before the verb."}],canDo:"Say a past action with the polite past tense V았/었어요 (먹었어요 · 갔어요)",id:"chapter-17-b5",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:4},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 어제 한 일",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"어제 한 일",chunks:["어제는 수업이 끝난 뒤에 친구를 만났어요. 같이 카페에 갔고, 저는 커피를 마셨어요. 친구는 차를 마셨어요. 그다음 영화를 봤어요.","영화는 조금 길었지만 마지막 장면이 정말 재미있었어요. 집에 올 때는 날씨가 추웠어요. 그래서 집에 오자마자 따뜻한 물을 마시고 바로 잤어요."],translation:"Yesterday, after class ended, I met a friend. We went to a cafe together, and I drank coffee. My friend drank tea. Then we watched a movie. The movie was a little long, but the last scene was really fun. When I came home, the weather was cold. So as soon as I got home, I drank warm water and went straight to sleep.",qas:[{q:"What did the writer do after class?",a:"친구를 만났어요 and 같이 카페에 갔어요."},{q:"Find three contracted past forms in the text.",a:"만났어요 (만나다), 갔고 (가다), 봤어요 (보다)."},{q:"Which adjective uses the ㅂ irregular past form?",a:"추웠어요 from 춥다."}]}],canDo:'Answer "what did you do yesterday?" in one clean sentence (어제 …었어요)',id:"chapter-17-b6",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:5},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly reports a completed action yesterday?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어제 친구랑 영화를 봤어요.",ok:!0},{text:"어제 친구랑 영화를 봐요.",ok:!1},{text:"어제 친구랑 영화를 볼 거예요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"봤어요 is the polite past form, so it fits 어제."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어땠어요 asks about:",sentence:null,options:[{text:"how something was in the past",ok:!0},{text:"where someone is now",ok:!1},{text:"what someone will do tomorrow",ok:!1}],explanation:"어땠어요 is the past form of 어떻다."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"친구___ 영화를 봤어요. (I watched a movie with a friend.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"랑",ok:!0},{text:"를",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"친구랑 means with a friend. 를 would make the friend the thing watched, and 에 would mark a place or direction."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I met a friend yesterday.'",tokens:["어제","친구를 만났어요"],correct:"어제 친구를 만났어요",explanation:"어제 sets the past time, and 만났어요 marks the completed meeting. 친구 is the person met, so 친구를 comes before the verb."}],canDo:"Conjugate the past tense correctly by vowel harmony (만났어요 · 봤어요 · 했어요)",id:"chapter-17-b7",chapterId:"chapter-17",index:6}]},{id:"chapter-18",number:18,title:"V-(으)ㄹ 거예요",goal:"Use -(으)ㄹ 거예요 for future plans and intentions",biteCount:7,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 내일~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"내일",romanization:"naeil",en:"tomorrow",pos:"noun",nuance:"내일 is a time word, so it can appear naturally without 에."},sentence:{ko:"내일 친구를 만날 거예요.",en:"I am going to meet a friend tomorrow."},target:"내일",options:["plan","tomorrow","rain"],note:"내일 naturally pairs with future plans."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"주말",romanization:"jumal",en:"weekend",pos:"noun",nuance:"주말 is Sino-Korean, 週末 — 週 'week' + 末 'end', literally 'week-end', and its opposites are 평일 平日 'weekday' and 주중 週中 'midweek'. It refers to Saturday and Sunday as a block. The contrast worth noting is 주말 vs 휴일: 주말 is specifically the weekend, while 휴일 休日 is any day off including public holidays — so a Wednesday holiday is a 휴일 but not a 주말. Habitual partners: 주말에 쉬다/놀다, 이번/지난/다음 주말, 주말마다, 즐거운 주말, and the farewell 주말 잘 보내세요. The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: learners borrow 'at/on' loosely and write 주말에서, but 에서 marks a LOCATION or a source ('from'); the time-when always takes 에 → 주말에. A second subtlety: for 'this weekend' use 이번 주말 (이번 = the current instance), not 오늘 주말; and 다음 주말 'next weekend' vs 다음 주 주말 can differ, so keep 다음 주말 for the upcoming Sat–Sun."},sentence:{ko:"이번 주말에 카페에 갈 거예요.",en:"I am going to go to a cafe this weekend."},target:"주말",options:["weekend","plan","rain"],note:"이번 주말에 sets the future time frame."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계획",romanization:"gyehoek",en:"plan",pos:"noun",nuance:"계획(計劃, 'count/reckon + plan') is a worked-out plan or scheme for the future. Its single most important quirk is the verb: Koreans BUILD a plan with 세우다 (literally 'to erect/stand up') — 계획을 세우다 — not 만들다 ('make') as the English calque suggests. Other partners: 계획이 있다 (have a plan) and the smooth adverb 계획대로 ('as planned'). Distinguish 계획 from 예정 豫定, which is a SCHEDULED arrangement that is already set ('내일 출발할 예정이에요' = I'm scheduled/due to leave tomorrow), and from 약속, a personal commitment/appointment with someone — 계획 is your own roadmap, 예정 is a fixed scheduling, 약속 involves another party. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly 계획을 만들다 (use 세우다), and translating 'plan to do' with 계획 as a verb — Korean uses -(으)려고 하다 or -(으)ㄹ 계획이다: 여행할 계획이에요 ('I plan to travel'), not 여행을 계획해요 in casual speech."},sentence:{ko:"주말 계획이 있어요?",en:"Do you have weekend plans?"},target:"계획",options:["plan","tomorrow","weekend"],note:"A natural lead-in before using -(으)ㄹ 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"만나다",romanization:"mannada",en:"to meet",pos:"verb",nuance:"만나다 takes a person as the object: 친구를 만나요, 선생님을 만나요."},sentence:{ko:"친구를 만날 거예요.",en:"I am going to meet a friend."},target:"만날",options:["to go","to meet","to eat"],note:"만나다 → 만날 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보다",romanization:"boda",en:"to see / watch",pos:"verb",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"주말에 영화를 볼 거예요.",en:"I am going to watch a movie this weekend."},target:"볼",options:["to go","to see / watch","to eat"],note:"보다 → 볼 거예요."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"주말에 뭐 할 거예요?",en:"What are you going to do this weekend?"},hl:"주말"}],canDo:"Say a future plan or intention with V-(으)ㄹ 거예요 (갈 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b1",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ 거예요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-(으)ㄹ 거예요",sub:"saying what you'll do (plans, intentions, predictions)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"내일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"만날",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"거예요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"내일 친구를 만날 거예요.",en:"I'm going to meet a friend tomorrow.",romanization:"naeil chingureul mannal geoyeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"저녁에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밥",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"거예요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"저녁에 집에서 밥 먹을 거예요.",en:"I'm going to eat at home this evening.",romanization:"jeonyeoge jibeseo bap meogeul geoyeyo."}],rule:{name:"V-(으)ㄹ 거예요",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"stem + ㄹ 거예요",ex:"가다 → 갈 거예요, 보다 → 볼 거예요, 하다 → 할 거예요"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"stem + 을 거예요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을 거예요, 읽다 → 읽을 거예요"},{when:"Stem already ends in ㄹ",add:"keep ㄹ, add 거예요 (don't add 을)",ex:"살다 → 살 거예요, 만들다 → 만들 거예요"}]},more:{func:`Use V-(으)ㄹ 거예요 to say what you plan or mean to do, or to guess what will happen. It's the everyday polite way to say "will" or "be going to" — perfect for weekend plans, what you'll eat, or what the weather will do.`,keyPoint:{label:"거예요 (your plan) vs. 게요 (a promise to me)",body:"Both look to the future. -(으)ㄹ 거예요 just states your plan or a guess: 제가 할 거예요 (I plan to do it). -(으)ㄹ게요 is something you promise the listener right now: 제가 할게요 (I'll do it / I promise). Quick tell: weather and other people can take 거예요 (비가 올 거예요), but never 게요 — rain can't promise you anything. You'll practice 게요 more later; for now, default to 거예요 for plans and guesses."},pronunciation:"거예요 is said like [거에요]. The ㄹ before it makes the 거 tense, so 갈 거예요 sounds like [갈 꺼예요] and 먹을 거예요 like [머글 꺼예요]."}}],canDo:"Say a future plan or intention with V-(으)ㄹ 거예요 (갈 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b2",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 가다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가다",romanization:"gada",en:"to go",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 에 for destination: 학교에 가요, 집에 가요. Do not use 에서 for destination."},sentence:{ko:"카페에 갈 거예요.",en:"I am going to go to a cafe."},target:"갈",options:["to meet","to go","to see / watch"],note:"가다 → 갈 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹다",romanization:"meokda",en:"to eat",pos:"verb",nuance:"먹다 is used for meals and food. For medicine, Korean often uses 약을 먹어요, literally “eat medicine,” which means “take medicine.”"},sentence:{ko:"저녁에 김밥을 먹을 거예요.",en:"I am going to eat gimbap for dinner."},target:"먹을",options:["to meet","to see / watch","to eat"],note:"먹다 → 먹을 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하다",romanization:"hada",en:"to do",pos:"verb",nuance:"하다 combines with many nouns: 공부하다, 일하다, 운동하다. It is one of the most important Korean verbs."},sentence:{ko:"집에서 숙제를 할 거예요.",en:"I am going to do homework at home."},target:"할",options:["to meet","to do","to see / watch"],note:"하다 → 할 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비",romanization:"bi",en:"rain",pos:"noun",nuance:"Korean has no single verb meaning 'to rain'; instead precipitation 'arrives', so you say 비가 와요 (rain comes) using 오다. A more formal, written variant is 비가 내리다 ('rain falls'), favored in forecasts and lyrics, while 비가 오다 dominates everyday speech. To say rain stops, use the dedicated verb 그치다 (비가 그쳤어요), not 끝나다. Note 비 also forms many compounds: 소나기 (sudden shower), 장마 (the summer monsoon rains), 가랑비 (drizzle). The big English-speaker pitfall is inventing a verb: learners say 비가 해요 or 비해요 by analogy with English 'it rains', but 하다 never combines with 비 — the natural pairing is strictly 비가 오다 / 비가 내리다. A second trap is 비를 맞다 ('to take the rain on your body', i.e. get drenched), which uses 맞다, not 받다."},sentence:{ko:"내일 비가 올 거예요.",en:"It will probably rain tomorrow."},target:"비",options:["tomorrow","weekend","rain"],note:"Weather uses -(으)ㄹ 거예요 as prediction."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"여행",romanization:"yeohaeng",en:"travel / trip",pos:"noun",nuance:"여행 is Sino-Korean, 旅行 ('journey-go'), meaning travel or a trip. The single most natural verb is 가다 — 여행을 가다 ('go on a trip') — while 여행하다 (with light verb 하다) also works but feels a touch more formal/written. Distinguish 여행 from 출장: 출장 is a business trip you're sent on for work, whereas 여행 is travel for pleasure or sightseeing, so don't call a work assignment a 여행. A traveler is 여행자/여행객 and an overseas trip is 해외여행. The English-speaker pitfall is choosing 보다 ('see') because tourists 'go sightseeing' — to travel you 가다 or 하다, not 보다 (sightseeing specifically is 구경하다 or 관광하다). Also note destination takes (으)로/에 (제주도로 여행을 가다), and beginners often drop the particle on 여행 itself — keep 여행을 가다."},sentence:{ko:"방학에 여행을 갈 거예요.",en:"I am going to travel during vacation."},target:"여행",options:["travel / trip","tomorrow","weekend"],note:"여행을 가다 is the common phrase."}],canDo:"Choose -ㄹ 거예요 vs -을 거예요 from the 받침 (볼 거예요 · 먹을 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b3",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:2},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 숙제~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"숙제",romanization:"sukje",en:"homework",pos:"noun",nuance:"숙제 is Sino-Korean 宿題 — 宿 'lodging/stay overnight' + 題 'topic/problem', literally a 'problem to take home overnight', i.e. work a teacher assigns to be done outside class. The verb is locked: Koreans say 숙제를 하다 'do homework', and 숙제를 내다 means a student turns it in (while a teacher 내다 a 숙제 means 'assign/set' it — context decides). Contrast 숙제 with 공부(工夫, 'study') and 과제(課題, 'assignment/coursework'): 공부하다 is the broad act of studying anything, whereas 숙제 is a specific assigned task, and 과제 is the more formal university word for a project or paper. The big English-speaker pitfall is translating 'do my homework' as 숙제를 만들다 'make homework' (✗ — that sounds like you created the assignment) or 숙제를 공부하다 (✗); the only natural pairing is 숙제를 하다."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 밤에 숙제를 할 거예요.",en:"I am going to do homework tonight."},target:"숙제",options:["tomorrow","homework","weekend"],note:"A practical student plan sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"약속",romanization:"yaksok",en:"appointment / plan / promise",pos:"noun",nuance:"약속(約束, literally 'bind + tie' — a tying-together of words) carries two English senses Koreans treat as one: a PROMISE (a verbal commitment) and an APPOINTMENT/plans to meet someone. So 약속이 있어요 almost always means 'I have plans (to meet a person),' which surprises English speakers expecting 'I have a promise.' The signature collocations come as an opposed pair: 약속을 지키다 (keep a promise, lit. 'protect') vs 약속을 어기다 (break it, lit. 'violate'). Contrast 약속 with 예약 豫約 (a RESERVATION/booking for a restaurant, hotel, or hospital — 식당 예약), which is institutional, and with 계획 (your own plan, no other party). The English-speaker pitfalls: the 하다 particle slip 약속이 해요 (use 약속을 해요); using 약속 for a restaurant booking (that is 예약); and saying 약속을 깨다 — while understood, the idiomatic 'break a promise' is 약속을 어기다, whereas 깨다 ('shatter') fits cancelling an appointment (약속을 깨다 = bail on plans)."},sentence:{ko:"내일 약속이 있어서 일찍 나갈 거예요.",en:"I have plans tomorrow, so I am going to leave early."},target:"약속",options:["appointment / plan / promise","tomorrow","weekend"],note:"약속 often means a social appointment."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"영화",romanization:"yeonghwa",en:"movie",pos:"noun",nuance:"영화 is Sino-Korean, 映畵 ('project-picture'), literally a projected image, and it is the standard word for a movie/film. It pairs with 보다 ('see/watch'), never 듣다 ('listen'), because film is something you view. A single film is counted with the bound noun 편 (영화 두 편 = two movies), not 개. Don't confuse 영화 with 드라마: 드라마 is a serialized TV series, while 영화 is a self-contained cinema film, so a Korean TV soap is a 드라마, not a 영화. The cinema building is 영화관 (or the loanword 극장). The classic English-speaker pitfall is letting 'a movie' steer you to the generic counter 개 (영화 한 개 sounds wrong) — use 편; and don't calque 'go to a movie' as 영화에 가다, since you say 영화(를) 보러 가다, 'go in order to watch a movie'."},sentence:{ko:"친구랑 영화를 볼 거예요.",en:"I am going to watch a movie with a friend."},target:"영화",options:["tomorrow","movie","weekend"],note:"보다 → 볼 거예요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"카페",romanization:"kape",en:"cafe",pos:"noun",nuance:"카페 is a loanword (from French/English 'café') meaning a coffee shop — but in Korea it is as much a social and study space as a place to drink coffee; people sit for hours to chat, work on laptops, or go on a 카페 투어 of photogenic spots. The key contrast learners blur is 카페 vs 커피: 카페 is the *place*, 커피 is the *drink*, so '커피 마시러 카페에 가요' (go to the cafe to drink coffee). It overlaps with 커피숍 and the older 다방 (a dated tea-room), but 카페 is the modern default. Being borrowed, it has no hanja. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly that 카페/커피 mix-up, plus the particles — 카페에 가요 for going there but 카페에서 공부해요 for doing something there — and remember the purpose marker for 'to go drink coffee' is the verb stem + ~(으)러: 커피 마시러 카페에 가요."},sentence:{ko:"새 카페에 갈 거예요.",en:"I am going to go to a new cafe."},target:"카페",options:["cafe","tomorrow","weekend"],note:"카페에 marks destination."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"친구",romanization:"chingu",en:"friend",pos:"noun",nuance:"친구 can mean friend or same-age peer depending on context."},sentence:{ko:"친구랑 만날 거예요.",en:"I am going to meet up with a friend."},target:"친구",options:["tomorrow","weekend","friend"],note:"친구랑 makes the plan conversational."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"토요일에는 친구를 만나서 새 카페에 갈 거예요.",en:"On Saturday I am going to meet a friend and go to a new cafe."},hl:"카페"}],canDo:"Make a guess about what will happen (비가 올 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b4",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:3},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends make weekend plans and adjust them if the weather changes.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"주말에 뭐 할 거예요?",en:"What are you going to do this weekend?",romanization:"jumare mwo hal geoyeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"토요일에는 친구를 만나서 새 카페에 갈 거예요.",en:"On Saturday I am going to meet a friend and go to a new cafe.",romanization:"toyoireneun chingureul mannaseo sae kape-e gal geoyeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"일요일에는요?",en:"What about Sunday?",romanization:"iryoireneunyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"비가 오면 집에서 시험공부를 할 거예요.",en:"If it rains, I am going to study for the exam at home.",romanization:"biga omyeon jibeseo siheomgongbureul hal geoyeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"저는 영화 볼 거예요. 같이 볼래요?",en:"I am going to watch a movie. Do you want to watch together?",romanization:"jeoneun yeonghwa bol geoyeyo. gachi bollaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"좋아요. 숙제 끝나면 연락할게요.",en:"Sounds good. When I finish my homework, I'll contact you.",romanization:"joayo. sukje kkeunnamyeon yeonrakhalgeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I'm going to travel tomorrow.'",tokens:["내일","여행을 갈 거예요"],correct:"내일 여행을 갈 거예요",explanation:"내일 sets the future time. 여행을 가다 is the natural chunk for going on a trip, and 갈 거예요 marks the plan."}],canDo:"Say a future plan or intention with V-(으)ㄹ 거예요 (갈 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b5",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:4},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 이번 주말 계획",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"이번 주말 계획",chunks:["이번 주말에는 조금 바쁠 거예요. 토요일 오전에는 숙제를 할 거예요. 오후에는 친구를 만나서 새 카페에 갈 거예요. 카페에서 커피를 마시고 다음 주 여행 계획을 세울 거예요.","일기예보를 보니까 일요일에는 비가 올 거예요. 그래서 집에서 영화를 보고 한국어 단어를 복습할 거예요. 시간이 있으면 저녁에 산책도 할 거예요."],translation:"This weekend will be a little busy. On Saturday morning, I am going to do homework. In the afternoon, I am going to meet a friend and go to a new cafe. At the cafe, we will drink coffee and make plans for next week's trip. According to the weather forecast, it will probably rain on Sunday. So I am going to watch a movie at home and review Korean vocabulary. If I have time, I will also take a walk in the evening.",qas:[{q:"What will the writer do on Saturday morning?",a:"숙제를 할 거예요."},{q:"Find two future plan forms and one prediction form.",a:"Plans: 숙제를 할 거예요, 카페에 갈 거예요, 복습할 거예요. Prediction: 비가 올 거예요."},{q:"Why is 갈 거예요 used but 먹을 거예요 would use 을?",a:"가다 has a vowel-ending stem, so it takes ㄹ: 갈 거예요. 먹다 has a consonant-ending stem, so it takes 을: 먹을 거예요."}]}],canDo:"Choose -ㄹ 거예요 vs -을 거예요 from the 받침 (볼 거예요 · 먹을 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b6",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:5},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"A friend asks about your weekend plan. Which answer fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"토요일에 카페에 갈 거예요.",ok:!0},{text:"토요일에 카페에 갔어요.",ok:!1},{text:"토요일에 카페에 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-ㄹ 거예요 presents a future plan or intention."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence is a weather prediction, not a promise?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"내일 비가 올 거예요.",ok:!0},{text:"내일 비가 올게요.",ok:!1},{text:"내일 비가 왔어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Rain cannot make a promise, so use -ㄹ 거예요 for prediction."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"주말___ 친구를 만날 거예요. (I will meet a friend on the weekend.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"에",ok:!0},{text:"에서",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"주말에 marks when the plan happens. 에서 would mark an action place, not the time of the plan."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I'm going to travel tomorrow.'",tokens:["내일","여행을 갈 거예요"],correct:"내일 여행을 갈 거예요",explanation:"내일 sets the future time. 여행을 가다 is the natural chunk for going on a trip, and 갈 거예요 marks the plan."}],canDo:"Make a guess about what will happen (비가 올 거예요)",id:"chapter-18-b7",chapterId:"chapter-18",index:6}]},{id:"chapter-19",number:19,title:"Question words (누구, 뭐, 어디, 언제, 왜, 어떻게, 몇, 얼마)",goal:"Choose the question word that matches the missing information.",biteCount:7,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 뭐~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"뭐",romanization:"mwo",en:"what",pos:"question-word",nuance:"뭐 is the spoken form of 무엇. It is very common in everyday Korean."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 뭐 해요?",en:"What are you doing today?"},target:"뭐",options:["where","who","what"],note:"Use 뭐 for things, actions, food, plans, or objects."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어디",romanization:"eodi",en:"where",pos:"question-word",nuance:"어디 combines with 에 and 에서 depending on destination vs action place."},sentence:{ko:"주말에 어디에 가요?",en:"Where are you going on the weekend?"},target:"어디",options:["what","where","when"],note:"Pair 어디 with 에 for destination/location and 에서 for action place."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"언제",romanization:"eonje",en:"when",pos:"question-word",nuance:"언제 asks about time. It usually does not need 에."},sentence:{ko:"수업은 언제 시작해요?",en:"When does class start?"},target:"언제",options:["when","where","who"],note:"언제 asks for time without needing a counter."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"왜",romanization:"wae",en:"why",pos:"adverb",nuance:"왜 is the everyday 'why', asking the reason or cause behind something, and it lands directly in front of the verb or adjective (왜 늦었어요?), or stands alone as 왜요? The closest cousin is 어째서, but where 어째서 sounds slightly puzzled, bookish, or 'how on earth come', 왜 is the neutral, all-purpose choice you'll hear constantly in spoken Korean. Tone matters enormously here: a clipped, flat 왜? to a stranger or elder reads as irritated or even confrontational ('what?!'), so attaching 요 (왜요?) or softening to 왜 그러세요? keeps you polite. It collocates naturally with 왜냐하면 (because) as the answer, and with 안/못 in negative why-questions (왜 안 해요?). The English-speaker pitfall is word order: English lets 'why' float to the end ('You're late, why?'), but Korean never parks 왜 sentence-finally, and you must not translate 'Why don't you...?' as a literal 왜 안 — that Korean form is a genuine demand for a reason, not a soft suggestion."},sentence:{ko:"왜 한국어를 공부해요?",en:"Why do you study Korean?"},target:"왜",options:["what","why","where"],note:"왜 expects a reason, not a place or time answer."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어떻게",romanization:"eotteoke",en:"how; in what way",pos:"question-word",nuance:"어떻게 asks method or manner. 어떻게 해요? is a very useful “what should I do?” phrase."},sentence:{ko:"한국어 단어를 어떻게 외워요?",en:"How do you memorize Korean words?"},target:"어떻게",options:["who","how; in what way","why"],note:"어떻게 asks method. For opinion, use 어때요?"},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"어디에서 한국어를 배워요?",en:"Where do you learn Korean?"},hl:"어디"}],canDo:"Ask who, what, where with question words in the answer's slot (누구 · 뭐 · 어디)",id:"chapter-19-b1",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · Question words (누구, 뭐, 어디, 언제, 왜, 어떻게, 몇, 얼마)",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"Question words (누구, 뭐, 어디, 언제, 왜, 어떻게, 몇, 얼마)",sub:"",rows:[{when:"who — 누구",add:"누구 + 가 → 누가 (subject); 누구 + 를 (object); 누구 + 예요",ex:"누가 와요? / 누구를 만나요? / 이 사람 누구예요?"},{when:"what — 뭐 (everyday)",add:"뭐 + 예요; 뭐 + 를 → 뭘 (object). 무엇 is the written form, rare in speech",ex:"이거 뭐예요? / 뭘 먹어요? / 뭐 해요?"},{when:"where — 어디",add:"어디 + 예요; 어디 + 에 (where you go / where something is); 어디 + 에서 (where you do something)",ex:"집이 어디예요? / 어디에 가요? / 어디에서 일해요?"},{when:"when / why / how — 언제, 왜, 어떻게",add:"no particle — they work like adverbs on their own",ex:"언제 와요? / 왜 안 와요? / 어떻게 가요?"},{when:"how many / how much — 몇 + counter, 얼마",add:"몇 + 개/명/시/살 …; 얼마 + 예요 (price)",ex:"몇 개예요? / 몇 시예요? / 이거 얼마예요?"}],examples:[{ko:"이 사람 누구예요?",en:"Who is this person?",romanization:"i saram nuguyeyo?",note:"누구 sits right where the answer goes: 제 친구예요 (It's my friend). Same slot, just swap the word."},{ko:"점심에 뭘 먹어요?",en:"What do you eat for lunch?",romanization:"jeomsime mwol meogeoyo?",note:"뭘 (뭐+를) stays in the object slot before the verb. Answer 밥을 먹어요 fills the very same slot."}],more:{func:"Question words ask for one missing piece of info — who, what, where, when, why, how, how many, how much. The big thing: in Korean the question word sits in the exact spot the answer would go. It does not jump to the front like English 'where' or 'what' does.",keyPoint:{label:"The question word stays where the answer goes",body:"English moves the question word up front: 'WHAT do you eat?' Korean leaves it in place, right before the verb: 뭐 먹어요? → 밥 먹어요. Look at the slot: 밥 먹어요 / 뭐 먹어요 — same spot, you just swap 밥 for 뭐. So think of the answer first, then drop the question word into its slot. (어디 still needs 에 or 에서 — that comes next.)"},pronunciation:"누구 + 가 squeezes down to 누가 (the 구 disappears, it doesn't just link). 뭐 + 를 squeezes to 뭘. 무엇을 links over the patchim and sounds like [무어슬]."}}],canDo:"Ask who, what, where with question words in the answer's slot (누구 · 뭐 · 어디)",id:"chapter-19-b2",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 누구~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"누구",romanization:"nugu",en:"who",pos:"question-word",nuance:"누구 changes naturally with particles: 누구를, 누구한테, 누구하고."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 누구를 만나요?",en:"Whom are you meeting today?"},target:"누구",options:["what","where","who"],note:"Attach the role: 누구가 becomes 누가, but 누구를 and 누구랑 stay visible."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"얼마나",romanization:"eolmana",en:"how much; how long; to what degree",pos:"adverb",nuance:"얼마나 asks 'to what degree / how much / how long', measuring intensity, distance, frequency, duration, or size, and it sits before the verb or adjective it scales (얼마나 예뻐요? = how pretty is it?). The trap is its lookalike 얼마: 얼마 asks a price or a bare quantity (얼마예요? = how much is it [in money]?), whereas 얼마나 adds the adverbial -나 to ask about a *degree along a scale*. Mixing them is the number-one English-speaker error, because English collapses both into 'how much'. It collocates tightly with measurement verbs and adjectives: 얼마나 걸리다 (take how long), 얼마나 멀다/크다/비싸다 (how far/big/expensive), and with other adverbs to form 얼마나 자주 (how often) and 얼마나 많이 (how much in amount). A second subtle point: when answering, the matching degree word is 이렇게/그렇게 or 너무, so 얼마나 비싸요? naturally invites 너무 비싸요. Don't append a counter to 얼마나 itself; the noun being measured comes before it (시간이 얼마나 ...)."},sentence:{ko:"회사까지 얼마나 걸려요?",en:"How long does it take to get to work?"},target:"얼마나",options:["where","how much; how long; to what degree","when"],note:"얼마나 asks about degree, distance, time length, or frequency."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"몇",romanization:"myeot",en:"how many; what number",pos:"determiner",nuance:"몇 means 'how many' and, grammatically, behaves like a number: it drops straight into the slot before a counter (몇 개, 몇 명, 몇 시, 몇 잔), and since it pairs with the native-number counters, it inherits their world (몇 시 'what time', 몇 살 'how old'). A defining subtlety is that the SAME 몇 also means 'a few / several' when it is not a question — '몇 개 샀어요' can be 'I bought a few' depending on intonation and context. Two boundaries trip English speakers: (1) 몇 almost always needs a counter, so 'how many?' about countable things is 몇 개/몇 명, not bare 몇; (2) don't confuse 몇 시 (what TIME, native + 시) with 며칠 (what DATE/how many days, which behaves on the Sino-Korean date system) — different question, different number family. Compare also 얼마나 'how much/to what extent', which asks degree rather than a countable number."},sentence:{ko:"스터디에 몇 명이 와요?",en:"How many people are coming to the study group?"},target:"몇",options:["which","how many; what number","where"],note:"몇 needs a number word or counter such as 시, 명, 개, 번."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"무슨",romanization:"museun",en:"what; what kind of",pos:"determiner",nuance:"무슨 sits before a noun to ask 'what / what kind of', typically when you have no information yet and just want the thing identified — 무슨 영화 봤어요? simply asks for the title or genre. The sharp contrast is with 어떤: 무슨 + noun seeks the *identity or category* (무슨 색이에요? → 'it's blue'), whereas 어떤 + noun seeks *qualities or a choice among options* (어떤 색이 좋아요? → 'the brighter one'). 무슨 has hardened into many fixed, high-frequency idioms you should memorize whole: 무슨 일이에요? (what's wrong?), 무슨 뜻이에요? (what does it mean?), 무슨 요일이에요? (what day?), and the exasperated 무슨 소리예요? / 무슨 말이에요? (what are you on about?). The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: first, 무슨 must always be glued to a following noun (never bare like English 'What?'); second, learners reach for 어떤 in these set phrases — 어떤 일이에요? is grammatical but means 'what sort of task', not the idiomatic 'what's wrong?'."},sentence:{ko:"무슨 음악을 좋아해요?",en:"What kind of music do you like?"},target:"무슨",options:["where","when","what; what kind of"],note:"Use 무슨 before a noun when asking the kind, name, or category."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어느",romanization:"eoneu",en:"which",pos:"determiner",nuance:"어느 means 'which' and is a determiner: it ALWAYS sits in front of a noun and selects one option from a limited, often visible set (어느 것, 어느 나라, 어느 쪽). The synonym you must not blur is 무슨: 어느 chooses from a known, bounded group ('which of these?'), while 무슨 asks 'what kind of' from an open field (무슨 음악 좋아해요? 'what kind of music?'). So 어느 영화 leans toward 'which (of the movies we're choosing among)?' and 무슨 영화 toward 'what (kind of) movie?' The English-speaker pitfall is using 어느 like the pronoun 'which one' on its own — but it cannot stand alone; you must attach a noun, and for 'which one' specifically you say 어느 것/어느 거. Learn 어느 정도 as a unit; it idiomatically means 'to what extent / roughly how much.'"},sentence:{ko:"어느 카페에서 만날까요?",en:"Which cafe shall we meet at?"},target:"어느",options:["how many; what number","which","what"],note:"Use 어느 when choosing among options the listener can imagine."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"누구랑 같이 연습해요?",en:"Who do you practice with?"},hl:"누구"}],canDo:"Ask when and why without changing word order (언제 · 왜)",id:"chapter-19-b3",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:2},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 질문~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"질문",romanization:"jilmun",en:"question",pos:"noun",nuance:"질문 is Sino-Korean 質問 — 質 'substance/inquire into' + 問 'ask', literally 'to inquire and ask', i.e. a question put to someone. The set verb is 질문을 하다 (or the single verb 질문하다); the person you ask takes 에게/한테 (or honorific 께 for a teacher): 선생님께 질문을 하다. Its natural partner is 대답/답(答) — a 질문 is answered by a 대답. The big redundancy trap for English speakers is 질문을 물어보다 ✗: 물어보다 already means 'to ask', so this literally says 'ask a question-asking' — say either 질문을 하다 or just 물어보다, never both. Distinguish 질문 (a question seeking information/an answer) from 문제(問題, a 'problem' or exam item) — '3 questions on the test' are 문제 세 개, but 'I have a question for you' is 질문이 있어요. Note answering takes 에: 질문에 답하다, not 질문을 답하다."},sentence:{ko:"질문이 있으면 지금 물어보세요.",en:"If you have a question, please ask now."},target:"질문",options:["method; way","question","what"],note:"질문 pairs naturally with 하다, 있다, and 물어보다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"대답",romanization:"daedap",en:"answer; reply",pos:"noun",nuance:"대답 is Sino-Korean 對答 — 對 'face/respond-to' + 答 'reply', literally 'a facing reply', i.e. the spoken/written response to a question or call. It is the natural partner of 질문(質問): a 질문 is met by a 대답, and the verb is 대답을 하다 / 대답하다. The sharp contrast to master is 대답 vs 정답(正答, 正 'correct' + 答): 대답 is simply 'a/the response someone gives' (it can be right or wrong), while 정답 is specifically 'the correct answer' to a problem — so on a quiz '정답은 3번이에요' = 'the correct answer is number 3', but 그 학생의 대답 = 'that student's response'. Also note 답 alone is a shorter everyday form of both. The particle pitfall mirrors 질문: you answer a question with 에 — 질문에 대답하다 / 질문에 답하다, NOT 질문을 대답하다. English 'answer the phone/door' does not use 대답 either — that is 전화를 받다 / 문을 열다."},sentence:{ko:"왜 대답이 없어요?",en:"Why is there no answer?"},target:"대답",options:["answer; reply","method; way","what"],note:"대답 is a spoken or written reply to a question."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이유",romanization:"iyu",en:"reason",pos:"noun",nuance:"이유 is Sino-Korean (理由, 理 'principle/logic' + 由 'cause/origin'), the 'reason' or grounds behind something — the answer to 'why?'. Its sharpest contrast is with 원인 (原因, 'cause'): 이유 is the justification or motive a person gives ('the reason I'm late'), often subjective and explainable, while 원인 is the objective, often impersonal cause of a result ('the cause of the fire', 화재의 원인). So you ask a friend 무슨 이유로 늦었어? but an investigator looks for the 원인 of an accident. Also distinguish 까닭, a more native, slightly literary synonym of 이유. Register-neutral and extremely common. Core collocations: 이유가 있다/없다, 이유를 묻다/대다/설명하다, 이유 없이 (for no reason), 그런 이유로 (for that reason), ~는 이유는 (the reason that ~ is). The English-speaker pitfall: don't bolt 이유 onto a 'because' clause redundantly — Korean already has 왜냐하면 ... 때문이에요 / ~기 때문에 to say 'because', so '왜냐하면 그 이유는...' is clunky; pick one. Also, 'the reason is because...' should be ~는 이유는 ... 때문이에요, where the sentence ends in 때문이다, not a second 이유."},sentence:{ko:"그 이유가 뭐예요?",en:"What is the reason?"},target:"이유",options:["method; way","what","reason"],note:"이유 helps expand 왜 questions into clearer follow-ups."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"방법",romanization:"bangbeop",en:"method; way",pos:"noun",nuance:"방법 is Sino-Korean (方法, 方 'direction/manner' + 法 'rule/method'), the 'way', 'method', or 'means' of doing something — the procedure or approach to reach a goal. Compare it with 방식 (方式, 'style/mode/manner'): 방법 is the concrete how-to ('a method to fix it'), while 방식 is the overall style or fashion in which something is done (생활 방식 = lifestyle, 사고방식 = way of thinking) — you wouldn't say 사고방법. Also distinguish 수단 (手段, 'means/instrument', often the tool or even an unscrupulous means: 교통수단 = means of transport, 수단과 방법을 가리지 않다 = by any means necessary). Register-neutral, very common. Core collocations: 방법이 있다/없다, 방법을 찾다/알다/모르다/바꾸다, [동사]~는 방법 (a way to do ~), 해결 방법 (solution), 사용 방법 (how to use). The English-speaker pitfall is the 'a way to do X' frame: it's [verb in ~는 form] + 방법, e.g. 'a way to study' is 공부하는 방법, NOT 공부 방법 in spoken contexts (though 공부 방법 as a fixed noun-noun compound is fine for 'study method'). Don't say 방법으로 어떻게 when you just mean 어떻게 ('how') — 방법 is the noun 'method', not the question word 'how'."},sentence:{ko:"좋은 방법이 있어요?",en:"Is there a good way?"},target:"방법",options:["reason","method; way","what"],note:"방법 helps turn 어떻게 into a fuller question about method."}],canDo:"Ask how, how many, and how much (어떻게 · 몇 · 얼마나)",id:"chapter-19-b4",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:3},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A learner asks follow-up questions after hearing that someone studies Korean online.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"어디에서 한국어를 배워요?",en:"Where do you learn Korean?",romanization:"eodieseo hangugeoreul baewoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"집에서 온라인 수업으로 배워요.",en:"I learn at home through an online class.",romanization:"jibeseo ollain sueobeuro baewoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"언제 시작했어요?",en:"When did you start?",romanization:"eonje sijakaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"작년부터 시작했어요.",en:"I started last year.",romanization:"jangnyeonbuteo sijakaesseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"왜 한국어를 공부해요?",en:"Why do you study Korean?",romanization:"wae hangugeoreul gongbuhaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"한국 친구들과 더 편하게 이야기하고 싶어서요.",en:"Because I want to talk more comfortably with Korean friends.",romanization:"hanguk chingudeulgwa deo pyeonhage iyagihago sipeoseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"보통 어떻게 연습해요?",en:"How do you usually practice?",romanization:"botong eotteoke yeonseupaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"드라마를 보고 문장을 따라 말해요.",en:"I watch dramas and repeat the sentences.",romanization:"deuramareul bogo munjangeul ttara malhaeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"누구랑 같이 연습해요?",en:"Who do you practice with?",romanization:"nugurang gachi yeonseupaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"스터디 친구들이랑 연습해요.",en:"I practice with my study group friends.",romanization:"seuteodi chingudeurirang yeonseupaeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'When are you coming?'",tokens:["언제","와요?"],correct:"언제 와요?",explanation:"언제 asks for time and sits before the verb. Korean keeps the question word in the answer slot rather than moving it far away."}],canDo:"Ask who, what, where with question words in the answer's slot (누구 · 뭐 · 어디)",id:"chapter-19-b5",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:4},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A study-plan chat",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A study-plan chat",chunks:["민서는 새 스터디에 들어가기 전에 지훈에게 여러 가지를 물어봤어요. 먼저 어디에서 모이는지 물었고, 지훈은 학교 앞 카페에서 만난다고 대답했어요. 민서는 몇 시에 시작하는지도 물었어요.","지훈은 토요일 두 시에 시작한다고 했어요. 민서가 왜 그 스터디를 만들었냐고 묻자, 지훈은 혼자 공부하면 자꾸 미뤄서 친구들과 같이 연습하고 싶었다고 했어요. 마지막으로 민서는 어떻게 준비하면 좋으냐고 물었고, 지훈은 짧은 자기소개와 질문 세 개를 준비하면 된다고 말했어요."],translation:"Before joining a new study group, Minseo asked Jihoon several questions. She asked where they meet, what time they start, why he made the group, and how she should prepare. Jihoon answered each question with the missing place, time, reason, and method.",qas:[{q:"민서는 처음에 무엇을 물어봤어요?",a:"어디에서 모이는지 물어봤어요."},{q:"스터디는 몇 시에 시작해요?",a:"토요일 두 시에 시작해요."},{q:"민서는 마지막으로 무엇을 물어봤어요?",a:"어떻게 준비하면 좋은지 물어봤어요."}]}],canDo:"Ask when and why without changing word order (언제 · 왜)",id:"chapter-19-b6",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:5},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"You want to ask where someone studies. Which form fits the action place?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어디에서 공부해요?",ok:!0},{text:"어디에 공부해요?",ok:!1},{text:"어디를 공부해요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"공부하다 is an action, so the place of the action takes -에서."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which answer naturally responds to 왜 한국어를 공부해요?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구들과 이야기하고 싶어서요.",ok:!0},{text:"한국어를 잘 못해서 미안해요.",ok:!1},{text:"한국어를 공부한 지 두 달 됐어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"왜 expects a reason, often answered with -아서/어서요 or -고 싶어서요."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어디___ 일해요? (Where do you work?)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"에서",ok:!0},{text:"에",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"일하다 is an action, so the place where it happens takes 에서. 에 would fit a destination or static location, not work happening there."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'When are you coming?'",tokens:["언제","와요?"],correct:"언제 와요?",explanation:"언제 asks for time and sits before the verb. Korean keeps the question word in the answer slot rather than moving it far away."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which question asks about method or way?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어떻게 가요?",ok:!0},{text:"언제 가요?",ok:!1},{text:"왜 가요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"어떻게 asks “how” or by what method. 언제 asks time, and 왜 asks reason."}],canDo:"Ask how, how many, and how much (어떻게 · 몇 · 얼마나)",id:"chapter-19-b7",chapterId:"chapter-19",index:6}]},{id:"chapter-20",number:20,title:"Two number systems: native (하나·둘·셋) vs. Sino-Korean (일·이·삼)",goal:"Use native Korean numbers for hours and common counters: 한 시, 두 잔, 세 명.",biteCount:7,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 하나~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하나",romanization:"hana",en:"one (native number)",pos:"number",nuance:'하나 is the native-Korean (고유어) word for "one," the counting system Koreans use for tangible things, people, age, and the clock hour — as opposed to its Sino-Korean twin 일, which rules dates, money, minutes, and phone numbers. The single biggest fact to internalize: when 하나 sits directly in front of a counter (개, 명, 시, 살, 마리), it loses its last syllable and becomes 한 — 한 개, 한 명, 한 시 — while the full 하나 only stands alone (as in counting "하나, 둘" or taking a particle, 하나만). English speakers reliably trip here by saying 하나 개 or, worse, importing the Sino number and saying 일 개; both sound clearly wrong to a Korean ear. There is no hanja because it is purely native, so do not look for one.'},sentence:{ko:"사과 하나 주세요.",en:"Please give me one apple."},target:"하나",options:["one (native number)","o'clock; hour marker","minute"],note:"Before many counters, 하나 becomes 한: 한 개, 한 명, 한 시."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"둘",romanization:"dul",en:"two (native number)",pos:"number",nuance:`둘 is native-Korean "two," used for counting concrete things, people, and the clock hour, in contrast to Sino 이 which handles minutes, money, floors, and dates. Like 하나→한, 둘 contracts before a counter, dropping its final ㄹ to become 두: 두 개, 두 시, 두 명, 두 잔. The bare 둘 survives only when it stands alone or takes a particle — note the very common 둘 다 ('both') and 둘 중에 ('between the two'). English speakers often forget the contraction and say 둘 개, or reach for the Sino 이 (이 개), both of which sound off; they also tend to say 두 다 by mistake, but the fixed idiom is 둘 다. It is a pure native word, so it has no hanja.`},sentence:{ko:"커피 두 잔 주세요.",en:"Please give me two cups of coffee."},target:null,options:["o'clock; hour marker","minute","two (native number)"],note:"Before counters, 둘 becomes 두."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"셋",romanization:"set",en:"three (native number)",pos:"number",nuance:`셋 is native-Korean "three," the form used to count objects, people, and the clock hour, while Sino 삼 covers minutes, money, dates, and floors. Before a counter it contracts to 세 (세 개, 세 시, 세 명, 세 권), and the bare 셋 only appears standalone, with a particle, or in the social phrase 셋이서 ('as three people together'). A frequent English-speaker error is leaving it uncontracted as 셋 개 or borrowing Sino 삼 개; both are immediately wrong to natives. Another subtle pitfall: 세 (three) and 네 (four) look and sound similar, so learners mix them up — keep 세=3, 네=4 firmly apart. 셋 is purely native and has no hanja.`},sentence:{ko:"세 시에 수업이 있어요.",en:"I have class at three o'clock."},target:null,options:["o'clock; hour marker","three (native number)","minute"],note:"Before counters, 셋 becomes 세."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"일",romanization:"il",en:"one (Sino-Korean number)",pos:"number",nuance:`일 is the Sino-Korean (漢字語) number "one," from the hanja 一 (a single horizontal stroke). It governs the abstract/measured world: dates, money (원), minutes, phone numbers, floors, bus and problem numbers, and any count from the hundreds up — the exact opposite of native 하나, which counts tangible things, people, and the clock hour. So one o'clock is 한 시 but one minute is 일 분; one apple is 사과 한 개 but one won is 일 원. English speakers, used to a single set of numbers, constantly blur the line — saying 일 시 for one o'clock or 하나 분 for one minute. A handy memory hook: anything that already feels like a 'code' or a 'measurement' (clock minutes, addresses, prices) is Sino 일.`},sentence:{ko:"일 분만 기다려 주세요.",en:"Please wait just one minute."},target:"일",options:["o'clock; hour marker","one (Sino-Korean number)","minute"],note:"Use 일 with minutes, dates, prices, floors, and phone-style numbers."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이",romanization:"i",en:"two (Sino-Korean number)",pos:"number",nuance:`이 is the Sino-Korean number "two," from the hanja 二 (two horizontal strokes). It runs dates, money, minutes, floors, math, and counts in the hundreds and up — versus native 둘/두, which counts physical things, people, and the clock hour. So two o'clock is 두 시 but two minutes is 이 분; two cups is 두 잔 but two won is 이 원. A real trap unique to 이: it is identical in sound to the demonstrative 이 ('this') and the subject/topic particle context around it, so 이 분 can mean either 'two minutes' or 'this person' (분 = honorific 'person') depending on context — listen carefully. English speakers also tend to use native 둘 for prices or floors (둘 층); those are Sino: 이 층. Treat measured, coded things as Sino 이.`},sentence:{ko:"이월에 한국에 가요.",en:"I am going to Korea in February."},target:"이",options:["o'clock; hour marker","two (Sino-Korean number)","minute"],note:"Use 이 for Sino-Korean number settings such as months and prices."}],canDo:"Tell the o'clock hour with native numbers (세 시 · 네 시)",id:"chapter-20-b1",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · Two number systems: native (하나·둘·셋) vs. Sino-Korean (일·이·삼)",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"Two number systems: native (하나·둘·셋) vs. Sino-Korean (일·이·삼)",sub:"",rows:[{when:"Counting things, people, or animals (with a counter)",add:"Native 하나·둘·셋·넷, but 1–4 shorten before a counter → 한·두·세·네",ex:"사과 한 개 (one apple), 친구 두 명 (two friends)"},{when:"Saying the o'clock hour (시)",add:"Native number + 시 (1–4 shorten here too)",ex:"세 시 (3 o'clock), 네 시 (4 o'clock)"},{when:"Minutes, money, dates, phone numbers",add:"Sino-Korean 일·이·삼·사",ex:"삼십 분 (30 minutes), 오천 원 (5,000 won)"},{when:"Age",add:"Native + 살 in daily talk; Sino + 세 on official forms",ex:"스무 살 (20 years old, everyday), 이십 세 (on forms)"}],examples:[{ko:"물 한 병 주세요.",en:"One bottle of water, please.",romanization:"mul han byeong juseyo.",note:"Counting a thing, so native — and 하나 shortens to 한 before the counter 병."},{ko:"지금 세 시 삼십 분이에요.",en:"It's 3:30 now.",romanization:"jigeum se si samsip bun-ieyo.",note:"The hour 시 takes native 세, but the minute 분 takes Sino 삼십 — both systems in one time."}],more:{func:"Korean has two full sets of numbers, and which one you use depends on what you're saying — not on which you like better. Native numbers count things, people, and the o'clock hour. Sino-Korean numbers handle money, minutes, dates, and phone numbers. The clock uses both at once: native for the hour, Sino for the minute.",keyPoint:{label:"The clock uses both systems",body:"The hour (시) is native: 한 시, 두 시, 세 시. The minute (분) is Sino: 일 분, 십 분, 삼십 분. So 3:20 is 세 시 이십 분 — native for the hour, Sino for the minute. If you can remember this one phrase, you've got the whole rule. (Which counter goes with which number is a later topic; for now just lock in hour vs. minute.)"},pronunciation:"여섯 개 sounds like [여섣 깨] and 일곱 시 like [일곱 씨] — the counter's first consonant tenses after the number's final consonant. Also remember the short forms before a counter: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네, 스물→스무."}}],canDo:"Tell the o'clock hour with native numbers (세 시 · 네 시)",id:"chapter-20-b2",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 삼~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"삼",romanization:"sam",en:"three (Sino-Korean number)",pos:"number",nuance:`삼 is the Sino-Korean number "three," from the hanja 三 (three horizontal strokes). It governs dates, money, minutes, floors, arithmetic, and larger counts — in contrast to native 셋/세, which counts tangible objects, people, and the clock hour. So three o'clock is 세 시 but three minutes is 삼 분; three notebooks is 세 권 but 30,000 won is 삼만 원. The recurring English-speaker mistake is using 삼 to tell the hour (삼 시) or reaching for native 세 in a price (세만 원). A useful anchor: cooking and timer durations in minutes are always Sino, so '3 minutes' on a ramen pack is 삼 분, never 세 분. Keep measured/coded contexts on the Sino side, 삼.`},sentence:{ko:"삼천 원이에요.",en:"It is 3,000 won."},target:"삼",options:["three (Sino-Korean number)","o'clock; hour marker","minute"],note:"Use 삼 in prices, dates, and minutes, but not for clock hour three."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"몇",romanization:"myeot",en:"how many; what number",pos:"determiner",nuance:"몇 means 'how many' and, grammatically, behaves like a number: it drops straight into the slot before a counter (몇 개, 몇 명, 몇 시, 몇 잔), and since it pairs with the native-number counters, it inherits their world (몇 시 'what time', 몇 살 'how old'). A defining subtlety is that the SAME 몇 also means 'a few / several' when it is not a question — '몇 개 샀어요' can be 'I bought a few' depending on intonation and context. Two boundaries trip English speakers: (1) 몇 almost always needs a counter, so 'how many?' about countable things is 몇 개/몇 명, not bare 몇; (2) don't confuse 몇 시 (what TIME, native + 시) with 며칠 (what DATE/how many days, which behaves on the Sino-Korean date system) — different question, different number family. Compare also 얼마나 'how much/to what extent', which asks degree rather than a countable number."},sentence:{ko:"지금 몇 시예요?",en:"What time is it now?"},target:"몇",options:["o'clock; hour marker","how many; what number","minute"],note:"몇 combines with a counter or number unit: 몇 시, 몇 분, 몇 명, 몇 개."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시",romanization:"si",en:"o'clock; hour marker",pos:"counter",nuance:"시 is the counter for the o'clock HOUR (from 時 'time/hour'), and it is one of the great beginner traps because Korean splits its two number systems across the clock: the HOUR uses NATIVE numbers (한 시, 두 시, 세 시, 네 시, 다섯 시...), while the MINUTES counter 분 uses SINO-Korean numbers (일 분, 십 분, 삼십 분). So 3:30 is 세 시 삼십 분 — native for the hour, Sino for the minutes — and mixing them up ('삼 시' or '세십 분') is the classic mistake. Watch the irregular shape changes the native numbers make right before 시: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네, 스물→스무 (스무 시 in 24-hour form). To say 'at' a time, attach 에: 두 시에 'at two o'clock.' Don't confuse the noun 시 'o'clock' with its homophones 시 'poem' (詩) or the city suffix 시 (市, as in 부산시) — the counter use is always preceded by a native number."},sentence:{ko:"두 시에 만나요.",en:"Let's meet at two o'clock."},target:"시",options:["o'clock; hour marker","minute","won (Korean money unit)"],note:"Clock hours use native numbers before 시."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"분",romanization:"bun",en:"minute",pos:"counter",nuance:"분 is the time unit 'minute', written with the hanja 分 (to divide/a part) — a minute being a division of the hour. The crucial rule: 분 ALWAYS takes Sino-Korean numbers (일·이·삼·사·오·...), so it's 오 분, 삼십 분, never the native 하나·둘·셋. This creates Korea's famous mixed clock, where the HOUR uses native numbers + 시 (한 시, 두 시) but the MINUTE uses Sino numbers + 분 (십 분, 삼십 분): 한 시 십 분 = 1:10. Note 분 also means 'a respected person' (한 분 = one person, honorific) and is the honorific counter for people — same syllable, totally different word, disambiguated by context. The English-speaker pitfall: applying the native numbers used for 시 to 분 as well (✗한 분 for one minute — that actually means 'one person, honorific'); the correct 'one minute' is 일 분. Collocates with 후/전 (after/before), 동안 (for), 정도 (about), 만 (only), and 걸리다 (to take)."},sentence:{ko:"오 분만 기다려 주세요.",en:"Please wait just five minutes."},target:"분",options:["o'clock; hour marker","minute","won (Korean money unit)"],note:"Minutes use Sino-Korean numbers."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시간",romanization:"sigan",en:"time; hour",pos:"noun",nuance:"시간 works with 있어요/없어요 and is essential for scheduling."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 시간이 있어요?",en:"Do you have time today?"},target:"시간",options:["time; hour","price","one (native number)"],note:"시간 can mean available time or duration, so context matters."}],canDo:"Say minutes and prices with Sino-Korean numbers (삼십 분 · 사천 원)",id:"chapter-20-b3",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:2},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 원~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"원",romanization:"won",en:"won (Korean money unit)",pos:"counter",nuance:"원 is the Korean currency unit (the symbol is ₩) and, like all money and clock-number words, it REQUIRES Sino-Korean numbers: 일·이·삼·사·오·육·칠·팔·구·십·백·천·만 (so 천 원, 만 원, 오천 원). The single biggest stumbling block for English speakers is the 만 'ten-thousand' place: Korean groups large numbers by 10,000, not 1,000, so 만 원 = 10,000 won (not 1,000), 십만 = 100,000, 백만 = 1,000,000. Reading prices therefore means re-chunking — 30,000 is 삼만 (3×10,000), 15,000 is 만 오천. Habitual partners: 얼마예요?, 만 원짜리 'a 10,000-won bill/item', ~원만 'just ~won', 거스름돈 'change'. The headline pitfall, beyond the comma-grouping, is using NATIVE numbers with 원 (한 원, 두 원) — money never takes native numbers; it's 천 원, 만 원. Contrast this with counters like 개/명, which take native numbers — Korean splits the two systems, and 원 firmly sits on the Sino side. Also note 원 is read after the full number, e.g. 사천오백 원, with no 'and'."},sentence:{ko:"이거 오천 원이에요.",en:"This is 5,000 won."},target:"원",options:["won (Korean money unit)","o'clock; hour marker","minute"],note:"Prices use Sino-Korean numbers before 원."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가격",romanization:"gagyeok",en:"price",pos:"noun",nuance:"가격(價格) is Sino-Korean — 價 'value/price' + 格 'standard' — so it literally means the 'value-standard' set on a thing, the official posted price. Its native-Korean twin is 값, and the line is feel and register: 값 is shorter, warmer, and used in fixed everyday phrases (값이 싸다, 값을 치르다), while 가격 sounds more neutral and 'tag/system' (가격표 price tag, 가격 인상 price hike) and is what you read on signs. The biggest English-speaker trap is the adjective: a high price is 비싸요 (expensive), a low price is 싸요 (cheap) — Koreans do NOT say 가격이 높아요/낮아요 the way English says 'high/low price', so importing 'high' here sounds off. Note too that to ask a clerk you rarely say the noun at all: the natural line is just 얼마예요? ('how much?'). 가격 collocates with 비싸다, 싸다, 오르다 (rise), 내리다 (drop), and 깎다 (haggle down)."},sentence:{ko:"가격이 얼마예요?",en:"How much is the price?"},target:"가격",options:["time; hour","price","date"],note:"In shops, 이거 얼마예요? is often more natural than saying 가격 every time."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"날짜",romanization:"naljja",en:"date",pos:"noun",nuance:"날짜 means a calendar date — a specific day pinned to the month and year. It is a native-Korean compound built on 날 'day' (날 + the bound element -짜), so it is not Sino-Korean and has no hanja, even though the numbers you read inside a date (일·이·삼…) are Sino. Keep three look-alikes apart: 날짜 'date (which day of the month)' vs 요일 'day of the week (Monday…)' vs 날 'a day in general'. To ask a date Koreans say 며칠이에요?, and the answer uses Sino-Korean day numbers (오월 십오일 'May 15th'), not the native 하나·둘 set — that is the classic pitfall. Habitual collocations: 날짜를 정하다 'set a date', 날짜를 잡다 'pin down a date', 날짜를 바꾸다 'change a date', 날짜가 지나다 'the date passes'."},sentence:{ko:"예약 날짜가 언제예요?",en:"What is the reservation date?"},target:"날짜",options:["date","price","birthday"],note:"Dates use Sino-Korean numbers with 월 and 일."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"생일",romanization:"saengil",en:"birthday",pos:"noun",nuance:"생일 is 'birthday', Sino-Korean 生日 — 生 'birth/life' + 日 'day', literally 'birth-day'. The culturally loaded fact is that Koreans eat 미역국 (seaweed soup) on a birthday, because mothers traditionally ate it after giving birth. Register matters sharply here: for grandparents, bosses, or anyone you must honor, the polite word is the honorific 생신 (生辰), and the greeting upgrades to 생신 축하드립니다, not the casual 생일 축하해요 you'd say to a friend. The standard greeting is 생일 축하해요 / 생일 축하합니다, literally 'I celebrate (your) birthday' — note it is the verb 축하하다 plus the noun, not an adjective. Collocations: 생일 파티 'birthday party', 생일 선물 'birthday gift', 생일을 챙기다 'to make a fuss of someone's birthday'. Pitfall: don't say 생일 to or about an elder when respect is due — switch to 생신."},sentence:{ko:"생일이 언제예요?",en:"When is your birthday?"},target:"생일",options:["price","birthday","date"],note:"생일 questions often lead to month and day practice."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"나이",romanization:"nai",en:"age",pos:"noun",nuance:"나이 is the native Korean word for a person's age, and it sits at the center of Korean social life because age sets who speaks formally to whom. Register is everything: to an adult, an elder, or a stranger you ask 나이가 어떻게 되세요? (or even more deferential 연세가 어떻게 되세요?, using the honorific 연세 for grandparents), while the blunt 몇 살이에요? is reserved for children or close friends — asking your boss 몇 살이에요? sounds rude. The near-synonym pair is 나이 vs 연세: same meaning, but 연세 (年歲, Sino-Korean) is the respectful 'years' you grant to someone older, so you raise the register by swapping the word, not by adding an ending. Crucially, age is measured with 많다/적다, not size words: 나이가 많아요 = 'is old', 나이가 적어요 = 'is young' — never 나이가 커요, a direct-translation trap from English 'big'. When you state an actual number, age takes NATIVE numbers + the counter 살: 스무 살 (20), 서른 살 (30) — saying 이십 살 with Sino-Korean numbers is a classic beginner error. One more pitfall: the verb is 되세요 (from 되다, 'to become/amount to'), so 어떻게 되세요? literally asks 'how does it come to be?' — treat the whole phrase as a fixed polite formula rather than translating it word by word."},sentence:{ko:"나이가 어떻게 되세요?",en:"May I ask how old you are?"},target:"나이",options:["price","date","age"],note:"Use this polite formula with adults; actual age uses native numbers with 살."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 생일 케이크는 만 이천 원이에요.",en:"Yes. The birthday cake is 12,000 won."},hl:"생일"}],canDo:"Count people and things with native numbers plus a counter (네 명)",id:"chapter-20-b4",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:3},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Friends arrange a meeting time and check prices before buying coffee and a birthday cake.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"우리 몇 시에 만날까요?",en:"What time shall we meet?",romanization:"uri myeot sie mannalkkayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"세 시 십 분에 지하철역 앞에서 만나요.",en:"Let's meet at 3:10 in front of the subway station.",romanization:"se si sip bune jihacheoryeok apeseo mannayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"커피는 몇 잔 살까요?",en:"How many cups of coffee should we buy?",romanization:"keopineun myeot jan salkkayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"두 잔이면 돼요. 한 잔에 이천오백 원이에요.",en:"Two cups are enough. One cup is 2,500 won.",romanization:"du janimyeon dwaeyo. han jane icheonobaek wonieyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그럼 두 잔에 오천 원이네요.",en:"Then two cups are 5,000 won.",romanization:"geureom du jane ocheon wonineyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 생일 케이크는 만 이천 원이에요.",en:"Yes. The birthday cake is 12,000 won.",romanization:"ne. saengil keikeuneun man icheon wonieyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Let's meet at 3:10.'",tokens:["세 시 십 분에","만나요"],correct:"세 시 십 분에 만나요",explanation:"Hours use native Korean numbers, so 3 o’clock is 세 시. Minutes use Sino-Korean numbers, so 10 minutes is 십 분."}],canDo:"Tell the o'clock hour with native numbers (세 시 · 네 시)",id:"chapter-20-b5",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:4},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A birthday meet-up",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A birthday meet-up",chunks:["민서와 지훈은 토요일에 친구 생일을 준비해요. 두 사람은 세 시 십 분에 지하철역 앞에서 만나기로 했어요. 커피는 두 잔을 사고, 한 잔에 이천오백 원이라서 모두 오천 원이에요. 케이크는 만 이천 원이에요.","민서는 예약 날짜도 확인했어요. 생일은 이월 삼일이고, 케이크는 토요일 네 시 전에 찾을 수 있어요. 그래서 두 사람은 시간과 가격을 다시 확인하고 가게에 갔어요."],translation:"Minseo and Jihoon prepare for a friend's birthday on Saturday. They plan to meet at 3:10, buy two coffees, check the cake price, confirm the birthday date, and pick up the cake before 4:00.",qas:[{q:"민서와 지훈은 몇 시 몇 분에 만나기로 했어요?",a:"세 시 십 분에 만나기로 했어요."},{q:"커피 두 잔은 모두 얼마예요?",a:"오천 원이에요."},{q:"생일은 언제예요?",a:"이월 삼일이에요."}]}],canDo:"Say minutes and prices with Sino-Korean numbers (삼십 분 · 사천 원)",id:"chapter-20-b6",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:5},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which time expression is natural for 3:10?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"세 시 십 분",ok:!0},{text:"삼 시 세 분",ok:!1},{text:"셋 시 열 분",ok:!1}],explanation:"Hours use native Korean numbers; minutes use Sino-Korean numbers."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which counter fits coffee?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"두 잔",ok:!0},{text:"두 명",ok:!1},{text:"두 권",ok:!1}],explanation:"잔 counts cups and glasses."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"커피 두 잔___ 주세요. (Please give me two cups of coffee.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"두 잔 is the counted object being requested, so 을 fits when the particle is shown. In casual ordering, Koreans often drop it, but 을 is the grammatical object marker."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Let's meet at 3:10.'",tokens:["세 시 십 분에","만나요"],correct:"세 시 십 분에 만나요",explanation:"Hours use native Korean numbers, so 3 o’clock is 세 시. Minutes use Sino-Korean numbers, so 10 minutes is 십 분."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which price expression is natural for 3,000 won?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"삼천 원",ok:!0},{text:"세천 원",ok:!1},{text:"셋 원",ok:!1}],explanation:"Prices use Sino-Korean numbers, so 3,000 won is 삼천 원. 세 and 셋 are native forms and do not fit this price expression."}],canDo:"Count people and things with native numbers plus a counter (네 명)",id:"chapter-20-b7",chapterId:"chapter-20",index:6}]},{id:"chapter-21",number:21,title:"A-(으)ㄴ N · V-는 N",goal:"Use A-ㄴ/은 N for adjective descriptions: 작은 가방, 조용한 카페.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 예쁜~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"예쁜",romanization:"yeppeun",en:"pretty; beautiful (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"예쁜 is the noun-modifying form of 예쁘다, so it must be followed by a noun: 예쁜 가방, 예쁜 색, 예쁜 사람. Korean descriptive verbs use -(으)ㄴ before a noun in the present state; 예쁘다 loses ㅡ and becomes 예쁜, never 예쁘는. 예쁘다 is the everyday word for something visually pretty, cute, or pleasing. 아름다운 can sound broader, more elevated, or emotionally beautiful, while 귀여운 specifically means cute. Because 예쁜 directly evaluates appearance, using it about a person is friendly but can feel personal depending on the relationship."},sentence:{ko:"예쁜 가방을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I am looking for a pretty bag."},target:"예쁜",options:["pretty; beautiful (modifier)","who lives; where someone lives","that someone reads / is reading"],note:"예쁘다 becomes 예쁜 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"큰",romanization:"keun",en:"big; large (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"큰 is the noun-modifying form of 크다. It can describe physical size (큰 가방), seriousness (큰 문제), importance or impact (큰 변화), and a broad amount or degree (큰 도움). Because 크다 is a descriptive verb, the present modifier is 큰, not 크는. The sentence-ending form is 커요. Do not assume every English ‘big’ maps mechanically to 큰: a large number is 큰 수, but a loud sound is 큰 소리 and a high price is 비싼 가격, not 큰 가격 in ordinary speech."},sentence:{ko:"큰 가방은 여행할 때 편해요.",en:"A big bag is convenient when traveling."},target:null,options:["who lives; where someone lives","big; large (modifier)","that someone wears"],note:"크다 becomes 큰 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"작은",romanization:"jageun",en:"small (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"작은 is the noun-modifying form of 작다. It describes physical size (작은 방), limited amount or scale (작은 회사, 작은 실수), or a low/soft degree (작은 목소리). Since 작다 ends in a consonant, the present descriptive modifier uses -은: 작은. It is different from 적은, which means ‘few/little in quantity’: 작은 가방 is a physically small bag, while 적은 돈 is a small amount of money."},sentence:{ko:"작은 지갑을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I am looking for a small wallet."},target:"작은",options:["small (modifier)","who lives; where someone lives","that someone wears"],note:"작다 becomes 작은 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"새로운",romanization:"saeroun",en:"new (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"새로운 is the noun-modifying form of 새롭다, a ㅂ-irregular descriptive verb: 새롭다 → 새로운. It often means new in the sense of different, unfamiliar, newly introduced, or novel — 새로운 방법, 새로운 환경. The shorter 새 also comes directly before nouns and often means a newly acquired or brand-new item: 새 가방, 새 휴대폰. The two overlap, but 새로운 tends to highlight newness as a quality or change, while 새 often simply identifies the new item."},sentence:{ko:"새로운 디자인이 마음에 들어요.",en:"I like the new design."},target:"새로운",options:["who lives; where someone lives","that someone wears","new (modifier)"],note:"새롭다 becomes 새로운; 새 is also common before nouns."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"좋은",romanization:"joeun",en:"good (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"좋은 is the noun-modifying form of 좋다: 좋은 사람, 좋은 방법, 좋은 날씨. It means the noun itself is good, suitable, favorable, or high quality. Do not confuse it with 좋아하는, which means ‘that someone likes’: 좋은 영화 is a good movie, while 제가 좋아하는 영화 is a movie I like. A very useful extension is V-기 좋은 N, meaning a noun that is good/suitable for doing something: 들기 좋은 가방, 걷기 좋은 날씨. The sentence-ending form is 좋아요."},sentence:{ko:"출근할 때 들기 좋은 가방이에요.",en:"It is a good bag to carry to work."},target:"좋은",options:["that someone looks for / asks for","that someone saw","good (modifier)"],note:"좋다 becomes 좋은; do not confuse it with 좋아하는."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"나쁜",romanization:"nappeun",en:"bad (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"나쁜 is the noun-modifying form of 나쁘다: 나쁜 습관, 나쁜 날씨, 나쁜 선택. It can describe poor quality, harmful effects, an unfavorable situation, or moral wrongdoing. Calling a person 나쁜 사람 is a strong judgment of character, so Korean often softens criticism with 안 좋은 사람/인상/상황 when the meaning is merely unfavorable rather than morally bad. The predicate form is 나빠요, and the comparative result ‘got worse’ is 나빠졌어요."},sentence:{ko:"나쁜 습관은 조금씩 고치고 있어요.",en:"I am fixing bad habits little by little."},target:"나쁜",options:["that someone looks for / asks for","bad (modifier)","that someone saw"],note:"나쁘다 becomes 나쁜 before a noun."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"어제 본 작은 가방을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I am looking for the small bag I saw yesterday."},hl:"작은"}],canDo:"Put an adjective before a noun with A-(으)ㄴ N (작은 가방 · 예쁜 옷)",id:"chapter-21-b1",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A-(으)ㄴ N",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A-(으)ㄴ N",sub:"Adjectives describing a noun",rows:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄴ",ex:"예쁘다 → 예쁜 (가방)"},{when:"Adjective stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 은",ex:"작다 → 작은 (집)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop the ㄹ)",add:"ㄹ drops, + ㄴ",ex:"길다 → 긴 (시간)"},{when:"있다/없다 type (맛있다, 재미있다)",add:"+ 는 (acts like a verb)",ex:"맛있다 → 맛있는 (밥)"}],examples:[{ko:"예쁜 가방이에요.",en:"It's a pretty bag.",romanization:"yeppeun gabangieyo.",note:"예쁘다 ends in a vowel, so just add ㄴ → 예쁜, right before 가방."},{ko:"작은 집에 살아요.",en:"I live in a small house.",romanization:"jageun jibe sarayo.",note:"작다 ends in a consonant, so add 은 → 작은. The adjective goes straight onto the noun — no 이/가."}],more:{func:"To put an adjective in front of a noun (a *pretty* bag, a *small* room), you can't use the plain dictionary form. You change the adjective into a modifying form with -(으)ㄴ and put it right before the noun.",keyPoint:{label:"예쁜 가방 vs 가방이 예뻐요",body:"Korean uses an adjective in two ways. Before a noun it has to become the modifier form (예쁜 가방 = 'a pretty bag'). At the end of a sentence it stays as the predicate (가방이 예뻐요 = 'The bag is pretty'). Same adjective, two jobs — don't put the plain 예뻐요 form in front of a noun."},pronunciation:"좋은 is said [조은] (joeun): the ㅎ drops out between vowels and the sound links straight into the next syllable. 작은 links the same way as [자근] (jageun)."}}],canDo:"Put an adjective before a noun with A-(으)ㄴ N (작은 가방 · 예쁜 옷)",id:"chapter-21-b2",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 찾는~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"찾는",romanization:"channeun",en:"that someone looks for / asks for",pos:"verb modifier",nuance:"찾는 is the present/habitual noun-modifying form of 찾다. It can describe something someone is currently looking for (제가 찾는 가방), a person someone asks for (사장님을 찾는 손님), or an item people frequently choose and request (손님이 많이 찾는 모델). The subject of 찾다 comes before the modifier clause, and the noun being modified comes after it. Compare 찾는 with 찾은: 찾는 물건 is the thing you are looking for; 찾은 물건 is the thing you found."},sentence:{ko:"손님이 많이 찾는 모델이에요.",en:"It is a model customers ask for a lot."},target:"찾는",options:["good (modifier)","bad (modifier)","that someone looks for / asks for"],note:"찾다 becomes 찾는 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"본",romanization:"bon",en:"that someone saw",pos:"completed verb modifier",nuance:"본 is the completed noun-modifying form of 보다: 어제 본 영화, 전에 본 사람. It means the seeing or watching happened before the reference point. 보는 is the present/habitual form (‘the movie I am watching / usually watch’), while 볼 is the future or intended form (‘the movie I will watch’). Because 보다 is vowel-final, -(으)ㄴ becomes ㄴ and attaches as 본. Do not build the modifier from the polite past 봤어요; Korean returns to the verb stem and attaches the modifier ending."},sentence:{ko:"어제 본 가방이 아직 있어요?",en:"Do you still have the bag I saw yesterday?"},target:"본",options:["good (modifier)","bad (modifier)","that someone saw"],note:"보다 becomes 본 when it modifies a noun as a completed action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사는",romanization:"saneun",en:"who lives; where someone lives",pos:"verb modifier",nuance:"In this card, 사는 is the present noun-modifying form of 살다, ‘to live’: 서울에 사는 사람, 친구가 사는 동네. 살다 is an ㄹ-stem verb, so ㄹ drops before -는: 살 + 는 → 사는. The same written form can also come from 사다, ‘to buy’: 제가 자주 사는 물건. Context and nearby particles decide whether 사는 means living or buying. For a completed living period, 살던 is often natural (‘the place I used to live’), while 산 can mean ‘bought’ from 사다 or ‘lived’ in some completed constructions."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 사는 동네는 조용해요.",en:"The neighborhood where my friend lives is quiet."},target:"사는",options:["pretty; beautiful (modifier)","big; large (modifier)","who lives; where someone lives"],note:"살다 drops ㄹ before 는."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"입는",romanization:"imneun",en:"that someone wears",pos:"verb modifier",nuance:"입는 is the present/habitual noun-modifying form of 입다: 자주 입는 옷, 면접 때 입는 옷, 겨울에 입는 코트. It usually describes what someone wears regularly, in a certain situation, or as an ongoing action. 입은 is the completed/state-result modifier and often describes clothes someone has put on or is currently wearing: 검은 코트를 입은 사람. This distinction matters because clothing verbs can focus either on the habitual action (입는) or the resulting dressed state (입은). Pronunciation changes to [임는], but spelling remains 입는."},sentence:{ko:"면접 때 입는 옷은 단정해야 해요.",en:"The clothes you wear for an interview should be neat."},target:"입는",options:["big; large (modifier)","small (modifier)","that someone wears"],note:"입다 becomes 입는 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"읽는",romanization:"ingneun",en:"that someone reads / is reading",pos:"verb modifier",nuance:"읽는 is the present/habitual noun-modifying form of 읽다: 요즘 읽는 책, 학생이 읽는 글. It can mean ‘is reading’ or ‘usually reads’ depending on context. The spelling preserves 읽-, but pronunciation changes to [잉는]. 읽은 marks completed reading (‘the book I read’), and 읽을 marks future or intended reading (‘the book I will read’). Do not form the modifier from 읽어요; attach the ending directly to the dictionary stem."},sentence:{ko:"이건 제가 요즘 읽는 책이에요.",en:"This is the book I am reading these days."},target:"읽는",options:["that someone reads / is reading","pretty; beautiful (modifier)","big; large (modifier)"],note:"읽다 becomes 읽는; pronunciation changes to ingneun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹는",romanization:"meongneun",en:"that someone eats / is eating",pos:"verb modifier",nuance:"먹는 is the present/habitual noun-modifying form of 먹다: 자주 먹는 음식, 아이가 먹는 간식. It may describe what someone is eating now, usually eats, or eats in a certain context. The pronunciation is [멍는], but the spelling stays 먹는. 먹은 marks a completed eating event (‘the food I ate’), and 먹을 marks future/intended food (‘what I will eat’). In phrases like 먹는 약, it can also describe medicine taken orally rather than food."},sentence:{ko:"제가 자주 먹는 음식은 비빔밥이에요.",en:"The food I often eat is bibimbap."},target:"먹는",options:["pretty; beautiful (modifier)","that someone eats / is eating","big; large (modifier)"],note:"먹다 becomes 먹는 before a noun."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"직원",ko:"손님들이 많이 찾는 새로운 모델이에요.",en:"It is a new model that customers ask for a lot."},hl:"찾는"}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a current or habitual action with V-는 N (찾는 가방)",id:"chapter-21-b3",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-는 N",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-는 N",sub:"A present/habitual action describing a noun",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"사람이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구예요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"밥을 먹는 사람이 친구예요.",en:"The person eating is my friend.",romanization:"babeul meongneun sarami chinguyeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자주",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교예요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"제가 자주 가는 학교예요.",en:"It's the school I go to often.",romanization:"jega jaju ganeun hakgyoyeyo."}],rule:{name:"V-는 N",rows:[{when:"Any verb stem (vowel- or consonant-final)",add:"+ 는",ex:"먹다 → 먹는 / 가다 → 가는 (사람)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop the ㄹ)",add:"ㄹ drops, + 는",ex:"살다 → 사는 (집)"},{when:"있다/없다",add:"+ 는",ex:"있다 → 있는 (시간)"}]},more:{func:"To describe a noun by an action happening now or done regularly (the person who is *eating*, the book I *read* these days), add -는 to the verb stem and put it before the noun. It works like an English 'who/that' clause ('the person who eats').",keyPoint:{label:"먹는 사람 vs 예쁜 사람",body:"Verbs and adjectives use different modifier endings in the present. A verb (an action) takes -는: 먹는 사람 ('the person who eats'). An adjective (a state) takes -(으)ㄴ: 예쁜 사람 ('a pretty person'). Just ask 'is this a doing-word or a describing-word?' to pick the ending."},pronunciation:"먹는 is said [멍는] (meongneun): a ㄱ before ㄴ turns into an [ㅇ] sound (nasal assimilation). 읽는 → [잉는] (ingneun) the same way."}}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a current or habitual action with V-는 N (찾는 가방)",id:"chapter-21-b4",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 조용한~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"조용한",romanization:"joyonghan",en:"quiet (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"조용한 is the noun-modifying form of 조용하다: 조용한 카페, 조용한 동네, 조용한 사람. With a place, it means little noise; with a person, it often means calm, reserved, or not very talkative rather than literally silent. The adverb is 조용히 (‘quietly’), so 조용히 말하다 describes how someone speaks, while 조용한 목소리 describes the voice itself. Since 하다-descriptive verbs take -ㄴ, 조용하다 becomes 조용한."},sentence:{ko:"조용한 카페에서 공부하고 싶어요.",en:"I want to study at a quiet cafe."},target:"조용한",options:["who lives; where someone lives","quiet (modifier)","that someone wears"],note:"조용하다 becomes 조용한."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바쁜",romanization:"bappeun",en:"busy (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"바쁜 is the noun-modifying form of 바쁘다: 바쁜 사람, 바쁜 날, 바쁜 시간. It describes having many tasks, appointments, or demands. It does not mean a place is crowded; for a crowded store, train, or street, use 붐비는. A shop can be 바쁜 when staff have a lot of work, but 붐비는 when many customers are physically present. 바쁘다 loses ㅡ before ㄴ and becomes 바쁜; the predicate form is 바빠요."},sentence:{ko:"바쁜 사람에게는 짧게 말하는 게 좋아요.",en:"It is good to speak briefly to a busy person."},target:"바쁜",options:["who lives; where someone lives","that someone wears","busy (modifier)"],note:"바쁘다 becomes 바쁜 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"친절한",romanization:"chinjeolhan",en:"kind; friendly (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"친절한 is the noun-modifying form of 친절하다 and describes kind, considerate, or helpful treatment: 친절한 직원, 친절한 설명, 친절한 안내. It is not the same as 친한, which means socially close: 친절한 친구 is a kind friend, while 친한 친구 is a close friend. The adverb 친절하게 describes how an action is done, and the predicate is 친절해요. In service contexts, 친절한 often focuses on clear, patient help rather than emotional warmth alone."},sentence:{ko:"친절한 직원이 설명해 줬어요.",en:"A kind staff member explained it to me."},target:"친절한",options:["who lives; where someone lives","kind; friendly (modifier)","that someone wears"],note:"친절하다 becomes 친절한."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"필요한",romanization:"piryohan",en:"necessary; needed (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"필요한 is the noun-modifying form of 필요하다: 필요한 서류, 필요한 정보, 도움이 필요한 사람. In a full sentence, what is needed takes 이/가 — 여권이 필요해요 — but before a noun, 필요한 directly marks the necessary item. Do not say 필요하는; 필요하다 is a descriptive 하다 word and becomes 필요한. In formal writing you may see 필요로 하는 (‘requiring’), but everyday speech usually prefers 필요한."},sentence:{ko:"필요한 물건만 살게요.",en:"I will buy only the things I need."},target:"필요한",options:["who lives; where someone lives","necessary; needed (modifier)","that someone wears"],note:"필요하다 becomes 필요한 before a noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"중요한",romanization:"jungyohan",en:"important (modifier)",pos:"adjective modifier",nuance:"중요한 is the noun-modifying form of 중요하다: 중요한 일, 중요한 약속, 중요한 정보. It marks something as having high significance, priority, or consequence. In a full sentence, the important thing takes 이/가 — 시간이 중요해요. The adverb 중요하게 means ‘importantly / with importance,’ but Korean often prefers clearer verbs such as 중요하게 생각하다 (‘consider important’) or 중요하게 다루다 (‘handle carefully’). ‘Most important’ is 가장 중요한."},sentence:{ko:"중요한 서류는 가방 안에 넣었어요.",en:"I put the important documents inside the bag."},target:"중요한",options:["who lives; where someone lives","that someone wears","important (modifier)"],note:"중요하다 becomes 중요한 before a noun."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 필요한 물건도 많이 들어가요.",en:"Yes, many necessary things fit inside too."},hl:"필요한"}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a finished action with V-(으)ㄴ N (어제 본 영화)",id:"chapter-21-b5",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄴ N",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㄴ N",sub:"A finished (past) action describing a noun",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄴ",ex:"보다 → 본 (영화)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 은",ex:"먹다 → 먹은 (밥)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop the ㄹ)",add:"ㄹ drops, + ㄴ",ex:"만들다 → 만든 (음식)"},{when:"ㄷ-irregular (받침 ㄷ → ㄹ)",add:"ㄷ → ㄹ, + 은",ex:"듣다 → 들은 (노래)"}],examples:[{ko:"어제 본 영화가 좋았어요.",en:"The movie I saw yesterday was good.",romanization:"eoje bon yeonghwaga joasseoyo.",note:"보다 is a vowel stem → 본; 어제 ('yesterday') shows it's finished, so we use -(으)ㄴ, not -는."},{ko:"아까 먹은 밥이 맛있었어요.",en:"The meal I ate earlier was delicious.",romanization:"akka meogeun babi masisseosseoyo.",note:"먹다 ends in a consonant → 먹은 [머근]; the meal is already eaten — a finished action."}],more:{func:"To describe a noun by an action that's already over (the movie I *saw* yesterday, the person who *came*), add -(으)ㄴ to the verb stem and put it before the noun. It marks the action as done — the past version of the 'who/that' clause.",keyPoint:{label:"본 영화 vs 보는 영화",body:"On a verb, the ending sets the time. -(으)ㄴ = finished/past (어제 본 영화 = 'the movie I saw'). -는 = now or habitual (지금 보는 영화 = 'the movie I'm watching now'). Pick the ending by *when* the action happens compared to now."},pronunciation:"먹은 links as [머근] (meogeun) and 읽은 as [일근] (ilgeun): the final consonant slides into the next syllable. 만든 (from 만들다) is just [만든] (mandeun)."}}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a finished action with V-(으)ㄴ N (어제 본 영화)",id:"chapter-21-b6",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A customer identifies the exact bag in a shop by using adjective and verb modifiers.",lines:[{speaker:"직원",ko:"어떤 가방을 찾으세요?",en:"What kind of bag are you looking for?",romanization:"eotteon gabangeul chajeuseyo?"},{speaker:"민서",ko:"어제 본 작은 가방을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I am looking for the small bag I saw yesterday.",romanization:"eoje bon jageun gabangeul chatgo isseoyo."},{speaker:"직원",ko:"손님들이 많이 찾는 새로운 모델이에요.",en:"It is a new model that customers ask for a lot.",romanization:"sonnimdeuri manhi channeun saeroun moderieyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"출근할 때 들기 좋은 가방인가요?",en:"Is it a good bag to carry to work?",romanization:"chulgeunhal ttae deulgi joheun gabangingayo?"},{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 필요한 물건도 많이 들어가요.",en:"Yes, many necessary things fit inside too.",romanization:"ne, piryohan mulgeondo manhi deureogayo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'the book I am reading'",tokens:["제가","읽는","책"],correct:"제가 읽는 책",explanation:"Present action modifiers use V-는 before the noun. 읽다 becomes 읽는 when it describes 책."}],canDo:"Put an adjective before a noun with A-(으)ㄴ N (작은 가방 · 예쁜 옷)",id:"chapter-21-b7",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Finding the right bag",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Finding the right bag",chunks:["민서는 출근할 때 들기 좋은 가방을 찾고 있었어요. 직원에게 어제 본 작은 가방이 아직 있는지 물어봤어요. 직원은 그 가방이 손님들이 많이 찾는 새로운 모델이라고 설명했어요.","안에는 필요한 물건이 꽤 많이 들어가고, 디자인도 예뻤어요. 민서는 바쁜 아침에도 들고 다니기 편한 가방이 필요했기 때문에 그 가방을 다시 한번 확인했어요."],translation:"Minseo was looking for a bag that would be good to carry to work. She asked whether the small bag she saw yesterday was still available. The staff explained that it was a new model customers often ask for, and Minseo checked it again because she needed a comfortable bag for busy mornings.",qas:[{q:"민서는 어떤 가방을 찾고 있었어요?",a:"출근할 때 들기 좋은 가방을 찾고 있었어요."},{q:"직원은 그 가방을 어떤 모델이라고 설명했어요?",a:"손님들이 많이 찾는 새로운 모델이라고 설명했어요."},{q:"민서는 왜 그 가방을 다시 확인했어요?",a:"바쁜 아침에도 들고 다니기 편한 가방이 필요했기 때문이에요."}]}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a current or habitual action with V-는 N (찾는 가방)",id:"chapter-21-b8",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"In a shop, which phrase identifies the item most clearly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어제 본 작은 가방",ok:!0},{text:"어제 봐요 가방",ok:!1},{text:"가방 작은 어제",ok:!1}],explanation:"Korean modifiers come before the noun they describe."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which phrase uses V-는 N correctly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"손님이 많이 찾는 모델",ok:!0},{text:"손님이 많이 찾아요 모델",ok:!1},{text:"모델 손님이 많이 찾는",ok:!1}],explanation:"찾는 modifies 모델 with an action-based description."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"작은 가방___ 찾고 있어요. (I am looking for a small bag.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"찾다 is an action, and 작은 가방 is what you are looking for, so it takes 을. 이 would make the bag the subject instead."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'the book I am reading'",tokens:["제가","읽는","책"],correct:"제가 읽는 책",explanation:"Present action modifiers use V-는 before the noun. 읽다 becomes 읽는 when it describes 책."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which phrase means “the bag I saw yesterday”?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어제 본 가방",ok:!0},{text:"어제 보는 가방",ok:!1},{text:"가방 어제 봤어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"A completed action before a noun uses V-(으)ㄴ, so 보다 becomes 본. 가방 어제 봤어요 is a full sentence, not a noun phrase."}],canDo:"Describe a noun by a finished action with V-(으)ㄴ N (어제 본 영화)",id:"chapter-21-b9",chapterId:"chapter-21",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-22",number:22,title:"N을/를 좋아해요 · N보다 더",goal:"Use N을/를 좋아해요 for personal preferences: 커피를 좋아해요.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 좋아하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"좋아하다",romanization:"joahada",en:"to like",pos:"verb",nuance:"좋아하다 is an action verb meaning 'to like / be fond of', so it describes a person actively having a preference and takes the object marker 을/를: 커피를 좋아해요. Contrast it with the adjective 좋다 ('to be good/nice'), which describes the thing itself and takes 이/가: 커피가 좋아요 ('coffee is nice / I feel good about coffee'). The dividing line is who is in the spotlight — 좋아하다 puts the liker on stage, 좋다 puts the liked thing on stage. Be careful with 사랑하다 ('to love'): Koreans reserve it for deep romantic or familial love and rarely say it about food or hobbies, where English happily uses 'love' — so 'I love coffee' is 커피를 좋아해요, not 커피를 사랑해요. As a 하다 verb it conjugates regularly: 하다 → 해요, giving 좋아해요 / 좋아합니다 / 좋아했어요. The classic English-speaker pitfall is copying English subject logic and saying 커피가 좋아해요 with 이/가; once it is a 좋아하다 sentence, the food must take 을/를."},sentence:{ko:"저는 한국 음식을 좋아해요.",en:"I like Korean food."},target:"좋아해",options:["to choose","more","to like"],note:"좋아하다 takes the liked item with 을/를."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"싫어하다",romanization:"sireohada",en:"to dislike",pos:"verb",nuance:"싫어하다 is the action verb 'to dislike / hate' and, like its opposite 좋아하다, takes the object marker 을/를: 벌레를 싫어해요. Its adjective partner is 싫다 ('to be unpleasant / not want to'), which takes 이/가 and points at the thing or the feeling itself: 벌레가 싫어요. The verb describes a person's standing dislike, while 싫다 often voices an in-the-moment 'ugh, I don't want to'. The near-synonym to watch is 미워하다, which is 'to resent/hate a person' with an emotional, almost grudge-like color — you 미워하다 someone who hurt you, but you 싫어하다 cold weather or homework. Register matters most here: 싫어해요 is socially strong, so saying 그 사람을 싫어해요 to someone's face is quite blunt; Koreans habitually soften with 별로 ('not really') or simply switch to 별로 안 좋아해요. As a 하다 verb it is regular: 하다 → 해요 (싫어해요 / 싫어합니다 / 싫어했어요). Pitfall: marking the object with 이/가 (벌레가 싫어해요) by mirroring English — keep 을/를 with the verb."},sentence:{ko:"저는 너무 단 커피를 싫어해요.",en:"I dislike coffee that is too sweet."},target:"싫어해",options:["to choose","more","to dislike"],note:"싫어하다 follows the same object pattern as 좋아하다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"더",romanization:"deo",en:"more",pos:"adverb",nuance:"더 is the basic comparative marker meaning 'more,' and crucially Korean has no '-er/more...than' suffix — you simply put 더 before the adjective and (optionally) mark the standard of comparison with the particle 보다: A가 B보다 더 크다 = 'A is bigger than B.' Its direct antonym is 덜 ('less'). A common booster is 훨씬 ('by far, much'), which stacks in front: 훨씬 더 좋다 = 'much better.' Watch two set phrases: 더 이상 + negative = 'no longer / not anymore' (더 이상 못 참아 = 'I can't take it anymore'), and 더 ~ -ㄹ수록 patterns for 'the more...'. The English-speaker pitfall: learners often drop 더 entirely because they expect the adjective itself to change form ('big' → 'bigger'), but 크다 never inflects for comparison — without 더 the sentence just says 'is big,' not 'is bigger.' Another trap is the 보다 direction: 보다 attaches to the thing you compare AGAINST (the 'than' item), so '내가 너보다 커' = 'I'm taller than you,' which feels backwards to English ears. Also don't confuse spoken 더 with the homophone-ish 덜; they are opposites."},sentence:{ko:"저는 차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee more than tea."},target:"더",options:["to like","to dislike","more"],note:"더 marks the stronger side of the comparison."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"제일",romanization:"jeil",en:"the most",pos:"adverb",nuance:"제일 means 'the most / number one' and is the casual, high-frequency spoken twin of 가장. It is Sino-Korean 第一 — literally 'order-one,' i.e. 'first/foremost' — which is why besides modifying adjectives (제일 크다) it can also stand alone like a noun meaning 'the best/top': 건강이 제일이다 = 'health comes first,' 네가 제일이야 = 'you're number one.' 가장 is its near-perfect synonym for the adverb use, the difference being register only: 제일 dominates everyday conversation while 가장 feels a bit more formal/written. Both pick the top of a group of three or more, with the field marked by 중에/에서. The English-speaker pitfall: as with 가장, don't use a superlative for just two items (use 더 instead), and don't glue 제일 onto the noun — 'the cheapest restaurant' is 제일 싼 식당, with 제일 modifying 싼. A further nuance learners miss: because 제일 can be nominal (第一 = 'the first/best'), set expressions like 제일이다 and 제일가다 ('to be foremost') exist, so 그게 제일이야 = 'that's the best option,' not literally 'that is most.' Keep 제일 for talk and texts; switch to 가장 in essays, reports, and formal announcements."},sentence:{ko:"저는 이 카페 커피를 제일 좋아해요.",en:"I like this cafe's coffee the most."},target:"제일",options:["to like","the most","to dislike"],note:"Use 제일 when choosing the top option."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"만큼",romanization:"mankeum",en:"as much as; as ... as",pos:"comparison pattern piece",nuance:"만큼 expresses an equal degree, amount, or extent: 친구만큼 빨라요 (‘as fast as my friend’), 필요한 만큼 가져가세요 (‘take as much as needed’), 생각만큼 어렵지 않아요 (‘not as hard as expected’). After a noun it functions like a particle and is attached: 친구만큼, 이만큼. After a modifying verb/adjective clause it behaves as a dependent noun and is spaced: 노력한 만큼, 먹고 싶은 만큼. Unlike 보다, which marks the comparison standard for ‘more/less than,’ 만큼 says the degree is equal or reaches that extent."},sentence:{ko:"생각만큼 비싸지 않아요.",en:"It is not as expensive as I thought."},target:"만큼",options:["to like","to dislike","as much as; as ... as"],note:"Practiced through pattern-041, not as a fake standalone word card."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보다",romanization:"boda",en:"than",pos:"comparison particle",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee more than tea."},target:"보다",options:["than","to like","to dislike"],note:"Comparison 보다 attaches to the baseline; it is different from the verb 보다, to see."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"저는 차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee more than tea."},hl:"보다"}],canDo:"Say what I like with the object marker, N을/를 좋아해요 (음악을 좋아해요)",id:"chapter-22-b1",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N을/를 좋아해요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N을/를 좋아해요",sub:"I like N",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"커피",mid:"를",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"좋아해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"저는 커피를 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee.",romanization:"jeo-neun keo-pi-reul jo-a-hae-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"한국",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"음식",mid:"을",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"좋아해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"한국 음식을 좋아해요.",en:"I like Korean food.",romanization:"han-guk eum-si-geul jo-a-hae-yo."}],rule:{name:"N을/를 좋아해요",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 을 좋아해요",ex:"음악 → 음악을 좋아해요"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 를 좋아해요",ex:"커피 → 커피를 좋아해요"},{when:"Casual speech (particle often dropped)",add:"drop 을/를",ex:"커피 좋아해요"}]},more:{func:'Use 좋아하다 to say you like something or someone. It is an action verb ("to like"), not the adjective 좋다 ("to be good"), so the thing you like is the object and takes 을/를, not 이/가.',keyPoint:{label:"좋다 vs 좋아하다",body:'좋다 is an adjective and takes 이/가: 커피가 좋아요 ("coffee is good to me"). 좋아하다 is a verb and takes 을/를: 커피를 좋아해요. Both can translate as "I like coffee," but the particle follows the word you actually use. For now, just lock in 을/를 with 좋아해요.'},pronunciation:"좋아해요 sounds like [조아해요] — the ㅎ in 좋 drops before the vowel, so don't say [졷아해요]."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I like Korean.",sentence:"저는 한국어___.",options:[{text:"를 좋아해요",ok:!0},{text:"을 좋아해요",ok:!1},{text:"좋아해요",ok:!1}],explanation:"좋아하다 takes the object with 을/를, never 이/가."}],canDo:"Say what I like with the object marker, N을/를 좋아해요 (음악을 좋아해요)",id:"chapter-22-b2",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 취향~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취향",romanization:"chwihyang",en:"taste; personal preference",pos:"noun",nuance:"취향 is subjective. 좋아해요 states a preference, while 취향 names the style or taste behind that preference."},sentence:{ko:"이 카페는 제 취향이에요.",en:"This cafe is my taste."},target:"취향",options:["food","taste; personal preference","movie"],note:"취향 names the personal taste behind a preference."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취미",romanization:"chwimi",en:"hobby",pos:"noun",nuance:"취미 is Sino-Korean, 趣味 ('interest-taste'), and means a hobby or personal interest. It famously opens small talk: 취미가 뭐예요? — and note it takes 가 there (existence/identity), so a frequent error is ❌취미를 있어요 instead of 취미가 있어요, because 있다 ('to have/exist') is not a transitive verb taking 을/를. To turn an activity into a named hobby, Korean nominalizes the verb with -기: 보다 → 영화 보기 ('movie-watching'), 모으다 → 우표 모으기 ('stamp-collecting'); answering 제 취미는 영화 보기예요 is the model pattern. Distinguish 취미 from 관심: 관심 is broader 'interest/attention' (관심이 있어요 = I'm interested in it), whereas 취미 is specifically a leisure activity you do for fun. The English-speaker pitfall is forcing 있다 into a 'have'-with-object frame (취미를 있어요) — use 취미가 있어요; and don't say ❌취미를 해요 for 'do a hobby' — name the activity itself (영화를 봐요) or say 취미를 즐겨요."},sentence:{ko:"취미가 뭐예요?",en:"What is your hobby?"},target:"취미",options:["movie","hobby","music"],note:"A natural topic for 좋아해요 and 비교하다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"음식",romanization:"eumsik",en:"food",pos:"noun",nuance:"음식 is the general word for food or cuisine. It combines naturally with Korean, spicy, delicious, and eat."},sentence:{ko:"어떤 음식을 제일 좋아해요?",en:"What food do you like the most?"},target:"음식",options:["food","taste; personal preference","movie"],note:"Food is one of the safest preference topics."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"영화",romanization:"yeonghwa",en:"movie",pos:"noun",nuance:"영화 is Sino-Korean, 映畵 ('project-picture'), literally a projected image, and it is the standard word for a movie/film. It pairs with 보다 ('see/watch'), never 듣다 ('listen'), because film is something you view. A single film is counted with the bound noun 편 (영화 두 편 = two movies), not 개. Don't confuse 영화 with 드라마: 드라마 is a serialized TV series, while 영화 is a self-contained cinema film, so a Korean TV soap is a 드라마, not a 영화. The cinema building is 영화관 (or the loanword 극장). The classic English-speaker pitfall is letting 'a movie' steer you to the generic counter 개 (영화 한 개 sounds wrong) — use 편; and don't calque 'go to a movie' as 영화에 가다, since you say 영화(를) 보러 가다, 'go in order to watch a movie'."},sentence:{ko:"저는 액션 영화보다 코미디 영화를 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like comedy movies more than action movies."},target:"영화",options:["taste; personal preference","movie","hobby"],note:"Good for practicing 보다 더."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"음악",romanization:"eumak",en:"music",pos:"noun",nuance:"음악 is Sino-Korean, 音樂 ('sound-pleasure/joy'), and is the general word for music. Its everyday partner verb is 듣다 ('hear/listen'), which is a ㄷ-irregular: before a vowel the ㄷ becomes ㄹ, so 듣 + 어요 → 들어요 and 듣 + 으면 → 들으면 — memorize this because beginners often write the wrong ❌들다. Distinguish 음악 from 노래: 노래 is a specific 'song' you can sing (with words and a tune), while 음악 is music as a whole, including instrumental pieces, so background café music is 음악, not 노래. Two near-verbs differ too — 듣다 is to receive sound, but 틀다 is to switch a track on from a device. The English-speaker pitfall is using 보다 ('watch') because you 'watch a music video' in English; for plain audio say 음악을 들어요, and avoid the false stem 들다 — the dictionary form is 듣다."},sentence:{ko:"저는 운동보다 음악 듣는 걸 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like listening to music more than exercising."},target:"음악",options:["taste; personal preference","music","hobby"],note:"Activities often become -는 걸 phrases."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"커피",romanization:"keopi",en:"coffee",pos:"noun",nuance:"In speech, 커피 마셔요 without 를 is also natural, but learn the full frame first."},sentence:{ko:"커피를 차보다 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee more than tea."},target:"커피",options:["taste; personal preference","hobby","coffee"],note:"커피 is a practical object for likes and cafe comparisons."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"커피랑 차 중에 뭐가 더 좋아요?",en:"Between coffee and tea, which do you like more?"},hl:"커피"}],canDo:"Compare two things with N보다 더 (커피보다 더 좋아요)",id:"chapter-22-b3",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N보다 더",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N보다 더",sub:"more than N",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"어제",mid:"보다",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"더",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"추워요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"오늘이 어제보다 더 추워요.",en:"Today is colder than yesterday.",romanization:"o-neu-ri eo-je-bo-da deo chu-wo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"저",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화",mid:"보다",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"더",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"재미있어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이 영화가 저 영화보다 더 재미있어요.",en:"This movie is more fun than that one.",romanization:"i yeong-hwa-ga jeo yeong-hwa-bo-da deo jae-mi-i-sseo-yo."}],rule:{name:"N보다 더",rows:[{when:"Baseline noun (what you compare against)",add:"+ 보다",ex:"차 → 차보다"},{when:"Add 더 before the adjective/verb",add:"N보다 + 더 + Adj/Verb",ex:"차보다 더 좋아요"},{when:'For "less," use 덜 instead of 더',add:"N보다 + 덜 + Adj",ex:"어제보다 덜 추워요"}]},more:{func:`Attach 보다 to the thing you're comparing against (the baseline) to mean "more than N." 더 ("more") usually comes right before the adjective or verb. 더 is optional, but adding it makes the comparison clear.`,keyPoint:{label:"보다 marks the baseline, not the winner",body:'In "A is more than B," 보다 goes on B — the thing you measure against — not on the one that wins: A이/가 B보다 더 ~. English speakers often want to put "than" on the smaller side, but in Korean 보다 always sits on the baseline noun. 더 is optional and just makes "more" explicit.'},pronunciation:""}}],canDo:"Compare two things with N보다 더 (커피보다 더 좋아요)",id:"chapter-22-b4",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 차~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"차",romanization:"cha",en:"tea",pos:"noun",nuance:"차 (茶) is the Sino-Korean word for 'tea,' the brewed leaf or herbal drink, and because it is a beverage it must pair with 마시다 (to drink), never 먹다 (to eat) — though casual speakers do say 차를 먹다 colloquially, learners should default to 마시다. The same syllable 차 (車) also means 'car/vehicle,' so only context separates 차를 마시다 (drink tea) from 차를 타다 (ride a car); they are written identically. Unlike English 'tea,' Korean 차 covers herbal and grain infusions too, so 보리차 (barley tea) and 옥수수차 (corn tea) are 차 even with no tea leaves. A common English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 먹다 by analogy with 'have some tea,' but in standard Korean a polite host says 차 드세요 (드시다 being the honorific of 마시다/먹다). Natural collocations include 차를 우리다 (steep tea) and 차 한 잔 하다 (literally 'do one cup of tea,' meaning to chat over tea)."},sentence:{ko:"오늘은 커피보다 차가 더 좋아요.",en:"Today tea sounds better than coffee."},target:"차",options:["taste; personal preference","tea","hobby"],note:"Use 차 as a natural comparison partner for coffee."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"운동",romanization:"undong",en:"exercise",pos:"noun",nuance:"운동 is Sino-Korean, 運動 ('move-motion'), and is the umbrella word for physical exercise; it normally appears as 운동하다 (운동을 해요). Crucially it is NOT counted or pluralized like English 'a workout' — you don't say 운동들 for several sessions; you say 운동을 자주 해요. The nearest relative is 스포츠 (a borrowed word for organized sports as a category, e.g. watching 스포츠 on TV), whereas 운동 is the act of exercising your own body, so '운동선수' = athlete but '스포츠 경기' = a sports match. The very same 運動 also means a social 'movement/campaign' (민주화 운동 the democracy movement), so context decides. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 만들다 ('make') by analogy with 'make exercise' — Korean uses the light verb 하다; and remember the desire ending goes on the verb (운동하고 싶어요), never on the bare noun."},sentence:{ko:"친구만큼 운동을 좋아해요.",en:"I like exercise as much as my friend does."},target:"운동",options:["taste; personal preference","exercise","food"],note:"Useful with 만큼 and 좋아하다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비교하다",romanization:"bigyohada",en:"to compare",pos:"verb",nuance:"비교하다 is the Sino-Korean verb 比較(비교, 'compare-contrast') + 하다, meaning to set two or more things side by side to weigh their differences. The thing(s) compared take 을/를, and the second item of a pair takes 와/과 (formal) or 하고/랑 (casual): A를 B와 비교하다. Being a 하다 verb it conjugates to 해요 (never 하요) and 했어요. Distinguish it from 대조하다 (對照, 'contrast'), which highlights opposites for academic effect, while 비교하다 is the neutral everyday word ('compare prices, compare two phones'). The big English-speaker pitfall is the particle: learners copy English 'compare A to B' and say 가격에 비교해요, but the object marker must be 을/를 — 가격을 비교해요. Note too the social weight of 비교당하다 ('to be compared', passive): Koreans dislike being measured against others, so 비교하지 마 ('stop comparing me') is emotionally loaded."},sentence:{ko:"가격과 취향을 비교해 봐요.",en:"Let's compare the price and our preferences."},target:"비교해",options:["to choose","more","to compare"],note:"Use 비교하다 for deliberate comparison."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"선택하다",romanization:"seontaekada",en:"to choose",pos:"verb",nuance:"선택하다 is 選擇(선택, 'select-pick') + 하다 and means to pick a defined option from a presented set — menus, app settings, multiple-choice answers, life paths. It is the bookish, neutral register; in spoken everyday Korean the native verb 고르다 ('pick out') is warmer and more common ('과일을 골랐어요'). Crucially, contrast it with 결정하다 (決定, 'decide'): 선택하다 is choosing one option among several that already exist, while 결정하다 is settling a matter or reaching a conclusion ('유학을 결정했어요' = decided to study abroad). As a 하다 verb it gives 선택해요 / 선택했어요. The English-speaker pitfall is the particle and the 고르다 split: say 메뉴를 선택해요 with 을/를 (not 메뉴에), and don't force 선택하다 into casual chat where a native would simply say 골라요. Also, 'choose to do X' is not 선택하다 alone — Korean prefers ~기로 하다/결심하다."},sentence:{ko:"저는 커피를 선택했어요.",en:"I chose coffee."},target:"선택했",options:["to compare","to choose","more"],note:"A practical verb after comparing options."}],canDo:"Say something is as much / not as much as another with N만큼 (커피만큼)",id:"chapter-22-b5",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N만큼",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N만큼",sub:"as much as N / as Adj as N",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"카페",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"라떼",mid:"만큼",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"맛있는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"커피는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이 카페 라떼만큼 맛있는 커피는 없어요.",en:"There's no coffee as tasty as this cafe's latte.",romanization:"i ka-pe rat-te-man-keum ma-sin-neun keo-pi-neun eop-seo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"생각",mid:"만큼",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"비싸지",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"않아요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"생각만큼 비싸지 않아요.",en:"It's not as expensive as I thought.",romanization:"saeng-gang-man-keum bi-ssa-ji a-na-yo."}],rule:{name:"N만큼",rows:[{when:"Baseline noun (the level you match)",add:"+ 만큼",ex:"생각 → 생각만큼"},{when:"With an adjective (equal degree)",add:"N만큼 + Adj",ex:"라떼만큼 맛있어요"},{when:"Very common with a negative",add:"N만큼 + Adj/Verb + 안/-지 않다",ex:"생각만큼 안 비싸요"}]},more:{func:`Attach 만큼 to a noun to mean "as much as N" or "to the same degree as N." It marks an equal level in a comparison, and the adjective or verb comes after it. It's very common in "not as ... as I expected" sentences with 생각.`,keyPoint:{label:"만큼 (equal) vs 보다 (more)",body:'Use 보다 for "more than" — one side wins: 친구보다 잘해요 = better than my friend. Use 만큼 for "as much as" — the two are equal: 친구만큼 잘해요 = as well as my friend. Swapping them turns "the same" into "more," so keep them apart.'},pronunciation:"라떼만큼 is straightforward, but 책만큼 sounds like [챙만큼] — the ㄱ in 책 turns into [ㅇ] before the nasal ㅁ."}}],canDo:"Say something is as much / not as much as another with N만큼 (커피만큼)",id:"chapter-22-b6",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Friends choose a cafe drink and compare their preferences without mixing 좋아하다, 좋다, 보다, and 만큼.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"커피랑 차 중에 뭐가 더 좋아요?",en:"Between coffee and tea, which do you like more?",romanization:"keopirang cha junge mwoga deo joayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"저는 차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요.",en:"I like coffee more than tea.",romanization:"jeoneun chaboda keopireul deo joahaeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 카페 라떼만큼 맛있는 커피가 또 있어요?",en:"Is there another coffee as tasty as this cafe's latte?",romanization:"i kape rattemankeum masinneun keopiga tto isseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아직 못 찾았어요. 여기가 제 취향에 제일 맞아요.",en:"I have not found one yet. This place fits my taste the best.",romanization:"ajik mot chajasseoyo. yeogiga je chwihyange jeil majayo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I like coffee more than tea.' (Start with 차보다.)",tokens:["차보다","커피를","더 좋아해요"],correct:"차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요",explanation:"N보다 sets the comparison baseline. 더 좋아해요 says the second option is preferred more."}],canDo:"Say what I like with the object marker, N을/를 좋아해요 (음악을 좋아해요)",id:"chapter-22-b7",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Choosing by taste",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Choosing by taste",chunks:["민서와 지훈은 카페에서 음료를 고르고 있었어요. 민서는 차가 깔끔해서 좋다고 했지만, 지훈은 차보다 커피를 더 좋아한다고 했어요. 특히 이 카페 라떼는 생각만큼 비싸지 않고 맛도 좋아서 지훈의 취향에 잘 맞았어요.","민서는 커피와 차를 비교해 본 뒤에 오늘은 차를 선택했어요. 두 사람은 취향이 조금 달라도 서로의 선택을 자연스럽게 이해했어요."],translation:"Minseo and Jihoon were choosing drinks at a cafe. Minseo liked tea because it felt clean, while Jihoon liked coffee more than tea. This cafe's latte was not as expensive as he expected and fit his taste well. After comparing coffee and tea, Minseo chose tea today.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 차보다 무엇을 더 좋아해요?",a:"커피를 더 좋아해요."},{q:"이 카페 라떼는 생각만큼 비쌌어요?",a:"아니요, 생각만큼 비싸지 않았어요."},{q:"민서는 무엇을 선택했어요?",a:"차를 선택했어요."}]}],canDo:"Compare two things with N보다 더 (커피보다 더 좋아요)",id:"chapter-22-b8",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly uses 좋아하다?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"커피를 좋아해요.",ok:!0},{text:"커피가 좋아해요.",ok:!1},{text:"커피에 좋아해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"좋아하다 is a verb, so the liked item takes 을/를."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You like coffee more than tea. Which sentence is correct?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요.",ok:!0},{text:"커피보다 차를 더 좋아해요.",ok:!1},{text:"차만큼 커피를 더 좋아해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"보다 attaches to the baseline, tea."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"커피___ 좋아해요. (I like coffee.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"좋아하다 is a verb, so the thing liked takes 을/를. 커피가 좋아요 is a different adjective sentence meaning coffee is good."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I like coffee more than tea.' (Start with 차보다.)",tokens:["차보다","커피를","더 좋아해요"],correct:"차보다 커피를 더 좋아해요",explanation:"N보다 sets the comparison baseline. 더 좋아해요 says the second option is preferred more."}],canDo:"Say something is as much / not as much as another with N만큼 (커피만큼)",id:"chapter-22-b9",chapterId:"chapter-22",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-23",number:23,title:"N(으)로 · V-(으)러 가요/와요",goal:"Use N(으)로 for direction or transport method: 오른쪽으로, 지하철로.",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 지하철~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지하철",romanization:"jihacheol",en:"subway",pos:"noun",nuance:"지하철 is the subway system; 지하철역 is the subway station."},sentence:{ko:"지하철로 가는 게 빨라요.",en:"Going by subway is fast."},target:"지하철",options:["subway","exit","right side"],note:"Use 지하철로 for 'by subway' and 지하철을 타요 for boarding."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"버스",romanization:"beoseu",en:"bus",pos:"noun",nuance:"버스 is useful with 타다, 기다리다, 내리다, and 로/으로 for transportation method."},sentence:{ko:"버스로 가면 조금 오래 걸려요.",en:"If you go by bus, it takes a little long."},target:"버스",options:["station","exit","bus"],note:"버스로 marks travel method."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"택시",romanization:"taeksi",en:"taxi",pos:"noun",nuance:"택시로 가요 and 택시를 타요 are both natural, but they answer different questions: method versus boarding action."},sentence:{ko:"시간이 없으면 택시로 가세요.",en:"If you do not have time, go by taxi."},target:"택시",options:["taxi","station","exit"],note:"택시로 means by taxi."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"역",romanization:"yeok",en:"station",pos:"noun",nuance:"역 (驛, 'relay station / post-horse stage') means a train or subway station and almost always attaches to a place or line name — 서울역, 강남역, 부산역. It is the everyday navigation anchor of Korean city life, with people meeting and giving directions by 출구 (exit) number. Distinguish it from 정류장 (停留場), which is a *bus stop* — buses stop at a 버스 정류장, not a 역, so don't mix them. The historical hanja 驛 refers to old relay-horse posts, now repurposed for rail. Beware two look-alikes: 역할 (役割, 'role') and the particle/adverb 역시 ('as expected') share the syllable 역 but are unrelated words. The reliable English-speaker error is particles: 역에 가요 for going to the station (에 = destination) but 역에서 만나요 / 기차를 타요 for an action at the station (에서); never say 역에서 가요 when you mean 'go there'."},sentence:{ko:"역에서 2호선으로 갈아타세요.",en:"Transfer to Line 2 at the station."},target:"역",options:["bus","taxi","station"],note:"The station where an action happens takes 에서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"정류장",romanization:"jeongnyujang",en:"bus stop",pos:"noun",nuance:"정류장 is the stop itself. 역 is a station, usually larger and often for subway or train."},sentence:{ko:"버스 타러 정류장에 가요.",en:"I go to the bus stop to take the bus."},target:"정류장",options:["taxi","station","bus stop"],note:"정류장에 is the destination; 타러 is the purpose."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"출구",romanization:"chulgu",en:"exit",pos:"noun",nuance:"출구 is 出口 — 出 (go out) + 口 (opening), literally the 'exit-mouth', the way you leave a building or station; in the subway each is numbered (몇 번 출구), so 'meet at exit 7' is everyday Korean. It is the exact opposite of 입구 (入口, entrance), and the shared 口 'opening' character ties the 出/入 pair together. Distinguish 출구 from 비상구 (非常口, 'emergency exit'), the marked fire-escape door — a normal way out is a 출구, the emergency one is a 비상구. To leave through it you say 출구로 나가다, where 으로 marks the exit as your route. English speakers blur 출구 and 입구 because both end in 구; fix the memory with 출 = out, 입 = in. And note exits are counted with the native-Sino number + 번 (삼 번 출구), not with English ordinals."},sentence:{ko:"삼 번 출구로 나가세요.",en:"Go out through exit three."},target:"출구",options:["exit","subway","bus"],note:"출구로 marks the route out."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"지하철로 가는 게 빨라요, 버스로 가는 게 빨라요?",en:"Is it faster to go by subway or by bus?"},hl:"지하철"}],canDo:"Say how I travel and which way to go (지하철로, 2번 출구로)",id:"chapter-23-b1",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N(으)로",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N(으)로",sub:"by / toward / through (means, direction, route)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"버스",mid:"로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"저는 버스로 학교에 가요.",en:"I go to school by bus.",romanization:"jeo-neun beo-seu-ro hak-gyo-e ga-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이쪽",mid:"으로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"오세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이쪽으로 오세요.",en:"Come this way.",romanization:"i-jjog-eu-ro o-se-yo."}],rule:{name:"N(으)로",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"+ 으로",ex:"집 → 집으로 (toward home), 오른쪽 → 오른쪽으로 (to the right)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 로",ex:"버스 → 버스로 (by bus), 학교 → 학교로 (toward school)"},{when:"Noun ends in ㄹ",add:"+ 로 (like a vowel)",ex:"지하철 → 지하철로 (by subway), 서울 → 서울로 (toward Seoul)"}]},more:{func:"N(으)로 marks how you travel (by bus, by subway), the direction you head (this way, toward home), or the path you go through (out that door). It points at the way you go, not the exact spot you end up.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)로 vs -에 (direction vs the place you reach)",body:"-에 names the place you arrive at: 학교에 가요 = go to school (you end up there). -(으)로 names the direction you head or the way you go: 학교로 가요 = go toward / in the direction of school. Use -(으)로 when the point is which way, and -에 when the point is the place you reach."},pronunciation:"After a noun ending in ㄹ, just add 로 with no 으, and the two ㄹ sounds run together: 지하철 + 로 → [지하철로]. Don't say 지하철으로."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I go home by subway.",sentence:"지하철___ 집에 가요.",options:[{text:"로",ok:!0},{text:"으로",ok:!1}],explanation:"지하철 ends in ㄹ, so it takes plain 로 (not 으로)."}],canDo:"Say how I travel and which way to go (지하철로, 2번 출구로)",id:"chapter-23-b2",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 오른쪽~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"오른쪽",romanization:"oreunjjok",en:"right side",pos:"noun",nuance:"오른쪽 is the native compound noun for the right side or rightward direction, from 오른- ('right') + 쪽 ('side/direction'); it attaches to a noun (문 오른쪽) or pairs with a direction particle for motion (오른쪽으로 = to the right). The -쪽 piece is shared with 왼쪽/위쪽/앞쪽, all meaning 'the ~ side'. Its opposite is 왼쪽 (left), and mixing the two is the top direction-giving mistake — anchor 오른 firmly to 'right'. The formal/Sino-Korean counterpart is 우 (右), found in signage like 우회전 (right turn) and the set phrase 좌우 (左右, left-and-right), while 오른쪽 is the everyday spoken form. Register: 오른쪽 is conversational, 우 is official/written. English-speaker pitfalls: (1) 오른쪽에서 있어요 for existence instead of 오른쪽에 있어요; (2) confusing 오른쪽 with 왼쪽; and (3) using 오른쪽 by itself for 'turn right' — the direction particle is required: 오른쪽으로 도세요 / 오른쪽으로 가세요, not 오른쪽 도세요."},sentence:{ko:"오른쪽으로 가세요.",en:"Go to the right."},target:"오른쪽",options:["subway","right side","bus"],note:"Direction nouns take 으로."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"왼쪽",romanization:"oenjjok",en:"left side",pos:"noun",nuance:"왼쪽 is the native compound noun for the left side or leftward direction, built from 왼- ('left') + 쪽 ('side/direction'); it attaches to a noun (문 왼쪽) or stands with a direction particle for movement (왼쪽으로 = to the left). The -쪽 element is the same one in 위쪽/아래쪽/앞쪽, marking 'the ~ side'. Its direct opposite is 오른쪽 (right), and confusing the two is the single most common error when giving directions — practice the pair until 왼 reliably means left. Note also the more formal/literary 좌 (左, Sino-Korean) seen in 좌회전 (left turn, on road signs) versus everyday spoken 왼쪽. Register: 왼쪽 is the normal conversational word; 좌 appears in fixed/official terms. English-speaker pitfalls: (1) 왼쪽에서 있어요 for existence instead of 왼쪽에 있어요; (2) swapping 왼쪽 and 오른쪽 under pressure; and (3) using 왼쪽 alone for 'turn left' (왼쪽 도세요 is incomplete) — you need the direction particle: 왼쪽으로 도세요 / 왼쪽으로 가세요."},sentence:{ko:"출구에서 왼쪽으로 가세요.",en:"Go left from the exit."},target:"왼쪽",options:["subway","bus","left side"],note:"왼쪽으로 marks movement direction."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"직진",romanization:"jikjin",en:"going straight ahead",pos:"noun",nuance:"직진 is Sino-Korean, 直進, literally 'straight-advance' (直 straight + 進 to go forward) — proceeding straight ahead without turning, the third member of the navigation trio with 우회전 and 좌회전. Spoken GPS commands chain them: 직진하다가 좌회전 ('go straight, then turn left'). Its everyday spoken cousins on foot are 쭉 가세요 or 똑바로 가세요 ('go straight on'), so like its turn-siblings, 직진 itself leans toward driving and GPS speech; saying 직진하세요 to someone walking sounds a little mechanical. It pairs with 하다 (직진하다), 계속, 신호, 차선. The English-speaker pitfall is the verb: 직진 is a noun, so 'go straight' is 직진하다, not 직진 가다 — and don't substitute the bare verb 가다 for it. Also, a 직진 차선 (through lane) legally forbids turning, a nuance the plain English 'straight' doesn't carry, so read lane markings before assuming you can turn."},sentence:{ko:"이 길로 계속 직진하세요.",en:"Keep going straight on this road."},target:"직진",options:["subway","going straight ahead","bus"],note:"Often used in directions with 길로."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"환승",romanization:"hwanseung",en:"transfer",pos:"noun",nuance:"환승 means transferring between buses and subways. If you tap the same transit card, transfers within a time limit are usually discounted or free."},sentence:{ko:"시청역에서 환승해요.",en:"I transfer at City Hall Station."},target:"환승",options:["subway","transfer","bus"],note:"Official transit word; 갈아타다 is the everyday verb."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"갈아타다",romanization:"garata-da",en:"to transfer",pos:"verb",nuance:"갈아타다 is a transparent compound of 갈다 'to change/replace' + 타다 'to ride', so it literally means 'change-ride' — you get off one vehicle and board a different one. The dividing line learners must feel is against plain 타다 (just 'to board/get on') and against 환승하다, the Sino-Korean twin (換乘, 換 'exchange' + 乘 'ride', literally 'change-ride') printed on every subway sign and announcement: 갈아타다 is the warm spoken word a friend uses ('여기서 갈아타'), while 환승하다 is the signage/official register ('환승 게이트'). The grammar trap is the particle: the vehicle you move TO takes 로/으로 (지하철로 갈아타다), the same directional 로 as 'change INTO', and the station where you do it takes 에서 — English 'transfer to the subway' tempts you to drop the particle or use 에. Because 타다 is regular, every form is regular too: the stem 타- already ends in ㅏ, so the 아 of 아요 merges in and disappears, giving 갈아타요 (not 갈아타아요), and the past is 갈아탔어요. Habitual collocations: 다른 노선으로 갈아타다, 같은 방향으로 갈아타다."},sentence:{ko:"2호선으로 갈아타세요.",en:"Transfer to Line 2."},target:"갈아타",options:["to transfer","to ride; to take","subway"],note:"The line or vehicle you transfer into takes 으로/로."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"타다",romanization:"tada",en:"to ride; to take",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 타다 for getting on or riding transportation: 버스를 타요, 지하철을 타요."},sentence:{ko:"버스를 타러 정류장에 가요.",en:"I go to the bus stop to take the bus."},target:null,options:["to ride; to take","to transfer","subway"],note:"The vehicle you board takes 을/를."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"이 길로 직진하고 오른쪽으로 가세요.",en:"Go straight on this road and go right."},hl:"오른쪽"}],canDo:"Tell someone the purpose of a trip with V-(으)러 가요 (밥 먹으러 가요)",id:"chapter-23-b3",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)러 가요/와요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)러 가요/와요",sub:"go/come to do something",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 으러",ex:"먹다 → 먹으러 (가요), 찾다 → 찾으러 (가요)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ 러",ex:"보다 → 보러 (가요), 사다 → 사러 (가요)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"+ 러 (like a vowel)",ex:"놀다 → 놀러 (가요), 만들다 → 만들러 (가요)"}],examples:[{ko:"점심을 먹으러 식당에 가요.",en:"I'm going to the restaurant to eat lunch.",romanization:"jeom-sim-eul meog-eu-reo sik-dang-e ga-yo.",note:"먹다 ends in a consonant → 먹으러. The thing you go to do comes first, then 가요."},{ko:"친구를 만나러 학교에 왔어요.",en:"I came to school to meet a friend.",romanization:"chin-gu-reul man-na-reo hak-gyo-e wass-eo-yo.",note:"만나다 ends in a vowel → 만나러. Here the movement verb is 오다 (왔어요)."}],more:{func:`V-(으)러 tags onto an action verb to say why you're going or coming, and it's always followed by a movement verb like 가다, 오다, or 다니다. It answers "what did you go/come to do?"`,keyPoint:{label:"-(으)러 vs -기 위해서 (going to do vs why in general)",body:"-(으)러 is only for the reason you go or come, so it has to be followed by a movement verb (가다/오다/다니다): 밥을 먹으러 가요. -기 위해서 gives the reason for any action: 살을 빼려고 운동해요 sounds more natural here, but the textbook pair is -기 위해서. The simple rule: if it's about going or coming somewhere to do something, use -(으)러 가요."},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in a consonant, the 으러 links right onto that consonant: 먹 + 으러 → [머그러]. No pause — the ㄱ slides into 으."}}],canDo:"Tell someone the purpose of a trip with V-(으)러 가요 (밥 먹으러 가요)",id:"chapter-23-b4",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 내리다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"내리다",romanization:"naerida",en:"to get off",pos:"verb",nuance:"내리다 is the opposite of 타다 for transportation. Use 에서 for the place where you get off."},sentence:{ko:"다음 정류장에서 내려요.",en:"Get off at the next bus stop."},target:"내려",options:["to walk","to get off","subway"],note:"The place where you get off takes 에서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"걷다",romanization:"geotda",en:"to walk",pos:"verb",nuance:"걷다 means to walk on foot and is a ㄷ irregular verb: before a vowel ending the final ㄷ softens to ㄹ, so the 요-form is 걸어요 (not 걷어요) and the past is 걸었어요. Before a consonant ending the ㄷ stays put, so the formal form remains 걷습니다 and you say 걷지 마세요, 걷고 싶어요. The nearest relative is 산책하다 ('take a stroll'): 걷다 is the raw act of walking anywhere, while 산책하다 implies a leisurely walk for pleasure, so 'I walk to work' is 걸어서 출근해요, never 산책해서 출근해요. The trap for English speakers is that 걷다 doesn't take an object the way 'walk the dog' does — Korean uses 산책시키다 for that, and the path you walk takes 을/를 (공원을 걸어요), so don't reach for 에. Also remember 걸어서 ('on foot') is one fixed chunk for describing how you travel."},sentence:{ko:"역까지 걸어서 십 분 걸려요.",en:"It takes ten minutes on foot to the station."},target:"걸어",options:["to walk","to get off","subway"],note:"걸어서 is the everyday 'on foot' transport answer."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"길",romanization:"gil",en:"road; way",pos:"noun",nuance:"길 can mean road, path, way, or route. 길을 잃었어요 is a survival phrase."},sentence:{ko:"이 길로 직진하세요.",en:"Go straight on this road."},target:"길",options:["subway","road; way","bus"],note:"길로 marks the route."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"방향",romanization:"banghyang",en:"direction",pos:"noun",nuance:"방향 is Sino-Korean 方向 (方 'direction/region' + 向 'to face/head toward'), the abstract heading or orientation of movement. The central contrast is 방향 vs 쪽: 쪽 is the concrete, point-at-it 'side/way' right in front of you (이쪽, 저쪽, 오른쪽), while 방향 is the abstract course of travel you reason about — 'the opposite direction', 'the right direction', 'which way leads there'. So you point 이쪽 ('this way, here') but you verify 방향이 맞아요? ('am I heading right?') and warn 반대 방향이에요 ('that's the wrong way'). Compare 위치 too: 위치 is where something is (static position), 방향 is which way it faces or you move. Register neutral, fine in speech and signs (e.g., 부산 방향 on a road sign = 'toward Busan'). English-speaker pitfalls: for movement use 으로 (반대 방향으로 가요), not 에 (방향에 가요 is wrong); and don't confuse 방향 with the look-alike 방학 (school vacation) — same 방, unrelated meanings."},sentence:{ko:"이 방향이 맞아요?",en:"Is this the right direction?"},target:"방향",options:["subway","bus","direction"],note:"Useful when checking whether you are going the right way."}],canDo:"Give simple directions with everyday transport words (역, 출구, 내리다, 갈아타다)",id:"chapter-23-b5",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A traveler asks how to reach the subway station, which exit to use, and where to transfer.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"홍대입구역에 가려면 어디로 가요?",en:"Which way do I go to get to Hongik University Station?",romanization:"hongdaeipguyeoge garyeomyeon eodiro gayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"이 길로 직진하고 오른쪽으로 가세요.",en:"Go straight on this road and go right.",romanization:"i gillo jikjinhago oreunjjogeuro gaseyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"지하철로 가는 게 빨라요, 버스로 가는 게 빨라요?",en:"Is it faster to go by subway or by bus?",romanization:"jihacheollo ganeun ge ppallayo, beoseuro ganeun ge ppallayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"지하철로 가세요. 시청역에서 2호선으로 갈아타면 돼요.",en:"Go by subway. You can transfer to Line 2 at City Hall Station.",romanization:"jihacheollo gaseyo. sicheongnyeogeseo ihoseoneuro garatamyeon dwaeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"친구 만나러 가는 길이라서 빨리 가야 해요.",en:"I am on my way to meet a friend, so I have to go quickly.",romanization:"chingu mannareo ganeun giriraseo ppalli gaya haeyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"그럼 삼 번 출구로 나가서 왼쪽으로 가세요.",en:"Then go out through exit three and go left.",romanization:"geureom sam beon chulguro nagaseo oenjjogeuro gaseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Please go to the right.'",tokens:["오른쪽으로","가세요"],correct:"오른쪽으로 가세요",explanation:"오른쪽으로 gives direction toward the right. 가세요 is the polite direction/request form."}],canDo:"Say how I travel and which way to go (지하철로, 2번 출구로)",id:"chapter-23-b6",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Getting to the right exit",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Getting to the right exit",chunks:["민서는 친구를 만나러 홍대입구역에 가고 있었어요. 처음에는 버스로 갈까 생각했지만, 시간이 없어서 지하철로 가기로 했어요. 시청역에서 2호선으로 갈아타고, 홍대입구역에서 삼 번 출구로 나가야 했어요.","출구에서 왼쪽으로 조금 걸으면 약속 장소가 나와요. 민서는 길을 다시 확인하고 이 길로 직진한 뒤에 왼쪽으로 갔어요."],translation:"Minseo was going to Hongik University Station to meet a friend. She considered taking the bus, but because she did not have time, she decided to go by subway. She had to transfer to Line 2, go out exit three, and walk a little to the left from the exit.",qas:[{q:"민서는 왜 홍대입구역에 가고 있었어요?",a:"친구를 만나러 가고 있었어요."},{q:"민서는 어디에서 2호선으로 갈아타요?",a:"시청역에서 갈아타요."},{q:"홍대입구역에서 몇 번 출구로 나가야 해요?",a:"삼 번 출구로 나가야 해요."}]}],canDo:"Tell someone the purpose of a trip with V-(으)러 가요 (밥 먹으러 가요)",id:"chapter-23-b7",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a route or direction?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 길로 쭉 가세요.",ok:!0},{text:"이 길에 쭉 가세요.",ok:!1},{text:"이 길이 쭉 가세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-로 marks the route or direction here. 이 길을 쭉 가세요 is also natural because 을 can mark a traveled path, so it is not used as a distractor."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means 'I am going to meet a friend'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구 만나러 가요.",ok:!0},{text:"친구를 만났어요.",ok:!1},{text:"친구에게 가요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-러 가요 gives the purpose of going."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지하철___ 가요. (I go by subway.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"로",ok:!0},{text:"에",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"Transport method takes (으)로. 에 would mark a destination, not the way you travel."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Please go to the right.'",tokens:["오른쪽으로","가세요"],correct:"오른쪽으로 가세요",explanation:"오른쪽으로 gives direction toward the right. 가세요 is the polite direction/request form."}],canDo:"Give simple directions with everyday transport words (역, 출구, 내리다, 갈아타다)",id:"chapter-23-b8",chapterId:"chapter-23",index:7}]},{id:"chapter-24",number:24,title:"V-아/어 보다 · V-아/어 주세요",goal:"Use V아/어 보세요 and V아/어 볼까요? for trying an action: 입어 보세요, 먹어 볼까요?",biteCount:10,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 해 보다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"해 보다",romanization:"hae boda",en:"to try doing",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"혼자 해 보세요.",en:"Try doing it by yourself."},target:"해 보",options:["to recommend","to try doing","to be okay; fine"],note:"하다 becomes 해 before 보다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"입어 보다",romanization:"ibeo boda",en:"to try on clothes",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"이 옷 한번 입어 보세요.",en:"Try on these clothes once."},target:"입어 보",options:["to recommend","to try on clothes","to be okay; fine"],note:"입다 becomes 입어 before 보다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹어 보다",romanization:"meogeo boda",en:"to try eating",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"이 음식 먹어 볼까요?",en:"Should we try this food?"},target:"먹어 볼",options:["to try eating","to recommend","to be okay; fine"],note:"먹다 becomes 먹어 before 보다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가 보다",romanization:"ga boda",en:"to try going",pos:"verb phrase",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"이 식당 한번 가 볼까요?",en:"Should we try going to this restaurant?"},target:"가 볼",options:["to recommend","to try going","to be okay; fine"],note:"가다 becomes 가 before 보다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"추천하다",romanization:"chucheonhada",en:"to recommend",pos:"verb",nuance:"추천하다 is neutral and practical. In speech, 추천해 주세요 is much more useful than the dictionary form."},sentence:{ko:"다른 색도 추천해 주세요.",en:"Please recommend another color too."},target:"추천해",options:["to take on a challenge","to recommend","to help"],note:"추천해 주세요 is the useful request form."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"한번",romanization:"hanbeon",en:"once; just to try",pos:"adverb",nuance:"한번 does not always literally mean one time. With -아/어 보다, it often means 'just give it a try.'"},sentence:{ko:"한번 입어 보세요.",en:"Try it on once."},target:"한번",options:["to recommend","once; just to try","to be okay; fine"],note:"Often softens a trial suggestion."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"괜찮다",romanization:"gwaenchanta",en:"to be okay; fine",pos:"adjective",nuance:"괜찮아요 can mean okay, fine, no thanks, or not bad. Context decides."},sentence:{ko:"이 색 괜찮아요?",en:"Is this color okay?"},target:"괜찮아",options:["to try doing","to be okay; fine","to try on clothes"],note:"Useful when checking a choice after trying."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 옷 한번 입어 볼까요?",en:"Should I try on these clothes?"},hl:"한번"}],canDo:"Nudge someone to try something with V-아/어 보다 (한번 먹어 보세요)",id:"chapter-24-b1",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 보다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-아/어 보다",sub:"try doing V (give it a go)",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아 보다",ex:"가다 → 가 보다 (try going), 오다 → 와 보다 (try coming)"},{when:"Stem ends in any other vowel",add:"-어 보다",ex:"먹다 → 먹어 보다 (try eating), 읽다 → 읽어 보다 (try reading)"},{when:"하- verb",add:"-여 보다 → 해 보다",ex:"하다 → 해 보다 (try doing), 공부하다 → 공부해 보다 (try studying)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 책 한번 읽어 보세요.",en:"Try reading this book.",romanization:"i chaek hanbeon ilgeo boseyo.",note:'한번 ("give it a try") goes hand in hand with -아/어 보다 to soften a suggestion.'},{ko:"이 음식 먹어 봤어요?",en:"Have you tried this food?",romanization:"i eumsik meogeo bwasseoyo?",note:'Past 봤어요 asks about experience — "have you ever tried it?"'}],more:{func:`Add -아/어 보다 to a verb to mean "try doing something" — do it once and see how it goes. It's the warm, everyday way to nudge someone: "go ahead and try it."`,keyPoint:{label:"V-아/어 보다 vs. plain V",body:'먹으세요 just says "eat it." 먹어 보세요 says "try it and see how it is" — it frames the action as something new to test out, which makes the suggestion feel friendlier and less pushy.'},pronunciation:"In 읽어 보세요, the ㄺ of 읽- links forward, so it sounds like [일거 보세요] — the ㄱ carries into the next syllable."}}],canDo:"Nudge someone to try something with V-아/어 보다 (한번 먹어 보세요)",id:"chapter-24-b2",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 어때요~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어때요",romanization:"eottaeyo",en:"how is it?; what do you think?",pos:"question phrase",nuance:"어때요 is the polite conversational form of 어떻다 and asks for an evaluation, condition, reaction, or opinion: 이 색 어때요?, 새 직장은 어때요?, 요즘 몸은 어때요? It does not ask for a method; 어떻게 means ‘how / in what way.’ The past is 어땠어요 (‘How was it?’), and the more honorific form 어떠세요 is common when asking about a respected person’s health, preference, or situation. In suggestions, N 어때요? means ‘How about N?’ — 다음 주는 어때요?"},sentence:{ko:"이 색 어때요?",en:"How is this color?"},target:"어때요",options:["how is it?; what do you think?","to try doing","to try on clothes"],note:"어때요 asks for an opinion, not a method."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도전하다",romanization:"dojeonhada",en:"to take on a challenge",pos:"verb",nuance:"도전하다 = to take something on, rise to a challenge, brave a new or daunting endeavor — overwhelmingly positive and aspirational (a job change, a new hobby, scary spicy food, a hard exam). The hanja 挑戰 literally means 挑 'provoke, stir up' + 戰 'battle', i.e. 'to pick a fight', and that combative root explains both senses: figuratively you 'do battle' with a goal. Particle pitfall for English speakers: it takes 에, not 을/를 — 마라톤에 도전하다 ('take on the marathon'), 시험에 도전하다. Contrast it with 노력하다 (sustained, grinding effort over time) and 시도하다 (a neutral, often one-off 'attempt'): 도전하다 specifically frames the target as bold or intimidating, with a brave-the-unknown flavor. Note: it does NOT mean confronting a person in an argument — 'to challenge someone' in that sense is 이의를 제기하다 or 도전장을 내밀다, not plain 도전하다. The everyday encouragement is 도전해 봐."},sentence:{ko:"새로운 음식에 한번 도전해 보세요.",en:"Try taking on a new food."},target:"도전해",options:["to recommend","to help","to take on a challenge"],note:"Use for a challenge, not every tiny attempt."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도와주다",romanization:"dowajuda",en:"to help",pos:"verb",nuance:"도와주다 means 'to help (for someone's benefit)' and is built from 돕다 ('to help') + 주다 ('to give'), so the 주다 already bakes in the warm sense of doing the help for another person. The base 돕다 is a ㅂ-irregular (돕 + 아요 → 도와요), which is why the compound surfaces as 도와- and the polite present is 도와줘요, past 도와줬어요. The person helped takes the object marker 을/를: 친구를 도와줘요. The most useful register fact is the request form: because 주다 is inside the verb, 'please help me' is simply 도와주세요 — adding a second giving verb to make 도와줘 주세요 is redundant and wrong. Contrast plain 돕다 (which feels more neutral/written, 어려운 이웃을 돕다 'help a struggling neighbor') with 도와주다 (the warm, conversational everyday form). The English-speaker pitfall is exactly that double 주세요, plus marking the helped person with 에게 by analogy to 'give to' — but help is done to a person directly, so it is 저를 도와줘요, not 저에게 도와줘요."},sentence:{ko:"조금 도와주세요.",en:"Please help me a little."},target:"도와주",options:["to recommend","to take on a challenge","to help"],note:"도와주세요 is the core polite help request."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"천천히",romanization:"cheoncheonhi",en:"slowly",pos:"adverb",nuance:"천천히 is polite and clear for asking someone to slow down."},sentence:{ko:"천천히 다시 말해 주세요.",en:"Please say it again slowly."},target:"천천히",options:["slowly","first","to try doing"],note:"A practical help request."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"같이",romanization:"gachi",en:"together",pos:"adverb",nuance:"같이 can mean together or like/as in other contexts; here it means together."},sentence:{ko:"같이 갈까요?",en:"Shall we go together?"},target:"같이",options:["first","together","again"],note:"Pairs naturally with ㄹ까요 suggestions."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먼저",romanization:"meonjeo",en:"first",pos:"adverb",nuance:"먼저 means 'first / beforehand / ahead (of others)' — it ranks one action or person EARLIER in a sequence or in time. The key contrast is 먼저 vs 처음: 먼저 is relative ('before the other thing/person', e.g. 먼저 가요 'go ahead of you'), while 처음 means 'the first time ever / the beginning' (처음 만나요 'meeting for the first time'). So 먼저 봤어요 = 'I saw it first (before you)', but 처음 봤어요 = 'I saw it for the first time'. 먼저 is also a courtesy word: 먼저 가세요 / 먼저 하세요 ('go ahead, you first') is everyday politeness, and 먼저 갈게요 / 먼저 들어가겠습니다 softens leaving before others. Register-neutral. The English-speaker pitfall is mapping every 'first' to one word — choose 먼저 for order-relative 'first/ahead', but 처음 for 'first-ever/at first', and keep 먼저 before the verb."},sentence:{ko:"먼저 입어 보세요.",en:"Try it on first."},target:"먼저",options:["together","first","again"],note:"Useful when sequencing a suggestion."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"다시",romanization:"dasi",en:"again",pos:"adverb",nuance:"다시 is one of the most useful repair words for learners."},sentence:{ko:"다시 보여 주세요.",en:"Please show me again."},target:"다시",options:["together","again","first"],note:"Pairs with 주세요 requests."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 먼저 입어 보세요. 색이 잘 어울려요.",en:"Yes, try them on first. The color suits you well."},hl:"먼저"}],canDo:"Make a polite request with V-아/어 주세요 (알려 주세요)",id:"chapter-24-b3",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 주세요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어 주세요",sub:"please do V (for me)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"문",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"열",mid:"어",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"문 좀 열어 주세요.",en:"Please open the door for me.",romanization:"mun jom yeoreo juseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"천천히",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"말해",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"천천히 말해 주세요.",en:"Please speak slowly.",romanization:"cheoncheonhi malhae juseyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어 주세요",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아 주세요",ex:"앉다 → 앉아 주세요 (please sit), 보다 → 봐 주세요 (please look)"},{when:"Stem ends in any other vowel",add:"-어 주세요",ex:"열다 → 열어 주세요 (please open), 기다리다 → 기다려 주세요 (please wait)"},{when:"하- verb",add:"-여 주세요 → 해 주세요",ex:"하다 → 해 주세요 (please do), 말하다 → 말해 주세요 (please tell me)"}]},more:{func:"Add -아/어 주세요 to a verb to politely ask someone to do something for you. It's the normal, warm way to make a request — softer than a bare command.",keyPoint:{label:"V-(으)세요 vs. V-아/어 주세요",body:`앉으세요 is a polite instruction aimed at what the listener does ("please sit"). 앉아 주세요 adds 주다 ("do it for me"), so it lands as a favor you'd be grateful for — exactly the feel of most polite English requests.`},pronunciation:"열어 주세요 links across the ㄹ → [여러 주세요]; the ㄹ of 열- slides into the next syllable."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Please wait a moment.",sentence:"잠깐만 기다려 ___.",options:[{text:"어 주세요",ok:!1},{text:"주세요",ok:!0}],explanation:"기다리다 → 기다려 주세요; the waiting is for your sake, so 주다 fits."}],canDo:"Make a polite request with V-아/어 주세요 (알려 주세요)",id:"chapter-24-b4",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 부탁하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"부탁하다",romanization:"butakada",en:"to ask a favor",pos:"verb",nuance:"부탁하다 is 付託(부탁)하다, hanja 付 'hand over' + 託 'entrust', so the image is entrusting a task into someone's hands — you are asking them to do something FOR you, leaning on their goodwill. Contrast it sharply with 요청하다 and 시키다: 부탁하다 is personal and humble (you owe a thank-you), 요청하다 is formal/institutional (자료를 요청하다 'request documents'), and 시키다 is to MAKE/order someone do it from a position of authority — never use 시키다 with someone older or you sound bossy. The famous phrase 잘 부탁합니다 has no clean English equivalent; it pre-thanks someone for the cooperation you're about to receive and is near-mandatory in introductions. English speakers misfire two ways: they try to use 부탁하다 to order food or goods (that is 주문하다), and they attach the favor with the wrong particle — the person is 에게/한테, and the favor itself, if stated, is the object: 친구에게 부탁을 했어요. As a 하다 verb it is fully regular, negating as 부탁 안 해요."},sentence:{ko:"작은 부탁 하나 해도 될까요?",en:"May I ask one small favor?"},target:"부탁",options:["to ask a favor","to recommend","to take on a challenge"],note:"Useful before a request."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"옷",romanization:"ot",en:"clothes",pos:"noun",nuance:"옷 is a pure native Korean word (no hanja) meaning clothes/clothing for the body, and the verb you MUST pair it with is 입다 (to wear on the torso/body) — never 신다, which is reserved for footwear on the feet (신발을 신다), nor 쓰다 (hats/glasses) nor 끼다 (gloves/rings). English 'wear/put on' covers every garment with one verb, so this split is the central pitfall: 옷을 신어요 is a classic beginner error. To take clothes off is 벗다, to change them 갈아입다. For 'these clothes look good on you,' Korean says 옷이 잘 어울려요 (the clothes suit you), not a direct translation of 'you look good.' Note 옷 is treated as singular/uncountable grammatically (no plural needed): one outfit or a wardrobe full is still just 옷. The dressier counterpart 의상 (costume/attire, 衣裳) is for performances or formal contexts. Register is neutral and everyday."},sentence:{ko:"이 옷 한번 입어 볼까요?",en:"Should I try on these clothes?"},target:"옷",options:["to try doing","to try eating","clothes"],note:"Core noun for trying on clothing."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"색",romanization:"saek",en:"color",pos:"noun",nuance:"색 is the Sino-Korean root 色 ('color'), and it appears both as a standalone noun and, more often in casual speech, expanded to 색깔 — the two are interchangeable, with 색깔 feeling slightly warmer and more spoken. As a suffix, 색 attaches to color stems to make full color nouns: 빨간색, 파란색, 노란색. There is also a fixed set of bare Sino-Korean color words — 녹색(綠色) green, 황색(黃色) yellow, 흑색(黑色) black — used in formal or technical registers. The English-speaker pitfall is the object particle: 좋아하다 ('to like') takes a direct object, so 'I like this color' must be 이 색을 좋아해요, with 을, even though English 'like' feels stative. Note too that 좋아하다 expresses a held preference, whereas 마음에 들다 ('it pleases me') is for a fresh, in-the-moment liking of one specific thing."},sentence:{ko:"다른 색도 추천해 주세요.",en:"Please recommend another color too."},target:"색",options:["color","to try doing","to try eating"],note:"Useful in clothing and shopping suggestions."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어울리다",romanization:"eoullida",en:"to suit; to look good on",pos:"verb",nuance:"어울리다 carries two everyday meanings bound by one idea — 'to harmonize / fit together well': (1) to suit or look good on someone (이 옷이 잘 어울려요 = this suits you), and (2) to mingle, blend in, or get along with people (친구들하고 잘 어울려요). The particle pattern is the crux for English speakers, because English 'suits you' makes 'you' the object: in Korean the thing that suits is the SUBJECT and takes 이/가 (옷이 어울려요, never 옷을 어울려요), the person it suits is unmarked or takes 한테/에게, and a matching partner or group takes 하고/와/과 (치마하고 어울리다, 사람들하고 어울리다); a setting it fits takes 에 (분위기에 어울리다). Conjugation is the regular ㅣ-vowel contraction, not an irregular: 어울리 + 어 fuses to 어울려, giving polite 어울려요 and past 어울렸어요 (not 어울리어요). A useful contrast: 어울리다 ('look good / harmonize') vs 맞다 ('fit in size / be correct') — clothes that 어울리다 flatter you, clothes that 맞다 are the right size, and the two are not the same compliment."},sentence:{ko:"이 색이 잘 어울려요.",en:"This color suits you well."},target:"어울려",options:["to recommend","to suit; to look good on","to take on a challenge"],note:"Use the 'to suit' sense, not the 'hang out' sense."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바꾸다",romanization:"bakkuda",en:"to change; exchange",pos:"verb",nuance:"바꾸다 means to replace one thing with another, or to alter something into a different state — money (돈을 바꾸다), seats (자리를 바꾸다), plans (계획을 바꾸다), even your mind (마음을 바꾸다). The dividing line from 고치다 is essential: 바꾸다 ends with a DIFFERENT thing, 고치다 keeps the SAME thing repaired. Returning a defective shirt for a new one is 옷을 바꾸다; mending the torn seam is 옷을 고치다. The destination of the change takes the particle -(으)로: 작은 걸로 바꿔 주세요 ('change it for the small one'). Note also 바뀌다, the intransitive twin: 바꾸다 is 'I change X' (transitive, takes 을/를), while 바뀌다 is 'X changes by itself' (신호등이 바뀌었어요 = 'the light changed') — mixing these up is a frequent error. Conjugation is regular: 바꾸 + 어요 → 바꿔요, past 바꿨어요. English-speaker pitfall: 'change my mind' is 마음을 바꾸다, not 생각을 고치다, and 'change clothes (get dressed)' is 옷을 갈아입다, not 옷을 바꾸다."},sentence:{ko:"사이즈가 크면 바꿔 주세요.",en:"If the size is big, please exchange it."},target:"바꿔",options:["to recommend","to change; exchange","to take on a challenge"],note:"바꿔 주세요 is a practical service request."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보이다",romanization:"boida",en:"to be seen; to show",pos:"verb",nuance:"보이다 is the passive/spontaneous counterpart of 보다 (to look): something enters your field of vision on its own, so the thing seen takes 이/가 (산이 보여요 = 'the mountain is visible'), NOT 을/를. The 요-form contracts 보이-+-어요 → 보여요 (past 보였어요). The sharp contrast learners need is 보이다 vs 보다: 보다 is an active choice ('I look at / watch'), 보이다 is involuntary ('it is seen / comes into view'). A second, very frequent meaning is 'to look/seem' — adjective stem + 보이다 (행복해 보여요 'you look happy'; 비싸 보여요 'it looks expensive'); here English 'looks' maps cleanly. With 주다 it flips to active 'show' (보여 주다). The English-speaker pitfall: saying 산을 보여요 to mean 'I see the mountain' — that is wrong; use 산이 보여요 (it is visible) or 산을 봐요 (I look at it)."},sentence:{ko:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주세요.",en:"Please show me one size smaller too."},target:"보여",options:["to recommend","to be seen; to show","to take on a challenge"],note:"보여 주세요 is the request form used in shops."}],canDo:"Float a friendly suggestion with V-(으)ㄹ까요? (같이 갈까요?)",id:"chapter-24-b5",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ까요?",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㄹ까요?",sub:"shall we? / should I?",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ까요?",ex:"가다 → 갈까요? (shall we go?), 마시다 → 마실까요? (shall we drink?)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을까요?",ex:"먹다 → 먹을까요? (shall we eat?), 앉다 → 앉을까요? (shall we sit?)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-까요? (the ㄹ stays, no 을)",ex:"놀다 → 놀까요? (shall we hang out?), 만들다 → 만들까요? (shall we make it?)"}],examples:[{ko:"우리 같이 밥 먹을까요?",en:"Shall we eat together?",romanization:"uri gachi bap meogeulkkayo?",note:'우리 같이 ("we, together") makes the group "shall we?" clear.'},{ko:"내일 영화 볼까요?",en:"Shall we watch a movie tomorrow?",romanization:"naeil yeonghwa bolkkayo?",note:"보다 ends in a vowel → 볼까요?; a casual invite to do something together."}],more:{func:`Add -(으)ㄹ까요? to a verb to suggest doing something together ("shall we?") or to ask what the listener thinks about you doing it ("should I?"). It's the standard way to float a friendly idea.`,keyPoint:{label:"V-(으)ㄹ까요? vs. V-아/어요",body:`먹어요 states or mildly pushes ("(let's) eat"). 먹을까요? turns it into a real question that hands the decision to the listener — you propose, they answer. Reach for -(으)ㄹ까요? when you actually want their okay, not just to announce the plan.`},pronunciation:"The 까 is always tense (ㄲ): 먹을까요? sounds like [머글까요]. The ㄱ of 먹- links forward into 을."}}],canDo:"Float a friendly suggestion with V-(으)ㄹ까요? (같이 갈까요?)",id:"chapter-24-b6",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㅂ시다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㅂ시다",sub:`formal "let's" for groups`,rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㅂ시다",ex:"가다 → 갑시다 (let's go), 시작하다 → 시작합시다"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-읍시다",ex:"먹다 → 먹읍시다 (let's eat), 읽다 → 읽읍시다"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"drop ㄹ, then + ㅂ시다",ex:"만들다 → 만듭시다, 살다 → 삽시다"}],examples:[{ko:"회의를 시작합시다.",en:"Let's start the meeting.",romanization:"hoeuireul sijakhapsida.",note:"A natural meeting-room use: formal, clear, and group-directed."},{ko:"다음 역에서 내립시다.",en:"Let's get off at the next station.",romanization:"daeum yeogeseo naeripsida.",note:"Announcement-like and organized, not a casual friend-to-friend suggestion."}],more:{func:"Use -(으)ㅂ시다 to suggest that a group do something in a formal or public setting. It sounds organized and official, so it fits meetings, announcements, campaigns, and speeches. For friends, use -자; for a softer suggestion, use -(으)ㄹ까요?",keyPoint:{label:"-자 < -(으)ㄹ까요? < -(으)ㅂ시다",body:"가자 is casual and close. 갈까요? is softer because it asks the listener's opinion. 갑시다 is formal and group-leading — useful in meetings or public speech, but too stiff for close friends."},pronunciation:"In 시작합시다 and 건배합시다, the ㅂ before 시다 is held clearly, and 시다 is always pronounced tense, like [씨다] — 합시다 sounds like [합씨다] at any speed, though the spelling stays 합시다. Don't turn 합시다 into 하십니다 — that is a different ending."}}],canDo:"Nudge someone to try something with V-아/어 보다 (한번 먹어 보세요)",id:"chapter-24-b7",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:6},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A shopper tries on clothes, checks the color, and makes polite requests to the staff.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 옷 한번 입어 볼까요?",en:"Should I try on these clothes?",romanization:"i ot hanbeon ibeo bolkkayo?"},{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 먼저 입어 보세요. 색이 잘 어울려요.",en:"Yes, try them on first. The color suits you well.",romanization:"ne, meonjeo ibeo boseyo. saegi jal eoullyeoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 색도 괜찮은데, 다른 색도 추천해 주세요.",en:"This color is okay, but please recommend another color too.",romanization:"i saekdo gwaenchaneunde, dareun saekdo chucheon hae juseyo."},{speaker:"직원",ko:"그럼 이 색도 한번 입어 보세요.",en:"Then try on this color too.",romanization:"geureom i saekdo hanbeon ibeo boseyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"사이즈가 크면 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주세요.",en:"If the size is big, please show me one size smaller too.",romanization:"saijeuga keumyeon han chisu jageun geotdo boyeo juseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Shall we go together?'",tokens:["같이","갈까요?"],correct:"같이 갈까요?",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ까요? suggests or asks what to do. 같이 makes the suggestion “together.”"}],canDo:"Nudge someone to try something with V-아/어 보다 (한번 먹어 보세요)",id:"chapter-24-b8",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:7},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Trying before deciding",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Trying before deciding",chunks:["민서는 옷 가게에서 재킷을 고르고 있었어요. 직원은 먼저 입어 보라고 말했어요. 민서는 이 색도 괜찮지만 다른 색도 보고 싶어서 추천해 달라고 했어요.","직원은 더 밝은 색을 한번 입어 보라고 했고, 민서는 그 색이 더 잘 어울린다고 느꼈어요. 사이즈가 조금 커서 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 달라고 부탁했어요. 이렇게 민서는 바로 결정하지 않고 입어 본 뒤에 선택했어요."],translation:"Minseo was choosing a jacket at a clothing store. The staff suggested she try it on first. Minseo asked for another color recommendation, tried a brighter color, and then asked to see one size smaller before deciding.",qas:[{q:"직원은 민서에게 먼저 무엇을 해 보라고 했어요?",a:"먼저 입어 보라고 했어요."},{q:"민서는 왜 추천해 달라고 했어요?",a:"다른 색도 보고 싶어서 추천해 달라고 했어요."},{q:"민서는 사이즈가 커서 무엇을 부탁했어요?",a:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 달라고 부탁했어요."}]}],canDo:"Make a polite request with V-아/어 주세요 (알려 주세요)",id:"chapter-24-b9",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:8},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"A shopper wants to try on clothes. Which sentence fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 옷 입어 볼까요?",ok:!0},{text:"이 옷 입었어요?",ok:!1},{text:"이 옷 입는 중이었어요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어 볼까요 asks about trying an action before deciding."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which is a polite service request?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주세요.",ok:!0},{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 봐요.",ok:!1},{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 보였어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어 주세요 turns the action into a polite request."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"다른 색___ 보여 주세요. (Please show me another color.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"보여 주다 takes the thing shown as an object, so 다른 색을 fits. 이 would make the color the subject."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Shall we go together?'",tokens:["같이","갈까요?"],correct:"같이 갈까요?",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ까요? suggests or asks what to do. 같이 makes the suggestion “together.”"}],canDo:"Float a friendly suggestion with V-(으)ㄹ까요? (같이 갈까요?)",id:"chapter-24-b10",chapterId:"chapter-24",index:9}]},{id:"chapter-25",number:25,title:"A/V-지만 · A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도",goal:"Use A/V-지만 for clean contrast: 비싸지만 맛있어요.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 그런데~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그런데",romanization:"geureonde",en:"but; by the way",pos:"adverb",nuance:"그런데 (spoken: 근데) is the soft, all-purpose 'but' that also works as 'by the way' to glide into a new topic — this double life surprises English speakers most. As contrast it is far gentler than 하지만 or 그렇지만; it doesn't slam two ideas together, it just notes that something slightly different follows. As a topic-changer it carries no contrast at all: 그런데, 우리 언제 만나요? simply means 'So/Anyway, when shall we meet?'. It is closely tied to the mid-sentence ending -는데, which sets background before a turn (비싼데 살까? = it's pricey, but shall I buy it?). The everyday pitfall is over-translating it as a strong 'however'; if you mean a firm, contrastive 'however', reach for 하지만. And don't write the long form in chat — Koreans type 근데."},sentence:{ko:"좋아요. 그런데 조금 비싸요.",en:"It is good. But it is a little expensive."},target:"그런데",options:["but; by the way","continuously; keep","to be expensive"],note:"A softer pivot than 하지만."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하지만",romanization:"hajiman",en:"but; however",pos:"adverb",nuance:"하지만 is the standard, clear-cut 'but / however' for contrasting two full sentences, and it reads a touch more formal and decisive than 그런데/근데. The crucial contrast to internalize is 하지만 vs 그런데: 하지만 is purely contrastive — it never means 'by the way' and never changes the topic, whereas 그런데 is soft and frequently just pivots subjects. So if you only mean a plain 'but', 하지만 is the safe pick; if you're easing into a new question, you want 그런데. It is essentially the sentence-initial sibling of the connective -지만 (어렵지만 재미있어요 = 어려워요. 하지만 재미있어요). The pitfalls: never drop it into the middle of one sentence — it heads the contrasting sentence — and don't use it to mean 'by the way', which is 그런데's job, not 하지만's."},sentence:{ko:"맛있어요. 하지만 비싸요.",en:"It is delicious. However, it is expensive."},target:"하지만",options:["but; however","continuously; keep","to be expensive"],note:"Use at the start of a new sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그렇지만",romanization:"geureochiman",en:"but; however",pos:"adverb",nuance:"그렇지만 means 'but / however / nevertheless' and is the close sibling of 하지만 — in most beginner sentences they swap freely. It is literally 그렇- ('be so') + -지만 ('but'), so it carries a faint undertone of 'that may be true, but…', conceding the prior point a hair more than the flat 하지만. Register-wise it leans slightly more formal and polished, comfortable in writing or careful, polite speech (감사합니다. 그렇지만 사양하겠습니다). Set it against two neighbors: 그런데/근데 is the soft, casual 'but' that can also mean 'by the way', which 그렇지만 never does; and 그래도 is 'even so', a concessive 'I'll do it anyway' rather than a neutral 'however'. The pitfall is the same as for any of these connectors — it heads the contrasting sentence, so never bury it in the middle of one clause."},sentence:{ko:"조금 불편해요. 그렇지만 가능해요.",en:"It is a little inconvenient. However, it is possible."},target:"그렇지만",options:["continuously; keep","but; however","to be expensive"],note:"A slightly fuller form of 하지만."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그래도",romanization:"geuraedo",en:"still; even so",pos:"adverb",nuance:"그래도 means 'even so / but still' — you fully accept the previous fact, yet what follows defies what that fact would predict, carrying a flavor of perseverance or resolve. It is the sentence-initial partner of the concessive ending -아도/-어도 ('even if'); 비싸요. 그래도 사요 mirrors 비싸도 사요. The dangerous look-alike is 그래서 ('so / therefore'): one letter apart, opposite meaning — 그래서 follows the expected result, 그래도 goes AGAINST it. So 피곤해요. 그래서 자요 (tired, so I sleep) vs 피곤해요. 그래도 안 자요 (tired, but still I won't sleep). It also differs from plain contrast words like 하지만: 하지만 just sets two ideas side by side, while 그래도 specifically concedes 'granting all that…' and pushes through. Don't use it where there's no 'expected-vs-actual' gap; without that tension it sounds illogical."},sentence:{ko:"비싸요. 그래도 한번 가 볼까요?",en:"It is expensive. Even so, shall we try going?"},target:"그래도",options:["continuously; keep","to be expensive","still; even so"],note:"그래도 keeps the plan or feeling despite the previous sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비싸다",romanization:"bissada",en:"to be expensive",pos:"adjective",nuance:"비싸요 is useful for shopping but can sound blunt if said directly to staff."},sentence:{ko:"비싸지만 맛있어요.",en:"It is expensive, but delicious."},target:"비싸",options:["to be expensive","to be easy","to be tired"],note:"A core contrast adjective for shopping and restaurants."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"싸다",romanization:"ssada",en:"to be cheap",pos:"adjective",nuance:"싸다 can also mean “to wrap” in other contexts, but 싸요 in shopping usually means cheap."},sentence:{ko:"싸지만 품질이 좋아요.",en:"It is cheap, but the quality is good."},target:"싸지",options:["to be easy","to be cheap","to be tired"],note:"Useful for positive contrast."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 식당은 비싸지만 정말 맛있어요.",en:"This restaurant is expensive, but really delicious."},hl:"비싸"}],canDo:"Join two opposing ideas with A/V-지만 (비싸지만 좋아요)",id:"chapter-25-b1",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-지만",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-지만",sub:"but",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"책은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"너무",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"길어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이 책은 재미있지만 너무 길어요.",en:"This book is fun, but it's too long.",romanization:"i chaegeun jaemiitjiman neomu gireoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"한국어는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"어렵",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"재미있어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"한국어는 어렵지만 재미있어요.",en:"Korean is hard, but it's fun.",romanization:"hangugeoneun eoryeopjiman jaemiisseoyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-지만",rows:[{when:"Verb or adjective stem (consonant or vowel)",add:"-지만",ex:"먹다 → 먹지만, 비싸다 → 비싸지만, 가다 → 가지만"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었/였- + 지만",ex:"먹다 → 먹었지만, 가다 → 갔지만, 공부하다 → 공부했지만"},{when:'Noun + 이다 ("is")',add:"이지만 (after consonant) / 지만 (after vowel)",ex:"학생 → 학생이지만, 친구 → 친구지만"}]},more:{func:'Use -지만 to join two clauses that go against each other, like English "but." The first clause says one thing, and the second says something that pushes the other way.',keyPoint:{label:"-지만 vs. 하지만",body:"-지만 connects two clauses inside one sentence: 비싸지만 맛있어요. 하지만 is a separate word that starts a new sentence: 비싸요. 하지만 맛있어요. Same meaning, but don't drop 하지만 in the middle of a clause — that spot belongs to -지만."},pronunciation:"After a final ㄱ or ㅈ-type sound the next syllable tightens: 먹지만 sounds like [먹찌만], 좋지만 like [조치만]. The 지만 part is never the loud part of the word."}}],canDo:"Join two opposing ideas with A/V-지만 (비싸지만 좋아요)",id:"chapter-25-b2",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 어렵다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어렵다",romanization:"eoryeopda",en:"to be difficult",pos:"adjective",nuance:"어렵다 is ㅂ irregular: 어려워요, 어려운. It is useful in classroom repair conversations because learners often need to say a sentence or lesson is difficult."},sentence:{ko:"어렵지만 재미있어요.",en:"It is difficult, but fun."},target:"어렵",options:["to be difficult","to be easy","to be tired"],note:"ㅂ irregular appears in 어려워도."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쉽다",romanization:"swipda",en:"to be easy",pos:"adjective",nuance:"쉽다 is ㅂ irregular: 쉬워요, 쉬운. It helps learners talk about difficulty level, but the opposite 어렵다 is often needed in the same lesson."},sentence:{ko:"쉬워도 실수할 수 있어요.",en:"Even if it is easy, you can make a mistake."},target:"쉬워",options:["to be easy","to be difficult","to be tired"],note:"쉽다 becomes 쉬워도."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"피곤하다",romanization:"pigonhada",en:"to be tired",pos:"adjective",nuance:"피곤하다 is physical/general tiredness — worn out, drained, exhausted — typically after work, study, travel, or poor sleep. It is Sino-Korean 疲困 (疲 weary + 困 exhausted), literally 'weary-exhausted', which is why it reads as bodily fatigue rather than sleepiness. Keep it apart from 졸리다 ('sleepy'): you can be 피곤하다 yet wide awake (drained but not nodding off), and 졸리다 is specifically wanting to sleep — don't swap them. It is also milder and more everyday than 지치다 ('to be worn out/burned out'), which implies you've reached your limit. As a regular 하다-adjective it is 피곤해요 (not 피곤하요). One common English-speaker slip: 피곤하다 normally describes a PERSON being tired; to call an activity 'tiring' you also use 피곤하다 (피곤한 일 = 'tiring work'), but to call a person 'tiresome/draining' Koreans say 피곤한 사람 — meaning 'a person who is exhausting to deal with', a nuance worth knowing so you don't misread it as 'a tired person.'"},sentence:{ko:"피곤해도 계속할 거예요.",en:"I will continue even if I am tired."},target:"피곤해",options:["to be tired","to be difficult","to be easy"],note:"Good with -아/어도 and 계속."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계속",romanization:"gyesok",en:"continuously; keep",pos:"adverb",nuance:"계속 means an action or state keeps going WITHOUT a break — 'continuously / the whole time / keep ~ing'. It's Sino-Korean 繼續 'connect-continue', which is exactly the image: things joined end to end with no gap. It can fuse with 하다 into the verb 계속하다 'to continue (something)' and appears as the connector 계속해서 'on and on'. The contrast to nail is 계속 vs 자꾸: 계속 is unbroken duration (비가 계속 와요 'it rains nonstop'), while 자꾸 is 'again and again, repeatedly' with gaps between tries, and usually carries a tinge of annoyance (자꾸 전화가 와요 'calls keep coming, ugh'). So 'the baby cried the whole time' is 계속 울었어요, but 'the baby kept waking up (on and off)' leans 자꾸 깼어요. Also distinguish the durative '-고 있다' (in progress right now) — 계속 stresses unbroken continuation over a stretch. No particle, before the verb; collocates with 오다, 가다, 하다, and direction verbs like 직진하다."},sentence:{ko:"힘들어도 계속할 거예요.",en:"I will keep going even if it is hard."},target:"계속",options:["but; by the way","but; however","continuously; keep"],note:"Often appears in concession sentences."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"문제",romanization:"munje",en:"problem",pos:"noun",nuance:"문제(問題, 'ask + topic') has two everyday senses English keeps apart: a trouble or difficulty ('we have a problem') AND a question on a test or exercise ('problem 3'). Crucially, both are solved with the same verb 풀다 (literally 'to untie/loosen') — 문제를 풀다 works for a math item and for a real-life difficulty alike, where English would switch to 'answer' vs 'solve.' Other key partners: 문제가 생기다 (a problem arises), 문제가 되다 (to become an issue), and the handy one-word 문제없어요 ('no problem'). Contrast 문제 with 질문 (a question you ASK someone to get information) — a test 문제 is a problem to solve, but raising your hand to ask is 질문하다, not 문제하다. The English-speaker pitfall is the subject/object split: existence and arising take 이 (문제가 있다/생기다), but solving takes 를 (문제를 풀다) — learners often say 문제가 풀어요."},sentence:{ko:"문제가 있어도 괜찮아요.",en:"It is okay even if there is a problem."},target:"문제",options:["problem","but; by the way","but; however"],note:"A practical concession noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"장점",romanization:"jangjeom",en:"advantage; strength",pos:"noun",nuance:"장점 is Sino-Korean (長點, 長 'long/superior' + 點 'point'), literally a 'strong point' — a merit, advantage, or strength of a thing, plan, or person. It is almost always used in deliberate contrast with its antonym 단점 (短點, 'short point' = weakness/drawback); the pair 장점과 단점 is a fixed collocation, like English 'pros and cons'. Compare 장점 with 강점 (強點, 'strong point/forte'): 강점 emphasizes a competitive strength relative to others (우리 회사의 강점 = our company's competitive edge), while 장점 is the neutral 'good point' of anything. Also note 좋은 점 (a more colloquial 'the good thing about it'). Register-neutral and common in reviews, interviews, and comparisons. Collocations: 장점이 있다/많다, 장점을 살리다 (leverage), 장점을 살펴보다 (examine the merits), 큰 장점, 최대 장점 (greatest advantage). The English-speaker pitfall: don't translate 'advantage' as 이득 or 이점 indiscriminately — 이득 (利得) is material 'gain/profit', and 이점 (利點) is 'an advantageous point/benefit' often in a strategic sense; for the everyday 'good point of X', 장점 is the natural choice. Also, when describing a person's virtue, 장점 (good trait) differs from 매력 (charm/appeal) — a reliable friend has a 장점, a charismatic one has 매력."},sentence:{ko:"단점도 있지만 장점이 더 많아요.",en:"There are drawbacks too, but there are more advantages."},target:"장점",options:["advantage; strength","but; by the way","but; however"],note:"Pairs naturally with 단점."}],canDo:"Say something happened in spite of A with A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도 (어려운데도 공부해요)",id:"chapter-25-b3",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도",sub:"even though, and still",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"약을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹었",mid:"는데도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"아직",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"아파요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"약을 먹었는데도 아직 아파요.",en:"Even though I took medicine, I'm still sick.",romanization:"yageul meogeonneundedo ajik apayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"돈이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많",mid:"은데도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"안",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"써요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"돈이 많은데도 잘 안 써요.",en:"Even though he has a lot of money, he barely spends it.",romanization:"doni maneundedo jal an sseoyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도",rows:[{when:"Verb stem (present)",add:"-는데도",ex:"먹다 → 먹는데도, 오다 → 오는데도"},{when:"Adjective stem ending in a vowel",add:"-ㄴ데도",ex:"비싸다 → 비싼데도, 아프다 → 아픈데도"},{when:"Adjective stem ending in a consonant",add:"-은데도",ex:"좋다 → 좋은데도, 많다 → 많은데도"},{when:"Past tense (verb or adjective)",add:"-았/었/였- + 는데도",ex:"먹다 → 먹었는데도, 비싸다 → 비쌌는데도"}]},more:{func:'Use -(으)ㄴ/는데도 for "even though A, B anyway," where B is surprising given A. It points out that the result happened in spite of the first clause.',keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄴ/는데도 vs. -지만",body:`-지만 is a flat "but." -(으)ㄴ/는데도 adds "and still," with a sense of surprise that B happened anyway. 약을 먹었지만 아파요 just states two facts; 약을 먹었는데도 아파요 sounds more like "I took the medicine and I'm STILL sick."`},pronunciation:'Past forms nasalize: 먹었는데도 sounds like [머건는데도], 했는데도 like [핸는데도] — the ㅅ turns into an ㄴ sound before 는. The 도 at the end gets a small push for the "and still" feeling.'}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Even though I study every day, Korean is hard.",sentence:"매일 공부하___ 한국어가 어려워요.",options:[{text:"는데도",ok:!0},{text:"은데도",ok:!1}],explanation:"Present verb 공부하- takes -는데도, the normal verb shape."}],canDo:"Say something happened in spite of A with A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도 (어려운데도 공부해요)",id:"chapter-25-b4",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 단점~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"단점",romanization:"danjeom",en:"drawback; weakness",pos:"noun",nuance:"단점 is Sino-Korean (短點, 短 'short/lacking' + 點 'point'), literally a 'short point' — a weakness, drawback, flaw, or shortcoming of a thing, plan, or person. It is the fixed antonym of 장점 (長點, merit), and the pair 장점과 단점 functions exactly like English 'pros and cons / strengths and weaknesses'. The key contrast to master is 단점 vs 약점 (弱點, 'weak point/vulnerability'): 단점 is a general flaw or downside that's just part of something (이 차의 단점 = this car's drawback, e.g. poor mileage), while 약점 is an exploitable vulnerability or sensitive spot — a rival attacks your 약점, a coach targets the opponent's 약점, and a personal sore subject is a 약점, not a 단점. Also compare 결점 (缺點, a 'defect/fault', slightly stronger, hints at something missing) and 흠 (a native word for a 'flaw/blemish'). Register-neutral. Collocations: 단점이 있다/없다, 단점을 고치다/보완하다 (fix/compensate), 단점을 인정하다 (admit a flaw), 큰 단점. The English-speaker pitfall: in self-introductions, 'my weakness' should be 단점 (a manageable shortcoming), not 약점 — calling it your 약점 sounds like you're confessing an exploitable vulnerability. And don't say 단점이 나쁘다 ('the drawback is bad') — that's redundant; just 단점이 있다."},sentence:{ko:"단점이 있어도 저는 괜찮아요.",en:"Even if there is a drawback, I am okay with it."},target:"단점",options:["but; by the way","but; however","drawback; weakness"],note:"Useful with -아/어도."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"괜찮다",romanization:"gwaenchanta",en:"to be okay; fine",pos:"adjective",nuance:"괜찮아요 can mean okay, fine, no thanks, or not bad. Context decides."},sentence:{ko:"조금 불편해도 괜찮아요.",en:"It is okay even if it is a little inconvenient."},target:"괜찮아",options:["to be difficult","to be okay; fine","to be easy"],note:"괜찮아요 softens concession."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"불편하다",romanization:"bulpyeonhada",en:"to be inconvenient; uncomfortable",pos:"adjective",nuance:"불편하다 fronts the Sino-Korean negative prefix 不 (불, 'not') onto 便 (편, 'convenient'), literally 'not-convenient', and is the all-purpose antonym of 편하다. It covers physical discomfort (tight shoes, a hard bed), practical inconvenience (poor transport, a clunky process), social unease (feeling awkward around someone), and gently, being unwell or physically impaired (몸이 불편하다 is the courteous phrase for illness or disability). It is the polite backbone of complaints — service desks ask 불편한 점이 있으셨어요? ('was anything inconvenient?'). Boundary note: 불편하다 is the broad 'uncomfortable/inconvenient', while 어색하다 zeroes in on the awkward unfamiliarity between people who aren't close, and 아프다 is plain physical pain. As a 하다 adjective it conjugates to 불편해요. English-speaker pitfall: the literal calque 안 편하다 is understood but sounds like a learner workaround — the natural single lexical antonym is 불편하다; and don't use 불편하다 for sharp pain, which is 아파요."},sentence:{ko:"조금 불편하지만 가능해요.",en:"It is a little inconvenient, but possible."},target:"불편하",options:["to be difficult","to be inconvenient; uncomfortable","to be easy"],note:"A realistic planning contrast."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가능하다",romanization:"ganeunghada",en:"to be possible",pos:"adjective",nuance:"가능하다 (可能, 可 'can/permissible' + 能 'ability') means 'possible / doable / available', and it is wonderfully practical for daily service questions — 예약 가능해요? ('Is a reservation possible?'), 카드 가능해요? ('Can I pay by card?'). In these, a noun sits directly before it and it neatly replaces a longer '-ㄹ 수 있다' clause, sounding crisp and businesslike. The softener 가능하면 ('if possible') makes requests polite, and the fixed frame 가능한 한 + adjective/adverb means 'as ~ as possible' (가능한 한 빨리 'as soon as possible') — note this is different from 가능한 N ('a possible N'). Its antonym is 불가능하다. One pitfall: while 가능하다 and '-ㄹ 수 있다' both render English 'can', '-ㄹ 수 있다' attaches to a verb stem describing an action you're able to do (갈 수 있어요 'I can go'), whereas 가능하다 attaches to a noun or stands alone judging feasibility — don't merge them into 갈 가능해요."},sentence:{ko:"어렵지만 가능해요.",en:"It is difficult, but possible."},target:"가능해",options:["to be possible","to be difficult","to be easy"],note:"Common with contrast."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"상관없다",romanization:"sanggwaneopda",en:"to not matter",pos:"adjective",nuance:"상관없다 is neutral and practical. It can sound indifferent, so use 괜찮아요 when you want a warmer tone."},sentence:{ko:"비싸도 상관없어요.",en:"It does not matter even if it is expensive."},target:"상관없어",options:["to be expensive","to be cheap","to not matter"],note:"A strong concession response."}],canDo:"Say a result holds no matter what with V-아/어도 (피곤해도 가요)",id:"chapter-25-b5",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어도",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어도",sub:"even if / even though",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"아무리",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"어도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"배가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"안",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"불러요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"아무리 먹어도 배가 안 불러요.",en:"No matter how much I eat, I don't get full.",romanization:"amuri meogeodo baega an bulleoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"시간이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없",mid:"어도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"밥은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"시간이 없어도 밥은 먹어요.",en:"Even when I have no time, I still eat.",romanization:"sigani eopseodo babeun meogeoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어도",rows:[{when:"Stem with ㅏ or ㅗ as the last vowel",add:"-아도",ex:"가다 → 가도, 오다 → 와도, 보다 → 봐도"},{when:"Stem with any other vowel",add:"-어도",ex:"먹다 → 먹어도, 읽다 → 읽어도, 마시다 → 마셔도"},{when:"하다 verbs and adjectives",add:"해도",ex:"공부하다 → 공부해도, 피곤하다 → 피곤해도"},{when:"Adjective stem 없다/있다",add:"-어도",ex:"없다 → 없어도, 맛있다 → 맛있어도"}]},more:{func:'Use -아/어도 for "even if" or "even though." It means that no matter how the first clause turns out, the second clause still holds.',keyPoint:{label:"-아/어도 vs. -(으)ㄴ/는데도",body:'Both can come out as "even though" in English, but -아/어도 covers "even IF" — it works for things that may or may not happen (비가 와도 = even if it rains, real or not). -(으)ㄴ/는데도 is for a real fact happening right now (비가 오는데도 = even though it IS raining). Use -아/어도 for conditions, -는데도 for facts.'},pronunciation:"Watch the vowel squeeze: 오다 → 와도, 보다 → 봐도, 마시다 → 마셔도. The 도 stays quiet; the squeezed syllable carries the beat."}}],canDo:"Say a result holds no matter what with V-아/어도 (피곤해도 가요)",id:"chapter-25-b6",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Friends decide whether to go to a popular restaurant despite price, crowds, and rain.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 식당은 비싸지만 정말 맛있어요.",en:"This restaurant is expensive, but really delicious.",romanization:"i sikdangeun bissajiman jeongmal masisseoyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"그런데 오늘은 사람이 너무 많아요.",en:"But there are too many people today.",romanization:"geureonde oneureun sarami neomu manayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"사람이 많아도 음식이 빨리 나와요.",en:"Even if there are many people, the food comes out quickly.",romanization:"sarami manhado eumsigi ppalli nawayo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"비가 오는데도 기다리는 사람이 많네요.",en:"Even though it is raining, there are many people waiting.",romanization:"biga oneundedo gidarineun sarami manneyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그래도 한번 가 볼까요?",en:"Even so, shall we try going?",romanization:"geuraedo hanbeon ga bolkkayo?"}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'It is hard, but fun.'",tokens:["어렵지만","재미있어요"],correct:"어렵지만 재미있어요",explanation:"-지만 gives a simple but contrast. The sentence balances the negative point with the positive result."}],canDo:"Join two opposing ideas with A/V-지만 (비싸지만 좋아요)",id:"chapter-25-b7",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A good plan with drawbacks",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A good plan with drawbacks",chunks:["민서는 새 식당에 가 보고 싶었어요. 그 식당은 비싸지만 음식이 맛있고 서비스도 좋다고 했어요. 지훈은 사람이 너무 많아서 걱정했지만, 민서는 사람이 많아도 음식이 빨리 나온다고 말했어요.","밖에는 비가 오는데도 기다리는 사람이 많았어요. 지훈은 조금 불편해도 괜찮다고 했고, 두 사람은 그래도 한번 가 보기로 했어요."],translation:"Minseo wanted to try a new restaurant. It was expensive, but people said the food was good and the service was good. Jihoon worried because there were many people, but Minseo said the food comes out quickly even when it is crowded. Even though it was raining, many people were waiting. They decided to try going anyway.",qas:[{q:"그 식당은 비싸지만 무엇이 좋아요?",a:"음식이 맛있고 서비스도 좋아요."},{q:"사람이 많아도 무엇이 빨리 나와요?",a:"음식이 빨리 나와요."},{q:"밖에는 비가 오는데도 무엇이 많았어요?",a:"기다리는 사람이 많았어요."}]}],canDo:"Say something happened in spite of A with A/V-(으)ㄴ/는데도 (어려운데도 공부해요)",id:"chapter-25-b8",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence contrasts price and quality?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비싸지만 맛있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"비싸서 맛있을 거예요.",ok:!1},{text:"비싸는 동안 맛있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-지만 connects two contrasting facts."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence keeps the plan despite rain?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 와도 가 볼까요?",ok:!0},{text:"비가 오다가 가 볼까요?",ok:!1},{text:"비가 오려고 가 볼까요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어도 means even if or even though."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"비___ 와도 갈 거예요. (Even if it rains, I will go.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"가",ok:!0},{text:"를",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"비 is the subject of 오다, so it takes 가. 를 would make rain an object, which does not fit the weather verb 오다."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'It is hard, but fun.'",tokens:["어렵지만","재미있어요"],correct:"어렵지만 재미있어요",explanation:"-지만 gives a simple but contrast. The sentence balances the negative point with the positive result."}],canDo:"Say a result holds no matter what with V-아/어도 (피곤해도 가요)",id:"chapter-25-b9",chapterId:"chapter-25",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-26",number:26,title:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 · V/A-나 봐요 / -(으)ㄴ가 봐요",goal:"Use A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for a soft prediction or impression.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 아마~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아마",romanization:"ama",en:"probably",pos:"adverb",nuance:"아마 means 'probably / perhaps,' expressing the speaker's reasonable guess, and its grammar is almost inseparable: it is normally completed by a conjecture ending — most often -ㄹ/을 거예요 ('will probably'), or -ㄹ 것 같다, -겠-, -ㄹ걸요. The bare statement 아마 비가 와요 sounds unfinished; you want 아마 비가 올 거예요. A near-synonym is 아마도, which is just a softer, slightly more deliberative version of 아마 (interchangeable; 아마도 can feel a touch more written or musing). Distinguish the certainty scale: 아마 is a fairly confident guess (~maybe 70-80%), 어쩌면/혹시 lean toward 'possibly, by any chance' (lower, more tentative), and 분명히/틀림없이 mean 'surely/definitely' (high certainty). The English-speaker pitfall is using 아마 with a flat present/past as if it were the English adverb that can float anywhere ('he's probably home'): in Korean the verb itself must carry the guessing form, so 'he's probably home' = 아마 집에 있을 거예요, not 아마 집에 있어요. Also avoid 아마 with established facts — 아마 물은 H2O일 거예요 sounds odd because you're hedging a certainty. Place 아마 near the front of the clause, and let the sentence-final ending do the conjecture work."},sentence:{ko:"아마 조금 늦을 것 같아요.",en:"I will probably be a little late."},target:"아마",options:["sky","probably","to be cloudy; to be blurry"],note:"아마 naturally reinforces a soft guess."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하늘",romanization:"haneul",en:"sky",pos:"noun",nuance:"하늘 is the pure-Korean word for the physical sky, and by extension 'heaven' in religious or poetic contexts (하느님 'God' literally contains it). It is one of the headword + 에 nouns: because the sky is a static location where things merely exist, you say 하늘에 구름이 있어요, never 하늘에서, since 에서 marks a place where an action happens. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly this 에 vs 에서 choice — 'a bird is flying IN the sky' becomes 하늘에서 새가 날아요 (action!), but 'there are clouds in the sky' stays 하늘에 (existence). A second trap is color: English keeps 'blue' fixed, but Korean 파랗다 is a ㅎ-irregular adjective, so 'the sky is blue' is 하늘이 파래요 (the ㅎ drops and a vowel shifts), not 파랗아요. Habitual collocations are 하늘이 맑다, 하늘이 흐리다, 하늘을 보다."},sentence:{ko:"하늘이 흐려요.",en:"The sky is cloudy."},target:"하늘",options:["rain","umbrella","sky"],note:"A common clue before a weather guess."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"흐리다",romanization:"heurida",en:"to be cloudy; to be blurry",pos:"adjective",nuance:"흐리다 is the all-purpose word for 'unclear,' covering CLOUDY weather (흐린 날 an overcast day), MURKY water (흐린 물), and BLURRY/out-of-focus images or hazy memories (사진이 흐리다, 기억이 흐리다). Its opposite is 맑다 (clear), and for weather these two are the everyday pair: 맑아요 (clear/sunny) ↔ 흐려요 (cloudy). The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 흐리다 to mean DIRTY: murky water that's hard to see through is 흐리다, but water or a surface that is dirty/filthy is 더럽다 — 흐리다 is about lack of CLARITY, not presence of grime. Note also a near-synonym for blurriness: 뿌옇다/희미하다 also describe a hazy, faint look, but 흐리다 is the plain default learners should master first. The conjugation trap: like other ㅣ-stems, 흐리다 contracts in the 해요-form to 흐려요 (not 흐리어요), past 흐렸어요. As a descriptive adjective it does not take 있다: 하늘이 흐려요, not 흐려 있어요."},sentence:{ko:"하늘이 흐려서 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"The sky is cloudy, so I think it will rain."},target:"흐려",options:["to be late","to be busy","to be cloudy; to be blurry"],note:"Weather clue plus prediction."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비",romanization:"bi",en:"rain",pos:"noun",nuance:"Korean has no single verb meaning 'to rain'; instead precipitation 'arrives', so you say 비가 와요 (rain comes) using 오다. A more formal, written variant is 비가 내리다 ('rain falls'), favored in forecasts and lyrics, while 비가 오다 dominates everyday speech. To say rain stops, use the dedicated verb 그치다 (비가 그쳤어요), not 끝나다. Note 비 also forms many compounds: 소나기 (sudden shower), 장마 (the summer monsoon rains), 가랑비 (drizzle). The big English-speaker pitfall is inventing a verb: learners say 비가 해요 or 비해요 by analogy with English 'it rains', but 하다 never combines with 비 — the natural pairing is strictly 비가 오다 / 비가 내리다. A second trap is 비를 맞다 ('to take the rain on your body', i.e. get drenched), which uses 맞다, not 받다."},sentence:{ko:"비가 올지도 몰라요.",en:"It might rain."},target:"비",options:["sky","umbrella","rain"],note:"비 takes 이/가 and pairs with 오다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"우산",romanization:"usan",en:"umbrella",pos:"noun",nuance:"우산 is Sino-Korean 雨傘, literally 雨 'rain' + 傘 'umbrella/parasol' — so the word transparently means 'rain-umbrella', which is your built-in reminder it's for rain. The verb is the deceptively busy 쓰다: to put up/use an umbrella is 우산을 쓰다, the same 쓰다 that covers wearing a hat (모자를 쓰다) and glasses (안경을 쓰다) — Korean groups 'things over your head/face' under 쓰다, distinct from 입다 (clothes on the torso/legs) and 신다 (footwear). The contrast to file away is 우산 vs 양산: both are 傘, but 우산 (雨傘) is the rain umbrella while 양산 (陽傘, 'sun-shade') is a parasol against sunlight — different words for different weather. Useful collocations: 우산을 쓰다/접다/펴다 (put up / fold / open an umbrella), 우산을 챙기다 (remember to grab an umbrella), 우산을 깜빡하다 (forget the umbrella). A folding umbrella is 접는 우산 or 3단 우산, a long one 장우산. Pitfall: English 'wear/carry an umbrella' must become 쓰다 (use over the head), never 입다, and don't reuse 우산 for a sun parasol — that's 양산."},sentence:{ko:"사람들이 우산을 꺼내네요.",en:"People are taking out umbrellas."},target:"우산",options:["sky","umbrella","rain"],note:"A visible clue for 비가 오나 봐요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"늦다",romanization:"neutda",en:"to be late",pos:"adjective",nuance:"늦다 is a descriptive verb, so it behaves like an adjective in Korean. Use 늦게 for the adverb 'late': 늦게 왔어요."},sentence:{ko:"길이 막혀서 조금 늦을 것 같아요.",en:"Traffic is heavy, so I think I will be a little late."},target:"늦을",options:["to be late","to be cloudy; to be blurry","to be certain"],note:"A high-frequency message sentence."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"하늘이 갑자기 흐려졌어요. 곧 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"The sky suddenly got cloudy. I think it will rain soon."},hl:"하늘"}],canDo:"Make a soft guess with A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-26-b1",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",sub:"",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"올",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"오늘 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"I think it'll rain today.",romanization:"oneul biga ol geot gatayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 영화 재미있을 것 같아요.",en:"This movie looks like it'll be fun.",romanization:"i yeonghwa jaemiisseul geot gatayo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel (verb or adjective)",add:"stem + -ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"오다 → 올 것 같아요 (it looks like it'll rain)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (verb or adjective)",add:"stem + -을 것 같아요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을 것 같아요 (I think I'll eat it)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"the ㄹ drops, then add -ㄹ 것 같아요 (so it looks unchanged)",ex:"살다 → 살 것 같아요 (it seems they'll live/stay)"},{when:"Guess about the past",add:"stem + -았/었을 것 같아요",ex:"가다 → 갔을 것 같아요 (I think they already left)"}]},more:{func:"A soft guess or gentle opinion — 'I think it'll…' or 'it looks like…'. This is the everyday, all-purpose way to make what you say sound less certain and a bit softer.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 vs. -는/(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",body:"Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for the future or something you haven't checked yet (비가 올 것 같아요 = you're looking at the clouds). Use -는 것 같아요 / -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요 for something happening right now or already done that you can sense (비가 오는 것 같아요 = you can hear the rain). The ㄹ form is more of a hunch; the 는/ㄴ form is closer to what you're actually noticing."},pronunciation:"것 같아요 sounds like [걷 까타요]: the final ㅅ of 것 turns into a [t] stop, which tenses the next ㄱ into [ㄲ], so you hear 'geot-kkatayo'."}}],canDo:"Make a soft guess with A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-26-b2",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 바쁘다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바쁘다",romanization:"bappeuda",en:"to be busy",pos:"adjective",nuance:"바쁘다 becomes 바빠요 in polite speech. It is a high-frequency reason word because 바빠서 못 가요 politely explains why a plan is impossible."},sentence:{ko:"답장이 없네요. 바쁜가 봐요.",en:"There is no reply. I guess they are busy."},target:"바쁜",options:["to be cloudy; to be blurry","to be busy","to be certain"],note:"Adjectives use -(으)ㄴ가 봐요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"피곤하다",romanization:"pigonhada",en:"to be tired",pos:"adjective",nuance:"피곤하다 is physical/general tiredness — worn out, drained, exhausted — typically after work, study, travel, or poor sleep. It is Sino-Korean 疲困 (疲 weary + 困 exhausted), literally 'weary-exhausted', which is why it reads as bodily fatigue rather than sleepiness. Keep it apart from 졸리다 ('sleepy'): you can be 피곤하다 yet wide awake (drained but not nodding off), and 졸리다 is specifically wanting to sleep — don't swap them. It is also milder and more everyday than 지치다 ('to be worn out/burned out'), which implies you've reached your limit. As a regular 하다-adjective it is 피곤해요 (not 피곤하요). One common English-speaker slip: 피곤하다 normally describes a PERSON being tired; to call an activity 'tiring' you also use 피곤하다 (피곤한 일 = 'tiring work'), but to call a person 'tiresome/draining' Koreans say 피곤한 사람 — meaning 'a person who is exhausting to deal with', a nuance worth knowing so you don't misread it as 'a tired person.'"},sentence:{ko:"목소리가 작아요. 피곤한가 봐요.",en:"Their voice is quiet. I guess they are tired."},target:"피곤한",options:["to be tired","to be cloudy; to be blurry","to be certain"],note:"Use for inferred tiredness in someone else."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"확실하다",romanization:"hwaksilhada",en:"to be certain",pos:"adjective",nuance:"확실하다 (確實, 確 'firm/sure' + 實 'real/true') expresses certainty and confidence — you're sure something is true, settled, or reliable. The adverb 확실히 ('certainly, definitely, for sure') is very common when answering or confirming (확실히 그래요 'that's definitely so'). The sharp contrast is with 분명하다: 확실하다 centres on the speaker's subjective confidence ('I'm sure of it'), while 분명하다 centres on a thing being objectively obvious/unambiguous — so 나는 확실해 ('I'm certain') but 증거가 분명해 ('the evidence is clear-cut'). It also brushes against 틀림없다 ('there's no mistake / definitely'), which is even more emphatic. Crucially, 확실하다 is for certainty, not possibility: to say 'maybe' you need 아마 ('probably') or '-ㄹ지도 몰라요' ('might'), not 확실하다. The natural adverb is 확실히 in everyday speech (확실하게 is a touch stiffer)."},sentence:{ko:"확실하지 않으니까 확인해 봐요.",en:"Since we are not sure, let's check."},target:"확실하",options:["to be cloudy; to be blurry","to be late","to be certain"],note:"Useful when deciding how strong your guess is."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가능성",romanization:"ganeungseong",en:"possibility; chance",pos:"noun",nuance:"가능성 is a noun, so it pairs with 있다, 없다, 높다, and 낮다. It is a little more formal than just saying -ㄹ지도 몰라요."},sentence:{ko:"비 올 가능성이 있어요.",en:"There is a chance it will rain."},target:"가능성",options:["sky","possibility; chance","rain"],note:"A noun-based way to talk about probability."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"단서",romanization:"danseo",en:"clue",pos:"noun",nuance:"단서 is more everyday and lighter than 증거 when you are only guessing. 증거 sounds more like evidence or proof."},sentence:{ko:"우산이 단서예요.",en:"The umbrella is the clue."},target:"단서",options:["clue","sky","rain"],note:"A clue supports -나 봐요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"추측하다",romanization:"chucheukada",en:"to guess; to infer",pos:"verb",nuance:"추측하다 is more explicit than -나 봐요. In conversation, the pattern often does the work, and 추측하다 names the thinking process."},sentence:{ko:"단서로 추측할 수 있어요.",en:"We can infer from the clue."},target:"추측할",options:["to stop; to let up","to guess; to infer","probably"],note:"Names the thinking process behind the grammar."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"확실하지 않으니까 카페 안에서 기다려요.",en:"Since we are not sure, let's wait inside the cafe."},hl:"확실하"}],canDo:"Guess from a clue I notice with V/A-나 봐요 (흐린가 봐요)",id:"chapter-26-b3",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-나 봐요 / -(으)ㄴ가 봐요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-나 봐요 / -(으)ㄴ가 봐요",sub:"",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"다",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹었",mid:"나",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"봐요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구가 밥을 다 먹었나 봐요.",en:"Looks like my friend finished eating.",romanization:"chinguga babeul da meogeonna bwayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"선생님이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"바쁜가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"봐요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"선생님이 바쁜가 봐요.",en:"The teacher seems busy.",romanization:"seonsaengnimi bappeunga bwayo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-나 봐요 / -(으)ㄴ가 봐요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem (and 있다/없다)",add:"stem + -나 봐요",ex:"오다 → 오나 봐요 (looks like someone's coming)"},{when:"Adjective stem ending in a vowel",add:"stem + -ㄴ가 봐요",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁜가 봐요 (she seems busy)"},{when:"Adjective stem ending in a consonant",add:"stem + -은가 봐요",ex:"좋다 → 좋은가 봐요 (it seems good)"},{when:"Past tense (verb and adjective)",add:"stem + -았/었나 봐요",ex:"가다 → 갔나 봐요 (looks like they left)"}]},more:{func:"A guess based on something you can see or hear — 'it seems like (because of what I notice)'. Unlike -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요, this one always means you're reading some clue in front of you.",keyPoint:{label:"-나 봐요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"-나 봐요 needs a real clue and is usually about other people or the world around you, not your own feelings — you wouldn't say 제가 배고프나 봐요 about yourself. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 can be a pure hunch with no clue and can be about you too (저 배고플 것 같아요). If you can point to something you noticed, -나 봐요 sounds more natural."},pronunciation:"Past forms like 먹었나 봐요 blend across the seam: 먹었나 comes out as [머건나] (the ㅆ becomes [n] before 나), so you hear 'meo-geon-na bwayo'."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Looks like it's raining outside.",sentence:"밖에 비가 오___.",options:[{text:"나 봐요",ok:!0},{text:"봐요",ok:!1}],explanation:"오다 → 오나 봐요; you hear the sound or see wet umbrellas."}],canDo:"Guess from a clue I notice with V/A-나 봐요 (흐린가 봐요)",id:"chapter-26-b4",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 모르다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"모르다",romanization:"moreuda",en:"to not know",pos:"verb",nuance:"모르다 is the standalone verb 'to not know / be unaware of', the lexical opposite of 알다, and it likewise takes the object marker 을/를: 길을 몰라요. It is a 르-irregular: when an 아/어 ending attaches, the 르 stem doubles the ㄹ and the vowel drops, so 모르 + 아요 → 몰라요 (never 모르아요) and the past is 몰랐어요. The crucial register tool is 겠: 잘 모르겠어요 ('I'm not quite sure') is markedly softer and more natural than a blunt 몰라요, which can sound curt or even dismissive depending on tone, so Koreans lean on the 겠 form constantly when admitting ignorance politely. Compare 모르다 with 헷갈리다 ('to be confused / mix things up'): 모르다 is having no information at all, while 헷갈리다 is having information but getting it tangled. The English-speaker pitfall is the conjugation — applying a regular pattern to get 모르아요 instead of the irregular 몰라요 — and forgetting that the smooth, courteous version is 잘 모르겠어요."},sentence:{ko:"늦을지도 몰라요.",en:"They might be late."},target:"몰라",options:["to stop; to let up","probably","to not know"],note:"In -ㄹ지도 몰라요, 모르다 is part of a fixed possibility pattern."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"곧",romanization:"got",en:"soon",pos:"adverb",nuance:"곧 means 'soon,' signaling that something will happen within a short time from the present. It strictly looks forward — 곧 can never describe the past. The key contrast is 곧 vs 금방: 금방 ('in a jiffy') is very casual and works both ways in time (it can even mean 'a moment ago' as in 금방 왔어요), while 곧 is cleaner, slightly more written/formal, and future-only, which is why station and airport announcements favor 곧. A second native-speaker pitfall is mixing 곧 (soon) with 이따가 (later today): 곧 means 'very shortly from now,' whereas 이따가 means 'later on, after some hours' — 곧 갈게요 ('I'm coming right away') is very different from 이따가 갈게요 ('I'll come by later'). 곧 is native Korean. The fixed compound 곧이어 ('coming up next') is worth memorizing as a unit."},sentence:{ko:"곧 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"I think it will rain soon."},target:"곧",options:["sky","to be cloudy; to be blurry","soon"],note:"Soon, from the current moment."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"금방",romanization:"geumbang",en:"soon; in a moment",pos:"adverb",nuance:"금방 is a two-faced time word: with a FUTURE verb it means 'in a moment / very soon' (금방 올게요 'I'll be right back'), and with a PAST verb it means 'just now / a moment ago' (금방 갔어요 'left just now'). The tense of the verb decides which reading you get, so the same word swings both ways around the present — that's the thing to internalize. The sharp contrast is 금방 vs 방금: 방금 'just now' is strictly PAST (방금 도착했어요 'I just arrived') and cannot mean 'soon', whereas 금방 covers both directions. Origin-wise 금방 is Sino-Korean 今方 'now-just', and it's thoroughly casual, everyday speech. The classic English-speaker error is forcing 방금 into a future sentence or assuming 금방 only means 'soon' and missing its 'a moment ago' use. It takes no particle, sits before the verb, and habitually pairs with 오다/가다, 되다 ('be ready'), and 끝나다."},sentence:{ko:"비가 금방 그칠지도 몰라요.",en:"The rain might stop soon."},target:"금방",options:["sky","soon; in a moment","to be cloudy; to be blurry"],note:"Often used for a short wait or quick change."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그치다",romanization:"geuchida",en:"to stop; to let up",pos:"verb",nuance:"그치다 is intransitive: 비가 그쳐요. If you stop doing an action yourself, use 멈추다 or 그만하다 depending on the sentence."},sentence:{ko:"비가 그쳤어요.",en:"The rain stopped."},target:"그쳤",options:["to stop; to let up","to guess; to infer","to not know"],note:"Weather uses 이/가 with 그치다."}],canDo:"Flag an uncertain possibility with A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요 (늦을지도 몰라요)",id:"chapter-26-b5",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요",sub:"",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"내일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"안",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"올지도",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"몰라요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"내일 친구가 안 올지도 몰라요.",en:"My friend might not come tomorrow.",romanization:"naeil chinguga an oljido mollayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가면",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없을지도",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"몰라요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"지금 가면 시간이 없을지도 몰라요.",en:"If we go now, we might not have time.",romanization:"jigeum gamyeon sigani eopseuljido mollayo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel (verb or adjective)",add:"stem + -ㄹ지도 몰라요",ex:"오다 → 올지도 몰라요 (it might rain)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (verb or adjective)",add:"stem + -을지도 몰라요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을지도 몰라요 (he might eat)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"the ㄹ drops, then add -ㄹ지도 몰라요",ex:"멀다 → 멀지도 몰라요 (it might be far)"},{when:"Possibility about the past",add:"stem + -았/었을지도 몰라요",ex:"가다 → 갔을지도 몰라요 (they might have already left)"}]},more:{func:"Says something 'might' or 'may' happen — a real but uncertain possibility, often one you want the other person to keep in mind, like 'it might rain, so just in case…'.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 leans toward 'probably' — you think it's fairly likely. -(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요 means 'it's just a possibility' — less likely, often flagged as something to watch out for just in case (비가 올 것 같아요 = I expect rain; 비가 올지도 몰라요 = it might rain, so bring an umbrella to be safe)."},pronunciation:"-지도 몰라요 keeps a clear [지도]. 몰라요 comes from 모르다 (모르 + 아요 → 몰라요), said [몰라요] with a strong double ㄹ."}}],canDo:"Flag an uncertain possibility with A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요 (늦을지도 몰라요)",id:"chapter-26-b6",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends look at weather and message clues before meeting someone.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"하늘이 갑자기 흐려졌어요. 곧 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"The sky suddenly got cloudy. I think it will rain soon.",romanization:"haneuri gapjagi heuryeojyeosseoyo. got biga ol geot gatayo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"사람들이 우산을 꺼내네요. 벌써 비가 조금 오나 봐요.",en:"People are taking out umbrellas. I guess it is already raining a little.",romanization:"saramdeuri usaneul kkeonaeneyo. beolsseo biga jogeum ona bwayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"수진 씨 답장도 없어요. 많이 바쁜가 봐요.",en:"Su-jin is not replying either. I guess she is really busy.",romanization:"sujin ssi dapjangdo eopseoyo. mani bappeunga bwayo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"버스도 막히면 조금 늦을지도 몰라요.",en:"If the bus is stuck too, she might be a little late.",romanization:"beoseudo makimyeon jogeum neujeuljido mollayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"확실하지 않으니까 카페 안에서 기다려요.",en:"Since we are not sure, let's wait inside the cafe.",romanization:"hwaksilhaji aneunikka kape aneseo gidaryeoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'There is no reply. I guess they are busy.'",tokens:["답장이 없네요.","바쁜가 봐요"],correct:"답장이 없네요. 바쁜가 봐요",explanation:"The missing reply is the clue. 바쁜가 봐요 gives an evidence-based guess about someone else."}],canDo:"Make a soft guess with A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-26-b7",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Reading clues before a meeting",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Reading clues before a meeting",chunks:["민서는 약속 장소로 가는 길에 하늘이 갑자기 흐려지는 것을 봤어요. 그래서 곧 비가 올 것 같다고 생각했어요. 지하철역 앞에서는 사람들이 우산을 꺼내고 있었어요. 민서는 “비가 조금 오나 봐요”라고 말했어요.","그런데 친구 수진 씨는 아직 답장이 없었어요. 지훈은 “수진 씨가 바쁜가 봐요. 조금 늦을지도 몰라요”라고 했어요. 두 사람은 확실하지 않으니까 밖에서 기다리지 않고 카페 안으로 들어갔어요."],translation:"Minseo saw the sky suddenly getting cloudy on the way to the meeting place, so she thought it would rain soon. In front of the subway station, people were taking out umbrellas, so she inferred that it was raining. Their friend Su-jin had not replied yet, so Jihoon guessed she might be busy and a little late. Since they were not sure, they waited inside a cafe instead of outside.",qas:[{q:"민서는 무엇을 보고 비가 올 것 같다고 생각했어요?",a:"하늘이 갑자기 흐려지는 것을 봤어요."},{q:"사람들이 우산을 꺼내는 것은 어떤 단서예요?",a:"“비가 조금 오나 봐요”라고 추측할 수 있는 단서예요."},{q:"두 사람은 왜 카페 안으로 들어갔어요?",a:"수진 씨가 바쁜지 늦는지 확실하지 않아서 카페 안으로 들어갔어요."}]}],canDo:"Guess from a clue I notice with V/A-나 봐요 (흐린가 봐요)",id:"chapter-26-b8",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"You see dark clouds and want a soft prediction. Which sentence fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 올 것 같아요.",ok:!0},{text:"비가 왔어요.",ok:!1},{text:"비가 오는 바람에요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-ㄹ 것 같아요 makes a soft prediction from the clue."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"People are taking out umbrellas. Which form best shows inference from evidence?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 오나 봐요.",ok:!0},{text:"비가 올 거예요.",ok:!1},{text:"비가 오기 위해서요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-나 봐요 says your guess comes from a clue you noticed."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You are not sure, but the bus may be late. Which sentence fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"버스가 늦을지도 몰라요.",ok:!0},{text:"버스가 늦어야 해요.",ok:!1},{text:"버스가 늦지 않아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-ㄹ지도 몰라요 keeps the possibility open."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"하늘___ 흐려요. (The sky is cloudy.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이",ok:!0},{text:"을",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"하늘 is the subject being described, so it takes 이. 을 would make the sky an object, which does not fit 흐리다."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'There is no reply. I guess they are busy.'",tokens:["답장이 없네요.","바쁜가 봐요"],correct:"답장이 없네요. 바쁜가 봐요",explanation:"The missing reply is the clue. 바쁜가 봐요 gives an evidence-based guess about someone else."}],canDo:"Flag an uncertain possibility with A/V-(으)ㄹ지도 몰라요 (늦을지도 몰라요)",id:"chapter-26-b9",chapterId:"chapter-26",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-27",number:27,title:"V-고 있다 · V-는 중이에요 / N 중이에요",goal:"Use V-고 있어요 for an action in progress.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 지금~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지금",romanization:"jigeum",en:"now",pos:"adverb",nuance:"지금 usually comes near the beginning of the sentence or before the verb phrase."},sentence:{ko:"지금 뭐 하고 있어요?",en:"What are you doing now?"},target:"지금",options:["middle; in progress","during; for a duration","now"],note:"The default opening for an ongoing-action check-in."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"중",romanization:"jung",en:"middle; in progress",pos:"noun",nuance:"중 feels more immediate than broad -고 있어요. It often sounds like 'right in the middle of it.'"},sentence:{ko:"회의 중이에요.",en:"I am in a meeting."},target:"중",options:["during; for a duration","meeting","middle; in progress"],note:"N 중 is a concise status reply."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"동안",romanization:"dongan",en:"during; for a duration",pos:"noun",nuance:"동안 is about overlapping time or duration. It does not mean the contrastive English 'while' in sentences like 'while this is cheap, that is expensive.'"},sentence:{ko:"기다리는 동안 책을 읽었어요.",en:"I read a book while waiting."},target:"동안",options:["during; for a duration","middle; in progress","meeting"],note:"동안 marks the overlapping time span."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공부하다",romanization:"gongbuhada",en:"to study",pos:"verb",nuance:"공부하다 is noun + 하다, so the short negative is often 공부 안 해요."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 공부하고 있어요.",en:"I am studying Korean."},target:"공부하",options:["to study","to wait","to eat"],note:"A basic V-고 있어요 example."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기다리다",romanization:"gidarida",en:"to wait",pos:"verb",nuance:"기다려 주세요 is one of the most useful service and classroom requests."},sentence:{ko:"친구를 기다리는 중이에요.",en:"I am waiting for a friend."},target:"기다리",options:["to study","to make","to wait"],note:"Waiting is a natural ongoing action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전화하다",romanization:"jeonhwahada",en:"to call by phone",pos:"verb",nuance:"전화하다 is noun + 하다, so 전화 안 해요 is a natural short negative."},sentence:{ko:"지금 전화하는 중이에요.",en:"I am on a call right now."},target:"전화하",options:["now","middle; in progress","to call by phone"],note:"전화 중이에요 is also common."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"지금 뭐 하고 있어요?",en:"What are you doing right now?"},hl:"지금"}],canDo:"Say an action is in progress with V-고 있다 (공부하고 있어요)",id:"chapter-27-b1",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-고 있다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-고 있다",sub:"an action in progress",rows:[{when:"Any verb stem (vowel or consonant): drop -다, add -고 있다",add:"-고 있다",ex:"먹다 → 먹고 있다 (eating), 보다 → 보고 있다 (watching)"},{when:"Polite present (talking now)",add:"-고 있어요",ex:"읽다 → 읽고 있어요 (am reading)"},{when:"Past — 'was -ing'",add:"-고 있었어요",ex:"자다 → 자고 있었어요 (was sleeping)"}],examples:[{ko:"지금 밥을 먹고 있어요.",en:"I'm eating right now.",romanization:"jigeum babeul meokgo isseoyo.",note:"지금 + -고 있어요 is the go-to pair for something happening this very moment."},{ko:"친구가 책을 읽고 있어요.",en:"My friend is reading a book.",romanization:"chinguga chaegeul ikko isseoyo.",note:"Other people work the same way; just change the subject to 친구가."}],more:{func:"Add -고 있다 to a verb stem to say an action is happening right now. It is the closest match to English 'be -ing' (am/is/are doing).",keyPoint:{label:"-고 있어요 vs. plain -아요/-어요",body:"먹어요 can mean 'I eat (usually)' or even 'I'll eat.' 먹고 있어요 pins it down to 'I'm eating right now.' Reach for -고 있어요 when you want to make clear the action is happening as you speak."},pronunciation:"있어요 runs together as [이써요], and -고 tenses to [꼬] after ㄱ, so 먹고 있어요 sounds like [먹꼬 이써요]."}}],canDo:"Say an action is in progress with V-고 있다 (공부하고 있어요)",id:"chapter-27-b2",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 회의~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"회의",romanization:"hoeui",en:"meeting",pos:"noun",nuance:"회의 (漢字 會議, 會 'gather' + 議 'discuss') is a formal, work-style meeting where people gather to discuss and decide something — a board meeting, a team sync, a planning session. The near-synonym learners reach for is 모임, but 모임 is a social get-together (a dinner, a club, friends meeting up) with no agenda, so calling drinks with friends a 회의 sounds robotic; conversely calling a budget review a 모임 sounds too loose. It pairs naturally with 회의를 하다 (hold), 회의에 들어가다 (go into), 회의가 길어지다 (run long). The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: you are *in* a meeting as a location, so the action takes 회의에서, but you *enter* a meeting with 회의에 들어가다 — and never confuse 회의 with the look-alike 회사 (company) or 회비 (membership fee)."},sentence:{ko:"회의하는 동안 메시지를 못 봤어요.",en:"I could not see the message during the meeting."},target:"회의",options:["meeting","middle; in progress","during; for a duration"],note:"A common reason for a delayed reply."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"메시지",romanization:"mesiji",en:"message",pos:"noun",nuance:"메시지 is the loanword from English 'message' and covers chat/app messages broadly (a KakaoTalk message, a DM); the everyday near-synonym 문자 (literally 文字 'letters/characters') specifically means an SMS text, so '문자 보내' = 'text me' while '메시지' is the wider term. There's also 카톡, the verb-able brand shorthand for a KakaoTalk message, used constantly in real life (카톡 보낼게 'I'll Kakao you'). The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: first, the recipient is marked with 한테/에게 ('to a person'), not the English bare object — 친구한테 메시지를 보내다, where 친구한테 = 'to my friend' and 메시지를 is the thing sent; second, the message arriving uses intransitive 오다 with 이/가 (메시지가 오다), but you sending it uses 보내다 with 을/를 (메시지를 보내다). Don't carry the English 'send him a message' double-object pattern straight over."},sentence:{ko:"메시지를 못 봤어요.",en:"I did not see the message."},target:"메시지",options:["middle; in progress","message","during; for a duration"],note:"Often explained with an ongoing action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아직",romanization:"ajik",en:"still; yet",pos:"adverb",nuance:"아직 covers both 'still' and 'not yet', and which one surfaces depends on polarity: with a POSITIVE verb it means 'still' (아직 자요 'still sleeping'), and with a NEGATIVE verb it means 'not yet' (아직 안 잤어요 'haven't slept yet'). It is the natural counterpart to 벌써/이미 — 아직 안 끝났어요 ('not finished yet') is the mirror of 벌써 끝났어요 ('already finished'). It often rides with the ongoing form -고 있다 to stress an action in progress (아직 자고 있어요 'still sleeping right now') and shows up in fixed phrases like 아직 멀었어요 ('still a long way off'). Register-neutral. The English-speaker pitfall is the 아직/벌써 confusion and the negation logic: because English 'yet' lives in questions/negatives, learners forget that Korean 아직 itself flips meaning with the verb's polarity — pair 아직 with 안/못 to say 'not yet', and use it with a plain positive verb to say 'still'."},sentence:{ko:"아직 회의 중이에요.",en:"I am still in a meeting."},target:"아직",options:["still; yet","middle; in progress","during; for a duration"],note:"아직 pairs naturally with ongoing status."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"벌써",romanization:"beolsseo",en:"already",pos:"adverb",nuance:"벌써 means 'already' and often signals that an event or state is earlier than expected. It commonly appears with completed events and present states, and it can also modify an imminent plan in a surprised question such as 벌써 갈 거예요? ('Are you leaving already?'). 곧 means 'soon' and is not a replacement for that use."},sentence:{ko:"벌써 끝났어요?",en:"Is it already finished?"},target:"벌써",options:["already","middle; in progress","during; for a duration"],note:"Contrasts naturally with 아직."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계속",romanization:"gyesok",en:"continuously; keep doing",pos:"adverb",nuance:"계속 presents an action, state, or series as continuing across a span; it does not guarantee that there are literally no gaps. 자꾸 foregrounds recurrence and often adds irritation or undesirability, while 계속 foregrounds continuation, so 계속 전화가 와요 and 자꾸 전화가 와요 can both be natural with different emphasis."},sentence:{ko:"계속 공부하고 있어요.",en:"I am still studying / I keep studying."},target:"계속",options:["middle; in progress","during; for a duration","continuously; keep doing"],note:"계속 emphasizes the action continues."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"잠깐",romanization:"jamkkan",en:"for a moment; briefly",pos:"adverb",nuance:"잠깐 means 'for a short moment / briefly', a span felt in seconds to a few minutes — never a long stretch. It's everyday, conversational, and the derived 잠깐만요 is one of the single most useful phrases a learner can own: it's how you say 'wait a sec / excuse me, hold on' to stop someone politely. The near-twin to contrast is 잠시: 잠시 means the same 'briefly' but sounds a touch more formal and refined, so you hear 잠시 후에 ('shortly') and 잠시만 기다려 주세요 in announcements, whereas 잠깐 dominates casual speech. Both are essentially native-flavored time words taking no particle. The English-speaker pitfall is stretching 잠깐 to cover a genuinely long wait ('I waited a while' that lasted an hour isn't 잠깐) and adding 에 (✗ 잠깐에). Place it before the verb; it collocates with 기다리다, 쉬다, 시간 있다, and the attention-getting 잠깐만(요)."},sentence:{ko:"잠깐 쉬고 있어요.",en:"I am resting for a moment."},target:"잠깐",options:["middle; in progress","during; for a duration","for a moment; briefly"],note:"Useful for short status updates."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아까 회의하는 동안 메시지를 못 봤어요.",en:"Earlier, I could not see the message during the meeting."},hl:"회의"}],canDo:"Say I'm in the middle of something with V-는 중이에요 (회의 중이에요)",id:"chapter-27-b3",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-는 중이에요 / N 중이에요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-는 중이에요 / N 중이에요",sub:"in the middle of",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"중이에요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"지금 밥을 먹는 중이에요.",en:"I'm in the middle of eating right now.",romanization:"jigeum babeul meongneun jungieyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"중이에요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"지금 공부 중이에요.",en:"I'm studying right now.",romanization:"jigeum gongbu jungieyo."}],rule:{name:"V-는 중이에요 / N 중이에요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem + -는 중이에요 (vowel or consonant stem)",add:"-는 중이에요",ex:"먹다 → 먹는 중이에요 (in the middle of eating)"},{when:"ㄹ-stem verb: drop ㄹ, then add -는 중이에요",add:"-는 중이에요",ex:"만들다 → 만드는 중이에요 (in the middle of making)"},{when:"Noun + 중이에요 (no 을/를)",add:" 중이에요",ex:"회의 → 회의 중이에요 (in a meeting), 공부 → 공부 중이에요"}]},more:{func:"중 means 'the middle of.' Add -는 중이에요 to a verb stem, or just 중이에요 to a noun, to say you're 'in the middle of' something right now.",keyPoint:{label:"-는 중이에요 vs. -고 있어요",body:"Both mean 'be doing.' -는 중이에요 foregrounds an activity or situation as a current phase and is especially natural with bounded actions such as eating or waiting. 한국에 살고 있어요 is the neutral way to say 'I live in Korea'; 한국에 사는 중이에요 is also possible when the speaker deliberately frames living there as a current or temporary stage."},pronunciation:"먹는 → ㄱ before ㄴ turns into [ㅇ] (nasal), so 먹는 sounds like [멍는]: [멍는 중이에요]."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I'm waiting for my friend.",sentence:"친구를 기다리___.",options:[{text:"는 중이에요",ok:!0},{text:"중이에요",ok:!1}],explanation:"기다리다 ends in a vowel and just takes -는 중이에요."}],canDo:"Say I'm in the middle of something with V-는 중이에요 (회의 중이에요)",id:"chapter-27-b4",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 일하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"일하다",romanization:"ilhada",en:"to work",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 에서 for where an action happens: 회사에서 일해요."},sentence:{ko:"지금 일하는 중이에요.",en:"I am working right now."},target:"일하",options:["now","to work","middle; in progress"],note:"Natural quick reply to why you cannot talk."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쉬다",romanization:"swida",en:"to rest",pos:"verb",nuance:"쉬다 is used for taking a break, resting, or being off work/school."},sentence:{ko:"잠깐 쉬고 있어요.",en:"I am resting for a moment."},target:"쉬고",options:["to rest","now","middle; in progress"],note:"A simple ongoing action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹다",romanization:"meokda",en:"to eat",pos:"verb",nuance:"먹다 is used for meals and food. For medicine, Korean often uses 약을 먹어요, literally “eat medicine,” which means “take medicine.”"},sentence:{ko:"지금 밥 먹는 중이에요.",en:"I am eating right now."},target:"먹는",options:["to study","now","to eat"],note:"먹다 becomes 먹는 before 중."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"만들다",romanization:"mandeulda",en:"to make",pos:"verb",nuance:"만들다 means 'to make / create / build' something concrete or abstract (food, objects, plans, rules) and takes the object marker 을/를: 음식을 만들어요. It is a ㄹ-irregular verb, so the stem ㄹ drops before ㅂ, ㄴ, and 시 — 만듭니다, 만드세요, 만드니까 — but the ㄹ stays before vowel endings, giving the polite present 만들어요 (never 만드러요) and the suggestion 만들까요. A key contrast is 만들다 vs 하다: both can translate as 'make/do', but 만들다 implies producing a tangible result that did not exist before (케이크를 만들다 'make a cake'), whereas 하다 is to perform an activity (요리를 하다 'do cooking'). Also distinguish 짓다 ('to build/construct' houses, and to compose names/poems, 집을 짓다) from 만들다, which is the broad everyday 'make'. The English-speaker pitfall is mishandling the irregular in both directions: dropping the ㄹ before a vowel (만드러요) or keeping it before a consonant (만들습니다 instead of 만듭니다)."},sentence:{ko:"발표 자료를 만드는 중이에요.",en:"I am making presentation materials."},target:"만드는",options:["to study","to make","to wait"],note:"ㄹ drops before 는: 만드는."}],canDo:"Set one action against a stretch of time with V-는 동안 (기다리는 동안)",id:"chapter-27-b5",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-는 동안",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-는 동안",sub:"while / during",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"요리하",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"친구는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"청소했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"제가 요리하는 동안 친구는 청소했어요.",en:"While I cooked, my friend cleaned.",romanization:"jega yorihaneun dongan chinguneun cheongsohaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"동생이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"책을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"읽었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"동생이 자는 동안 저는 책을 읽었어요.",en:"While my little brother was sleeping, I read a book.",romanization:"dongsaengi janeun dongan jeoneun chaegeul ilgeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-는 동안",rows:[{when:"Verb stem + -는 동안 (vowel or consonant stem)",add:"-는 동안",ex:"먹다 → 먹는 동안 (while eating), 자다 → 자는 동안 (while sleeping)"},{when:"ㄹ-stem verb: drop ㄹ, then add -는 동안",add:"-는 동안",ex:"살다 → 사는 동안 (while living)"},{when:"Noun + 동안 ('for / during' a stretch of time)",add:" 동안",ex:"한 시간 → 한 시간 동안 (for one hour), 방학 → 방학 동안 (during vacation)"}]},more:{func:"Add -는 동안 to a verb stem to present a time interval in which the main event occurs. The main event may fill all of that interval or occur at some point within it; context determines the amount of overlap.",keyPoint:{label:"-는 동안 (always 는) vs. -(으)ㄹ 때",body:"-는 동안 always keeps -는, no matter the tense, because it marks an interval. 때 ('when') can point to a moment or occasion, while 동안 foregrounds the containing stretch of time. The main event may fill the interval or happen at a point within it."},pronunciation:"먹는 동안 → 먹는 nasalizes to [멍는] (ㄱ+ㄴ → ㅇ+ㄴ), so it's [멍는 동안]."}}],canDo:"Set one action against a stretch of time with V-는 동안 (기다리는 동안)",id:"chapter-27-b6",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A KakaoTalk check-in about current status and a delayed reply.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"지금 뭐 하고 있어요?",en:"What are you doing right now?",romanization:"jigeum mwo hago isseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"도서관에서 한국어를 공부하고 있어요.",en:"I am studying Korean at the library.",romanization:"doseogwaneseo hangugeoreul gongbuhago isseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"저는 카페에서 친구를 기다리는 중이에요.",en:"I am waiting for a friend at a cafe.",romanization:"jeoneun kapeeseo chingureul gidarineun jungieyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아까 회의하는 동안 메시지를 못 봤어요.",en:"Earlier, I could not see the message during the meeting.",romanization:"akka hoeuihaneun dongan mesijireul mot bwasseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"괜찮아요. 끝나면 잠깐 전화해 주세요.",en:"It is okay. Please call for a moment when you finish.",romanization:"gwaenchanayo. kkeunnamyeon jamkkan jeonhwahae juseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I am waiting for a friend now.' (Start with 지금.)",tokens:["지금","친구를","기다리고 있어요"],correct:"지금 친구를 기다리고 있어요",explanation:"지금 sets the current moment. 기다리고 있어요 shows the waiting is happening now."}],canDo:"Say an action is in progress with V-고 있다 (공부하고 있어요)",id:"chapter-27-b7",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Why the reply was late",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Why the reply was late",chunks:["지훈은 도서관에서 한국어를 공부하고 있었어요. 민서가 메시지를 보냈지만 지훈은 바로 답장하지 못했어요. 그때 지훈은 회의하는 동안 휴대폰을 보지 못했고, 회의가 끝난 뒤에도 발표 자료를 만드는 중이었어요.","민서는 카페에서 친구를 기다리는 중이라 시간이 조금 있었어요. 그래서 두 사람은 지훈이 끝나면 잠깐 전화하기로 했어요."],translation:"Jihoon was studying Korean at the library. Minseo sent a message, but Jihoon could not reply right away. During the meeting he could not check his phone, and after the meeting he was still making presentation materials. Minseo had a little time because she was waiting for a friend at a cafe, so they decided to call briefly when Jihoon finished.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 어디에서 한국어를 공부하고 있었어요?",a:"도서관에서 한국어를 공부하고 있었어요."},{q:"지훈은 왜 메시지를 바로 못 봤어요?",a:"회의하는 동안 휴대폰을 보지 못했어요."},{q:"민서는 무엇을 하고 있었어요?",a:"카페에서 친구를 기다리는 중이었어요."}]}],canDo:"Say I'm in the middle of something with V-는 중이에요 (회의 중이에요)",id:"chapter-27-b8",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence describes an action in progress?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한국어를 공부하고 있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"한국어를 공부했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"한국어를 공부할 거예요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-고 있어요 marks an ongoing action."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence is the best short status reply?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"회의 중이에요.",ok:!0},{text:"회의가 될 거예요.",ok:!1},{text:"회의를 좋아해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"N 중이에요 is a compact way to say an activity is in progress."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"During the meeting, I could not see the message.",sentence:null,options:[{text:"회의하는 동안 메시지를 못 봤어요.",ok:!0},{text:"회의하려고 메시지를 못 봤어요.",ok:!1},{text:"회의보다 메시지를 못 봤어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-는 동안 gives the time span during another action."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"회의___ 하는 중이에요. (I am in the middle of a meeting.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"회의를 하다 is the action phrase “to have/do a meeting.” 회의를 하는 중이에요 means you are in the middle of that action."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I am waiting for a friend now.' (Start with 지금.)",tokens:["지금","친구를","기다리고 있어요"],correct:"지금 친구를 기다리고 있어요",explanation:"지금 sets the current moment. 기다리고 있어요 shows the waiting is happening now."}],canDo:"Set one action against a stretch of time with V-는 동안 (기다리는 동안)",id:"chapter-27-b9",chapterId:"chapter-27",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-28",number:28,title:"V-고 나서 · V-자마자",goal:"Use V-고 나서 when one action is finished before the next starts.",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 먼저~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먼저",romanization:"meonjeo",en:"first",pos:"adverb",nuance:"먼저 means 'first / beforehand / ahead (of others)' — it ranks one action or person EARLIER in a sequence or in time. The key contrast is 먼저 vs 처음: 먼저 is relative ('before the other thing/person', e.g. 먼저 가요 'go ahead of you'), while 처음 means 'the first time ever / the beginning' (처음 만나요 'meeting for the first time'). So 먼저 봤어요 = 'I saw it first (before you)', but 처음 봤어요 = 'I saw it for the first time'. 먼저 is also a courtesy word: 먼저 가세요 / 먼저 하세요 ('go ahead, you first') is everyday politeness, and 먼저 갈게요 / 먼저 들어가겠습니다 softens leaving before others. Register-neutral. The English-speaker pitfall is mapping every 'first' to one word — choose 먼저 for order-relative 'first/ahead', but 처음 for 'first-ever/at first', and keep 먼저 before the verb."},sentence:{ko:"먼저 손을 씻어요.",en:"First, I wash my hands."},target:"먼저",options:["to wash","first","to eat"],note:"Marks step one."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그다음",romanization:"geudaeum",en:"then; after that",pos:"adverb",nuance:"그다음 is conversational and clear. In writing, 그 후 or 이후 can sound more formal."},sentence:{ko:"그다음 아침을 먹어요.",en:"Then I eat breakfast."},target:"그다음",options:["to wash","then; after that","to eat"],note:"Names the next step in a routine."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바로",romanization:"baro",en:"right away; immediately",pos:"adverb",nuance:"바로 carries two everyday senses split by context: 'right away / immediately' with actions (바로 가요, 바로 출발해요) and 'exactly / right (there)' with places and identifications (바로 여기, 바로 앞, 바로 그거예요). For the time sense, its near-synonym 곧 means 'soon / shortly' — a small gap remains — while 바로 means truly 'this instant, with zero delay', so 바로 가요 is more immediate than 곧 가요. For the location/identification sense, 바로 tightens 'next to/in front of' into 'directly, precisely' and has no single English twin; it behaves like 'right' in 'right next to'. It collocates tightly with position words (바로 옆/앞/뒤/위) and with emphatic confirmations (바로 그거, 바로 너 'you, exactly'). The English-speaker pitfall is assuming 바로 only means 'immediately' and missing its 'exactly/right-there' use, plus stranding it from the word it marks — keep 바로 hugging the verb or noun (바로 앞, not 앞 바로)."},sentence:{ko:"준비하고 나서 바로 출발했어요.",en:"After getting ready, I left right away."},target:"바로",options:["to wash","right away; immediately","to eat"],note:"바로 pairs naturally with sequence sentences."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"방금",romanization:"banggeum",en:"just now",pos:"adverb",nuance:"방금 (方今, 方 'just/at the time' + 今 'now') is the tightest 'recent past' word, pointing to something that happened only seconds to a couple of minutes ago. The contrast learners need most is 방금 vs 아까: 방금 is 'this very moment past,' while 아까 stretches to 'earlier today' (potentially hours back). 방금 나갔어요 = 'they left seconds ago'; 아까 나갔어요 = 'they left a while ago.' Compare also 방금 vs 막: both can mean 'just now,' but 막 emphasizes the instantaneous edge ('right as it happened') and pairs with 지금/참이다, whereas 방금 is a standalone time word and a touch more neutral. Because of its Sino-Korean root, the variant 방금 전(에) ('just a moment before') is common. 방금 is strictly immediate past — never future. Register is neutral and fits both casual and polite speech, which is why staff use it (방금 주문하신…)."},sentence:{ko:"방금 도착했어요.",en:"I just arrived."},target:"방금",options:["to wash","just now","to eat"],note:"Recent completion, not necessarily immediate sequence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이미",romanization:"imi",en:"already",pos:"adverb",nuance:"이미 means 'already,' emphasizing that an action or state is completed and now fixed — often colored by 'and there's no changing it now.' The sharp contrast every learner needs is 이미 vs 벌써. Both translate as 'already,' but 이미 is neutral and factual ('it is already done, that's just the situation'), while 벌써 adds emotional surprise that it happened so fast or so soon ('already?! that quick?'). 이미 끝났어요 = a calm statement of fact; 벌써 끝났어요?! = 'it's over already?!' with raised eyebrows. So choose 이미 when calmly noting that the moment/deadline has passed, and 벌써 when reacting to unexpected speed. 이미 is native Korean and is also future-blind: it only describes things already done, never things about to happen. It pairs naturally with completed clauses and with -더라고요, -았/었- past forms."},sentence:{ko:"이미 예약했어요.",en:"I already made a reservation."},target:"이미",options:["already","to wash","to eat"],note:"Completion before now."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"씻다",romanization:"ssitda",en:"to wash",pos:"verb",nuance:"씻다 means to wash with water and, despite the tense-looking ㅅ, is a perfectly regular verb: the ㅅ stays in every form, giving 씻어요, 씻었어요, 씻습니다 (the ㅅ never drops the way it does in true ㅅ-irregular verbs like 짓다→지어요). It covers washing hands, face, body, and rinsing food. Korean, however, distributes 'wash/clean' across several verbs you must keep apart: 씻다 is for rinsing body parts and food; 감다 is reserved for washing hair (머리를 감다, never 머리를 씻다); 닦다 is 'wipe/scrub/polish' and is what you use for brushing teeth (이를 닦다) and wiping surfaces; and 빨다 is for washing laundry (빨래를 빨다), often replaced by 세탁하다 for machine washing 빨래하다. The whole-body version is 씻다 generically, or the Sino-Korean 샤워하다 'shower'. The English-speaker pitfall is that English 'wash' is one word, so learners say 머리를 씻다 for 'wash my hair' (wrong → 감다) or 이를 씻다 for 'brush teeth' (wrong → 닦다)."},sentence:{ko:"손을 씻고 나서 밥을 먹어요.",en:"After washing my hands, I eat."},target:"씻고",options:["to wash","to eat","to grab; to pack; to take care of"],note:"A textbook-clear completion sequence."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"먼저 손을 씻고 나서 아침을 먹었어요.",en:"First, after washing my hands, I ate breakfast."},hl:"먼저"}],canDo:"Order two actions with V-고 나서 (먹고 나서 가요)",id:"chapter-28-b1",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-고 나서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-고 나서",sub:"after finishing, then",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"나서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"이를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"닦아요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"밥을 먹고 나서 이를 닦아요.",en:"After I eat, I brush my teeth.",romanization:"bap-eul meokgo naseo i-reul dakkayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"숙제를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하",mid:"고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"나서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"친구를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"만났어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"숙제를 하고 나서 친구를 만났어요.",en:"After I did my homework, I met a friend.",romanization:"sukje-reul hago naseo chingu-reul mannasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-고 나서",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ending in a vowel",add:"-고 나서",ex:"보다 → 보고 나서, 자다 → 자고 나서"},{when:"Verb stem ending in a consonant",add:"-고 나서",ex:"먹다 → 먹고 나서, 씻다 → 씻고 나서"},{when:"ㄹ-final stems (nothing drops here)",add:"-고 나서",ex:"놀다 → 놀고 나서, 만들다 → 만들고 나서"},{when:"Never put past tense on this verb",add:'keep the stem plain — 나서 already means "after"',ex:"먹었고 나서 (X) → 먹고 나서 (O)"}]},more:{func:'V-고 나서 means "after doing V (and finishing it)." It tells the listener that the first action is done before the next one starts. Usually the same person does both actions.',keyPoint:{label:"-고 나서 vs. plain -고",body:`Plain -고 just links two actions like "and": 밥을 먹고 이를 닦아요 (I eat and brush my teeth). Adding 나서 puts the spotlight on finishing the first one before starting the next. Reach for -고 나서 when you want "after I'm done with this, then that."`},pronunciation:"You hear it as [고 나서]. Note that 고 tenses after a stem ending in a stop sound: 먹고 나서 sounds like [먹꼬 나서], 받고 나서 like [받꼬 나서]. After a vowel (보고 나서) it stays soft. Keep 나서 light so it links right on."}}],canDo:"Order two actions with V-고 나서 (먹고 나서 가요)",id:"chapter-28-b2",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 먹다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹다",romanization:"meokda",en:"to eat",pos:"verb",nuance:"먹다 is used for meals and food. For medicine, Korean often uses 약을 먹어요, literally “eat medicine,” which means “take medicine.”"},sentence:{ko:"아침을 먹고 나서 출발해요.",en:"After eating breakfast, I leave."},target:"먹고",options:["to wash","to depart","to eat"],note:"Eating finishes before departure."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"준비하다",romanization:"junbihada",en:"to prepare; to get ready",pos:"verb",nuance:"준비하다 comes from 한자 準備 (準 'standard, level' + 備 'provide, equip'), literally 'get equipped to standard' — to ready things or yourself beforehand. It naturally collocates with events and outcomes: 시험을 준비하다, 여행을 준비하다, 저녁을 준비하다, 마음의 준비. Compare 마련하다 ('arrange/procure' funds or a place — 집을 마련하다) and 챙기다 ('gather up, pack' your own belongings — 여권 챙겼어? = did you grab your passport?); 준비하다 is the broad 'prepare,' while 챙기다 zooms in on not forgetting items. The classic English-speaker pitfall is the particle: 'prepare FOR the exam' tempts 시험에 준비하다, but Korean treats the exam as a direct object — 시험을 준비하다. Also don't reach for 만들다 'make' when you only mean getting ready. As a 하다 verb, negate inside (준비 안 해요), and its passive 준비되다 ('be ready') is everywhere: 준비됐어요."},sentence:{ko:"준비하고 나서 바로 출발했어요.",en:"After getting ready, I left right away."},target:"준비하",options:["first","to prepare; to get ready","then; after that"],note:"A natural morning or trip step."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"출발하다",romanization:"chulbalhada",en:"to depart",pos:"verb",nuance:"출발하다 is Sino-Korean 出發(출발: 出 go out + 發 set off/shoot) plus 하다, the crisp word for a journey or vehicle SETTING OFF from a starting line. Contrast it with 떠나다(leave): 출발하다 is neutral and logistical, fixed on the departure point and time (기차가 출발하다), so a station announcer uses it; 떠나다 carries the emotional weight of leaving something or someone behind. The starting place takes 에서 (부산에서 출발해요) and the time takes 에 (3시에 출발해요). Because it's a 하다 verb the stem 하 + 아 fuses to 해, so present is 출발해요 and the negative splits as 출발 안 해요. English-speaker pitfall: don't mark the start point with 에 — you depart FROM somewhere, so it's 공항에서 출발해요, and the opposite endpoint verb is 도착하다."},sentence:{ko:"지갑을 챙기고 나서 출발했어요.",en:"After grabbing my wallet, I left."},target:"출발했",options:["first","to depart","then; after that"],note:"The departure waits until the previous step is complete."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도착하다",romanization:"dochakada",en:"to arrive",pos:"verb",nuance:"도착하다 is Sino-Korean 到着(도착: 到 reach + 着 attach/touch down) plus 하다 — the moment of REACHING and touching a destination. Its mirror is 출발하다(depart), and the key is the particle: you arrive AT a place with 에 (호텔에 도착해요), whereas 출발하다 leaves FROM a place with 에서. Don't confuse it with 들어가다/들어오다(enter): 도착하다 is reaching the destination's threshold, not stepping inside a building. As a 하다 verb, 하 + 아 fuses to 해 (도착해요) and the short negative and inability split the noun off: 도착 안 해요, 도착 못 해요. The classic English-speaker error is copying 'arrive at' as 에서 — 'at' here is the static endpoint 에, so 공항에 도착해요, and 공항에서 도착해요 is wrong."},sentence:{ko:"도착하자마자 전화해 주세요.",en:"Please call as soon as you arrive."},target:"도착하",options:["first","to arrive","then; after that"],note:"A core -자마자 sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"확인하다",romanization:"hwaginhada",en:"to check",pos:"verb",nuance:"확인하다 (確認하다, 確 'certain/firm' + 認 'recognize') means to check, verify, or confirm that something is correct or true — facts, times, reservations, messages — making sure reality matches what you expect. The crucial contrast is with 찾다, which English 'check' can blur: 찾다 is 'to look FOR / search' something not yet located (열쇠를 찾다, 'look for the keys'), whereas 확인하다 is 'to verify' something you can already see (열쇠가 있는지 확인하다, 'check whether the keys are there'). It also differs from 알아보다 ('look into/inquire to find out', 가격을 알아보다). It's a regular noun+하다 verb: 확인하 + 여요 → 확인해요, past 확인했어요, with 안/못 splitting before 하다 (확인 안 해요, 확인 못 해요). English-speaker pitfall: 'check the box' or 'check off' is NOT 확인하다 — ticking a box is 체크하다/표시하다; and 'check out a book/check out (a hotel)' uses 빌리다 or 체크아웃하다, not 확인하다, which strictly means 'verify/confirm'."},sentence:{ko:"예약을 확인하고 나서 올라갔어요.",en:"After checking the reservation, I went up."},target:"확인하",options:["to check","to wash","to eat"],note:"Common travel or hotel sequence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"예약하다",romanization:"yeyakada",en:"to reserve; to book",pos:"verb",nuance:"예약하다 works for restaurants, hotels, tickets, classes, and appointments."},sentence:{ko:"이미 예약했어요.",en:"I already made a reservation."},target:"예약했",options:["first","then; after that","to reserve; to book"],note:"A completed action before arrival."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"먼저 손을 씻고 나서 아침을 먹었어요.",en:"First, after washing my hands, I ate breakfast."},hl:"먹었"}],canDo:"Say one thing follows another instantly with V-자마자 (도착하자마자 전화해요)",id:"chapter-28-b3",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-자마자",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-자마자",sub:"as soon as",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"집에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오",mid:"자마자",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"손을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"씻어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"집에 오자마자 손을 씻어요.",en:"As soon as I get home, I wash my hands.",romanization:"jib-e ojamaja son-eul ssiseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"영화가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"끝나",mid:"자마자",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"집에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"갔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"영화가 끝나자마자 집에 갔어요.",en:"As soon as the movie ended, I went home.",romanization:"yeonghwa-ga kkeunnajamaja jib-e gasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-자마자",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ending in a vowel",add:"-자마자",ex:"오다 → 오자마자, 보다 → 보자마자"},{when:"Verb stem ending in a consonant",add:"-자마자",ex:"듣다 → 듣자마자, 끝나다 → 끝나자마자"},{when:"ㄹ-final stems (nothing drops here)",add:"-자마자",ex:"열다 → 열자마자, 울다 → 울자마자"},{when:"Never put past tense on this verb",add:"keep the stem plain — tense goes on the second half only",ex:"왔자마자 (X) → 오자마자 (O)"}]},more:{func:'V-자마자 means "as soon as / the moment V happens." The second thing comes right after the first, with no time in between. The two halves can have the same person or different ones.',keyPoint:{label:"-자마자 vs. -고 나서",body:`Both put one action before another, but the timing is different. -고 나서 is "after finishing," and a pause is fine: 밥을 먹고 나서 좀 쉬었어요 (I ate, then rested a bit). -자마자 is "the very moment," no gap at all: 집에 오자마자 잤어요 (got home and went straight to sleep). Use -자마자 only when there's truly no time in between.`},pronunciation:"You hear it as [자마자], three even, light beats. After a stem ending in a stop sound it tenses a little: 듣자마자 sounds like [듣짜마자], 눕자마자 like [눕짜마자]. After a vowel (오자마자) it stays soft. No pause before it."}}],canDo:"Say one thing follows another instantly with V-자마자 (도착하자마자 전화해요)",id:"chapter-28-b4",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 챙기다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"챙기다",romanization:"chaenggida",en:"to grab; to pack; to take care of",pos:"verb",nuance:"챙기다 is one of the most useful 'caring' verbs in daily Korean, with a cluster of related senses: (1) gather/pack the things you need (짐을 챙기다), (2) make sure not to skip something, especially food (밥 챙겨 먹어 'make sure you eat'), and (3) look after a person attentively (동생 좀 챙겨 'keep an eye on your sibling'). Set it against 돌보다 (care for over time, like nursing a child or the sick): 챙기다 is quicker, more about 'making sure / not letting slip', while 돌보다 is sustained nurturing. It also contrasts with 챙겨 주다, where adding 주다 makes the care explicitly a favour for someone. The English trap is reaching for 들다 'lift' or 가지다 'have' when you mean 'grab/remember to bring' — that everyday act is 챙기다. Native warmth: 잘 챙겨 먹어요 is a standard way to say 'take care of yourself'."},sentence:{ko:"지갑을 챙기고 나서 출발했어요.",en:"After grabbing my wallet, I left."},target:"챙기",options:["to grab; to pack; to take care of","to wash","to eat"],note:"A common pre-departure action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지갑",romanization:"jigap",en:"wallet",pos:"noun",nuance:"지갑(紙匣) is Sino-Korean — 紙 'paper' + 匣 'small box/case' — historically a 'paper case', now the small folding holder for your money, cards, and ID. Don't confuse it with 가방, which is the larger bag/handbag/backpack you carry items in: a 지갑 typically goes INSIDE a 가방. Modern usage has stretched 지갑 to digital things too — your phone's payment app is a 모바일 지갑 ('mobile wallet') — but the core image is the physical billfold. A subtle pitfall is the verb of forgetting: English 'I forgot my wallet' becomes Korean 지갑을 두고 왔어요 ('I put it down and came'), NOT 지갑을 잊어버렸어요 — 잊어버리다 is for forgetting information/facts (a name, an appointment), while physically leaving an object behind is 두고 오다. For losing it irretrievably you use 잃어버리다. Collocations: 지갑을 열다 (open the wallet → spend), 지갑이 가볍다/두둑하다 (thin/fat wallet = broke/flush), 지갑을 챙기다 (grab your wallet), 동전 지갑 (coin purse)."},sentence:{ko:"지갑을 두고 온 걸 알았어요.",en:"I realized I had left my wallet behind."},target:"지갑",options:["first","then; after that","wallet"],note:"A realistic reason to return home."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"끄다",romanization:"kkeuda",en:"to turn off",pos:"verb",nuance:"끄다 is the direct opposite of 켜다 and means to cut the power to a device or to extinguish a flame, so the same verb that turns off a lamp also 'puts out' a fire (불을 끄다 covers both). The conjugation is the key trap: 끄다 is a ㅡ-dropping (으 불규칙) verb, so when you add 아/어, the bare ㅡ vowel disappears and the ㅓ of the ending takes over — giving 꺼요 and past 껐어요, never 끄어요. English speakers also confuse 끄다 with 닫다 (to close): you 닫다 a door or app window, but you 끄다 the power; saying 컴퓨터를 닫다 wrongly means 'close the laptop lid', not 'shut it down'. Register is neutral — 불 꺼 to a friend, 불을 꺼 주시겠어요? to a superior. A subtler point: to merely silence a phone, Koreans say 무음으로 하다 or 진동으로 하다, not 끄다, because 끄다 means the screen goes fully dark. Natural collocations: 불·전등을 끄다, 핸드폰·컴퓨터를 끄다, 에어컨·히터를 끄다, 촛불을 끄다."},sentence:{ko:"불을 끄고 나서 문을 닫았어요.",en:"After turning off the light, I closed the door."},target:"끄고",options:["to wash","to turn off","to eat"],note:"A natural leaving-home sequence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"닫다",romanization:"datda",en:"to close",pos:"verb",nuance:"닫다 is the opposite of 열다. Use it for doors, windows, stores, apps, and lids."},sentence:{ko:"문을 닫고 나서 나왔어요.",en:"After closing the door, I came out."},target:"닫고",options:["to wash","to close","to eat"],note:"Another common step before leaving."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 그런데 집에서 나오자마자 지갑을 두고 온 걸 알았어요.",en:"Yes, but as soon as I came out of the house, I realized I had left my wallet behind."},hl:"지갑"}],canDo:"Sequence a routine with everyday words (먼저, 그다음, 준비하다, 출발하다)",id:"chapter-28-b5",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A friend asks about a morning mistake and the steps that followed.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"아침에 어떤 순서로 준비했어요?",en:"What order did you get ready in this morning?",romanization:"achime eotteon sunseoro junbihaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"먼저 손을 씻고 나서 아침을 먹었어요.",en:"First, after washing my hands, I ate breakfast.",romanization:"meonjeo soneul ssitgo naseo achimeul meogeosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그다음 바로 출발했어요?",en:"Then did you leave right away?",romanization:"geudaeum baro chulbalhaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 그런데 집에서 나오자마자 지갑을 두고 온 걸 알았어요.",en:"Yes, but as soon as I came out of the house, I realized I had left my wallet behind.",romanization:"ne, geureonde jibeseo naojamaja jigabeul dugo on geol arasseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그래서 다시 올라갔어요?",en:"So did you go back up?",romanization:"geuraeseo dasi ollagasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 지갑을 챙기고 나서 다시 출발했어요.",en:"Yes, after grabbing my wallet, I left again.",romanization:"ne, jigabeul chaenggigo naseo dasi chulbalhaesseoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'After washing up, I slept.'",tokens:["씻고 나서","잤어요"],correct:"씻고 나서 잤어요",explanation:"-고 나서 makes the first action clearly finished before the second starts. 씻고 나서 comes before 잤어요."}],canDo:"Order two actions with V-고 나서 (먹고 나서 가요)",id:"chapter-28-b6",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A small morning mistake",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A small morning mistake",chunks:["지훈은 아침에 먼저 손을 씻고 나서 아침을 먹었어요. 그다음 불을 끄고 문을 닫고 집에서 나왔어요. 그런데 엘리베이터 앞에 도착하자마자 지갑을 두고 온 걸 알았어요.","그래서 다시 집에 올라갔어요. 지갑을 챙기고 나서 다시 출발했고, 역에 도착하자마자 친구에게 전화했어요. 친구는 이미 예약을 확인하고 기다리고 있었어요."],translation:"In the morning, Jihoon first washed his hands and ate breakfast. Then he turned off the light, closed the door, and came out. But as soon as he reached the elevator, he realized he had left his wallet behind. So he went back up. After grabbing his wallet, he left again, and as soon as he arrived at the station, he called his friend. His friend had already checked the reservation and was waiting.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 아침을 먹기 전에 무엇을 했어요?",a:"손을 씻었어요."},{q:"지훈은 언제 지갑을 두고 온 걸 알았어요?",a:"엘리베이터 앞에 도착하자마자 알았어요."},{q:"지훈은 지갑을 챙기고 나서 무엇을 했어요?",a:"다시 출발했어요."}]}],canDo:"Say one thing follows another instantly with V-자마자 (도착하자마자 전화해요)",id:"chapter-28-b7",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means 'after washing my hands'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"손을 씻고 나서",ok:!0},{text:"손을 씻었고 나서",ok:!1},{text:"손을 씻자 전에",ok:!1}],explanation:"The first verb before -고 나서 does not take past tense."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means 'as soon as I arrived'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"도착하자마자",ok:!0},{text:"도착하고 나서 나중에",ok:!1},{text:"도착했자마자",ok:!1}],explanation:"-자마자 attaches directly to the verb stem."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You want to say there was no delay before calling. Which fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"도착하자마자 전화했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"도착하고 나서 언젠가 전화했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"도착하는 동안 전화했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-자마자 stresses immediate sequence."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"밥___ 먹고 나서 출발했어요. (After eating, I left.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"밥 is what you eat, so it takes 을. 먹고 나서 then shows the next action happened after eating."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'After washing up, I slept.'",tokens:["씻고 나서","잤어요"],correct:"씻고 나서 잤어요",explanation:"-고 나서 makes the first action clearly finished before the second starts. 씻고 나서 comes before 잤어요."}],canDo:"Sequence a routine with everyday words (먼저, 그다음, 준비하다, 출발하다)",id:"chapter-28-b8",chapterId:"chapter-28",index:7}]},{id:"chapter-29",number:29,title:"V-다가 · V-아/어 있다",goal:"Use V-다가 when an action is interrupted, paused, or changed mid-way.",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 가다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가다",romanization:"gada",en:"to go",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 에 for destination: 학교에 가요, 집에 가요. Do not use 에서 for destination."},sentence:{ko:"학교에 가다가 전화를 받았어요.",en:"I got a call while going to school."},target:"가다",options:["to eat","to go","to see; to watch"],note:"가다가 is the core movement interruption frame."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"먹다",romanization:"meokda",en:"to eat",pos:"verb",nuance:"먹다 is used for meals and food. For medicine, Korean often uses 약을 먹어요, literally “eat medicine,” which means “take medicine.”"},sentence:{ko:"밥을 먹다가 전화가 왔어요.",en:"A call came while I was eating."},target:"먹다",options:["to go","to see; to watch","to eat"],note:"The meal is not the completed first step."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보다",romanization:"boda",en:"to see; to watch",pos:"verb",nuance:"보다 covers seeing, watching, and looking at something. Context tells which English verb fits."},sentence:{ko:"영화를 보다가 잠들었어요.",en:"I fell asleep while watching the movie."},target:"보다",options:["to see; to watch","to go","to eat"],note:"A common unintended switch."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"중간에",romanization:"junggane",en:"in the middle; midway",pos:"adverb",nuance:"중간에 does not always mean the exact mathematical center. It often means midway or before the process was finished."},sentence:{ko:"중간에 계획을 바꿨어요.",en:"I changed the plan midway."},target:"중간에",options:["in the middle; midway","to go","to eat"],note:"A direct plan-change phrase."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바꾸다",romanization:"bakkuda",en:"to change",pos:"verb",nuance:"바꾸다 means to replace one thing with another, or to alter something into a different state — money (돈을 바꾸다), seats (자리를 바꾸다), plans (계획을 바꾸다), even your mind (마음을 바꾸다). The dividing line from 고치다 is essential: 바꾸다 ends with a DIFFERENT thing, 고치다 keeps the SAME thing repaired. Returning a defective shirt for a new one is 옷을 바꾸다; mending the torn seam is 옷을 고치다. The destination of the change takes the particle -(으)로: 작은 걸로 바꿔 주세요 ('change it for the small one'). Note also 바뀌다, the intransitive twin: 바꾸다 is 'I change X' (transitive, takes 을/를), while 바뀌다 is 'X changes by itself' (신호등이 바뀌었어요 = 'the light changed') — mixing these up is a frequent error. Conjugation is regular: 바꾸 + 어요 → 바꿔요, past 바꿨어요. English-speaker pitfall: 'change my mind' is 마음을 바꾸다, not 생각을 고치다, and 'change clothes (get dressed)' is 옷을 갈아입다, not 옷을 바꾸다."},sentence:{ko:"계획을 바꿨어요.",en:"I changed the plan."},target:"바꿨",options:["to change","to go","to eat"],note:"The object being changed takes 을/를."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취소하다",romanization:"chwisohada",en:"to cancel",pos:"verb",nuance:"취소하다 (取消하다, 取 'take' + 消 'extinguish/erase') means to cancel — to call off or undo something already arranged: a reservation, an order, an appointment, a plan. The neat way to remember it: it's the natural opposite of 예약하다/신청하다 (book/apply). Distinguish it from 그만두다, which is 'to quit/stop' an ongoing activity or job (회사를 그만두다, 'quit the company'; 운동을 그만두다, 'stop exercising'): you 취소해요 a future arrangement, but you 그만둬요 something already in progress. It also differs from 미루다 ('postpone', 약속을 미루다, push the appointment later rather than scrap it) — canceling removes it, postponing reschedules it. As a noun+하다 verb it's regular: 취소하 + 여요 → 취소해요, past 취소했어요, with 안/못 before 하다 (취소 안 해요, 취소 못 해요). English-speaker pitfall: 'cancel my subscription / unsubscribe' is more often 해지하다 (解止, end a contract) than 취소하다, and don't use 취소하다 for 'quit a job' — that's 그만두다."},sentence:{ko:"수업이 취소됐어요.",en:"The class was canceled."},target:"취소",options:["to go","to cancel","to eat"],note:"A common reason a plan changes mid-way."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"그래서 중간에 계획을 바꿨어요?",en:"So did you change the plan midway?"},hl:"중간에"}],canDo:"Break off one action to switch to another with V-다가 (가다가 받았어요)",id:"chapter-29-b1",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-다가",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-다가",sub:"break off one action to do another",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가",mid:"다가",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"친구를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"만났어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"학교에 가다가 친구를 만났어요.",en:"I was on my way to school and ran into a friend.",romanization:"hakgyoe gadaga chingureul mannasseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"숙제를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하",mid:"다가",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"잠이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"들었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"숙제를 하다가 잠이 들었어요.",en:"I was doing my homework and fell asleep.",romanization:"sukjereul hadaga jami deureosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-다가",rows:[{when:"Add straight to the plain verb stem (no vowel change, same after vowel or consonant)",add:"-다가",ex:"가다 → 가다가, 먹다 → 먹다가, 자다 → 자다가"},{when:"Often cut to just -다 in everyday speech",add:"-다",ex:"집에 가다가 → 집에 가다 (친구를 만났어요)"},{when:"Past stem -았/었다가 = first action was finished, then undone or reversed",add:"-았다가 / -었다가",ex:"앉다 → 앉았다가 (일어났어요), 사다 → 샀다가 (환불했어요)"}]},more:{func:"V-다가 says you were in the middle of one action when you stopped partway and switched to (or got interrupted by) a second one. The first action does not finish — you break off from it. When both clauses describe intentional actions, their subject is normally the same; an unplanned event can instead be the second-clause subject (밥을 먹다가 전화가 왔어요).",keyPoint:{label:"V-다가 vs. V-고 나서",body:"V-고 나서 means you finish the first thing all the way, then do the next (밥을 먹고 나서 = after you're done eating). V-다가 means the first thing gets cut off in the middle and you switch (밥을 먹다가 = you were still eating and then stopped to...). 다가 = broke off; 고 나서 = finished."},pronunciation:"다가 is said plainly as [다가]. In -았다가/-었다가 the ㅆ makes the ㄷ tense, so 샀다가 sounds like [삳따가]."}}],canDo:"Break off one action to switch to another with V-다가 (가다가 받았어요)",id:"chapter-29-b2",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 문자~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"문자",romanization:"munja",en:"text message",pos:"noun",nuance:"문자 (文字, 'written character/letter') in daily speech is the everyday word for an SMS text message — short for 문자 메시지. The pitfall is its double life: literally 문자 means written characters/letters (e.g. 한글은 한국의 문자예요, 'Hangul is Korea's writing system'), so context decides. Crucially, 문자 is NOT a paper letter in the mail — that is 편지 (便紙). The near-synonym 카톡 (a KakaoTalk message) is what most people actually send now; 문자 specifically implies the carrier SMS (often bank/official notices), while a casual chat is 카톡 or 메시지. Register is neutral. Habitual collocations: 문자를 보내다/받다, 문자가 오다, 문자를 확인하다, 문자로 연락하다, and the source pattern 은행/회사에서 문자가 오다. Particle traps: 'by text' is 문자로 (means); the sender/source is marked 에서 for organizations (은행에서) or 한테서/에게서 for people. English speakers often want 'text' as a verb — Korean splits it into 문자를 보내다 (send a text)."},sentence:{ko:"수업 취소 문자를 받았어요.",en:"I got a class cancellation text."},target:"문자",options:["state; condition","to go","text message"],note:"The message interrupts the original plan."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"타다",romanization:"tada",en:"to ride; to take",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 타다 for getting on or riding transportation: 버스를 타요, 지하철을 타요."},sentence:{ko:"지하철을 타고 있다가 문자를 받았어요.",en:"I got a text while riding the subway."},target:"타고",options:["to ride; to take","to go","to eat"],note:"-고 있다가 makes the ongoing ride explicit."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"받다",romanization:"batda",en:"to receive",pos:"verb",nuance:"받다 is for receiving things, messages, help, service, or documents."},sentence:{ko:"전화를 받았어요.",en:"I got a phone call."},target:"받았",options:["to receive","to go","to eat"],note:"The incoming call can interrupt an action."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"앉다",romanization:"anjda",en:"to sit",pos:"verb",nuance:"앉아 주세요 is a polite request; 앉으세요 is also common and a little more direct."},sentence:{ko:"카페에 앉아 있어요.",en:"I am sitting at the cafe."},target:"앉아",options:["to eat","to see; to watch","to sit"],note:"앉아 있다 is a result-state phrase."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"서다",romanization:"seoda",en:"to stand",pos:"verb",nuance:"서다 means to stand or, for vehicles, to come to a stop. Because the stem already ends in 어, the 요-ending merges instead of doubling: you get 서요 and the past 섰어요, never 서어요 or 서었어요. Watch the two sister verbs: 서다 is the plain state/result 'to be standing / take a standing position', while 일어서다 ('rise to one's feet') stresses the motion of getting up from sitting — so 'please stand up (from your seat)' is 일어서 주세요, but 'please stand here' is 여기 서 주세요. For vehicles 서다 overlaps with 멈추다 ('to halt'); 멈추다 is more general and can describe machines or actions stopping, while 서다 is the everyday word for a bus or car pulling up. The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: the location where you stand takes 에 (여기에 서다), not the object marker, and don't say 줄에 서다 — the idiom is 줄을 서다, treating the line as something you 'do'. Korean also has no separate verb for 'stand something up'; that meaning needs the causative 세우다."},sentence:{ko:"사람들이 줄을 서 있어요.",en:"People are standing in line."},target:null,options:["to go","to stand","to eat"],note:"서 있다 describes a continuing posture or line state."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"열리다",romanization:"yeollida",en:"to open; to be opened",pos:"verb",nuance:"열다 needs an actor opening something. 열리다 lets the opened thing become the subject."},sentence:{ko:"문이 열려 있어요.",en:"The door is open."},target:"열려",options:["to open; to be opened","to go","to eat"],note:"The open state remains."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"지하철을 타고 있다가 수업 취소 문자를 받았어요.",en:"While I was riding the subway, I got a class cancellation text."},hl:"문자"}],canDo:"Describe a state left after an action with V-아/어 있다 (앉아 있어요, 열려 있어요)",id:"chapter-29-b3",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 있다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-아/어 있다",sub:"a state that stays after the action",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아 있다",ex:"앉다 → 앉아 있다, 오다 → 와 있다"},{when:"Any other stem vowel",add:"-어 있다",ex:"서다 → 서 있다, 열리다 → 열려 있다"},{when:"Passive / result verbs work too (the door opens, the light comes on)",add:"-아/어 있다",ex:"켜지다 → 켜져 있다, 쓰이다 → 쓰여 있다"}],examples:[{ko:"친구가 의자에 앉아 있어요.",en:"My friend is sitting on the chair.",romanization:"chinguga uijae anja isseoyo.",note:"They sat down earlier and are still in the seated state now."},{ko:"문이 열려 있어요.",en:"The door is open.",romanization:"muni yeollyeo isseoyo.",note:"Someone opened it before and it stays open — a lasting result. Note 열리다 (opens), not 열다 (open it)."}],more:{func:"V-아/어 있다 describes the state that is left over after an action is done. The action already happened, and the subject stays in that result (앉아 있다 = is sitting, having sat down). It goes with verbs that leave a state behind, like 앉다, 서다, 오다, 열리다.",keyPoint:{label:"V-아/어 있다 vs. V-고 있다",body:"V-고 있다 = an action happening right now (앉고 있어요 = is in the middle of sitting down). V-아/어 있다 = the state after it's done (앉아 있어요 = is already seated and staying that way). Use -아/어 있다 for the leftover result, -고 있다 for the action in progress."},pronunciation:"앉아 있어요 links across the words — the ㅈ carries over, so it sounds like [안자 이써요]. 열려 있어요 sounds like [열려 이써요]."}}],canDo:"Describe a state left after an action with V-아/어 있다 (앉아 있어요, 열려 있어요)",id:"chapter-29-b4",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 켜지다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"켜지다",romanization:"kyeojida",en:"to turn on; to be on",pos:"verb",nuance:"켜다 is the action of turning something on. 켜지다 focuses on the thing becoming or being on."},sentence:{ko:"불이 켜져 있어요.",en:"The light is on."},target:"켜져",options:["to go","to eat","to turn on; to be on"],note:"The on-state remains."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"상태",romanization:"sangtae",en:"state; condition",pos:"noun",nuance:"상태 (狀態, 狀 'shape/form' + 態 'condition') is the present condition of one specific thing — a person's health, an object, a system — captured at a moment. The sharp contrast is 상태 vs 상황: 상태 zooms in on the CONDITION of a single entity (건강 상태 'health condition'), while 상황 is the broader CIRCUMSTANCE/situation surrounding events (교통 상황 'traffic situation', 지금 상황이 복잡해요). Habitual partners: 상태가 좋다/나쁘다, 상태를 확인하다, 건강/배터리/도로 상태, ~한 상태 ('in a … state'). A handy grammar use: 술에 취한 상태로 = 'in a drunken state.' The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: 'the condition is good' is 상태가 좋다 ✓ (좋다 descriptive), never 상태를 좋다 ✗; and don't reach for 상황 when you mean one thing's condition."},sentence:{ko:"문이 열린 상태예요.",en:"The door is in an open state."},target:"상태",options:["state; condition","text message","to go"],note:"Names the state directly."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"갑자기",romanization:"gapjagi",en:"suddenly; all of a sudden",pos:"adverb",nuance:"갑자기 marks something abrupt, unplanned, and usually unexpected, often coloured with surprise or disruption — it is the direct semantic opposite of 점점. Compare it with 갑작스럽게 and 별안간: 갑작스럽게 is the adverb form of the adjective 갑작스럽다 and sounds a touch more written/formal, while 별안간 (瞥眼間, literally 'in the blink of an eye') is a vivid literary near-synonym you'll meet in stories more than in speech. In conversation 갑자기 왜 이래요/그래요 can sound slightly accusatory or alarmed depending on tone, so mind the situation. A frequent English-speaker pitfall is the spelling: it is 갑자기, never the tempting 갑짜기, even though the second syllable is pronounced tense. Also resist using it for slow change — 'it's gradually getting cold' is 점점 추워져요, not 갑자기 추워져요."},sentence:{ko:"가다가 갑자기 문자를 받았어요.",en:"I suddenly got a text while going."},target:"갑자기",options:["to go","suddenly; all of a sudden","to eat"],note:"Signals the interruption was unexpected."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쉬다",romanization:"swida",en:"to rest",pos:"verb",nuance:"쉬다 is used for taking a break, resting, or being off work/school."},sentence:{ko:"조금 쉬다가 다시 갈 거예요.",en:"I will rest a little and then go again."},target:"쉬다",options:["to rest","to see; to watch","to change"],note:"A temporary pause before resuming."}],canDo:"Explain a mid-action change with everyday words (중간에, 바꾸다, 취소하다)",id:"chapter-29-b5",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A class is canceled while someone is already on the way.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"학교에 가다가 왜 돌아왔어요?",en:"Why did you come back while going to school?",romanization:"hakgyoe gadaga wae dorawasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"지하철을 타고 있다가 수업 취소 문자를 받았어요.",en:"While I was riding the subway, I got a class cancellation text.",romanization:"jihacheoreul tago itdaga sueop chwiso munjareul badasseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그래서 중간에 계획을 바꿨어요?",en:"So did you change the plan midway?",romanization:"geuraeseo junggane gyehoegeul bakkwosseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 지금 카페에 앉아 있어요.",en:"Yes, now I am sitting at a cafe.",romanization:"ne, jigeum kapee anja isseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"카페 문은 열려 있어요?",en:"Is the cafe door open?",romanization:"kape muneun yeollyeo isseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 불도 켜져 있어요. 조금 쉬다가 다시 갈 거예요.",en:"Yes, the light is on too. I will rest a little and then go again.",romanization:"ne, buldo kyeojyeo isseoyo. jogeum swidaga dasi gal geoyeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I was going, then changed plans midway.'",tokens:["가다가","중간에","계획을 바꿨어요"],correct:"가다가 중간에 계획을 바꿨어요",explanation:"-다가 shows the original action was interrupted. 중간에 makes the mid-way change explicit."}],canDo:"Break off one action to switch to another with V-다가 (가다가 받았어요)",id:"chapter-29-b6",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A plan changes on the way",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A plan changes on the way",chunks:["지훈은 학교에 가다가 수업 취소 문자를 받았어요. 처음에는 그냥 학교까지 가려고 했지만, 지하철을 타고 있다가 문자를 보고 중간에 계획을 바꿨어요. 그래서 근처 카페로 갔어요.","카페 문이 열려 있었고 불도 켜져 있었어요. 지훈은 창가 자리에 앉아 있었어요. 조금 쉬다가 다시 집에 돌아가기로 했어요."],translation:"Jihoon got a class cancellation text while going to school. At first he planned to keep going, but while riding the subway he saw the text and changed the plan midway. So he went to a nearby cafe. The cafe door was open and the light was on. Jihoon was sitting by the window. After resting a bit, he decided to go back home.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 무엇을 하다가 문자를 받았어요?",a:"학교에 가다가 문자를 받았어요."},{q:"지훈은 왜 계획을 바꿨어요?",a:"수업 취소 문자를 받았기 때문이에요."},{q:"카페는 어떤 상태였어요?",a:"문이 열려 있었고 불도 켜져 있었어요."}]}],canDo:"Describe a state left after an action with V-아/어 있다 (앉아 있어요, 열려 있어요)",id:"chapter-29-b7",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence shows a mid-action interruption?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"학교에 가다가 전화를 받았어요.",ok:!0},{text:"학교에 가고 나서 전화를 받았어요.",ok:!1},{text:"학교에 가자마자 전화를 받았어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-다가 shows the phone call happened while the first action was underway."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence describes the continuing state after sitting?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"카페에 앉아 있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"카페에 앉고 있어요.",ok:!1},{text:"카페를 앉아 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"앉아 있어요 means the person is seated. The place takes 에."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means the door is already open?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"문이 열려 있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"문을 열고 있어요.",ok:!1},{text:"문을 열려 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Result state uses the thing as subject: 문이."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"문자___ 읽다가 전화를 했어요. (I was reading a text, then switched to calling.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"문자를 읽다 is the action phrase “read a text.” 읽다가 shows that reading was interrupted or shifted."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I was going, then changed plans midway.'",tokens:["가다가","중간에","계획을 바꿨어요"],correct:"가다가 중간에 계획을 바꿨어요",explanation:"-다가 shows the original action was interrupted. 중간에 makes the mid-way change explicit."}],canDo:"Explain a mid-action change with everyday words (중간에, 바꾸다, 취소하다)",id:"chapter-29-b8",chapterId:"chapter-29",index:7}]},{id:"chapter-30",number:30,title:"V/A-아서/어서 · V-는 바람에",goal:"Use V/A-아서/어서 for ordinary reason-result sentences.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 그래서~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"그래서",romanization:"geuraeseo",en:"so; therefore",pos:"adverb",nuance:"그래서 links a cause to its natural result — 'so / that's why' — and is the first cause-effect connector a beginner must own. It is the sentence-starting cousin of the clause ending -아서/-어서 (피곤해서 일찍 자요 = 피곤해요. 그래서 일찍 자요). The sharp boundary to learn is 그래서 vs 그러니까: both can mean 'so', but 그래서 reports a plain consequence, while 그러니까 pushes a conclusion onto the listener and is what you use before a command or suggestion (피곤하지? 그러니까 일찍 자 = so go to bed). Two real pitfalls: first, 그래서 cannot head an imperative or a let's-form — 그래서 일찍 자세요 sounds off, so switch to 그러니까. Second, English 'so' can also mean 'very' ('so tired'); that meaning is 너무/정말, never 그래서. Keep 그래서 strictly for result."},sentence:{ko:"길이 막혔어요. 그래서 조금 늦었어요.",en:"The road was jammed. So I was a little late."},target:"그래서",options:["suddenly","so; therefore","rain"],note:"Use 그래서 after the cause has already been stated."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비",romanization:"bi",en:"rain",pos:"noun",nuance:"Korean has no single verb meaning 'to rain'; instead precipitation 'arrives', so you say 비가 와요 (rain comes) using 오다. A more formal, written variant is 비가 내리다 ('rain falls'), favored in forecasts and lyrics, while 비가 오다 dominates everyday speech. To say rain stops, use the dedicated verb 그치다 (비가 그쳤어요), not 끝나다. Note 비 also forms many compounds: 소나기 (sudden shower), 장마 (the summer monsoon rains), 가랑비 (drizzle). The big English-speaker pitfall is inventing a verb: learners say 비가 해요 or 비해요 by analogy with English 'it rains', but 하다 never combines with 비 — the natural pairing is strictly 비가 오다 / 비가 내리다. A second trap is 비를 맞다 ('to take the rain on your body', i.e. get drenched), which uses 맞다, not 받다."},sentence:{ko:"비 때문에 약속을 취소했어요.",en:"I canceled the appointment because of the rain."},target:"비",options:["road; way","bus","rain"],note:"A clean N 때문에 sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"길",romanization:"gil",en:"road; way",pos:"noun",nuance:"길 can mean road, path, way, or route. 길을 잃었어요 is a survival phrase."},sentence:{ko:"퇴근 시간이라 길이 막혀요.",en:"The road is jammed because it is rush hour."},target:"길",options:["rain","road; way","bus"],note:"In Korean, the road is the subject of 막히다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"버스",romanization:"beoseu",en:"bus",pos:"noun",nuance:"버스 is useful with 타다, 기다리다, 내리다, and 로/으로 for transportation method."},sentence:{ko:"버스가 고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요.",en:"The bus broke down, so I ended up late."},target:"버스",options:["rain","road; way","bus"],note:"A transport noun that naturally pairs with 바람에."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"택시",romanization:"taeksi",en:"taxi",pos:"noun",nuance:"택시로 가요 and 택시를 타요 are both natural, but they answer different questions: method versus boarding action."},sentence:{ko:"비 때문에 택시를 못 탔어요.",en:"Because of the rain, I could not take a taxi."},target:"택시",options:["taxi","rain","road; way"],note:"Keeps 때문에 attached to a real noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"막히다",romanization:"makida",en:"to be blocked; to be jammed",pos:"verb",nuance:"막히다 is the passive/intransitive partner of the transitive 막다 ('to block'): the -히- infix turns 'someone blocks X' into 'X gets blocked', so 막히다 is something that HAPPENS to a road, drain, or nose rather than something you do. For traffic this is the single most useful verb — 길이 막히다 / 차가 막히다 — and its opposite is 뚫리다 ('to open up/clear'), with 안 막히다 the everyday 'no traffic'. Do not confuse it with 막다 itself: 'I block the road' is 길을 막다, but 'the road is jammed' is 길이 막히다, and the particle flips from 을 to 이/가 accordingly. Register is plain and universal. Because the subject is a non-person, the polite/imperative-looking forms (막혀 주세요, 막힐까요?) are essentially never used about traffic. The big English-speaker trap is treating it as transitive: there is no 'I am blocked by traffic' with you as subject — Korean puts the ROAD in the subject slot (길이 막혀요), and you simply say 길이 막혀서 늦었어요 ('I was late because the road was jammed')."},sentence:{ko:"길이 막혀서 약속에 늦었어요.",en:"The road was jammed, so I was late to the appointment."},target:"막혀",options:["to be blocked; to be jammed","to break down; to malfunction","to miss"],note:"The subject is 길 or 차, not the late person."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"비 때문에 택시도 못 탔어요?",en:"Because of the rain, you could not take a taxi either?"},hl:"택시"}],canDo:"Link a cause to its result with V/A-아서/어서 (늦어서 미안해요)",id:"chapter-30-b1",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-아서/어서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-아서/어서",sub:"because, so",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"시간이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없",mid:"어서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"못",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"갔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"시간이 없어서 못 갔어요.",en:"I had no time, so I couldn't go.",romanization:"si-gan-i eop-seo-seo mot ga-sseo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"한국어가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있",mid:"어서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"매일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"한국어가 재미있어서 매일 공부해요.",en:"Korean is fun, so I study it every day.",romanization:"han-gu-geo-ga jae-mi-i-sseo-seo mae-il gong-bu-hae-yo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-아서/어서",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아서",ex:"가다 → 가서, 좋다 → 좋아서"},{when:"Any other stem vowel",add:"-어서",ex:"먹다 → 먹어서, 없다 → 없어서"},{when:"하다 verbs/adjectives",add:"→ 해서",ex:"공부하다 → 공부해서, 피곤하다 → 피곤해서"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"-라서/이라서",ex:"학생이다 → 학생이라서, 친구다 → 친구라서"}]},more:{func:`Use -아서/어서 to link a reason to its result: the first part is the cause, the second is what comes from it. It's the everyday, neutral way to say "because" or "so."`,keyPoint:{label:"Tense goes on the last verb only",body:"Don't put past tense before -아서/어서. Say 고파서 먹었어요 (not 고팠어서). The part before -아서/어서 stays plain, and when something happened is shown only on the final verb."},pronunciation:"없어서 is read [업써서]: the ㅄ batchim links over, so ㅅ moves to the next syllable and tenses to [써]."}}],canDo:"Link a cause to its result with V/A-아서/어서 (늦어서 미안해요)",id:"chapter-30-b2",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 고장 나다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"고장 나다",romanization:"gojangnada",en:"to break down; to malfunction",pos:"verb",nuance:"고장 나다 means a machine, device, vehicle, appliance, or system stops working properly. The broken item is the subject with 이/가: 휴대폰이 고장 났어요. 고장 내다 is the active/causative counterpart, meaning someone broke it. 망가지다 is broader and can describe physical damage, ruined objects, plans, or conditions; 고장 나다 specifically focuses on malfunction. Standard spacing is 고장 나다 because 고장 is a noun and 나다 is the verb."},sentence:{ko:"버스가 고장 나는 바람에 오래 기다렸어요.",en:"The bus broke down, so I waited for a long time."},target:"고장 나",options:["to be blocked; to be jammed","to break down; to malfunction","to miss"],note:"A strong 바람에 cause because the problem was not planned."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"놓치다",romanization:"notchida",en:"to miss",pos:"verb",nuance:"놓치다 is for missing something you were actively trying to catch or hold — a bus, a train, a flight, a chance, or even the thread of a conversation (요점을 놓치다 = 'miss the point') — and it always takes a 을/를 object. Its make-up shows the meaning: 놓다 ('to let go/release') + 치다, so it literally pictures something slipping out of your grasp. The single biggest English-speaker pitfall is using it for missing a *person*: English 'I miss you' is NOT 놓치다 — that is 보고 싶다 ('want to see'), so 엄마가 보고 싶어요, never 엄마를 놓쳐요. Contrast 놓치다 ('fail to catch what was within reach') with 지나가다/지나치다 ('let pass / pass by'): you 놓치다 a bus you ran for, but a chance that quietly 지나가다 simply went by. It is a regular ㅣ-stem verb: 놓치 + 어 → 놓쳐요, and it frequently appears alongside 늦다 ('be late')."},sentence:{ko:"버스를 놓치는 바람에 수업에 늦었어요.",en:"I missed the bus, so I was late to class."},target:"놓치",options:["to be blocked; to be jammed","to miss","to break down; to malfunction"],note:"놓치다 often creates an unwanted result."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"늦다",romanization:"neutda",en:"to be late",pos:"verb",nuance:"Here 늦다 is the action verb meaning to arrive after the expected time, as in 약속에 늦다 or 수업에 늦다. Keep it distinct from the descriptive sense in 답장이 늦다 ('the reply is late/slow')."},sentence:{ko:"늦어서 죄송해요.",en:"I am sorry I am late."},target:"늦어",options:["to be late","to be blocked; to be jammed","to break down; to malfunction"],note:"The simplest apology frame uses -어서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취소하다",romanization:"chwisohada",en:"to cancel",pos:"verb",nuance:"취소하다 (取消하다, 取 'take' + 消 'extinguish/erase') means to cancel — to call off or undo something already arranged: a reservation, an order, an appointment, a plan. The neat way to remember it: it's the natural opposite of 예약하다/신청하다 (book/apply). Distinguish it from 그만두다, which is 'to quit/stop' an ongoing activity or job (회사를 그만두다, 'quit the company'; 운동을 그만두다, 'stop exercising'): you 취소해요 a future arrangement, but you 그만둬요 something already in progress. It also differs from 미루다 ('postpone', 약속을 미루다, push the appointment later rather than scrap it) — canceling removes it, postponing reschedules it. As a noun+하다 verb it's regular: 취소하 + 여요 → 취소해요, past 취소했어요, with 안/못 before 하다 (취소 안 해요, 취소 못 해요). English-speaker pitfall: 'cancel my subscription / unsubscribe' is more often 해지하다 (解止, end a contract) than 취소하다, and don't use 취소하다 for 'quit a job' — that's 그만두다."},sentence:{ko:"비 때문에 모임을 취소했어요.",en:"We canceled the gathering because of the rain."},target:"취소했",options:["to be blocked; to be jammed","to cancel","to break down; to malfunction"],note:"The thing canceled takes 을/를."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"약속",romanization:"yaksok",en:"appointment; promise",pos:"noun",nuance:"약속(約束, literally 'bind + tie' — a tying-together of words) carries two English senses Koreans treat as one: a PROMISE (a verbal commitment) and an APPOINTMENT/plans to meet someone. So 약속이 있어요 almost always means 'I have plans (to meet a person),' which surprises English speakers expecting 'I have a promise.' The signature collocations come as an opposed pair: 약속을 지키다 (keep a promise, lit. 'protect') vs 약속을 어기다 (break it, lit. 'violate'). Contrast 약속 with 예약 豫約 (a RESERVATION/booking for a restaurant, hotel, or hospital — 식당 예약), which is institutional, and with 계획 (your own plan, no other party). The English-speaker pitfalls: the 하다 particle slip 약속이 해요 (use 약속을 해요); using 약속 for a restaurant booking (that is 예약); and saying 약속을 깨다 — while understood, the idiomatic 'break a promise' is 약속을 어기다, whereas 깨다 ('shatter') fits cancelling an appointment (약속을 깨다 = bail on plans)."},sentence:{ko:"약속에 늦어서 미안해요.",en:"Sorry I am late to the appointment."},target:"약속",options:["rain","appointment; promise","road; way"],note:"약속 is the real-life event affected by the cause."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"모임",romanization:"moim",en:"gathering; meeting",pos:"noun",nuance:"모임 is native Korean, built from the verb 모이다 'to gather/come together' plus the noun-forming -ㅁ, so it literally means 'a gathering.' It is the broadest, most neutral word for people coming together for a shared purpose — a club, a reunion, a regular dinner crew — and unlike 파티 it carries no festive or Western flavor. The sharp contrast: 파티 is fun and event-like; 회식 is specifically a work-team meal; 모임 is the umbrella term that can be any of these in a low-key way, including serious or hobby groups (독서 모임 'book club', 동창 모임 'alumni reunion'). Particle logic mirrors other event nouns: 에 to attend (모임에 가요/나가요) and 이 to say one exists (모임이 있어요). The English-speaker pitfall is again 에서 with 가다 (모임에서 가요 ✗) — use 에 for the destination. A subtler point: because 모임 can name the standing group, 모임을 만들다 'form a group' is fine, whereas the same phrase sounds odd with one-off 파티."},sentence:{ko:"모임을 다음 주로 바꿨어요.",en:"We moved the gathering to next week."},target:"모임",options:["rain","road; way","gathering; meeting"],note:"A natural result when everyone is late or the weather is bad."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"왜 약속이 취소됐어요?",en:"Why was the appointment canceled?"},hl:"약속"}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected mishap with V-는 바람에 (고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요)",id:"chapter-30-b3",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-는 바람에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-는 바람에",sub:"because of (something unexpected)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"늦잠을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"바람에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"늦었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"늦잠을 자는 바람에 학교에 늦었어요.",en:"I overslept, so I was late for school.",romanization:"neut-jam-eul ja-neun ba-ram-e hak-gyo-e neu-jeo-sseo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"바람에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"옷이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"다",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"젖었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"비가 오는 바람에 옷이 다 젖었어요.",en:"It started raining, so my clothes got all wet.",romanization:"bi-ga o-neun ba-ram-e o-si da jeo-jeo-sseo-yo."}],rule:{name:"V-는 바람에",rows:[{when:"Verb stem (any)",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"늦다 → 늦는 바람에, 오다 → 오는 바람에"},{when:"Stem ending in ㄹ (drop the ㄹ)",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"울다 → 우는 바람에, 놀다 → 노는 바람에"},{when:"Result part",add:"usually past tense",ex:"늦잠을 자는 바람에 학교에 늦었어요."},{when:"Adjectives / nouns",add:"not used",ex:"(use 때문에 instead)"}]},more:{func:'Use -는 바람에 when an unplanned or unwanted event causes a result — usually a bad one. It carries the feeling of "and because of that, things went wrong."',keyPoint:{label:"-는 바람에 vs -아서/어서",body:`-아서/어서 is neutral and works for any reason. -는 바람에 adds the feeling "and this unexpected thing messed things up," so the result is almost always bad and in the past. Don't use 바람에 for a planned action or a good outcome: say 열심히 공부해서 합격했어요 (not 공부하는 바람에).`},pronunciation:"늦는 is read [는는]: the ㅈ batchim is realized as [ㄷ] in coda position, then nasalizes to [ㄴ] before the ㄴ of 는. Read 늦는 바람에 as [는는 바라메]."}}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected mishap with V-는 바람에 (고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요)",id:"chapter-30-b4",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 이유~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이유",romanization:"iyu",en:"reason",pos:"noun",nuance:"이유 is Sino-Korean (理由, 理 'principle/logic' + 由 'cause/origin'), the 'reason' or grounds behind something — the answer to 'why?'. Its sharpest contrast is with 원인 (原因, 'cause'): 이유 is the justification or motive a person gives ('the reason I'm late'), often subjective and explainable, while 원인 is the objective, often impersonal cause of a result ('the cause of the fire', 화재의 원인). So you ask a friend 무슨 이유로 늦었어? but an investigator looks for the 원인 of an accident. Also distinguish 까닭, a more native, slightly literary synonym of 이유. Register-neutral and extremely common. Core collocations: 이유가 있다/없다, 이유를 묻다/대다/설명하다, 이유 없이 (for no reason), 그런 이유로 (for that reason), ~는 이유는 (the reason that ~ is). The English-speaker pitfall: don't bolt 이유 onto a 'because' clause redundantly — Korean already has 왜냐하면 ... 때문이에요 / ~기 때문에 to say 'because', so '왜냐하면 그 이유는...' is clunky; pick one. Also, 'the reason is because...' should be ~는 이유는 ... 때문이에요, where the sentence ends in 때문이다, not a second 이유."},sentence:{ko:"늦은 이유를 설명했어요.",en:"I explained the reason I was late."},target:"이유",options:["rain","road; way","reason"],note:"Useful when someone asks 왜."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"문제",romanization:"munje",en:"problem",pos:"noun",nuance:"문제(問題, 'ask + topic') has two everyday senses English keeps apart: a trouble or difficulty ('we have a problem') AND a question on a test or exercise ('problem 3'). Crucially, both are solved with the same verb 풀다 (literally 'to untie/loosen') — 문제를 풀다 works for a math item and for a real-life difficulty alike, where English would switch to 'answer' vs 'solve.' Other key partners: 문제가 생기다 (a problem arises), 문제가 되다 (to become an issue), and the handy one-word 문제없어요 ('no problem'). Contrast 문제 with 질문 (a question you ASK someone to get information) — a test 문제 is a problem to solve, but raising your hand to ask is 질문하다, not 문제하다. The English-speaker pitfall is the subject/object split: existence and arising take 이 (문제가 있다/생기다), but solving takes 를 (문제를 풀다) — learners often say 문제가 풀어요."},sentence:{ko:"실수 때문에 문제가 생겼어요.",en:"A problem came up because of a mistake."},target:"문제",options:["problem","rain","road; way"],note:"A result noun that often follows 때문에."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사고",romanization:"sago",en:"accident",pos:"noun",nuance:"사고 (事故, 'matter-incident') covers any sudden harmful event — car crashes, workplace mishaps, falls — and is the umbrella word a 교통사고 or 산업재해 sits under. The make-or-break contrast is purely grammatical and Koreans listen for it: 사고가 나다 (with 가) means the accident merely HAPPENED, assigning no blame, while 사고를 내다 (with 를) means the subject CAUSED it and is therefore liable — get the particle wrong and you may accidentally confess fault at the scene. Compare also 사건, which is an 'incident/case' in the investigative or criminal sense (a 살인 사건 is a murder CASE), whereas 사고 leans toward unintended mishap. Natural partners are 교통사고, 사고가 나다/내다, 사고 현장, and the everyday 접촉사고 (a minor fender-bender)."},sentence:{ko:"사고 때문에 길이 막혔어요.",en:"The road was jammed because of an accident."},target:"사고",options:["rain","accident","road; way"],note:"A concrete noun cause for N 때문에."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"갑자기",romanization:"gapjagi",en:"suddenly",pos:"adverb",nuance:"갑자기 marks something abrupt, unplanned, and usually unexpected, often coloured with surprise or disruption — it is the direct semantic opposite of 점점. Compare it with 갑작스럽게 and 별안간: 갑작스럽게 is the adverb form of the adjective 갑작스럽다 and sounds a touch more written/formal, while 별안간 (瞥眼間, literally 'in the blink of an eye') is a vivid literary near-synonym you'll meet in stories more than in speech. In conversation 갑자기 왜 이래요/그래요 can sound slightly accusatory or alarmed depending on tone, so mind the situation. A frequent English-speaker pitfall is the spelling: it is 갑자기, never the tempting 갑짜기, even though the second syllable is pronounced tense. Also resist using it for slow change — 'it's gradually getting cold' is 점점 추워져요, not 갑자기 추워져요."},sentence:{ko:"갑자기 비가 오는 바람에 약속을 취소했어요.",en:"It suddenly rained, so we canceled the appointment."},target:"갑자기",options:["so; therefore","rain","suddenly"],note:"갑자기 makes the unexpected cause clearer."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"갑자기 비가 많이 오는 바람에 길이 막혔어요.",en:"It suddenly rained a lot, so the roads got jammed."},hl:"갑자기"}],canDo:"Point to a noun cause with N 때문에 (비 때문에 막혔어요)",id:"chapter-30-b5",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 때문에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 때문에",sub:"because of (a noun)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"비",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"못",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"갔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"비 때문에 학교에 못 갔어요.",en:"Because of the rain, I couldn't go to school.",romanization:"bi ttae-mu-ne hak-gyo-e mot ga-sseo-yo."},{tokens:[{pre:"일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"친구를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"못",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"만났어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"일 때문에 친구를 못 만났어요.",en:"Because of work, I couldn't meet my friend.",romanization:"il ttae-mu-ne chin-gu-reul mot man-na-sseo-yo."}],rule:{name:"N 때문에",rows:[{when:"Noun + 때문에",add:"때문에",ex:"비 → 비 때문에, 일 → 일 때문에"},{when:"Noun ends in any sound",add:"no 이/가 added",ex:"친구 → 친구 때문에, 돈 → 돈 때문에"},{when:"Person as the cause",add:"때문에",ex:"동생 → 동생 때문에"},{when:'"because it is / I am ~"',add:"(이)라서 or (이)기 때문에",ex:"주말이라서, 학생이기 때문에"}]},more:{func:'Use a noun + 때문에 to point to a person, thing, or situation as the cause. It means "because of ~" and is followed by a result.',keyPoint:{label:"때문에 vs 아서/어서",body:"때문에 follows a NOUN (비 때문에), while -아서/어서 follows a VERB or ADJECTIVE (비가 와서). Same meaning, just attached to a different kind of word. So if you have a noun, reach for 때문에; if you have a verb or adjective, use -아서/어서."},pronunciation:"때 starts with the tense consonant ㄸ — say it hard and tight, not as a soft [대]."}}],canDo:"Point to a noun cause with N 때문에 (비 때문에 막혔어요)",id:"chapter-30-b6",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends explain why a rainy-day gathering was canceled.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"왜 약속이 취소됐어요?",en:"Why was the appointment canceled?",romanization:"wae yaksogi chwisodwaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"갑자기 비가 많이 오는 바람에 길이 막혔어요.",en:"It suddenly rained a lot, so the roads got jammed.",romanization:"gapjagi biga mani oneun barame giri makhyeosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"비 때문에 택시도 못 탔어요?",en:"Because of the rain, you could not take a taxi either?",romanization:"bi ttaemune taeksido mot tasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 버스까지 고장 나는 바람에 다들 늦었어요.",en:"Right. Even the bus broke down, so everyone was late.",romanization:"ne. beoseukkaji gojang naneun barame dadeul neujeosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그래서 모임을 다음 주로 바꿨군요.",en:"So you moved the gathering to next week.",romanization:"geuraeseo moimeul daeum juro bakkwotgunyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"맞아요. 늦어서 미안하다고도 말했어요.",en:"Yes. I also said I was sorry for being late.",romanization:"majayo. neujeoseo mianhadagodo malhaesseoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I was late because I missed the bus.'",tokens:["버스를 놓쳐서","늦었어요"],correct:"버스를 놓쳐서 늦었어요",explanation:"-아서/어서 gives a neutral reason-result connection. 놓치다 becomes 놓쳐서 before the result 늦었어요."}],canDo:"Link a cause to its result with V/A-아서/어서 (늦어서 미안해요)",id:"chapter-30-b7",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Why the gathering moved",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Why the gathering moved",chunks:["토요일에 민서와 지훈은 카페에서 만나기로 했어요. 그런데 갑자기 비가 많이 와서 길이 막혔어요. 지훈은 버스를 탔지만, 버스가 고장 나는 바람에 한 정류장에서 오래 기다렸어요.","비 때문에 택시도 타기 어려웠어요. 결국 두 사람은 약속을 취소하고 모임을 다음 주로 바꿨어요. 지훈은 길이 막혀서 늦었다고 설명했고, 버스가 고장 나는 바람에 더 늦었다고 말했어요."],translation:"On Saturday, Minseo and Jihoon planned to meet at a cafe. But it suddenly rained a lot, so the roads got jammed. Jihoon took a bus, but the bus broke down and he waited at one stop for a long time. Because of the rain, it was hard to take a taxi too. In the end, the two canceled the appointment and moved the gathering to next week. Jihoon explained that he was late because the roads were jammed, and said he was even later because the bus broke down.",qas:[{q:"길은 왜 막혔어요?",a:"갑자기 비가 많이 와서 길이 막혔어요."},{q:"지훈은 왜 오래 기다렸어요?",a:"버스가 고장 나는 바람에 오래 기다렸어요."},{q:"두 사람은 모임을 언제로 바꿨어요?",a:"다음 주로 바꿨어요."}]}],canDo:"Blame an unexpected mishap with V-는 바람에 (고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요)",id:"chapter-30-b8",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a neutral reason-result explanation?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"길이 막혀서 늦었어요.",ok:!0},{text:"길이 막히기 위해 늦었어요.",ok:!1},{text:"길이 막혔지만 늦었어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아서/어서 connects a reason and result neutrally."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence sounds like an unexpected problem disrupted the plan?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"버스가 고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요.",ok:!0},{text:"버스가 고장 나기 전에 늦었어요.",ok:!1},{text:"버스가 고장 나도록 늦었어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-는 바람에 is for an unwanted or disruptive event."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses N 때문에 correctly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비 때문에 택시를 못 탔어요.",ok:!0},{text:"비가 때문에 택시를 못 탔어요.",ok:!1},{text:"비를 때문에 택시를 못 탔어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Put the noun directly before 때문에. Do not add 가 or 를."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"비___ 약속을 취소했어요. (I canceled because of rain.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"때문에",ok:!0},{text:"가 때문에",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"N 때문에 follows the noun directly. 비가 때문에 adds an unnecessary subject marker before 때문에."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I was late because I missed the bus.'",tokens:["버스를 놓쳐서","늦었어요"],correct:"버스를 놓쳐서 늦었어요",explanation:"-아서/어서 gives a neutral reason-result connection. 놓치다 becomes 놓쳐서 before the result 늦었어요."}],canDo:"Point to a noun cause with N 때문에 (비 때문에 막혔어요)",id:"chapter-30-b9",chapterId:"chapter-30",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-31",number:31,title:"V-기 위해서 / N을 위해서 · N 동안",goal:"Use V-기 위해서 and N을/를 위해서 for deliberate goals.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 한국어~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"한국어",romanization:"hangugeo",en:"Korean language",pos:"noun",nuance:"한국어 points to the language. 한국 사람 points to a Korean person."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 한국에 왔어요.",en:"I came to Korea in order to learn Korean."},target:"한국어",options:["Korean language","employment; getting a job","exam"],note:"The language is the goal of the stay."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"배우다",romanization:"baeuda",en:"to learn",pos:"verb",nuance:"배우다 means 'to learn / acquire a skill or subject' and takes the object marker 을/를: 한국어를 배워요. It is a regular verb, but the stem ends in the vowel 우, so when 어요 attaches the two vowels contract into 워 — 배우 + 어요 → 배워요 (not 배우어요), past 배웠어요. The natural partner verb is 가르치다 ('to teach'), the giving side of the same act; and the person you learn from takes 한테/에게서 (사람) — 선생님한테 배워요 — never the place particle 에서 for the source. Contrast 배우다 with 공부하다 (工夫하다, 'to study'): 공부하다 is the effort of studying (sitting down, reviewing, hitting the books), while 배우다 is the result of acquiring a skill or being taught it, often from someone. So you 공부하다 for an exam at your desk but 배우다 the piano from a teacher. The English-speaker pitfall is writing the uncontracted 배우어요, and saying 학원에서 배우다 to mean 'learn from the academy' — 에서 marks where, while the human source must take 한테/에게서."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요.",en:"I came in order to learn Korean."},target:"배우",options:["to pass an exam","to prepare","to learn"],note:"배우기 위해서 is the purpose frame."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공부하다",romanization:"gongbuhada",en:"to study",pos:"verb",nuance:"공부하다 is noun + 하다, so the short negative is often 공부 안 해요."},sentence:{ko:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 공부해요.",en:"I study in order to pass the exam."},target:"공부해",options:["to study","to pass an exam","to exercise"],note:"A deliberate goal with 위해서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"연습하다",romanization:"yeonseupada",en:"to practice",pos:"verb",nuance:"연습하다 is Sino-Korean 練習 — 練 'train/temper (as in tempering metal)' + 習 'repeatedly do/learn' — so 'train by repetition', i.e. drill a skill until it improves. The key contrast is with 공부하다 'to study': 공부하다 is taking in knowledge (grammar, facts, theory), whereas 연습하다 is repeated hands-on doing to build a SKILL — you 공부해 grammar but 연습해 speaking and piano. A closely related word is 훈련하다 (訓練, 'train'), which is more systematic/physical (soldiers, athletes), while 연습하다 is everyday practice. The thing practiced is a direct OBJECT with 을/를: 발음을 연습하다. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: don't say 발음에 연습하다 (no 에), and don't reach for 공부하다 when you mean drilling a skill. As a noun+하다 verb it conjugates on 하다 (연습해요, 연습했어요, formal 연습합니다); negative/impossible split the noun (연습 안 해요, 연습 못 해요)."},sentence:{ko:"세 달 동안 매일 연습할 거예요.",en:"I will practice every day for three months."},target:"연습할",options:["to practice","to pass an exam","to stay"],note:"A repeated action over a duration."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취업",romanization:"chwieop",en:"employment; getting a job",pos:"noun",nuance:"취업 is the process or result of getting employment. 직업 is your occupation or job type, and 회사 is the company. For a purpose sentence, 취업을 위해서 or 취업하기 위해서 is natural."},sentence:{ko:"취업을 위해서 한국어를 연습해요.",en:"I practice Korean in order to get a job."},target:"취업",options:["employment; getting a job","period","during; for a duration"],note:"Noun plus 을 위해서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"합격하다",romanization:"hapgyeokada",en:"to pass an exam",pos:"verb",nuance:"합격하다 is Sino-Korean 合格 — 合 'meet/match' + 格 'standard' — literally 'meet the standard', i.e. clear a cut-off and pass a test, interview, or admissions screen. Its opposite is 불합격하다 (不合格) or, much more colloquially, 떨어지다 'to fall/drop' (시험에 떨어졌어요 = I failed/flunked). Don't confuse 합격하다 with 통과하다 'to pass through': 통과하다 is moving past a checkpoint or a bill passing, while 합격하다 specifically means meeting a qualifying standard. The headline English-speaker pitfall is the particle: English 'pass THE exam' (object) makes learners write 시험을 합격하다, but Korean uses 에 — 시험에 합격하다 — because you're 'matching up TO the standard'. Note also 합격하다 (verb) vs 합격 (the noun, 'a pass/acceptance'): the noun is what you put on banners — 합격 축하해! As a noun+하다 verb it conjugates on 하다 (합격해요, 합격했어요, formal 합격합니다); negative/impossible split the noun (합격 안 해요, 합격 못 해요)."},sentence:{ko:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 준비해요.",en:"I prepare in order to pass the exam."},target:"합격하",options:["to learn","to pass an exam","to study"],note:"합격하다 becomes 합격하기."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요. 세 달 동안 머물 거예요.",en:"I came in order to learn Korean. I will stay for three months."},hl:"한국어"}],canDo:"State a purpose before the action (V-기 위해서 · N을/를 위해서)",id:"chapter-31-b1",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-기 위해서 / N을 위해서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-기 위해서 / N을 위해서",sub:"in order to, for the sake of",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"한국어를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"배우",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"한국에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"왔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 한국에 왔어요.",en:"I came to Korea to learn Korean.",romanization:"hangugeoreul baeugi wihaeseo hanguge wasseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"가족",mid:"을",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"매일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"열심히",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"일해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"가족을 위해서 매일 열심히 일해요.",en:"I work hard every day for my family.",romanization:"gajogeul wihaeseo maeil yeolsimhi ilhaeyo."}],rule:{name:"V-기 위해서 / N을 위해서",rows:[{when:"Any verb stem",add:"-기 위해서",ex:"먹다 → 먹기 위해서 / 사다 → 사기 위해서"},{when:"Noun ending in a consonant",add:"을 위해서",ex:"건강 → 건강을 위해서"},{when:"Noun ending in a vowel",add:"를 위해서",ex:"친구 → 친구를 위해서"},{when:"Shorter form (writing or quick speech)",add:"-기 위해 / N을 위해",ex:"건강을 위해 운동해요"}]},more:{func:'Shows purpose. The goal comes first, then the action you take to reach it. Use V-기 위해서 with a verb ("in order to do X") and N을/를 위해서 with a noun ("for N").',keyPoint:{label:"Verb goal vs. noun goal",body:`For a verb goal, use V-기 위해서 (배우기 위해서 = in order to learn). For a noun goal, use N을/를 위해서 (가족을 위해서 = for my family). Keep the particle 을/를 before 위해서 with a noun — don't drop it. Later you'll meet V-(으)려고, a more casual "in order to" that only works with verbs.`},pronunciation:"위해서 is often shortened to 위해 in writing and quick speech; both sound natural. 모으기 keeps each syllable clear: [모으기]."}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I work a part-time job to save money.",sentence:"돈을 모으___ 아르바이트를 해요.",options:[{text:"기 위해서",ok:!0},{text:"을 위해서",ok:!1}],explanation:"The goal (saving money) comes first, the action (working) second."}],canDo:"State a purpose before the action (V-기 위해서 · N을/를 위해서)",id:"chapter-31-b2",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 시험~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시험",romanization:"siheom",en:"exam",pos:"noun",nuance:"시험 is Sino-Korean 試驗 — 試 'try/test' + 驗 'examine', literally 'a trial examination'. The collocation English speakers must memorize is 시험을 보다 — Koreans 'see' an exam, they do not 'do' it, so 시험을 하다 is wrong. Passing and failing are vivid metaphors with the particle 에: 시험에 붙다 (literally 'stick to the exam') = pass, 시험에 떨어지다 ('fall off the exam') = fail — note these take 에, not 을. Contrast 시험 with 퀴즈(quiz, a small in-class test) and with 검사(檢査, an inspection/medical test like 건강 검사): a graded school test is always 시험, never 퀴즈 unless it is a tiny informal one. The classic 직역 trap is 'take an exam' → 시험을 가지다/취하다 ✗ (English 'take' never transfers); and learners often write 시험을 통과하다, which is understandable but bookish — the everyday word is 붙다."},sentence:{ko:"시험을 위해서 한 달 동안 준비했어요.",en:"I prepared for the exam for one month."},target:"시험",options:["exam","during; for a duration","health"],note:"A noun goal plus a duration."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"준비하다",romanization:"junbihada",en:"to prepare",pos:"verb",nuance:"준비하다 comes from 한자 準備 (準 'standard, level' + 備 'provide, equip'), literally 'get equipped to standard' — to ready things or yourself beforehand. It naturally collocates with events and outcomes: 시험을 준비하다, 여행을 준비하다, 저녁을 준비하다, 마음의 준비. Compare 마련하다 ('arrange/procure' funds or a place — 집을 마련하다) and 챙기다 ('gather up, pack' your own belongings — 여권 챙겼어? = did you grab your passport?); 준비하다 is the broad 'prepare,' while 챙기다 zooms in on not forgetting items. The classic English-speaker pitfall is the particle: 'prepare FOR the exam' tempts 시험에 준비하다, but Korean treats the exam as a direct object — 시험을 준비하다. Also don't reach for 만들다 'make' when you only mean getting ready. As a 하다 verb, negate inside (준비 안 해요), and its passive 준비되다 ('be ready') is everywhere: 준비됐어요."},sentence:{ko:"방학 동안 취업 준비를 할 거예요.",en:"I will prepare for employment during vacation."},target:"준비",options:["to learn","to study","to prepare"],note:"준비하다 links naturally to both purpose and duration."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기간",romanization:"gigan",en:"period",pos:"noun",nuance:"기간 is Sino-Korean 期間 (期 'a fixed term/appointed time' + 間 'interval') — a defined stretch of time with a clear START and END, such as an exam season, a vacation, a contract term, or a sale window. Contrast it sharply with 시간 時間: 시간 is generic 'time/hours/duration' you measure (세 시간 = three hours), while 기간 is a bounded named span you don't usually count in hours (시험 기간 = the exam period, not '×N hours'). Also distinguish 동안, which is the grammatical word for 'during/for' and attaches to spans (방학 동안), whereas 기간 is the noun naming the span itself (often combined: 방학 기간 동안). 기간 collocates with administrative life — 접수/신청 기간 (application period), 계약/임대 기간 (contract/lease term), 유효 기간 (validity period/expiry), 공사 기간 — and takes 에 ('within') or 동안 ('throughout'). The English-speaker pitfall: translating every 'time' as 시간 and saying ✗시험 시간이 길어요 to mean 'the exam season is long' — that actually reads as 'the exam's duration (one sitting) is long'; the season is 시험 기간."},sentence:{ko:"준비 기간 동안 매일 공부했어요.",en:"I studied every day during the preparation period."},target:"기간",options:["Korean language","employment; getting a job","period"],note:"기간 names the period; 동안 marks what happens through it."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"동안",romanization:"dongan",en:"during; for a duration",pos:"noun",nuance:"동안 is about overlapping time or duration. It does not mean the contrastive English 'while' in sentences like 'while this is cheap, that is expensive.'"},sentence:{ko:"세 달 동안 한국에 머물 거예요.",en:"I will stay in Korea for three months."},target:"동안",options:["during; for a duration","Korean language","employment; getting a job"],note:"동안 marks the time span."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"머물다",romanization:"meomulda",en:"to stay",pos:"verb",nuance:"머물다 is the shortened form of 머무르다. Use shortened-stem forms where licensed (머물고, 머물지, 머무는, 머뭅니다), while vowel-initial forms come from 르-irregular 머무르다: 머물러요 and 머물렀어요."},sentence:{ko:"한국에 머무는 동안 많이 연습할 거예요.",en:"While I stay in Korea, I will practice a lot."},target:"머무는",options:["to learn","to stay","to practice"],note:"머무는 동안 uses the verb modifier form."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"방학",romanization:"banghak",en:"school vacation",pos:"noun",nuance:"방학 is Sino-Korean 放學 — 放 'release/let go' + 學 'studies', literally 'releasing (from) studies', i.e. the official school break between terms (summer/winter). The crucial contrast is 방학 vs 휴가(休暇, 'leave/holiday'): 방학 belongs to the school calendar and applies to students/teachers, while 휴가 is the time off a worker takes from a job — a salaried adult on vacation is 휴가 중, never 방학 중. Also distinguish 방학 (a scheduled school break) from 휴일(休日, a single day off / public holiday like a national holiday) and 연휴(連休, a string of consecutive holidays). Note 방학을 하다 means '(the school) goes on break / break starts', an event, not 'I take a vacation'. The English-speaker pitfall is calling a working adult's trip 방학 (it must be 휴가), and forgetting that 'during vacation' uses the time particle 에 (방학에) or the duration noun 동안 (방학 동안), not 에서."},sentence:{ko:"방학 동안 여행할 거예요.",en:"I will travel during vacation."},target:"방학",options:["Korean language","employment; getting a job","school vacation"],note:"A common period noun before 동안."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요. 세 달 동안 머물 거예요.",en:"I came in order to learn Korean. I will stay for three months."},hl:"동안"}],canDo:"Say how long something lasts with a time-span noun (6개월 동안 · 방학 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b3",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 동안",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 동안",sub:"for / during N (a length of time)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"세",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"공부했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"세 시간 동안 공부했어요.",en:"I studied for three hours.",romanization:"se sigan dongan gongbuhaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"방학",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"부모님",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"있었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"방학 동안 부모님 집에 있었어요.",en:"I stayed at my parents' house during the vacation.",romanization:"banghak dongan bumonim jibe isseosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N 동안",rows:[{when:"Number + counter",add:"동안 (no particle)",ex:"세 시간 → 세 시간 동안"},{when:"Period noun",add:"동안",ex:"방학 → 방학 동안 / 일주일 → 일주일 동안"},{when:'"the whole ~" (stronger)',add:"내내",ex:"하루 종일 / 일 년 내내"},{when:'"how long"',add:"얼마 동안",ex:"얼마 동안 한국에 살았어요?"}]},more:{func:'Goes right onto a time-span noun to mean "for" or "during" that period. It tells you how long something lasts.',keyPoint:{label:"동안 vs. 에 vs. 까지",body:"N 동안 = how long something lasts (세 시간 동안 = for three hours). N 에 = a point in time (세 시에 = at three). N 까지 = a deadline (세 시까지 = by three). Use 동안 only for a stretch of time, never a single moment."},pronunciation:"동안 is [동안]. 일주일 is [일쭈일]; in careful speech say [일쭈일 동안]. Some speakers may tense 동안 to [똥안] in connected speech, but this is not liaison of final ㄹ."}}],canDo:"Say how long something lasts with a time-span noun (6개월 동안 · 방학 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b4",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 여행~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"여행",romanization:"yeohaeng",en:"travel; trip",pos:"noun",nuance:"여행 is Sino-Korean, 旅行 ('journey-go'), meaning travel or a trip. The single most natural verb is 가다 — 여행을 가다 ('go on a trip') — while 여행하다 (with light verb 하다) also works but feels a touch more formal/written. Distinguish 여행 from 출장: 출장 is a business trip you're sent on for work, whereas 여행 is travel for pleasure or sightseeing, so don't call a work assignment a 여행. A traveler is 여행자/여행객 and an overseas trip is 해외여행. The English-speaker pitfall is choosing 보다 ('see') because tourists 'go sightseeing' — to travel you 가다 or 하다, not 보다 (sightseeing specifically is 구경하다 or 관광하다). Also note destination takes (으)로/에 (제주도로 여행을 가다), and beginners often drop the particle on 여행 itself — keep 여행을 가다."},sentence:{ko:"한국에 있는 동안 여행도 하고 싶어요.",en:"While I am in Korea, I also want to travel."},target:"여행",options:["period","during; for a duration","travel; trip"],note:"A natural extra plan during a stay."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"건강",romanization:"geongang",en:"health",pos:"noun",nuance:"건강 is Sino-Korean 健康, where 健 means 'sturdy/robust' and 康 means 'peaceful/well,' together the sound, robust state of body and mind. It splits into a noun and an adjective: the noun 건강 ('health') pairs with 좋다/나쁘다 (good/bad) — never 높다/낮다 (high/low), which is the typical English-influenced slip from 'high level of health' — and you actively mind it with 챙기다 (건강을 챙기다, 'look after your health'); the adjective 건강하다 means 'to be healthy' (저는 건강해요 = 'I'm healthy'). Its honorific imperative 건강하세요 is a beloved set phrase, a warm farewell/blessing meaning 'stay healthy,' especially said to elders and at New Year. A near-synonym is 컨디션 (a loanword from 'condition') — but 컨디션 is your day-to-day fitness/energy level (오늘 컨디션이 안 좋아 = 'I'm not feeling great today'), whereas 건강 is your overall, long-term state of wellness; don't swap them."},sentence:{ko:"건강을 위해서 운동해요.",en:"I exercise for my health."},target:"건강",options:["period","during; for a duration","health"],note:"Noun plus 을 위해서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"운동하다",romanization:"undonghada",en:"to exercise",pos:"verb",nuance:"운동하다 = 한자 運動 (運 'move, transport' + 動 'move') + 하다, literally 'movement-movement,' i.e. to physically exercise. The same noun 운동 also means a sports movement or a social campaign, but with 하다 it's working out. A key feature for learners: Korean builds most sports the same way — noun + 하다 (축구하다 play soccer, 수영하다 swim), though racket sports take 치다 'hit' instead (테니스 치다). The contrast with 놀다 ('play, hang out, have fun') matters: 놀다 is unstructured fun, 운동하다 is purposeful physical activity, so don't say 놀다 when you mean training. Natural collocations: 헬스장에서 운동하다, 건강을 위해 운동하다, 운동 부족. As a 하다 verb the negatives split — 운동 안 해요, 운동 못 해요 — and you can swap in 운동을 안 하다 with the object marker for a touch more emphasis."},sentence:{ko:"건강을 위해서 일주일 동안 운동했어요.",en:"I exercised for one week for my health."},target:"운동했",options:["to exercise","to pass an exam","Korean language"],note:"Purpose and duration can appear in one sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"목적",romanization:"mokjeok",en:"purpose; goal",pos:"noun",nuance:"목적 (目的, '目 eye + 的 target' — the target your eye is fixed on) is the conscious aim or intention behind an action, more deliberate and goal-directed than 이유 (the reason behind it) or 원인 (the cause that produced it). Compare the near-synonym 목표: 목표 is a concrete, often measurable target you aim to hit (this month's sales 목표, a finish-line 목표), while 목적 is the underlying purpose or 'what it is for' — a company's 목적 may be 'to help people' but its 목표 is 'sell 1000 units'. Register leans formal/written: it dominates immigration forms (방문 목적), reports, and mission statements. Two pitfalls: do not pair it with 하다 (you 이루다/달성하다 a 목적, you never 목적을 하다), and do not confuse 목적 (purpose) with 목적지 (目的地, 'purpose-place' — the destination, an actual place)."},sentence:{ko:"한국에 온 목적은 공부예요.",en:"The purpose of coming to Korea is study."},target:"목적",options:["purpose; goal","Korean language","employment; getting a job"],note:"Names the idea behind 위해서 sentences."}],canDo:"Mark 'while something is happening' on a verb (공부하는 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b5",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-는 동안",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-는 동안",sub:"while V is happening",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"요리하",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"청소했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"제가 요리하는 동안 친구가 청소했어요.",en:"While I cooked, my friend cleaned.",romanization:"jega yorihaneun dongan chinguga cheongsohaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"아기가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"자",mid:"는",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"책을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"읽었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"아기가 자는 동안 책을 읽었어요.",en:"While the baby was sleeping, I read a book.",romanization:"agiga janeun dongan chaegeul ilgeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-는 동안",rows:[{when:"Action verb stem",add:"-는 동안",ex:"먹다 → 먹는 동안 / 자다 → 자는 동안"},{when:"Stem ending in ㄹ",add:"ㄹ 탈락 + -는 동안",ex:"살다 → 사는 동안 / 만들다 → 만드는 동안"},{when:"Two different subjects (common)",add:"A가 V-는 동안 B가 …",ex:"엄마가 자는 동안 청소했어요"},{when:"Same subject (also fine)",add:"V-는 동안 …",ex:"밥을 먹는 동안 텔레비전을 봤어요"}]},more:{func:'Goes on a verb to mean "while (someone) is doing X." It marks a stretch of time set by one ongoing action, during which something else happens.',keyPoint:{label:"V-는 동안 vs. N 동안",body:"Same word 동안, two ways to attach it. N 동안 takes a time noun (방학 동안 = during vacation). V-는 동안 takes a verb in the -는 form (밥을 먹는 동안 = while eating). If a verb names the time span, you must use -는 동안 — never put 동안 onto the dictionary form."},pronunciation:"먹는 동안 → [멍는 동안] (ㄱ before ㄴ turns into ㅇ). 사는 동안 has no cluster and stays smooth."}}],canDo:"Mark 'while something is happening' on a verb (공부하는 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b6",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A learner explains a Korea stay, study goal, and study period.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"한국에는 왜 왔어요?",en:"Why did you come to Korea?",romanization:"hangugeneun wae wasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요. 세 달 동안 머물 거예요.",en:"I came in order to learn Korean. I will stay for three months.",romanization:"hangugeoreul baeugi wihaeseo wasseoyo. se dal dongan meomul geoyeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"한국에 있는 동안 뭐 할 거예요?",en:"What will you do while you are in Korea?",romanization:"hanguge inneun dongan mwo hal geoyeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"취업을 위해서 매일 한국어를 연습할 거예요.",en:"I will practice Korean every day in order to get a job.",romanization:"chwieobeul wihaeseo maeil hangugeoreul yeonseupal geoyeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"시험 준비도 해요?",en:"Are you preparing for an exam too?",romanization:"siheom junbido haeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 합격하기 위해서 방학 동안 계속 공부할 거예요.",en:"Yes, I will keep studying during vacation in order to pass.",romanization:"ne, hapgyeokagi wihaeseo banghak dongan gyesok gongbuhal geoyeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I studied for three months.'",tokens:["세 달 동안","공부했어요"],correct:"세 달 동안 공부했어요",explanation:"N 동안 marks the time span. 세 달 동안 tells how long the studying continued."}],canDo:"State a purpose before the action (V-기 위해서 · N을/를 위해서)",id:"chapter-31-b7",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A three-month study plan",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A three-month study plan",chunks:["지훈은 한국어를 배우기 위해서 한국에 왔어요. 세 달 동안 서울에 머물 거예요. 한국에 있는 동안 매일 한국어를 연습하고, 주말에는 짧게 여행도 할 거예요.","지훈의 큰 목표는 취업이에요. 그래서 취업을 위해서 시험 준비도 하고 있어요. 방학 동안은 합격하기 위해서 더 오래 공부할 계획이에요."],translation:"Jihoon came to Korea in order to learn Korean. He will stay in Seoul for three months. While he is in Korea, he will practice Korean every day and take short trips on weekends. His big goal is employment. So he is also preparing for an exam in order to get a job. During vacation, he plans to study longer in order to pass.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 왜 한국에 왔어요?",a:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요."},{q:"지훈은 얼마 동안 한국에 머물 거예요?",a:"세 달 동안 머물 거예요."},{q:"지훈은 방학 동안 왜 더 오래 공부할 거예요?",a:"합격하기 위해서 더 오래 공부할 거예요."}]}],canDo:"Say how long something lasts with a time-span noun (6개월 동안 · 방학 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b8",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a deliberate purpose?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 왔어요.",ok:!0},{text:"한국어를 배우다 위해서 왔어요.",ok:!1},{text:"한국어를 배우는 동안 왔어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Use the -기 form before 위해서."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which phrase gives a duration?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"세 달 동안",ok:!0},{text:"세 달에 동안",ok:!1},{text:"세 달을 위해서",ok:!1}],explanation:"동안 attaches directly to the time span."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means 'while I am in Korea'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한국에 있는 동안",ok:!0},{text:"한국에 있기 위해서",ok:!1},{text:"한국에 있다 동안",ok:!1}],explanation:"있다 becomes 있는 before 동안."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"취업___ 위해서 한국어를 연습해요. (I practice Korean for employment.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"N을 위해서 marks the purpose or goal you are working for. 취업이 위해서 is not the purpose pattern."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I studied for three months.'",tokens:["세 달 동안","공부했어요"],correct:"세 달 동안 공부했어요",explanation:"N 동안 marks the time span. 세 달 동안 tells how long the studying continued."}],canDo:"Mark 'while something is happening' on a verb (공부하는 동안)",id:"chapter-31-b9",chapterId:"chapter-31",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-32",number:32,title:"V-기로 했어요 · V-게 됐어요",goal:"Use V-기로 했어요 for decisions that are already settled.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 결정하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"결정하다",romanization:"gyeoljeonghada",en:"to decide",pos:"verb",nuance:"결정하다 is built on the 한자 決定 (決 'resolve, cut through' + 定 'fix, settle'), so it literally means 'cut through and settle' — reaching a firm choice after deliberation. Three near-synonyms divide the space: 정하다 is the everyday, lighter 'set/decide' (그냥 정해요 = let's just settle it); 선택하다 (選擇) is 'select' from given options; 고르다 is the casual 'pick' (고르다 a fruit, a color). Use 결정하다 for consequential, considered decisions (a job, a move, a date) — for which thing to pick off a shelf, 고르다 or 선택하다 fits better. To decide to DO an action, the partner pattern is -기로 결정하다 (가기로 결정했어요); English speakers often forget the -기로 and say 결정했어요 가다. As a 하다 verb the negative splits: 결정 안 해요, 결정 못 해요. The heavier idiom 결정을 내리다 frames it as 'handing down' a decision."},sentence:{ko:"다음 달부터 운동하기로 결정했어요.",en:"I decided to exercise starting next month."},target:"결정했",options:["to exercise","to decide","to start"],note:"A direct verb for decision-making."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"운동하다",romanization:"undonghada",en:"to exercise",pos:"verb",nuance:"운동하다 = 한자 運動 (運 'move, transport' + 動 'move') + 하다, literally 'movement-movement,' i.e. to physically exercise. The same noun 운동 also means a sports movement or a social campaign, but with 하다 it's working out. A key feature for learners: Korean builds most sports the same way — noun + 하다 (축구하다 play soccer, 수영하다 swim), though racket sports take 치다 'hit' instead (테니스 치다). The contrast with 놀다 ('play, hang out, have fun') matters: 놀다 is unstructured fun, 운동하다 is purposeful physical activity, so don't say 놀다 when you mean training. Natural collocations: 헬스장에서 운동하다, 건강을 위해 운동하다, 운동 부족. As a 하다 verb the negatives split — 운동 안 해요, 운동 못 해요 — and you can swap in 운동을 안 하다 with the object marker for a touch more emphasis."},sentence:{ko:"아침마다 운동하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to exercise every morning."},target:"운동하",options:["to exercise","habit","health"],note:"A natural new-habit decision."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시작하다",romanization:"sijakhada",en:"to start",pos:"verb",nuance:"시작하다 means 'to start / begin' and is a Sino-Korean 하다 verb — 시작 (始作, literally 始 'begin' + 作 'make/do', i.e. 'set in motion') plus 하다. As a 하다 verb it conjugates regularly with 하다 → 해요: 시작해요 / 시작합니다 / 시작했어요. A point that trips learners is that 시작하다 works both transitively and intransitively: with a doer it takes an object (저는 일을 시작해요 'I start work', 을/를), and with no doer the thing simply begins and takes the subject marker (수업이 시작해요 'class starts', 이/가). Contrast it with the pure intransitive 시작되다 (始作되다, 'to be started/begin'), which is more formal and never takes an object — 회의가 시작됐어요 'the meeting began'. Its natural opposite is 끝나다 ('to end'). The English-speaker pitfalls are (1) conjugating the 하다 wrongly as 시작하요 instead of 시작해요, and (2) forcing an object onto the no-doer case (수업을 시작해요 when you simply mean class begins) — when nothing actively starts it, use 수업이 시작해요 or the passive 시작돼요."},sentence:{ko:"건강이 안 좋아져서 운동을 시작하기로 했어요.",en:"My health got worse, so I decided to start exercising."},target:"시작하",options:["to start","to get better","to increase; to improve"],note:"The new action begins because of a change."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계획",romanization:"gyehoek",en:"plan",pos:"noun",nuance:"계획(計劃, 'count/reckon + plan') is a worked-out plan or scheme for the future. Its single most important quirk is the verb: Koreans BUILD a plan with 세우다 (literally 'to erect/stand up') — 계획을 세우다 — not 만들다 ('make') as the English calque suggests. Other partners: 계획이 있다 (have a plan) and the smooth adverb 계획대로 ('as planned'). Distinguish 계획 from 예정 豫定, which is a SCHEDULED arrangement that is already set ('내일 출발할 예정이에요' = I'm scheduled/due to leave tomorrow), and from 약속, a personal commitment/appointment with someone — 계획 is your own roadmap, 예정 is a fixed scheduling, 약속 involves another party. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly 계획을 만들다 (use 세우다), and translating 'plan to do' with 계획 as a verb — Korean uses -(으)려고 하다 or -(으)ㄹ 계획이다: 여행할 계획이에요 ('I plan to travel'), not 여행을 계획해요 in casual speech."},sentence:{ko:"계획이 바뀌었어요.",en:"The plan changed."},target:"계획",options:["plan","to decide","to exercise"],note:"A common changed-schedule sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"습관",romanization:"seupgwan",en:"habit",pos:"noun",nuance:"습관 (習慣, 習 'to practice/learn' + 慣 'to grow accustomed') frames a habit as something drilled in until it feels natural, and it leans slightly formal and neutral. The key contrast is 습관 vs 버릇: 습관 is a broad, often deliberate routine (early rising, daily exercise, good study habits), while 버릇 is colloquial and usually a quirky or annoying unconscious tic — nail-biting, leg-shaking, a child's bad manners. Crucially, scolding a child you say 버릇없다 ('has no manners / is rude'), never 습관없다. Both pair with 고치다 ('fix') for breaking them and 들이다/생기다 for forming them (습관을 들이다 = cultivate a habit; 습관이 생기다 = a habit forms). To say you have one, use 습관이 있다. Pitfall: English 'I have a habit of doing X' must use the verb's -는 present-modifier form before 습관: 일찍 자는 습관 (the habit of sleeping early), not 일찍 자기 습관. Also do not use 가지다 here — habits 있다/되다/생기다, they are not 'had' with 가지다."},sentence:{ko:"운동하면서 습관이 바뀌었어요.",en:"As I exercised, my habits changed."},target:"습관",options:["to decide","habit","to exercise"],note:"The noun that names the lifestyle change."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"건강",romanization:"geongang",en:"health",pos:"noun",nuance:"건강 is Sino-Korean 健康, where 健 means 'sturdy/robust' and 康 means 'peaceful/well,' together the sound, robust state of body and mind. It splits into a noun and an adjective: the noun 건강 ('health') pairs with 좋다/나쁘다 (good/bad) — never 높다/낮다 (high/low), which is the typical English-influenced slip from 'high level of health' — and you actively mind it with 챙기다 (건강을 챙기다, 'look after your health'); the adjective 건강하다 means 'to be healthy' (저는 건강해요 = 'I'm healthy'). Its honorific imperative 건강하세요 is a beloved set phrase, a warm farewell/blessing meaning 'stay healthy,' especially said to elders and at New Year. A near-synonym is 컨디션 (a loanword from 'condition') — but 컨디션 is your day-to-day fitness/energy level (오늘 컨디션이 안 좋아 = 'I'm not feeling great today'), whereas 건강 is your overall, long-term state of wellness; don't swap them."},sentence:{ko:"건강이 좋아졌어요.",en:"My health got better."},target:"건강",options:["to decide","to exercise","health"],note:"A core state-change sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"좋아지다",romanization:"joajida",en:"to get better",pos:"verb",nuance:"좋아지다 is the adjective 좋다 (good) plus -아지다, the pattern that turns a state-word into 'become that state', so it literally means 'come to be good' — a gradual drift from worse to better, not a fixed quality. The key contrast is 좋다 vs 좋아지다: 좋다 just says something IS good right now (기분이 좋아요 = I feel good), while 좋아지다 reports a CHANGE toward good (기분이 좋아졌어요 = I feel better than before). It is warm and neutral in register, fine in both 반말 and 존댓말, and shows up constantly with 몸(body), 기분(mood), 날씨(weather), 사이(relationship), and 실력(skill). The classic English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 좋다 when you really mean a transition — 'My Korean got better' is 좋아졌어요, never 좋았어요, because 좋았어요 just means 'it was good (in the past)'. Note too that 좋아지다 means 'come to like' when the subject is a person feeling (그 사람이 좋아졌어요 = I came to like that person), so context decides between 'get better' and 'grow fond of'."},sentence:{ko:"한국어 실력이 좋아졌어요.",en:"My Korean ability improved."},target:"좋아졌",options:["to decide","to exercise","to get better"],note:"좋다 plus -아지다."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아침마다 운동하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to exercise every morning."},hl:"운동하"}],canDo:"Announce a decision you made (운동하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-32-b1",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-기로 했어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-기로 했어요",sub:"decided to V",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"내일부터",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한국어를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부하",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"내일부터 한국어를 공부하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to study Korean starting tomorrow.",romanization:"naeil-buteo hangugeo-reul gongbuhagiro haesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구하고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"같이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구하고 같이 영화를 보기로 했어요.",en:"My friend and I decided to watch a movie together.",romanization:"chinguhago gachi yeonghwa-reul bogiro haesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-기로 했어요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ending in a vowel",add:"stem + 기로 했어요",ex:"가다 → 가기로 했어요 (decided to go)"},{when:"Verb stem ending in a consonant",add:"stem + 기로 했어요",ex:"먹다 → 먹기로 했어요 (decided to eat)"},{when:"Decided NOT to do it",add:"안 + V-기로 했어요 (or stem + 지 않기로 했어요)",ex:"마시다 → 안 마시기로 했어요 (decided not to drink)"}]},more:{func:"Used to say you have decided or made up your mind to do something. Often the decision was made after thinking it over or agreeing with other people.",keyPoint:{label:"-기로 했어요 (a decision) vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요 (a plan)",body:"-기로 했어요 stresses that you reached a decision, often after thinking or agreeing with others. -(으)ㄹ 거예요 just says what you are going to do. 했어요 is past because the deciding already happened, even though the action itself is still coming up. (The negative -지 않기로 했어요 comes later.)"},pronunciation:"먹기로 sounds like [먹끼로] (meok-kkiro): after the ㄱ at the end of 먹, the next ㄱ becomes a tense [ㄲ]."}}],canDo:"Announce a decision you made (운동하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-32-b2",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 바뀌다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바뀌다",romanization:"bakkwida",en:"to change; to be switched",pos:"verb",nuance:"바뀌다 is the intransitive twin of 바꾸다: the thing that changes is itself the subject (이/가) and nobody is named as the doer, so it covers things getting switched, replaced, or mixed up on their own. The dividing line vs 변하다 is key — 변하다 means a thing's inner nature transforms (사람이 변했다 = he's become a different person), while 바뀌다 means one item is swapped out for another (번호가 바뀌었다 = the number was replaced). Use 바꾸다 when YOU actively swap something (번호를 바꿨어요) and 바뀌다 when it simply ends up different (번호가 바뀌었어요). The 요-form is 바뀌어요 because the stem ends in the vowel 위, which won't contract — so spelling matters: it's 바뀌-, and the polite past is 바뀌었어요, never the wrong 바껴요/바꼈어요 that English speakers reach for by analogy."},sentence:{ko:"회의 시간이 세 시로 바뀌었어요.",en:"The meeting time changed to three."},target:"바뀌",options:["to decide","to change; to be switched","to exercise"],note:"Useful for schedules and plans."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"달라지다",romanization:"dallajida",en:"to become different; to change",pos:"verb",nuance:"달라지다 spotlights the before-and-after gap: something is now clearly unlike how it used to be, which makes it the everyday verb for noticing change in a person's looks, mood, or a situation. It's the inchoative of the adjective 다르다 (to be different) — 다르 + -아지다 'become' — and that origin is the #1 pitfall: 다르다 states a static difference between two things (A는 B와 달라요 = A is different from B), while 달라지다 is the dynamic act of becoming different over time (분위기가 달라졌어요 = the mood changed). It overlaps with 변하다 and 바뀌다, but the dividing line is focus: 달라지다 = 'the result looks different now,' 변하다 = 'the inner nature transformed,' 바뀌다 = 'one thing got swapped for another.' Both 달라지다 and the thing changing take 이/가; the form 달라져요 comes from -지다 verbs taking 어 in the 요-form (지+어 → 져)."},sentence:{ko:"운동을 시작하고 하루가 달라졌어요.",en:"After starting exercise, my day became different."},target:"달라졌",options:["to decide","to become different; to change","to exercise"],note:"Emphasizes that the whole feel is different now."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"실력",romanization:"sillyeok",en:"skill level; actual ability",pos:"noun",nuance:"실력 is not confidence or effort; it is the ability itself."},sentence:{ko:"연습할수록 실력이 늘어요.",en:"The more I practice, the more my skill improves."},target:"실력",options:["health","body","skill level; actual ability"],note:"실력 pairs naturally with 늘다 and 좋아지다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"늘다",romanization:"neulda",en:"to increase; to improve",pos:"verb",nuance:"늘다 means an amount, number, or ability grows bigger, and it is the everyday word for skills improving — 실력이 늘다 is THE phrase for 'getting better at something'. It is a ㄹ-irregular verb: the final ㄹ drops before endings beginning with ㄴ, ㅂ, or ㅅ, which is why the formal present is 늡니다 (not *늘습니다) — the ㄹ vanishes before -ㅂ니다. Watch the synonym split: 늘다 is intransitive (the thing increases by itself — 실력이 늘어요), while its causative twin 늘리다 is transitive (you increase a thing on purpose — 시간을 늘려요 = I extend the time). Compare also 증가하다, the stiff Sino-Korean 'increase' used in reports and statistics (인구가 증가했다); 늘다 is the warm spoken equivalent. The English-speaker pitfall is two-fold: first, forgetting the ㄹ-drop and saying *늘습니다; second, saying 'I improved my Korean' as if you act on it — Koreans say 한국어가 늘었어요 (Korean increased) with the skill as the subject, not as an object you operate on."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 매일 쓰니까 실력이 늘었어요.",en:"Because I use Korean every day, my skill improved."},target:"늘었",options:["to decide","to increase; to improve","to exercise"],note:"Intransitive improvement: 실력이 늘다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"줄다",romanization:"julda",en:"to decrease",pos:"verb",nuance:"줄다 is the exact opposite of 늘다 — it means an amount, number, or size gets smaller — and the two are learned as a pair (늘다/줄다). Like 늘다 it is a ㄹ-irregular verb, so the ㄹ drops before -ㅂ니다, giving the formal 줍니다 (not *줄습니다). The crucial synonym contrast is 줄다 vs 줄이다: 줄다 is intransitive — the thing shrinks by itself (소리가 줄었어요 = the sound got quieter on its own), while 줄이다 is the causative — you deliberately reduce something (소리를 줄였어요 = I turned the sound down). Its formal report-style synonym is 감소하다 (Sino-Korean 減少, 'reduce-decrease'), used for statistics; 줄다 is the natural conversational word. The English-speaker pitfall mirrors 늘다: don't say *줄습니다 (the ㄹ drops to make 줍니다), and don't put an object with 줄다. 'I reduced my spending' needs the transitive 줄이다 (지출을 줄였어요); 'my spending decreased' uses 줄다 with the subject particle (지출이 줄었어요)."},sentence:{ko:"운동을 시작하고 스트레스가 줄었어요.",en:"After I started exercising, my stress decreased."},target:"줄었",options:["to decide","to decrease","to exercise"],note:"A natural result-change verb."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"살다",romanization:"salda",en:"to live",pos:"verb",nuance:"살다 means 'to live' in two senses — to reside somewhere and to be alive — and is a ㄹ-irregular verb. The stem ㄹ disappears before ㅂ, ㄴ, and the honorific 시, producing 삽니다 (not 살읍니다), 사세요 (not 살으세요), and 사니까; before vowel endings the ㄹ stays, so the polite present is 살아요. For where you live, the default particle is the static-location 에: 서울에 살아요. Interestingly, because living is an activity carried out over time, the dynamic-location 에서 is also acceptable and common, especially with hopes and experiences (한국에서 살고 싶어요) — this is one of the few cases where both 에 and 에서 work with the same verb. Contrast 살다 with 거주하다 (居住하다, Sino-Korean 'to reside'), a stiff, formal/written word used on official forms (거주지 'place of residence'); in speech you always say 살다. The English-speaker pitfall is keeping the ㄹ to say 살읍니다 instead of 삽니다, and reflexively choosing 에서 for every location when plain residence prefers 에."},sentence:{ko:"회사 일 때문에 한국에 살게 됐어요.",en:"Because of work, I ended up living in Korea."},target:"살게",options:["to live","to decide","to exercise"],note:"A classic circumstantial 게 되다 sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이사하다",romanization:"isahada",en:"to move house",pos:"verb",nuance:"이사하다 specifically means relocating your residence. 옮기다 is broader: 책상을 옮기다 moves furniture, while 집을 옮기다 or 거처를 옮기다 can also describe changing residence. Both 이사 안 해요 and 안 이사해요 are grammatical short negatives. Do not say 집을 이사해요; use 이사해요 or 집을 옮겨요."},sentence:{ko:"회사 때문에 갑자기 이사하게 됐어요.",en:"Because of work, I suddenly ended up moving."},target:"이사하",options:["habit","health","to move house"],note:"A change caused by circumstances."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"벌써 습관이 바뀌었어요?",en:"Have your habits already changed?"},hl:"바뀌"}],canDo:"Describe a change that came about from circumstances (-게 됐어요)",id:"chapter-32-b3",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-게 됐어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-게 됐어요",sub:"ended up V-ing / came to V",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"때문에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한국에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"살",mid:"게",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"됐어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"일 때문에 한국에 살게 됐어요.",en:"I ended up living in Korea because of work.",romanization:"il ttaemune hanguge salge dwaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"덕분에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한국",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좋아하",mid:"게",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"됐어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구 덕분에 한국 영화를 좋아하게 됐어요.",en:"Thanks to my friend, I came to like Korean movies.",romanization:"chingu deokbune hanguk yeonghwa-reul joahage dwaesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-게 됐어요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ending in a vowel",add:"stem + 게 됐어요",ex:"가다 → 가게 됐어요 (ended up going)"},{when:"Verb stem ending in a consonant",add:"stem + 게 됐어요",ex:"살다 → 살게 됐어요 (ended up living)"},{when:"With a negative (못/안)",add:"못/안 + V-게 됐어요",ex:"먹다 → 못 먹게 됐어요 (ended up not being able to eat)"}]},more:{func:"Used when something happens because of circumstances rather than your own choice. It shows a change or a result that came about, often through other people or how things worked out.",keyPoint:{label:"-기로 했어요 (my choice) vs. -게 됐어요 (not my choice)",body:"-기로 했어요 means 'I decided' — the will is yours. -게 됐어요 means 'it came about' — the result is from circumstances or other people. Koreans often use -게 됐어요 to sound modest, even with good news (for example, 결혼하게 됐어요, 'I'm getting married')."},pronunciation:"좋아하게 sounds like [조아하게] (jo-a-ha-ge): the ㅎ in 좋 goes silent before the vowel, so 좋아 links smoothly."}}],canDo:"Describe a change that came about from circumstances (-게 됐어요)",id:"chapter-32-b4",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 맡다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"맡다",romanization:"matda",en:"to take charge of; to be assigned",pos:"verb",nuance:"맡다 is the everyday native-Korean verb for taking on a role or responsibility — a task, a project, a part, even a saved seat. It feels more personal and active than the Sino-Korean 담당하다: where 담당하다 is the official 'assigned to me' on an org chart, 맡다 is the warm, often-volunteered 'I'll take this', so 제가 맡을게요 sounds team-friendly and proactive in a meeting. Crucial spelling and conjugation point: the stem ends in ㅌ, and it is a plain regular verb, NOT a ㄷ-irregular — so it stays 맡아요 / 맡았어요 / 맡을게요, and learners who mistakenly 'soften' the ㅌ to ㄹ (마라요, 마랐어요) are simply wrong. Its causative partner 맡기다 ('to entrust/hand over') flips the direction: you 맡다 a job yourself, but you 맡기다 it to someone else (저한테 맡기세요). Habitual collocations: 역할을 맡다, 책임을 맡다, 업무를 맡다, 자리를 맡다."},sentence:{ko:"새 일을 맡게 됐어요.",en:"I ended up taking charge of a new task."},target:"맡게",options:["to exercise","to get better","to take charge of; to be assigned"],note:"Good for work or school responsibilities."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"일찍",romanization:"iljjik",en:"early",pos:"adverb",nuance:"일찍 means 'early' in the sense of an EARLY POINT IN TIME — early hour of the day or ahead of a schedule — and is the natural opposite of 늦게(late). The classic confusion is 일찍 vs 빨리: 일찍 is about WHEN on the clock (일찍 일어나요 = 'wake up at an early hour'), while 빨리 is about SPEED/quickness (빨리 일어나요 = 'get up quick!'). So '일찍 오세요' invites someone to arrive at an early time, whereas '빨리 오세요' urges them to hurry. It pairs habitually with 자다, 일어나다, 출근하다, 도착하다, 출발하다. Register-neutral and polite-safe. The English-speaker pitfall is exactly this 일찍/빨리 mix-up, since English 'early' and 'fast/quick' feel adjacent but map to two different Korean adverbs; also remember 일찍 is an adverb already, so you never conjugate it like the adjective 이르다."},sentence:{ko:"요즘 일찍 자게 됐어요.",en:"These days I have come to sleep earlier."},target:"일찍",options:["to decide","early","to exercise"],note:"A practical new-habit sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"자다",romanization:"jada",en:"to sleep",pos:"verb",nuance:"자다 becomes 잘 before some future or ability forms: 잘 거예요, 잘 수 있어요."},sentence:{ko:"오늘부터 일찍 자기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to sleep early starting today."},target:"자기",options:["habit","to sleep","health"],note:"자다 becomes 자기로 in the decision pattern."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"몸",romanization:"mom",en:"body",pos:"noun",nuance:"몸 is the physical body taken as a whole, and crucially it's also the word Koreans reach for to describe how they FEEL: 몸이 아파요 (sick) and 몸이 안 좋아요 (off/unwell) are the everyday health phrases. The near-synonym is the Sino-Korean 신체(身體, 'body-substance'), but 신체 is clinical/official — 신체검사 (physical exam), 신체 활동 (physical activity) — and sounds stiff if you use it about yourself, so say 몸. Habitual collocations: 몸이 아프다, 몸이 안 좋다, 몸이 피곤하다, 몸이 무겁다, 몸조심하다, 몸이 약하다 (to have a weak constitution). The big English-speaker pitfall is the particle and the verb: you say 몸이 아프다 ('the body is sick'), so the body is the SUBJECT (이/가) — never 몸을 아파요. Also don't directly translate 'my body is heavy' as a weight statement; 몸이 무겁다 means you feel sluggish/lethargic, not that you weigh a lot."},sentence:{ko:"운동하고 나서 몸이 가벼워졌어요.",en:"After exercising, my body felt lighter."},target:"몸",options:["skill level; actual ability","to decide","body"],note:"몸 is the subject of the changed state."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가볍다",romanization:"gabyeopda",en:"light",pos:"adjective",nuance:"가볍다 means light in weight (가벼운 가방), and figuratively 'light' in many senses: 마음이 가볍다 (heart is light/relieved), 가벼운 감기 (a mild cold), 가벼운 농담 (a light joke), 가볍게 운동하다 (to exercise lightly), and even negatively 입이 가볍다 ('light-mouthed' = can't keep a secret, the exact opposite of 입이 무겁다). It is the opposite of 무겁다 (heavy). Conjugation is the ㅂ-irregular: ㅂ → 우 before a vowel ending, so 가벼워요 and the modifier 가벼운 — the same pattern as 무겁다→무거워, 춥다→추워. A synonym note: 가볍다 covers 'light/mild', but 'easy' (simple to do) is 쉽다, so 'an easy question' is 쉬운 문제, not 가벼운 문제 — though 가벼운 문제 can mean a 'minor/trivial' issue. Be careful too that 'light' as in brightness/color is 밝다 (밝은 색 = a light/bright color), not 가볍다. It collocates with 가방·노트북·옷·마음·발걸음·농담·감기. English-speaker pitfall: writing 가볍어요 instead of 가벼워요, and stretching 가볍다 to cover 'easy' (use 쉽다) or 'light-colored' (use 밝다)."},sentence:{ko:"몸이 가벼워졌어요.",en:"My body felt lighter."},target:"가벼워",options:["to decide","to exercise","light"],note:"가볍다 becomes 가벼워지다."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 일찍 자게 되고 몸도 가벼워졌어요.",en:"Yes. I came to sleep earlier, and my body feels lighter too."},hl:"일찍"}],canDo:"Say how a state changed over time (좋아졌어요 · 달라졌어요)",id:"chapter-32-b5",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A-아/어졌어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A-아/어졌어요",sub:"became / got (a change of state)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"한국어",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"점점",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있",mid:"어졌어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"한국어 공부가 점점 재미있어졌어요.",en:"Studying Korean has gradually gotten fun.",romanization:"hangugeo gongbuga jeomjeom jaemiisseojyeosseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많",mid:"아졌어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구가 많아졌어요.",en:"I have more friends now.",romanization:"chinguga manajyeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"A-아/어졌어요",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"stem + 아졌어요",ex:"좋다 → 좋아졌어요 (got better); 많다 → 많아졌어요 (became more)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"stem + 어졌어요",ex:"크다 → 커졌어요 (got bigger)"},{when:"하다 adjectives",add:"stem + 해졌어요",ex:"편하다 → 편해졌어요 (became comfortable)"}]},more:{func:"Added to adjectives to say a state changed — something 'became' or 'got' a certain way. It turns a description (is ~) into a change (became ~).",keyPoint:{label:"좋다 (a state) vs. 좋아졌어요 (a change of state)",body:"좋다 means 'is good' — it just describes. 좋아졌어요 means 'got/became good' — it shows a change. Use -아/어졌어요 whenever English would say 'became,' 'got,' or 'grew' plus an adjective. (For ㅡ-vowel stems like 크다, the ㅡ drops: 크 → 커졌어요 — more on those later.)"},pronunciation:"많아졌어요 sounds like [마나져써요] (ma-na-jyeo-sseo-yo): the ㅎ in 많 is silent and the ㄴ links to the next vowel."}}],canDo:"Say how a state changed over time (좋아졌어요 · 달라졌어요)",id:"chapter-32-b6",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A friend explains a new health decision and the changes that followed.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"다음 달부터 뭐가 달라져요?",en:"What will be different starting next month?",romanization:"daeum dalbuteo mwoga dallajyeoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아침마다 운동하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to exercise every morning.",romanization:"achimmada undonghagiro haesseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"직접 결정한 거예요?",en:"Did you decide that yourself?",romanization:"jikjeop gyeoljeonghan geoyeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 건강이 안 좋아져서 시작하기로 했어요.",en:"Yes, my health got worse, so I decided to start.",romanization:"ne, geongangi an joajyeoseo sijakhagiro haesseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"벌써 습관이 바뀌었어요?",en:"Have your habits already changed?",romanization:"beolsseo seupgwani bakkwieosseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 일찍 자게 되고 몸도 가벼워졌어요.",en:"Yes. I came to sleep earlier, and my body feels lighter too.",romanization:"ne. iljjik jage doego momdo gabyeowojyeosseoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'My habit changed.'",tokens:["습관이","바뀌었어요"],correct:"습관이 바뀌었어요",explanation:"바뀌다 describes something changing, so 습관이 is the subject. The past form 바뀌었어요 reports the completed change."}],canDo:"Announce a decision you made (운동하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-32-b7",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · A habit that changed the day",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"A habit that changed the day",chunks:["지훈은 건강이 안 좋아져서 아침마다 운동하기로 했어요. 처음에는 직접 결정한 일이었지만, 운동을 시작하고 나서 하루가 조금씩 달라졌어요. 밤에는 일찍 자게 됐고, 아침에는 몸이 가벼워졌어요.","한 달 후에는 습관도 많이 바뀌었어요. 지훈은 한국어도 매일 연습하기로 했고, 연습할수록 실력이 늘어서 더 자신감이 생겼어요."],translation:"Jihoon decided to exercise every morning because his health had gotten worse. At first it was a decision he made himself, but after he started exercising, his day gradually became different. At night he came to sleep earlier, and in the morning his body felt lighter. After one month, his habits changed a lot too. Jihoon also decided to practice Korean every day, and as he practiced, his ability improved and he gained more confidence.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 왜 운동하기로 했어요?",a:"건강이 안 좋아져서 운동하기로 했어요."},{q:"지훈은 밤에 어떻게 자게 됐어요?",a:"일찍 자게 됐어요."},{q:"한국어를 연습할수록 무엇이 늘었어요?",a:"실력이 늘었어요."}]}],canDo:"Describe a change that came about from circumstances (-게 됐어요)",id:"chapter-32-b8",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence states a settled decision?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"아침마다 운동하기로 했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"아침마다 운동하게 됐어요.",ok:!1},{text:"아침마다 운동이 좋아졌어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-기로 했어요 reports a decision that has already been made."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence sounds like the situation came about through circumstances?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"회사 때문에 한국에 살게 됐어요.",ok:!0},{text:"회사 때문에 한국에 살기로 했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"회사 때문에 한국에 살고 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-게 됐어요 shows how the situation came to be."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence describes a state change?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"몸이 가벼워졌어요.",ok:!0},{text:"몸이 가볍기로 했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"몸이 가벼웠어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"가벼웠어요 reports a past state; 가벼워졌어요 reports a change into that state."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"건강___ 좋아졌어요. (My health got better.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이",ok:!0},{text:"을",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"좋아지다 describes a subject changing state, so 건강 takes 이. 을 would make health the object of an action."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'My habit changed.'",tokens:["습관이","바뀌었어요"],correct:"습관이 바뀌었어요",explanation:"바뀌다 describes something changing, so 습관이 is the subject. The past form 바뀌었어요 reports the completed change."}],canDo:"Say how a state changed over time (좋아졌어요 · 달라졌어요)",id:"chapter-32-b9",chapterId:"chapter-32",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-33",number:33,title:"V-(스)ㅂ니다 · Choosing the style",goal:"Use formal polite endings like -습니다, -ㅂ니다, and -습니까 in public or formal settings.",biteCount:9,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 해요체~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"해요체",romanization:"haeyoche",en:"everyday polite speech style",pos:"noun",nuance:"해요체 is polite conversation, not formal ceremony language. It sounds warmer and more natural than 합쇼체 in daily life."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 아니면 처음에는 해요체로 말하는 게 안전해요.",en:"If they are not a friend, it is safe to speak in haeyo style at first."},target:"해요체",options:["formality","everyday polite speech style","presentation"],note:"Everyday polite default."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"합쇼체",romanization:"hapsyoche",en:"formal polite speech style",pos:"noun",nuance:"합쇼체 overlaps with the name 하십시오체. Learners mainly need to recognize the endings -ㅂ니다/습니다, -ㅂ니까/습니까, and -(으)십시오."},sentence:{ko:"발표에서는 합쇼체가 자연스러워요.",en:"In a presentation, formal polite style sounds natural."},target:"합쇼체",options:["formality","presentation","formal polite speech style"],note:"Formal public style."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"반말",romanization:"banmal",en:"casual non-polite speech",pos:"noun",nuance:"반말 is not 'bad Korean'. It is the right style in close relationships, but the wrong style in many public or first-meeting settings."},sentence:{ko:"친한 친구랑은 반말을 써요.",en:"I use casual speech with close friends."},target:"반말",options:["formality","presentation","casual non-polite speech"],note:"Relationship makes it acceptable."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"존댓말",romanization:"jondaenmal",en:"polite or respectful speech",pos:"noun",nuance:"존댓말 is about social respect. It does not automatically mean the most formal ending possible."},sentence:{ko:"처음에는 존댓말로 말하세요.",en:"At first, speak politely."},target:"존댓말",options:["polite or respectful speech","formality","presentation"],note:"Safe default for first meetings."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"말투",romanization:"maltu",en:"way of speaking; speech tone",pos:"noun",nuance:"말투 can describe grammar level, but also attitude. A sentence can be grammatically polite and still sound cold or awkward."},sentence:{ko:"발표에서는 말투를 조금 더 격식 있게 바꿔야 해요.",en:"In a presentation, you need to make your tone a little more formal."},target:"말투",options:["way of speaking; speech tone","formality","job interview"],note:"Tone includes ending, word choice, and attitude."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"격식",romanization:"gyeoksik",en:"formality",pos:"noun",nuance:"격식 is not just politeness. It is the formal frame expected by the setting."},sentence:{ko:"면접은 격식 있는 자리예요.",en:"An interview is a formal setting."},target:"격식",options:["everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style","formality"],note:"Setting determines speech level."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"발표에서는 어떤 말투를 써야 해요?",en:"What speech tone should I use in a presentation?"},hl:"말투"}],canDo:"Use the formal polite ending in formal settings (-(스)ㅂ니다, 합니다체)",id:"chapter-33-b1",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(스)ㅂ니다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(스)ㅂ니다",sub:"the formal polite ending (합니다체)",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel (no 받침)",add:"stem + ㅂ니다",ex:"가다 → 갑니다 / 오다 → 옵니다 / 크다 → 큽니다"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (has 받침)",add:"stem + 습니다",ex:"먹다 → 먹습니다 / 읽다 → 읽습니다 / 작다 → 작습니다"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ (the ㄹ drops)",add:"drop ㄹ, then + ㅂ니다",ex:"살다 → 삽니다 / 만들다 → 만듭니다 / 알다 → 압니다"},{when:"이다 (to be), after a noun",add:"noun + 입니다",ex:"학생이다 → 학생입니다 / 친구이다 → 친구입니다"},{when:"Question form",add:"-(스)ㅂ니까?",ex:"가다 → 갑니까? / 먹다 → 먹습니까?"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 학생입니다.",en:"I'm a student.",romanization:"jeo-neun hak-saeng-im-ni-da.",note:"이다 attaches to the noun 학생 as 입니다 — the standard way to introduce yourself."},{ko:"아침마다 밥을 먹습니다.",en:"I eat every morning.",romanization:"a-chim-ma-da ba-beul meok-seum-ni-da.",note:'먹다 has a 받침 (ㄱ), so it takes 습니다 → 먹습니다. 밥을 먹다 just means "to eat (a meal)".'}],more:{func:"This is the formal polite ending for statements. It sounds more formal than the everyday -아요/-어요 (해요체) ending, so you hear it in announcements, at work, in the news, and when you first meet someone.",keyPoint:{label:"받침 decides ㅂ니다 vs 습니다",body:"No 받침 → add ㅂ니다 (가 → 갑니다). Has 받침 → add 습니다 (먹 → 먹습니다). The one exception is ㄹ stems: the ㄹ drops and you use ㅂ니다 (살 → 삽니다), so they act like vowel stems."},pronunciation:"The ㅂ in -ㅂ니다 sounds like [m] before ㄴ: 갑니다 sounds like [감니다], 입니다 like [임니다], and 습니다 like [슴니다]."}}],canDo:"Use the formal polite ending in formal settings (-(스)ㅂ니다, 합니다체)",id:"chapter-33-b2",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 발표~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"발표",romanization:"balpyo",en:"presentation",pos:"noun",nuance:"발표(發表)는 '필 발 + 겉 표', 곧 '안에 있던 것을 겉으로 드러내 알림'이라는 뜻으로, 여러 사람 앞에서 자기 생각이나 조사한 내용을 말로 알리는 것을 가리켜요. 비슷한 '연설'은 정치인·대표가 청중에게 행하는 더 공식적이고 일방적인 말하기라 학교 과제에는 안 어울리고, '공지'는 '날짜·결과를 알리는 통보'라 말로 설명하는 발표와 다르며, '보고'는 윗사람에게 결과를 전달하는 데 초점이 있어요. 그래서 학생이 수업에서 하는 것은 '발표'가 딱 맞아요. '발표하다' 동사와 '발표를 하다' 형태가 둘 다 자연스럽고, '발표 준비/자료/순서'와 잘 어울려요. 한 가지 더, '합격자 발표'처럼 결과를 공표한다는 뜻도 있어 영어 announce와 겹치지만, 수업 맥락에서는 말로 설명하는 presentation을 뜻한다는 점을 구분하세요."},sentence:{ko:"지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다.",en:"I will begin the presentation now."},target:"발표",options:["everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style","presentation"],note:"A real 합쇼체 setting."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"면접",romanization:"myeonjeop",en:"job interview",pos:"noun",nuance:"면접 (面接, 'face-contact/meet') literally means meeting face-to-face, and refers to the job interview where you sit before evaluators. The most important thing to lock in is the FIXED verb: 면접을 보다, literally 'to see an interview,' which uses the same 보다 as 시험을 보다 (take a test) — Korean treats undergoing an evaluation as 'seeing' it. The synonym contrast: 면접 is the oral, face-to-face interview, while 시험 (試驗) is a written test/exam, and 면담 (面談) is a softer counseling-style talk (e.g., with a professor), not a hiring screen. After a 면접 you hope to 합격하다 (pass) rather than 떨어지다 (fall/fail) or 불합격하다. Register: neutral, used in any job-search context. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 하다: 면접을 하다 sounds off from the candidate's side — you 면접을 보다 (take it); the company that conducts it may 면접을 진행하다. Also note you pass 면접에 (with 에), patterning like 시험에 합격하다."},sentence:{ko:"면접에서는 존댓말로 또박또박 말해요.",en:"In an interview, I speak clearly in polite speech."},target:"면접",options:["job interview","casual non-polite speech","polite or respectful speech"],note:"Formal work setting."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"선생님",romanization:"seonsaengnim",en:"teacher",pos:"noun",nuance:"님 makes the word respectful. 선생님 is also used as a polite title."},sentence:{ko:"선생님께는 존댓말을 써요.",en:"I use polite speech with the teacher."},target:"선생님",options:["everyday polite speech style","teacher","formal polite speech style"],note:"Relationship-based speech level."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"친구",romanization:"chingu",en:"friend",pos:"noun",nuance:"친구 can mean friend or same-age peer depending on context."},sentence:{ko:"친한 친구에게는 반말이 자연스러워요.",en:"With a close friend, casual speech sounds natural."},target:"친구",options:["everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style","friend"],note:"Close relationship."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"상황",romanization:"sanghwang",en:"situation",pos:"noun",nuance:"상황 (狀況, '狀 condition/form + 況 circumstances' — the configuration of circumstances) describes the overall set of conditions surrounding a moment or event, broader than a single 경우 (a specific case or instance — 이런 경우 = 'in this one case', vs 이런 상황 = 'in this whole situation'). The reflexive phrase 상황에 따라(서) 다르다 ('it depends on the situation') is one of the most useful hedges in Korean, and 이런/그런 상황에서 sets up advice or reactions. Register is neutral. Two pitfalls: keep 상황 (the circumstances around an event) distinct from 상태 (狀態, the state/condition of a single thing or body — 건강 상태 'health condition', 배터리 상태 'battery state'); you describe a person's mood with a 상태, but the events around them with a 상황. And use 에 따라 (not 으로) for 'depending on': 상황에 따라, never 상황으로."},sentence:{ko:"상황에 따라 말투가 달라져요.",en:"The way of speaking changes depending on the situation."},target:"상황",options:["everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style","situation"],note:"Core idea of this chapter."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"말하다",romanization:"malhada",en:"to speak; to say",pos:"verb",nuance:"Use 로 for language: 한국어로 말해요, 영어로 말해요. 말하다 can mean both “speak” and “say,” so the object or quote decides the English translation."},sentence:{ko:"격식 있는 자리에서는 천천히 말합니다.",en:"In a formal setting, I speak slowly."},target:"말합",options:["everyday polite speech style","to speak; to say","formal polite speech style"],note:"Verb that carries the speech level."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"발표에서는 어떤 말투를 써야 해요?",en:"What speech tone should I use in a presentation?"},hl:"발표"}],canDo:"Choose between 해요체, 합니다체, and 반말 to fit the listener",id:"chapter-33-b3",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · Choosing the style",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"Choosing the style",sub:"해요체 vs 합니다체 vs 반말",rows:[{when:"해요체 — polite, the default for daily life",add:"stem + -아요/-어요/-해요",ex:"가다 → 가요 / 먹다 → 먹어요 / 공부하다 → 공부해요"},{when:"합니다체 — formal polite (work, announcements, first meeting)",add:"stem + -(스)ㅂ니다",ex:"가다 → 갑니다 / 먹다 → 먹습니다"},{when:"반말 — casual (close friends, younger people, family)",add:"해요체 minus 요",ex:"가요 → 가 / 먹어요 → 먹어 / 공부해요 → 공부해"},{when:"Same sentence, three styles (먹다)",add:"먹습니다 / 먹어요 / 먹어",ex:'all mean "(I) eat", from most formal to most casual'}],examples:[{ko:"안녕하세요? 저는 한국 사람이에요.",en:"Hello. I'm Korean.",romanization:"an-nyeong-ha-se-yo? jeo-neun han-guk sa-ram-i-e-yo.",note:"해요체 — the safe, friendly default for people you don't know well."},{ko:"안녕하십니까? 만나서 반갑습니다.",en:"Hello. Nice to meet you.",romanization:"an-nyeong-ha-sim-ni-kka? man-na-seo ban-gap-seum-ni-da.",note:"합니다체 — a formal first greeting; you'd hear this in business and formal settings."}],more:{func:"Korean changes the verb ending depending on who you're talking to. The three everyday styles are 해요체 (polite, all-purpose), 합니다체 (formal polite), and 반말 (casual, no -요). The listener and the situation pick the ending — not the meaning of the sentence — so the wrong choice can sound rude or oddly stiff.",keyPoint:{label:"The listener, not the meaning, picks the ending",body:"합니다체 = formal/distant (work, strangers, announcements). 해요체 = polite all-purpose (the safe everyday choice). 반말 = casual, only with close friends, younger people, or family. When unsure in ordinary conversation, 해요체 is usually the safest polite default; wording, tone, and highly formal settings still matter."},pronunciation:"For plain 반말 pairs such as 가./가?, spelling stays the same and rising pitch distinguishes the question. Other casual question endings, including -니? and -(으)ㄹ까?, also exist."}}],canDo:"Choose between 해요체, 합니다체, and 반말 to fit the listener",id:"chapter-33-b4",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 시작하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시작하다",romanization:"sijakhada",en:"to start",pos:"verb",nuance:"시작하다 means 'to start / begin' and is a Sino-Korean 하다 verb — 시작 (始作, literally 始 'begin' + 作 'make/do', i.e. 'set in motion') plus 하다. As a 하다 verb it conjugates regularly with 하다 → 해요: 시작해요 / 시작합니다 / 시작했어요. A point that trips learners is that 시작하다 works both transitively and intransitively: with a doer it takes an object (저는 일을 시작해요 'I start work', 을/를), and with no doer the thing simply begins and takes the subject marker (수업이 시작해요 'class starts', 이/가). Contrast it with the pure intransitive 시작되다 (始作되다, 'to be started/begin'), which is more formal and never takes an object — 회의가 시작됐어요 'the meeting began'. Its natural opposite is 끝나다 ('to end'). The English-speaker pitfalls are (1) conjugating the 하다 wrongly as 시작하요 instead of 시작해요, and (2) forcing an object onto the no-doer case (수업을 시작해요 when you simply mean class begins) — when nothing actively starts it, use 수업이 시작해요 or the passive 시작돼요."},sentence:{ko:"지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다.",en:"I will begin the presentation now."},target:"시작하",options:["to start","everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style"],note:"Formal opening verb."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"끝나다",romanization:"kkeunnada",en:"to end; to be over",pos:"verb",nuance:"끝나다 means 'to end / be over / finish' as something that happens by itself — it is intransitive, so the thing that ends takes the subject marker 이/가: 수업이 끝나요, past 끝났어요 (끝나 + 았어요). The essential pairing is with its transitive twin 끝내다 ('to finish/end something'), used when a person actively brings an action to a close with an object: 저는 일을 끝내요 ('I finish the work', 을/를). This 끝나다 (happens) vs 끝내다 (someone does it) split mirrors 시작하다 family logic and is one of Korean's classic intransitive/transitive verb pairs (like 켜지다/켜다, 열리다/열다). The natural opposite is 시작하다 ('to begin'). A nuance of completion: 다 끝났어요 ('it's all done') is the everyday way to announce something is finished, and 끝 alone is an interjection meaning 'the end'. The English-speaker pitfall is using 끝나다 with an object — saying 일을 끝나요 by analogy to English 'I end the work' — when an actor finishes something it must be the transitive 일을 끝내요."},sentence:{ko:"발표 끝났어요.",en:"The presentation is over."},target:"끝났",options:["to end; to be over","everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style"],note:"Natural 해요체 after the public speech."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"질문",romanization:"jilmun",en:"question",pos:"noun",nuance:"질문 is Sino-Korean 質問 — 質 'substance/inquire into' + 問 'ask', literally 'to inquire and ask', i.e. a question put to someone. The set verb is 질문을 하다 (or the single verb 질문하다); the person you ask takes 에게/한테 (or honorific 께 for a teacher): 선생님께 질문을 하다. Its natural partner is 대답/답(答) — a 질문 is answered by a 대답. The big redundancy trap for English speakers is 질문을 물어보다 ✗: 물어보다 already means 'to ask', so this literally says 'ask a question-asking' — say either 질문을 하다 or just 물어보다, never both. Distinguish 질문 (a question seeking information/an answer) from 문제(問題, a 'problem' or exam item) — '3 questions on the test' are 문제 세 개, but 'I have a question for you' is 질문이 있어요. Note answering takes 에: 질문에 답하다, not 질문을 답하다."},sentence:{ko:"질문 있으십니까?",en:"Do you have any questions?"},target:"질문",options:["everyday polite speech style","question","formal polite speech style"],note:"Formal Q&A phrase."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"대답",romanization:"daedap",en:"answer; reply",pos:"noun",nuance:"대답 is Sino-Korean 對答 — 對 'face/respond-to' + 答 'reply', literally 'a facing reply', i.e. the spoken/written response to a question or call. It is the natural partner of 질문(質問): a 질문 is met by a 대답, and the verb is 대답을 하다 / 대답하다. The sharp contrast to master is 대답 vs 정답(正答, 正 'correct' + 答): 대답 is simply 'a/the response someone gives' (it can be right or wrong), while 정답 is specifically 'the correct answer' to a problem — so on a quiz '정답은 3번이에요' = 'the correct answer is number 3', but 그 학생의 대답 = 'that student's response'. Also note 답 alone is a shorter everyday form of both. The particle pitfall mirrors 질문: you answer a question with 에 — 질문에 대답하다 / 질문에 답하다, NOT 질문을 대답하다. English 'answer the phone/door' does not use 대답 either — that is 전화를 받다 / 문을 열다."},sentence:{ko:"질문에는 짧게 대답해도 괜찮아요.",en:"It is okay to answer questions briefly."},target:"대답",options:["answer; reply","everyday polite speech style","formal polite speech style"],note:"Q&A setting."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"격식 있는 자리니까 합쇼체로 시작하세요.",en:"Since it is a formal setting, start in formal polite style."},hl:"시작하"}],canDo:"Switch into casual 반말 with a close friend (drop -요)",id:"chapter-33-b5",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 반말 4형",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"반말 4형",sub:"casual statements, questions, commands, and let's",rows:[{when:"Statement / question",add:"해요체 minus 요",ex:"먹어요 → 먹어. / 먹어?; 가요 → 가. / 가?"},{when:"Command",add:"verb stem + -아/어",ex:"앉다 → 앉아!, 오다 → 와!"},{when:`Proposal / "let's"`,add:"verb stem + -자",ex:"가다 → 가자, 먹다 → 먹자"},{when:"이다 / 아니다",add:"noun + 야/이야; 아니다 → 아니야",ex:"친구야, 학생이야, 아니야"}],examples:[{ko:"나 지금 집에 가.",en:"I'm going home now.",romanization:"na jigeum jibe ga.",note:"Statement: 가요 loses 요 → 가. 나 also matches the casual style."},{ko:"너 밥 먹었어?",en:"Did you eat?",romanization:"neo bap meogeosseo?",note:"Question: 먹었어요 loses 요 → 먹었어? The rising pitch makes it a question."}],more:{func:`반말 is the casual style used with close friends, younger people, and some family members. For many everyday statements and questions, you can start from the 해요체 form and remove 요: 먹어요 → 먹어. Commands use the same -아/어 shape, while casual "let's" uses -자.`,keyPoint:{label:"요-drop is grammar; permission is social",body:`The form is simple: remove 요 for many statements and questions, use -자 for "let's," and use 야/이야 after nouns. The hard part is social timing. Switch to 반말 only after the relationship allows it, often after someone asks 말 놔도 돼요? or suggests 우리 말 놓을까요?`},pronunciation:"In 반말 questions, spelling often stays the same as the statement and pitch does the work: 먹어. falls, 먹어? rises. With names, 지민아 flows as [지미나] because the final ㄴ links to 아."}}],canDo:"Switch into casual 반말 with a close friend (drop -요)",id:"chapter-33-b6",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:5},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A student prepares a formal presentation and then asks how to speak afterward.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"발표에서는 어떤 말투를 써야 해요?",en:"What speech tone should I use in a presentation?",romanization:"balpyoeseoneun eotteon maltureul sseoya haeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"격식 있는 자리니까 합쇼체로 시작하세요.",en:"Since it is a formal setting, start in formal polite style.",romanization:"gyeoksik inneun jarinikka hapsyochero sijakhaseyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그럼 처음에 뭐라고 말해요?",en:"Then what do I say at the beginning?",romanization:"geureom cheoeume mworago malhaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"“지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다”라고 하면 자연스러워요.",en:"It sounds natural to say, “I will begin the presentation now.”",romanization:"jigeumbuteo balpyoreul sijakhagetseumnida-rago hamyeon jayeonseureowoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"끝나고 친구에게도 그렇게 말해요?",en:"Do I speak like that to a friend afterward too?",romanization:"kkeunnago chinguegedo geureotge malhaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"아니요. 친구에게는 해요체로 “발표 끝났어요” 정도가 좋아요.",en:"No. With a friend, haeyo style like “The presentation is over” is good.",romanization:"aniyo. chinguegeneun haeyochero balpyo kkeunnasseoyo jeongdoga joayo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build a formal sentence: 'I study Korean.'",tokens:["저는","한국어를","공부합니다"],correct:"저는 한국어를 공부합니다",explanation:"합니다체 is appropriate for presentations and interviews. 한국어를 stays as the object before the formal verb."}],canDo:"Use the formal polite ending in formal settings (-(스)ㅂ니다, 합니다체)",id:"chapter-33-b7",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:6},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · One topic, two speech levels",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"One topic, two speech levels",chunks:["민서는 내일 수업에서 발표를 해요. 발표는 격식 있는 자리라서 처음에는 “지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다”라고 말할 거예요. 발표가 끝난 뒤에는 “질문 있으십니까?”라고 물을 수 있어요.","하지만 발표 후에 친구와 이야기할 때는 합쇼체를 계속 쓰지 않아요. 친구에게는 “발표 끝났어요”처럼 해요체가 더 자연스러워요. 친한 친구라면 반말도 가능하지만, 처음 만난 사람에게는 존댓말이 안전해요."],translation:"Minseo has a class presentation tomorrow. Since a presentation is a formal setting, she will begin by saying, 'I will begin the presentation now.' After the presentation, she can ask, 'Do you have any questions?' But when talking to a friend after the presentation, she does not keep using formal style. With a friend, haeyo style like 'The presentation is over' sounds more natural. Casual speech is possible with a close friend, but with someone you just met, polite speech is safe.",qas:[{q:"민서는 발표 처음에 뭐라고 말할 거예요?",a:"“지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다”라고 말할 거예요."},{q:"친구에게는 어떤 말투가 더 자연스러워요?",a:"해요체가 더 자연스러워요."},{q:"처음 만난 사람에게는 무엇이 안전해요?",a:"존댓말이 안전해요."}]}],canDo:"Choose between 해요체, 합니다체, and 반말 to fit the listener",id:"chapter-33-b8",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:7},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"For a formal presentation opening, choose:",sentence:null,options:[{text:"지금부터 발표를 시작하겠습니다.",ok:!0},{text:"지금부터 발표 시작해요.",ok:!1},{text:"지금부터 발표해.",ok:!1}],explanation:"합쇼체 fits a formal public opening."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"After the presentation, with a classmate, choose:",sentence:null,options:[{text:"발표 끝났어요.",ok:!0},{text:"발표를 종료하겠습니다.",ok:!1},{text:"발표 종료하옵니다.",ok:!1}],explanation:"해요체 is natural for ordinary polite conversation."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"With someone you just met, the safe default is:",sentence:null,options:[{text:"존댓말",ok:!0},{text:"반말",ok:!1},{text:"아무 말투나",ok:!1}],explanation:"Use polite speech first when the relationship is not close."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"선생님___ 질문이 있어요. (I have a question for the teacher.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"께",ok:!0},{text:"께서",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"께 marks the respected recipient of the question. 께서 marks a respected subject doing an action, and 를 would wrongly mark the teacher as an object."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build a formal sentence: 'I study Korean.'",tokens:["저는","한국어를","공부합니다"],correct:"저는 한국어를 공부합니다",explanation:"합니다체 is appropriate for presentations and interviews. 한국어를 stays as the object before the formal verb."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"After class, you speak casually with a close friend. Which ending fits best?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"수고했어.",ok:!0},{text:"수고하셨습니다.",ok:!1},{text:"수고하십니까?",ok:!1}],explanation:"With a close friend, 반말 can be natural. The other two are much more formal and would sound distant after class."}],canDo:"Switch into casual 반말 with a close friend (drop -요)",id:"chapter-33-b9",chapterId:"chapter-33",index:8}]},{id:"chapter-34",number:34,title:"-(으)시- · N께서 V-(으)시-",goal:"Use -(으)시- to honor the sentence subject.",biteCount:8,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 높임말~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"높임말",romanization:"nopimmal",en:"honorific language",pos:"noun",nuance:"높임말 is not only being polite to the listener. It can honor a third person, such as a teacher or parent."},sentence:{ko:"선생님에 대해 말할 때는 높임말을 써요.",en:"When talking about a teacher, I use honorific language."},target:"높임말",options:["honorific language","teacher","parents"],note:"The honored person can be the person being discussed."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"께서",romanization:"kkeseo",en:"honorific subject particle",pos:"particle",nuance:"께서 honors the subject. It is not the same as 께, which means 'to' a respected person."},sentence:{ko:"선생님께서 오세요.",en:"The teacher is coming."},target:"께서",options:["to a respected person","honorific subject particle","to give or do for a respected person"],note:"The teacher is the respected subject."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"께",romanization:"kke",en:"to a respected person",pos:"particle",nuance:"께 marks a respected recipient. 께서 marks a respected subject."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께 연락드려요.",en:"I contact my parents."},target:"께",options:["honorific subject particle","to a respected person","honorific language"],note:"Parents are the respected recipient."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계시다",romanization:"gyesida",en:"to be; to stay (honorific)",pos:"verb",nuance:"계시다 replaces 있다 only for respected people. For things, use 있다: 가방이 있어요, not 가방이 계세요."},sentence:{ko:"사장님은 지금 안 계세요.",en:"The boss is not here right now."},target:null,options:["to give or do for a respected person","to a respected person","to be; to stay (honorific)"],note:"Honorific form of 있다 for a respected person."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"드시다",romanization:"deusida",en:"to eat; to drink (honorific)",pos:"verb",nuance:"드시다 honors the eater/drinker. It does not honor the food."},sentence:{ko:"선생님이 커피를 드세요.",en:"The teacher drinks coffee."},target:null,options:["to give or do for a respected person","to eat; to drink (honorific)","to a respected person"],note:"Honors the person eating or drinking."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"주무시다",romanization:"jumusida",en:"to sleep (honorific)",pos:"verb",nuance:"주무시다 is a special honorific verb. Do not build it mechanically from 자다."},sentence:{ko:"부모님은 일찍 주무세요.",en:"My parents sleep early."},target:null,options:["to sleep (honorific)","to give or do for a respected person","to a respected person"],note:"Special honorific form of 자다."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"선생님께서 언제 오세요?",en:"When is the teacher coming?"},hl:"께서"}],canDo:"Raise the subject with the honorific suffix (-(으)시-)",id:"chapter-34-b1",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · -(으)시-",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"-(으)시-",sub:"the subject honorific suffix",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -시-",ex:"가다 → 가시다 → 가세요"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -으시-",ex:"읽다 → 읽으시다 → 읽으세요"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ (ㄹ drops)",add:"ㄹ + -시-",ex:"살다 → 사시다 → 사세요"},{when:"Polite speech ending",add:"-(으)시- → -(으)세요",ex:"앉다 → 앉으세요 / 오다 → 오세요"}],examples:[{ko:"선생님이 책을 읽으세요.",en:"The teacher is reading a book.",romanization:"seonsaengnim-i chaeg-eul ilgeuseyo.",note:"The subject 선생님 is honored, so 읽다 takes the -으시- form (consonant stem) → 읽으세요."},{ko:"할머니가 지금 주무세요.",en:"Grandmother is sleeping right now.",romanization:"halmeoni-ga jigeum jumuseyo.",note:"주무세요 already has -시- inside it (from the special honorific 주무시다); the honored subject is 할머니."}],more:{func:"You add -(으)시- to a verb or adjective stem to show respect toward the person doing the action or being described — usually someone older or higher than you. It raises the SUBJECT of the sentence, not the listener.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)시- vs. -아/어요",body:"-아/어요 is polite toward the LISTENER; -(으)시- raises the SUBJECT of the sentence. You can be polite to a friend (친구가 와요) without -시-, but to honor a teacher you add it (선생님이 오세요). Never use -시- about yourself: say 저는 가요, never 저는 가세요."},pronunciation:"읽으세요 is pronounced [일그세요] (ilgeuseyo) — the ㄱ of 읽 moves over to fill the 으 syllable."}}],canDo:"Raise the subject with the honorific suffix (-(으)시-)",id:"chapter-34-b2",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 말씀하시다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"말씀하시다",romanization:"malsseumhasida",en:"to say; to speak (honorific)",pos:"verb",nuance:"말씀하시다 honors the speaker. 말씀드리다 humbles your act of telling someone respected."},sentence:{ko:"선생님께서 말씀하셨어요.",en:"The teacher spoke."},target:"말씀하셨",options:["to say; to speak (honorific)","to give or do for a respected person","to a respected person"],note:"Honors the teacher as speaker."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"드리다",romanization:"deurida",en:"to give or do for a respected person",pos:"verb",nuance:"드리다 respects the recipient, not the subject. It is different from -(으)시-, which honors the subject."},sentence:{ko:"선생님께 말씀드렸어요.",en:"I told the teacher."},target:"드렸",options:["to give or do for a respected person","to be; to stay (honorific)","to eat; to drink (honorific)"],note:"Humbly describes your action toward a respected recipient."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"선생님",romanization:"seonsaengnim",en:"teacher",pos:"noun",nuance:"님 makes the word respectful. 선생님 is also used as a polite title."},sentence:{ko:"선생님께서 곧 오세요.",en:"The teacher is coming soon."},target:"선생님",options:["honorific language","teacher","parents"],note:"Common respected subject."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"부모님",romanization:"bumonim",en:"parents",pos:"noun",nuance:"부모님 includes the honorific suffix 님, but the surrounding form depends on grammatical role. Honor respected parents as the subject with forms such as 부모님께서 주세요 or 부모님이 계세요; honor them as the recipient with 부모님께 드렸어요. 부모님이 있어요 can mean 'I have parents,' and 한테/에게 may occur in casual speech, though 께 is the respectful choice."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께 먼저 연락드릴게요.",en:"I will contact my parents first."},target:"부모님",options:["honorific language","parents","teacher"],note:"Common respected recipient."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사장님",romanization:"sajangnim",en:"boss; business owner",pos:"noun",nuance:"사장님 is 사장 (‘company president / business owner’) plus the respectful title 님. In everyday shops, cafés, restaurants, salons, and small businesses, customers often call the owner or person running the place 사장님 even without knowing their name. In a company, 사장님 usually refers to the president/owner, not every direct supervisor; a supervisor may be 팀장님, 부장님, or 상사. When 사장님 is the respected subject, honorific particles and verbs commonly appear: 사장님께서 오셨어요, 사장님이 계세요."},sentence:{ko:"사장님은 지금 안 계세요.",en:"The boss is not here right now."},target:"사장님",options:["honorific language","teacher","boss; business owner"],note:"Common workplace honorific noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"고객님",romanization:"gogaengnim",en:"customer (honorific)",pos:"noun",nuance:"고객님 is 고객 (‘customer/client’) plus 님 and belongs to respectful service language. Staff use it when directly addressing or referring to a customer in stores, banks, clinics, call centers, delivery services, and online support. 손님 is broader and warmer for a guest or customer in a shop/restaurant; 고객 is more institutional, and 고객님 adds direct respect. Outside a service relationship, repeatedly calling someone 고객님 can sound transactional. When the customer is the honored subject or recipient, service language often uses 고객님께서 and 고객님께."},sentence:{ko:"고객님께 다시 안내해 드리겠습니다.",en:"I will guide the customer again."},target:"고객님",options:["teacher","parents","customer (honorific)"],note:"Service-register honorific noun."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"선생님께서 언제 오세요?",en:"When is the teacher coming?"},hl:"선생님"}],canDo:"Use 께서 and special honorific verbs (계시다 · 드시다 · 주무시다)",id:"chapter-34-b3",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N께서 V-(으)시-",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"N께서 V-(으)시-",sub:"께서 and the special honorific verbs",rows:[{when:"Subject particle 이/가 → 께서",add:"N + 께서",ex:"선생님이 → 선생님께서 / 할머니가 → 할머니께서"},{when:"있다 (to be / stay somewhere) → special form",add:"있다 → 계시다",ex:"할아버지께서 집에 계세요."},{when:"먹다 / 마시다 (eat / drink) → special form",add:"먹다 → 드시다 (잡수시다)",ex:"아버지께서 밥을 드세요."},{when:"자다 (to sleep) → special form",add:"자다 → 주무시다",ex:"어머니께서 주무세요."}],examples:[{ko:"할아버지께서 방에 계세요.",en:"Grandfather is in the room.",romanization:"harabeoji-kkeseo bang-e gyeseyo.",note:"있다 (existence) becomes the special honorific 계시다 → 계세요, and the subject takes 께서 instead of 가."},{ko:"선생님께서 점심을 드세요.",en:"The teacher is eating lunch.",romanization:"seonsaengnim-kkeseo jeomsim-eul deuseyo.",note:"먹다 → 드시다 (everyday honorific); 잡수시다 is the more formal version. The subject takes 께서."}],more:{func:"When the subject deserves respect, the subject particle 이/가 changes to 께서, and the verb takes -(으)시-. A few very common verbs have their own honorific forms you just have to memorize, instead of adding -시-.",keyPoint:{label:"있다: 있으세요 vs. 계세요",body:"When 있다 means existence/being somewhere ('to be there'), the honorific is 계세요 (할아버지께서 집에 계세요). When 있다 means possession ('to have'), use 있으세요, NOT 계세요 (선생님께서 시간이 있으세요). 계세요 is only for a person being present, never for having things."},pronunciation:"계세요 is usually said [게세요] (geseyo) in everyday speech — the ㅖ softens to ㅔ after ㄱ."}}],canDo:"Use 께서 and special honorific verbs (계시다 · 드시다 · 주무시다)",id:"chapter-34-b4",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:3},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N께 V-아/어 드리다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"N께 V-아/어 드리다",sub:"honoring the person you do something for",rows:[{when:"Recipient particle 에게/한테 → 께",add:"N + 께",ex:"동생한테 → 할머니께 / 친구에게 → 부모님께"},{when:"주다 (to give) → honorific",add:"주다 → 드리다",ex:"선물을 줘요 → 선물을 드려요"},{when:"V-아/어 주다 (do for someone) → -아/어 드리다",add:"V-아/어 + 드리다",ex:"도와주다 → 도와 드리다 / 읽어 주다 → 읽어 드리다"},{when:"ㅏ/ㅗ stem → -아 드리다; other vowels → -어 드리다",add:"V-아/어 + 드리다",ex:"사다 → 사 드려요 / 만들다 → 만들어 드려요"}],examples:[{ko:"할머니께 선물을 드렸어요.",en:"I gave Grandmother a present.",romanization:"halmeoni-kke seonmur-eul deuryeosseoyo.",note:"The recipient 할머니 is honored, so 주다 → 드리다 and 에게/한테 → 께."},{ko:"제가 부모님께 밥을 사 드렸어요.",en:"I bought my parents a meal.",romanization:"je-ga bumonim-kke bab-eul sa deuryeosseoyo.",note:"사 주다 ('buy for someone') → 사 드리다 because the recipient 부모님 deserves respect."}],more:{func:"While -(으)시- honors the subject, 드리다 honors the RECIPIENT — the person you do something FOR. 주다 ('give / do for someone') becomes 드리다, and the recipient particle 에게/한테 becomes 께.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)시- vs. -아/어 드리다",body:"-(으)시- raises the person DOING the action (subject); 드리다 raises the person RECEIVING it (recipient). 선생님께서 가르치세요 = the teacher (subject) teaches; 선생님께 가르쳐 드려요 = I teach FOR the teacher. The 드리다 sentence usually has 저/제가 as the (humble) subject."},pronunciation:"읽어 드려요 is pronounced [일거 드려요] (ilgeo deuryeoyo) — the ㄹㄱ of 읽 links into the next syllable."}}],canDo:"Honor the person I do something for (N께 V-아/어 드리다)",id:"chapter-34-b5",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:4},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A learner talks about a teacher and parents respectfully.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"선생님께서 언제 오세요?",en:"When is the teacher coming?",romanization:"seonsaengnimkkeseo eonje oseyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"곧 오셔서 먼저 말씀하실 거예요.",en:"They will come soon and speak first.",romanization:"got osyeoseo meonjeo malsseumhasil geoyeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"부모님께도 연락드렸어요?",en:"Did you contact your parents too?",romanization:"bumonimkkedo yeollakdeuryeosseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 어머니께 먼저 말씀드렸어요.",en:"Yes, I told my mother first.",romanization:"ne, eomeonikke meonjeo malsseumdeuryeosseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"사장님은 지금 계세요?",en:"Is the boss here now?",romanization:"sajangnimeun jigeum gyeseyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 회의실에 계세요.",en:"Yes, they are in the meeting room.",romanization:"ne, hoeuisire gyeseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I gave it to my parents.'",tokens:["부모님께","드렸어요"],correct:"부모님께 드렸어요",explanation:"께 honors the recipient, and 드리다 is humble for giving upward. This combination respects 부모님."}],canDo:"Raise the subject with the honorific suffix (-(으)시-)",id:"chapter-34-b6",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:5},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · Who receives the respect?",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"Who receives the respect?",chunks:["민서는 선생님에 대해 말할 때 높임말을 써요. 선생님이 주어일 때는 “선생님께서 오세요”라고 말해요. 선생님이 말하는 사람일 때는 “선생님께서 말씀하셨어요”라고 해요.","하지만 민서가 선생님에게 말한 일을 설명할 때는 “선생님께 말씀드렸어요”라고 해요. 께서는 주어를 높이고, 께는 받는 사람을 높여요. 그래서 높임말을 쓸 때는 먼저 누가 행동하는지, 그 행동이 누구에게 가는지 봐야 해요."],translation:"Minseo uses honorific language when talking about her teacher. When the teacher is the subject, she says, 'The teacher is coming.' When the teacher is the speaker, she says, 'The teacher spoke.' But when Minseo explains that she told something to the teacher, she says, 'I told the teacher.' 께서 honors the subject, and 께 honors the recipient. So when using honorifics, first check who is doing the action and who receives the action.",qas:[{q:"께서는 누구를 높여요?",a:"주어를 높여요."},{q:"께는 누구를 높여요?",a:"받는 사람을 높여요."},{q:"선생님이 말한 경우에는 어떻게 말해요?",a:"“선생님께서 말씀하셨어요”라고 말해요."}]}],canDo:"Use 께서 and special honorific verbs (계시다 · 드시다 · 주무시다)",id:"chapter-34-b7",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:6},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence honors the teacher as subject?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"선생님께서 오세요.",ok:!0},{text:"선생님께 질문해요.",ok:!1},{text:"제가 오세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"께서 marks the respected subject, and 오세요 honors that subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence honors the teacher as recipient?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"선생님께 말씀드렸어요.",ok:!0},{text:"선생님께서 말씀드렸어요.",ok:!1},{text:"선생님이 제가 말씀하셨어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"께 marks the respected recipient, and 드리다 humbly describes your action."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses 계시다 correctly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"부모님은 집에 계세요.",ok:!0},{text:"가방은 집에 계세요.",ok:!1},{text:"제가 집에 계세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"계시다 is for respected people, not objects or yourself."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"선생님___ 오셨어요. (The teacher came.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"께서",ok:!0},{text:"께",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"께서 marks a respected subject. 께 marks a respected recipient, not the person doing the action."},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I gave it to my parents.'",tokens:["부모님께","드렸어요"],correct:"부모님께 드렸어요",explanation:"께 honors the recipient, and 드리다 is humble for giving upward. This combination respects 부모님."}],canDo:"Honor the person I do something for (N께 V-아/어 드리다)",id:"chapter-34-b8",chapterId:"chapter-34",index:7}]}],o={generatedFrom:e,chapters:t};export{t as chapters,o as default,e as generatedFrom};
